HT_DATA.load("detail", {"spUNpyF58BY":{"wy":"HN's default recommendation for understanding the Fourier transform intuitively; many say it was the explanation that made it click.","q":{"x":"this was the first thing that ever really made sense to me","i":24286802,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":45762781,"author":"garbageman","month":"2025-10","story":"How the cochlea computes (2024)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains clearly to laypeople how a waveform breaks into components.","quote":"This video does a great job explaining what it is and how it works to the layman.","text":"It's an absolutely brilliant bit of maths that breaks a complex waveform into the individual components. Kind of like taking an orchestral song and then working out each individual instrument's contribution. Learning about this left me honestly aghast and in shock that it's not only possible but that someone (Joseph Fourier) figured it out and then shared it with the world. This video does a great job explaining what it is and how it works to the layman. 3blue1brown - [VIDEO]"},{"id":38689016,"author":"TimSchumann","month":"2023-12","story":"3Blue1Brown Calculus Blog Series","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualization revealed how the Fourier Transform works, not just what it does.","quote":"His visualization of the Fourier Transform[1] actually enabled me to understand","text":"Grant makes some amazing content. His visualization of the Fourier Transform[1] actually enabled me to understand the how it was happening behind the what was happening with one of the most used algorithms in computing. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34206912,"author":"sjaak","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Concepts that clicked only years after you first encountered them?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made the Fourier transform click for the commenter.","quote":"This is what made it click for me, can recommend it","text":"This is what made it click for me, can recommend it: 3Blue1Brown -- But what is the Fourier Transform? A visual introduction. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33637917,"author":"SJSque","month":"2022-11","story":"Demystifying Fourier analysis","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Highly recommended treatment of the Fourier transform.","quote":"I can also highly recommend 3Blue1Brown's treatment of the topic","text":"I can also highly recommend 3Blue1Brown's treatment of the topic: [VIDEO] (...which I see now is cited in the video that you link to!)"},{"id":29388499,"author":"faebi","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: Videos that changed your life or how you think","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explained how multiple frequencies coexist in one cable.","quote":"Then I learned about the Fourier transform in this beautiful video","text":"I never understood how multiple electrical frequencies can be in a copper cable. I always thought they must merge and therefore it must be impossible to find the right set of original frequencies and their strength. Then I learned about the Fourier transform in this beautiful video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29048708,"author":"andybak","month":"2021-10","story":"Circular Harmonics","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Channel everyone interested in this topic should follow.","quote":"Dude really needs to subscribe to 3Blue1Brown","text":"Dude really needs to subscribe to 3Blue1Brown: [VIDEO] (but anyone interested in stuff like this should be. I guess I'm surprised to see someone like this writing a blog post who isn't regularly checking out that channel)"},{"id":24286802,"author":"justjonathan","month":"2020-08","story":"Discrete Cosine Transform in Video Compression – Explain Like I'm Five","stance":"strong_rec","why":"First Fourier explanation that made sense, with amazing visuals from a great teacher.","quote":"this was the first thing that ever really made sense to me","text":"This is an explanation (from the amazing three blue one brown) of the DFT not the DCT, but this was the first thing that ever really made sense to me. It is explained by someone who really knows math incredibly well and is an amazing teacher, with amazing visuals: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45146563,"author":"N_Lens","month":"2025-09","story":"What Is the Fourier Transform?","stance":"rec","why":"More intuitive than the article, which has more practical examples.","quote":"I found the 3Blue1Brown explanation to be more intuitive to understand","text":"I found the 3Blue1Brown explanation to be more intuitive to understand, but this article has more practical examples/details. Here's the 3Blue1Brown explanation: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":42963365,"author":"mananaysiempre","month":"2025-02","story":"Pre-Trained Large Language Models Use Fourier Features for Addition (2024)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows well what the Fourier transform does, though not why it is useful.","quote":"Have you tried the 3Blue1Brown video on the topic[1]?","text":"(Almost-)linear models do linear things, it seems, and the Fourier transform is the quintessential linear thing. It is also an extremely neat piece of the real world, but I’m hesitant to guess your background and offer an explanation because your phrasing makes me suspect an engineering one. With concepts usually being the first to be culled in a course targeted at engineers, there could be quite a bit of concept debt to pay off before I could really offer something I could honestly call an explanation. Have you tried the 3Blue1Brown video on the topic[1]? It does not AFAIR offer any answers as to why the Fourier transform should exist or be useful, but it does show very well what it does in the immediate sense. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38655390,"author":"sizzzzlerz","month":"2023-12","story":"An Interactive Guide to the Fourier Transform","stance":"rec","why":"Good alternative explanation of the continuous Fourier Transform.","quote":"A good alternative explanation can be found on Grant Sanderson's 3 Blue 1 Brown channel","text":"Just a nit. The pair of equations the author showed at the beginning of the article are not the equations of the Fourier Transform and its inverse. The Transform is a continuous function operating on an infinite input. The equation for the Transform involves the use of the integral taken over +/- infinity. What is shown, using the summation operator, is a discrete form on the Transform where the input is a limited time series. A good alternative explanation can be found on Grant Sanderson's 3 Blue 1 Brown channel [VIDEO]"},{"id":36793718,"author":"superkuh","month":"2023-07","story":"What Is Radio Spectrum?","stance":"rec","why":"One of five resources for learning RF and modulation.","quote":"3blue1brown's video tutorial on the fourier transform","text":"There's no all in one stop for understanding radio but these 5 resources helped me a lot. The RF Get Down courses. This is by far the best material online to learn RF and how modulation works: [LINK] 3blue1brown's video tutorial on the fourier transform: [VIDEO] AT&T Archives: Similiarities of Wave Behavior [LINK] Quadrature Signals: Complex, But Not Complicated by Richard Lyons, [LINK] [LINK] Antenna Theory's website and forums, [LINK] I also highly recommend getting a $20 rtl-sdr USB software defined radio dongle. They're great toys for learning RF, what modulation is , and exploring the spectrum."},{"id":32295353,"author":"ryan-duve","month":"2022-07","story":"Shor, I’ll do it (2007)","stance":"rec","why":"Visual clarification of Fourier transform and periodicity for visual learners.","quote":"this video from 3Blue1Brown about the [classic] Fourier Transform might help clarify the relationship to periodicity","text":"If the part about the tack on the board under the clocks wasn't clear, and you're a visual learner, this video from 3Blue1Brown about the [classic] Fourier Transform might help clarify the relationship to periodicity: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27231162,"author":"itissid","month":"2021-05","story":"An Interactive Guide to the Fourier Transform (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Concise and intuitive Fourier explanation.","quote":"I think that is pretty concise and intuitive too","text":"What about the 3 blue 1 brown video on it? I think that is pretty concise and intuitive too but does not seem to appear on this thread [VIDEO]"},{"id":23166863,"author":"jmiserez","month":"2020-05","story":"Show HN: SHA-256 Animation","stance":"rec","why":"Super intuitive Fourier transform animation.","quote":"his Fourier transform animation is super intuitive","text":"3blue1brown is great, e.g. his Fourier transform animation is super intuitive: [VIDEO] He has opensourced his animation engine \"manim\" used in his videos: [LINK]"},{"id":21285468,"author":"quickthrower2","month":"2019-10","story":"The “Windmill” Problem on the 2011 International Mathematical Olympiad [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Beautiful visual introduction to the Fourier Transform.","quote":"I recommend watching the","text":"I recommend watching the \"But what is the Fourier Transform? A visual introduction.\" [VIDEO] . Beautiful."},{"id":19705619,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-04","story":"Why have so many physicists shrugged off the paradoxes of quantum mechanics?","stance":"rec","why":"Prerequisite episode explaining how the Fourier Transform works.","quote":"the previous episode[2] is a recommended prerequisite for background on how the Fourier Transform works","text":"3Blue1Brown has an extremely good explanation[1] of the intrinsic uncertainty, and why it's separate from measurement uncertainty. (the previous episode[2] is a recommended prerequisite for background on how the Fourier Transform works) > emerges through a different, as yet unknown, mechanism. In 3Blue1Bron's explanation[1], he shows how the intrinsic uncertainty is an inherent trade-off of trying to measure both position and frequency. A short wave packet only a few wavelengths long correlates with a narrow (precise) range of positions, but also correlates well with a very wide range of frequencies due. A Heisenberg-like uncertainty exists any time you are working with weave packets with length near the wavelength. 3Blue1Brown gives a very good example using Doppler radar. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45373378,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2025-09","story":"Smartphone Cameras Go Hyperspectral","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is a combination of things that almost breaks my brain, but it works, and it's brilliant! First, you need to understand coded masks[1,2]. This is a way to use an LCD or other array to mask off parts of a scene so that very specific parts of it are sampled, but the rest aren't, to get a single, high resolution analog value. Then you switch to other masks, and get more values. You can then work backwards in the math through the known mask shapes, to get the original image with far fewer samples that would be required one at a time. Think of the above as a 2d visual version of the Fourier transform[3,4]. This transform is used heavily to compress images throwing away most of the bits in an image without losing it's essence. The analysis they're talking about uses a very specially printed card. It isn't just something generated with a standard 4 ink printer, each \"dot\" is a separate unique ink with tightly controlled spectral curves, these form a virtual version of the above masks. When you view these through a sample, it can then give an idea of the spectral response of the camera, and the liquid, by using the many different known response curves of the \"dots\" to work backwards, and generate the 1 dimensional very tight response curve of a hyperspectral imager, by figuring out where each \"dot\" is in the scene, then averaging that dot's intensity across the RGB values of the picture taken by the camera. Today's cameras have sufficient resolution and bit depth that you get th"},{"id":37061078,"author":"monsieurbanana","month":"2023-08","story":"G9.js: Automatically Interactive Graphics","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a math video made of 20 minutes of changing graphics.","quote":"modern math videos on youtube, like this one","text":"Because it's a fast way to get that interactivity, rather than having to explicitly code it yourself. A good example are modern math videos on youtube, like this one (not saying that they're using G9.js in particular): [VIDEO] It's 20 minutes of changing graphics. You could code all of that with P5, and it would be an excruciatingly long process. G9.js would get you there in a fraction of the time."},{"id":23869462,"author":"Kye","month":"2020-07","story":"Music and Measure Theory (2015) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter followed the channel after seeing this video.","quote":"I followed this channel back when I found the video on Fourier transforms[1]","text":"This is one of the few people who connects math and music in a way that's both useful and interesting. I followed this channel back when I found the video on Fourier transforms[1], but never looked at other videos. I've never really thought about the math of consonance and dissonance, but I use it all the time without realizing it. For example: a hypersaw. That's 7 saws slightly detuned played at the same time. The more you detune them, the worse it sounds. If you own Serum, you can hear this in realtime if you make a 7 voice saw and play with the detune knob. You can use an LFO to modulate this and get a sound that's a little uneasy while still somehow being harmonious. It's perfect for dark/spooky music. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16857613,"author":"enkiv2","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"reference","why":"One entry in a long list of recommended talks.","quote":"","text":"Ted Nelson's Computers for Cynics probably doesn't contain technical information that's new to any of you, but Ted has a knack for reframing things in ways that make the arbitrariness of certain historical decisions clear: [LINK] On the subject of hypertext, The Web That Wasn't gives a nice history of the idea (for anybody who thinks it starts with TBL -- surprisingly many people!): [LINK] Another reframing-oriented talk is Clay Shirky's \"It's not information overload, it's filter failure\", which ultimately leads to Shirky suggesting the kinds of user-oriented filtering features that Mastodon has implemented: [LINK] At the intersection of neurology and information science, Peter Watts always has something interesting to say, and as a former marine biologist focusing on the nervous system of starfish, this is absolutely in his wheelhouse: [LINK] This one (\"moving away from defensive programming\") justified strong typing in a pretty clear way: [LINK] Dan Dennett is just as relevant as Doug Hofstadter when it comes to metacognition: [LINK] Forgotten Ideas in Computer Science starts slow, but if you don't have much of a historical background (like, if you're only vaguely aware of what happened in CS in the 70s), it's a laundry list of things you should look up and be aware of before you start your next project: [LINK] Everybody should understand procedural generation: [LINK] Likewise, since AI is hyped up right now, we should all remind ourselves that IA is a thing too: [LINK] Mo"}],"r":[["ZSRHeXYDLko",2],["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"s1i-dnAH9Y4":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as a beautiful, approachable explanation of analog mechanical computation, and many have rewatched it.","q":{"x":"It illustrates the problems and solutions near perfectly, and I've watched it way too many times.","i":18533594,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":41890981,"author":"kibwen","month":"2024-10","story":"Knocker uppers: Waking up the workers in industrial Britain (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Must-watch 1950s film on battleship mechanical fire control computers.","quote":"Everyone reading this right now needs to check out this 1950s naval training video","text":"Everyone reading this right now needs to check out this 1950s naval training video on the mechanical fire control computers of battleships: [VIDEO] For fun, before you watch, think for yourself how you'd implement addition, multiplication, squaring, etc. with only gears and other mechanisms."},{"id":18533594,"author":"boomlinde","month":"2018-11","story":"When mechanical analog computers ruled the waves (2014)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Illustrates problems and solutions near perfectly; rewatched many times.","quote":"It illustrates the problems and solutions near perfectly, and I've watched it way too many times.","text":"The whole training film on mechanical computers, for navy mechanics I believe: [VIDEO] It illustrates the problems and solutions near perfectly, and I've watched it way too many times."},{"id":18534011,"author":"Bjartr","month":"2018-11","story":"When mechanical analog computers ruled the waves (2014)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full training video behind the article's clips.","quote":"I highly recommend it. It's a fascinating trip into engineering, computing, and the past.","text":"I did not see any link to the full training video[0] the clips in this article were taken from. I highly recommend it. It's a fascinating trip into engineering, computing, and the past. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14452604,"author":"coreyp_1","month":"2017-05","story":"The Turing Tumble lets you and your kids build real mechanical computers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains mechanisms of fire control computers; beautiful and elegant.","quote":"It's beautiful in its elegance! I have watched it on multiple occasions just for the sheer beauty of the mechanisms involved.","text":"[VIDEO] This is an old military instructional video about fire (artillery) control computers, explaining the mechanical aspects of how they work. It's beautiful in its elegance! I have watched it on multiple occasions just for the sheer beauty of the mechanisms involved. And, yes, of course I'm a nerd!"},{"id":43864178,"author":"mrandish","month":"2025-05","story":"Millihertz 5 Mechanical Computer (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent 1950s film on fundamentals of mechanical fire control computers.","quote":"explaining the fundamentals of how mechanical fire control computers work to solve complex problems is excellent.","text":"I just smile hearing the term \"Millihertz Computer\". I'd love it if building and designing mechanical and analog computers grew as a hobby/educational activity as I find them both fascinating and somehow satisfying. Also, this 1950s Naval Training film explaining the fundamentals of how mechanical fire control computers work to solve complex problems is excellent. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39580865,"author":"eigenvalue","month":"2024-03","story":"Telegram for America (1956) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Old film that is a great explainer of these computers.","quote":"another one of these old films about them which is a great explainer","text":"Yes, these are awesome. There is another one of these old films about them which is a great explainer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33593289,"author":"ddulaney","month":"2022-11","story":"The Bendix Central Air Data Computer (CADC)","stance":"rec","why":"Great 1950s Navy film on individual fire control computer components.","quote":"there’s a great 1950’s US Navy training film on the individual components of analog fire control computers here","text":"If you’re interested in some of the individual components, there’s a great 1950’s US Navy training film on the individual components of analog fire control computers here: [VIDEO] The ideas are the same, but the technology is about a decade earlier than the CADC, so everything is noticeably more primitive."},{"id":20242519,"author":"adrianN","month":"2019-06","story":"Wootz steel","stance":"rec","why":"Very interesting video on mechanical fire control computers.","quote":"Here's a very interesting video about them","text":"Mechanical computers for fire control where the standard solution until very recently. Here's a very interesting video about them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19425272,"author":"leggomylibro","month":"2019-03","story":"The Comdyna GP-6 analogue computer that I use daily at work","stance":"rec","why":"1950s training video on battleship firing-solution computers.","quote":"You can watch an old training video about it from the '50s","text":"Apparently the Navy also used mechanical 'computers' on old battleships to calculate firing solutions. Sailors would adjust cranks and shafts to set the input values, and those inputs were connected to cams which were machined in the shape of solutions to the equations that needed to be solved. You can watch an old training video about it from the '50s: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16771411,"author":"peterkelly","month":"2018-04","story":"Walt Disney's MultiPlane Camera (1957) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"1953 explanation of US Navy mechanical analog computers.","quote":"One of my favourites is this 1953 explanation of the workings of mechanical analog computers used by the US Navy","text":"One of my favourites is this 1953 explanation of the workings of mechanical analog computers used by the US Navy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15114455,"author":"kqr","month":"2017-08","story":"One of the most complex wristwatches ever made","stance":"rec","why":"Approachable, informative intro to mechanical analog computation.","quote":"It's very approachable and informative.","text":"I recommend watching this video if you're interested in learning about multivariate analysis using mechanical computers. It's very approachable and informative. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12365013,"author":"Sharlin","month":"2016-08","story":"Optical solutions to NP-Complete problems","stance":"rec","why":"Old US Navy film on mechanical fire control calculators.","quote":"is pretty fascinating","text":"This old US Navy training film on mechanical fire control calculators (called \"computers\" back then, used to calculate firing solutions on warships) is pretty fascinating: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7420288,"author":"NamTaf","month":"2014-03","story":"Gears of war: When mechanical analog computers ruled the waves","stance":"rec","why":"1950s Navy training film on mechanical fire control computers.","quote":"Obligatory link to youtube video of a 1950s training video on the mechanical fire control computers","text":"Obligatory link to youtube video of a 1950s training video on the mechanical fire control computers (which I stole from HN last time this sort of topic came up): [VIDEO] edit: This video is the same footage featured throughout the article, in fact."},{"id":45354698,"author":"ants_everywhere","month":"2025-09","story":"Is life a form of computation?","stance":"reference","why":"Classic example of analog computers not operating on symbols.","quote":"the classic video on fire control computers","text":"analog computers don't generally compute by operating on symbols. For example see the classic video on fire control computers [VIDEO] OP's specific phrasing is that they \"map symbols to symbols\". Analog computers don't do that. Some can, but that's not their definition. Turing machines et al. are a model of computation in mathematics. Humans do math by operating on symbols, so that's why that model operates on symbols. It's not an inherent part of the definition."},{"id":45355594,"author":"lmm","month":"2025-09","story":"Is life a form of computation?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example in a debate over symbolic computation.","quote":"","text":"> analog computers don't generally compute by operating on symbols. For example see the classic video on fire control computers [VIDEO] > OP's specific phrasing is that they \"map symbols to symbols\". Analog computers don't do that. Some can, but that's not their definition. How is that not symbolic? Fundamentally that kind of computer maps the positions of some rods or gears or what have you to the positions of some other rods or gears or what have you, and the first rods or gears are symbolising motion or elevation or what have you and the final one is symbolising barrel angle or what have you. (And sure, you might physically connect the final gear directly to the actual gun barrel, but that's not the part that's computation; the computation is the part happening with the little gears and rods in the middle, and they have symbolic meanings)."},{"id":43662050,"author":"7373737373","month":"2025-04","story":"Vacheron Constantin breaks the world record for most complicated wristwatch","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as inspiration for analog complications in watches.","quote":"Maybe also analog ones!","text":"I do hope watchmakers start to integrate \"computational\" (instead of temporal) complications into their watches, like a mechanical turing machine or other tiny mechanical computers or calculators which I believe have never been constructed this small. Inspiration: Wooden Turing Machine: [LINK] Curta Calculator: [LINK] Zuse Z1 Computer: [LINK] Maybe also analog ones!: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41549039,"author":"RichardCA","month":"2024-09","story":"Founder Mode, hackers, and being bored by tech","stance":"reference","why":"Shows WWII fire control engineering as programming, using computer terminology unexplained.","quote":"The engineering knowledge that went into the WWII fire control computers was clearly a form of programming.","text":"The engineering knowledge that went into the WWII fire control computers was clearly a form of programming. You will also notice the words \"computer\", \"input\", and \"output\" are used without needing to be explained. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30529252,"author":"asciimov","month":"2022-03","story":"The Nimatron: first 'video game' debuted in 1939","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fixed mechanical computers with program in gears.","quote":"","text":"Over the years we have evolved the meaning of computer from something that calculates or reckons to a electronic device that runs software. In reality any black box that has a one-to-one relationship between inputs and outputs can be considered a computer. It might be easier to think of mechanical computers, such as the WW2 fire control computers aboard Navy Ships[0] or more famously the Antikythera mechanism[1]. These are fixed devices, they compute values from inputs. The \"program\" is stored in the gears camshafts and differentials and ratios between them. Similarly, fixed digital computers such as the Nimatron have their operations stored in relays and digital logic. These sorts of computers don't have a list of instructions. They just have schematics, inputs going through electronic circuits that wind up at outputs. You can do a lot of calculating with just simple logic gates. [0] - [VIDEO] [1] - [LINK]"},{"id":19978891,"author":"Animats","month":"2019-05","story":"Five Hundred and Seven Mechanical Movements (1908)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for differentials acting as adders in gun predictors.","quote":"A differential is an adder.","text":"A differential is an adder.[1] This is a differential hooked up as a doubler. Mechanical analog computers, especially gun predictors, had lots of setups like that. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7420520,"author":"D9u","month":"2014-03","story":"Gears of war: When mechanical analog computers ruled the waves","stance":"reference","why":"1953 training video on the same subject.","quote":"the following 1953 training video dealing with this same subject.","text":"Interestingly, several months ago I came across the following 1953 training video dealing with this same subject. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wIC-iGDTU40",1],["QUcTsFe1PVs",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["hB6eY73sLV0",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["M4AhABManTw",1],["qL0BB2PRY7k",1],["uzkD5SeuwzM",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["dqwpQarrDwk",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["Ja5Q75hf6QI",1],["uknEhXyZgsg",1],["MzzD2F73iGU",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["A8eCGOqgvH4",1],["oOlDewpCfZQ",1],["fHNmRkzxHWs",1],["SEDuVF7kiPU",1],["jMmUoZh3Hq4",1]]},"WpkDN78P884":{"wy":"Frequently recommended as an influential talk on decoupled, testable architecture, though some commenters push back on Martin's TDD advocacy.","q":{"x":"The talk that had the most influence on how I write software is Robert 'Uncle Bob' Martin's \"Architecture: The Lost Years","i":18218259,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":33185974,"author":"SevenNation","month":"2022-10","story":"Using a framework will harm the maintenance of your software","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Actionable guidance on isolating frameworks behind boundaries","quote":"There's an excellent, if older, presentation called \"Architecture, the Lost Years","text":"> Many people will argue that not using a framework means write everything yourself. This is a false dichotomy. We can use libraries and frameworks just fine. ... > But we should give them a clear, and well-isolated place in our project. ... There's an excellent, if older, presentation called \"Architecture, the Lost Years\" that addresses exactly this problem and gives very specific, actionable guidance on how to solve it: [VIDEO] But how to pull off this decoupling? The answer is both obvious and surprising. Define a boundary. On one side is the framework. On the other side is your code. Through the boundary, only dumb data structure can pass. JSON works. So do structs. So does XML. The point is that these are behaviorless blobs of data. This buys you a lot. You can run your application completely detached from the network, a database, and other expensive dependencies. So testing becomes much simpler and easier. And of course, you can replace the framework by writing new boundary code. The other big lesson has something to do with why frameworks get added to a project in the first place. When designing a system, the goal should be to defer major decisions for a long as possible. That's when the best information is available. Sometimes you can just defer until the point becomes moot, which is a major win. Reaching for a framework before being forced to do so sets up the kind of situation the author is talking about."},{"id":18218259,"author":"catwell","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Most influential talk on how the commenter writes software","quote":"The talk that had the most influence on how I write software is Robert 'Uncle Bob' Martin's \"Architecture: The Lost Years","text":"The talk that had the most influence on how I write software is Robert 'Uncle Bob' Martin's \"Architecture: The Lost Years\" [1]. Being a Ruby conference keynote, it helps if you know how Ruby on Rails works, but the lessons apply to all software. Another one would be \"Let It Crash! The Erlang Approach to Building Reliable Services\" by Brian Troutwine. [2] I don't know if you would call it \"technical\" but Brett Slatkin's talk on Cohort Analysis is something anyone interested in tools to analyze how our users behave should watch. [3] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":7637367,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2014-04","story":"TDD is fun","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent architecture talk by Uncle Bob","quote":"See also Uncle Bob's excellent architecture talk","text":"Excellent riposte! Mine was going to be somewhat less polite :-) Can't stress enough that TDD is not about testing (sth. DHH, like many if not most critics gets wrong). It is about development and design. The fact that you get a pretty decent test suite out of it is a nice side effect. (Love me those side-effects, wouldn't want to program without 'em). I also find that whenever I skimp, it always comes back to bite me. And usually it was because I hadn't understood the problem-space well enough to write a proper test. That's one important part of TDD for me: I have to understand the requirements well enough to actually write a test for them. And the OP is also right in that Rails is a bad environment for TDD, as far as I can tell, because all the magic and auto-integration gets in the way and has to be laboriously stubbed/mocked etc. Maybe it's time the community got over Rails. (See also Uncle Bob's excellent architecture talk: [VIDEO] )."},{"id":30951682,"author":"Barrera","month":"2022-04","story":"Unit Testing is Overrated (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Source of decoupling network from logic with dumb data structures","quote":"The whole thing is worth watching to put it into context.","text":"> Although the design above is perfectly valid in terms of OOP, neither of these classes are actually unit-testable. Because LocationProvider depends on its own instance of HttpClient and SolarCalculator in turn depends on LocationProvider, it’s impossible to isolate the business logic that may be contained within methods of these classes. The design of those classes is bad, and the perceived need to reach for mocks (or interfaces) is telling you that. The next step the author takes is to go for interfaces. That's not what I'd do. Instead, I'd decouple network from calculation with intermediate dumb data structures. The network side produces the data structure and the calculation side consumes it. The network side can further be broken down with a transformation of the API call result into the dumb data structure used for calculations. This idea comes from an old Bob Martin presentation [1]. The whole thing is worth watching to put it into context. This is the kind of thing that never seems to get discussed in these pieces praising integration tests over unit tests. For even better results, ditch the classes altogether and just use functions. It's quite surprising how badly designs come off the rails with the \"everything is a class\" model. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17886981,"author":"cellularmitosis","month":"2018-08","story":"My bag of tricks – loose notes, design patterns, rules-of-thumb","stance":"rec","why":"Helped commenter understand testable code design","quote":"This \"Architecture: the lost years\" keynote from uncle bob was helpful for me","text":"This \"Architecture: the lost years\" keynote from uncle bob was helpful for me: [VIDEO] I've also struggled with this notion of \"untestable\" code. Untestable code seems to usually be a big pile of function calls. Testable code seems to be little islands of functionality which are connected by interfaces where only data is exchanged. Practically, this seems to be about shying away from interfaces which look like 'doThis()', 'doThat()', and more like 'apply(data)'. Less imperative micro-management and more functional-core/imperative-shell. Edit: a network analogy might be: think about the differences between a remote procedure call API vs. simply exchanging declarative data (REST)."},{"id":12260796,"author":"dreistdreist","month":"2016-08","story":"Ask HN: 8 years working, now 3-4 months off to learn. Looking for advice","stance":"rec","why":"Decent architecture talk among study resources","quote":"This uncle bob talk is not too bad","text":"Microsoft Application Architecture Guide [LINK] Domain Driven Design (The book is hard to read, but worth it I think) [LINK] This uncle bob talk is not too bad [VIDEO] I think the microsoft guide is the most comprehensive, but don't limit yourself to it."},{"id":11757273,"author":"jxxcarlson","month":"2016-05","story":"Hanami – web framework for Ruby","stance":"rec","why":"Explains interactors for keeping large apps manageable","quote":"See this old Uncle Bob video re interactors","text":"My (limited)experience is that when a rails app gets too big, it becomes hard to manage. That was my case, anyway. The architecture of hanami promotes longterm developer sanity. The fact that views are objects and the existence of interactors is a real plus. See this old Uncle Bob video re interactors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8659845,"author":"programminggeek","month":"2014-11","story":"Crystal: the programming language","stance":"rec","why":"Talks on value-oriented, side-effect-free design thinking","quote":"A few talks that are around this style of thinking","text":"It might do that wildly inefficient thing... Or, you might do something where you have a list of pointers, and you point at a different value instead of mutating an existing value. I haven't dug into the details of how immutable data structures can be made to work efficiently, but part of the charm is that in many cases you don't mutate the array at all. What I mean is, there are certain behaviors around mutation that programmers do because they can. When you take away the ability to mutate data, you design differently and without side effects. All of a sudden testing becomes easier, faster, cheaper for large parts of your codebase. You have simpler solutions that are potentially easier to reason about because the complex (and sometimes elegant) solutions aren't so readily available. A few talks that are around this style of thinking: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] Boundaries are good, values are good, simple things that work together are good. The more we can take the good parts and form them together into a cohesive language/framework/platform, the better our software will be."},{"id":6470693,"author":"Anchor","month":"2013-09","story":"Slow Tests Are the Symptom, Not the Cause","stance":"rec","why":"Uncle Bob explains separating entities, interactors and boundaries","quote":"Here's Uncle Bob talking on his version of the idea extensively","text":"Here's Uncle Bob talking on his version of the idea extensively: [VIDEO] The main idea is to separate the system into entities, interactors and boundaries, which correspond roughly to model, controllers and interfaces+DTOs (Data Transfer Objects), but with the difference that model classes are not exposed through the interfaces. Rather, the DTOs are used to communicate the changes in the data. We have been using this approach for a year now, with REST on top, and it so far has worked beautifully. The entities map naturally to REST resources and DTOs makes nice request and response classes. Armed with CQRS (now I sound enterprise-y), the data storage is easy to work with using the same DTOs (the data storage and its interface do not know anything about the entities either). On the DB implementation level, we work on the DTOs, which are trivial to store to the database (even without ORM, although it does remove much of the boilerplate). Only one class - the entity repository - is needed to convert the DTOs to entities, and this only happens on create, update, and delete. Reads (due to CQRS) go directly to DB without instantiating any entities - neat. We also use 'unit of work' -pattern to abstract the notion of transaction on create, update, and delete operations, which simplifies the interactor code, hiding the nature of the data storage (making it a swappable in this respect too). But to stay on topic, our tests are fast too as the interactors can be tested by injecting an in"},{"id":5599537,"author":"jared314","month":"2013-04","story":"Code Organization in Large AngularJS and JavaScript Applications","stance":"rec","why":"Bob Martin keynote makes a sound point about code organization","quote":"I don't agree with everything he said, but the code organization point is sound.","text":"This reminds me of the beginning of Bob Martin's keynote[0] at Ruby Midwest 2011[1]. I don't agree with everything he said, but the code organization point is sound. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":5416270,"author":"chao-","month":"2013-03","story":"I’m done with the web","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Many of the author's complaints read similar to this one: Now, people compare Backbone to Cappuccino, or Ember to Rails. Cappuccino, Backbone, and Rails all serve completely different purposes, and saying you don’t need one because you have another is just asinine. Three questions: 1. Who are these people that compare those three frameworks? 2. Why do you let them get under your skin? 3. What does that have you do with you \"being done with the web\" (or web community)? I don't understand any of these. So some morons want to compare apples and oranges? This has nothing to do with \"the web\" and crops up in all industries and spheres of life. Welcome to humanity, enjoy your stay. As best I can tell, my lack of empathy must come from a different definition of \"web community\". When the author complains about how The community has tossed out everything we’ve learned from the last 30 years of building applications in favor of being able to put something together quickly with complete disregard for maintainability or extendability. part of me wants to agree with him, flip a table, and join the melee [1]. But at the same time, I was listening to the Ruby Rogues episode with Sandi Metz last night [2]. There is a 20-minute discussion about utility of does-it-all frameworks, their use in a project, the types of projects you use them for, and the point at which all projects will necessarily need to have designed a better solution , or massively adapted the framework, to better fit their pr"},{"id":7671516,"author":"jshen","month":"2014-04","story":"RIP TDD","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"we have bob here telling us we are absolutely wrong if we don't follow his religion.","text":"Neither of us ever said all TDD folks are cultist. We are both tired of being told that we are absolutely doing it wrong if we don't practice TDD. For an example of people telling us this please see this talk by bob Martin and jump to the 58 minute mark. [VIDEO] Bob is not an obscure figure by any stretch of the imagination. You have admitted to having no real data to back up the claim that writing tests first leads to better code, yet we have bob here telling us we are absolutely wrong if we don't follow his religion."},{"id":7673719,"author":"jshen","month":"2014-04","story":"When TDD Doesn't Work","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"And Bob is guilty of that religiosity himself.","text":"\"Over the years many people have complained about the so-called \"religiosity\" of some of the proponents of Test Driven Development. \" And Bob is guilty of that religiosity himself. See here [VIDEO] Jump to the 58 minute mark if it doesn't automatically."},{"id":7676633,"author":"jshen","month":"2014-04","story":"How testability can help","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is an extremist ideology, and many of us are tired of having this thrown in our face over and over again.","text":"I think his first sentence is a strawman. What I'm tired of from the TDD crowd is that they insist that people are doing it wrong if they don't do to the extreme demand of others. A great example of this is Bob Martin saying that you are absolutely doing it wrong if you don't write your tests before your code and if you don't restrict your writing of app code to the pure case of making a failing test which you wrote first pass. This is an extremist ideology, and many of us are tired of having this thrown in our face over and over again. Here is Bob saying this: [VIDEO] No one said, \"testability is a meaningless goal\", and it's dishonest to say that. We're saying that the extreme to which the TDD crowd takes this good general guideline is bad, and their insistence that anyone who doesn't do it that way is doing it wrong is growing very tired and someone has to speak up against it."}],"r":[["cbeJIwF1pVY",1],["DJFKl_5JTnA",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["SEDuVF7kiPU",1],["VDfX44fZoMc",1],["JrMiSQAGOS4",1],["ZzsK8Am6dKU",1],["dGVqrGmwOAw",1],["rX0ItVEVjHc",1],["Z4oYSByyRak",1]]},"XuBdf9jYj7o":{"wy":"HN uses it as the standard short answer to whether car-dominated cities can become bike-friendly, since the Dutch had to fight for it.","q":{"x":"There is a great video about the protest movement and the transformation of dutch infrastructure here","i":9148349,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9148349,"author":"bullfightonmars","month":"2015-03","story":"Mike Ey, Microsoft HoloLens designer, killed in hit and run","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Protest movement that transformed Dutch infrastructure.","quote":"There is a great video about the protest movement and the transformation of dutch infrastructure here","text":"Those \"antifragile\" solutions have been used to great effect in Netherlands where they have built a massive cycling, walking, and transit infrastructure. They were able to build this infrastructure due to a popular protest movement, 'Stop de Kindermoord' (Stop the Child Murder) which was in reaction to exactly the problem we face in the US of deaths and injury caused by motorists. There is a great video about the protest movement and the transformation of dutch infrastructure here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8822708,"author":"m0nty","month":"2015-01","story":"Junction design in the Netherlands [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Development of Dutch cycling infrastructure in under seven minutes.","quote":"this is an excellent video","text":"The obsession with helmets in many countries is born of an idea that violence on the roads is inevitable. It's a bit like responding to knife or gun crime by saying everyone should wear kevlar vests, problem solved! Rather than insisting that knives and guns are not used in public spaces. Some people have real problems grasping this. Cars are seen as inherently dangerous, pedestrians and everyone else should (ideally) stay off the roads, and nobody should challenge our \"right\" to drive where and when we like. But driving is still a choice, albeit coerced since there are so few alternatives, and that choice has profound implications for others. Why should I wear protective clothing because you choose to drive a vehicle which puts me in danger? In the Netherlands, to respond to your point, they have largely eliminated motoring-related danger for cyclists. If anyone is interested in the development of Dutch cycling infrastructure, this is an excellent video: [VIDEO] (How the Dutch got their cycle paths, 6m 29s) David Hembrow has an informative blog about all things cycling in Holland: [LINK]"},{"id":29159450,"author":"Gareth321","month":"2021-11","story":"The popularity of e-bikes isn’t slowing down","stance":"rec","why":"Netherlands made space for bikes through creative engineering and will.","quote":"I encourage you to watch","text":"I encourage you to watch [this video.]( [VIDEO] ) No one believed they had enough space in the Netherlands either. Turns out they did, with some creative engineering, time, money, and sheer force of will. It's not easy. It takes a long time. But with a steady hand and the ability to cut through lobby interests, it can be done. Note that they didn't need to knock down many buildings in the NL. They did things like reducing 3-4 lanes to 2; making roads and footpaths narrower; reclaiming roads and re-routing vehicle traffic elsewhere; and of course better utilising a lot of wasted curbside land. Plus lots of redrawing of city plans to ensure future building and renovations incorporated the vision."},{"id":25807307,"author":"caturopath","month":"2021-01","story":"The Children Strike of 1972","stance":"rec","why":"Dutch history of safe bike infrastructure.","quote":"A Dutch Youtuber gives some of this history in a pretty good video","text":"A Dutch Youtuber gives some of this history in a pretty good video at [VIDEO] Another awesome urban design channel focused on the Netherlands is [LINK] -- it covers the safe bike/pedestrian infrastructure but lots of other cool things"},{"id":24087738,"author":"iknowstuff","month":"2020-08","story":"Dementia on the Retreat in the U.S. and Europe","stance":"rec","why":"Dutch street design saves time and money.","quote":"I highly recommend researching the Dutch street design.","text":"I highly recommend researching the Dutch street design. By explicitly avoiding freeways and encouraging biking, they save so much time (no traffic) and money (think long term health benefits of cycling, no pollution, cheaper infrastructure, lower reliance on fossil fuels, fewer accidents, more visits to local businesses) it's insane the rest of the world isn't copying them ASAP. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":24087823,"author":"iknowstuff","month":"2020-08","story":"Dementia on the Retreat in the U.S. and Europe","stance":"rec","why":"Dutch street design saves time and money.","quote":"I highly recommend researching the Dutch street design.","text":"I highly recommend researching the Dutch street design. By explicitly avoiding freeways and encouraging biking, they save so much time (no traffic) and money (think long term health benefits, cheaper infrastructure, lower reliance on fossil fuels, fewer accidents, more visits to local businesses) it's insane the rest of the world isn't copying them ASAP. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":12676209,"author":"jorgenhorstink","month":"2016-10","story":"The Dutch Reach: Clever Workaround to Keep Cyclists from Getting “Doored”","stance":"rec","why":"Context on why Dutch cycling infrastructure was built.","quote":"If you like this topic, you'll like the video...","text":"A couple of months ago I watched a short video about how we got our cycling lanes. I think it provides some interesting context on why we built our cycling infrastructure. If you like this topic, you'll like the video... [VIDEO]"},{"id":6703669,"author":"Brakenshire","month":"2013-11","story":"Is It O.K. to Kill Cyclists?","stance":"rec","why":"Mini-documentary on cities embracing the bike like the Netherlands.","quote":"this mini-documentary is worthwhile looking at","text":"Yes, definitely a big problem in the UK too. Although I think support for cycling is perhaps more cross-party than in the US, at least in the political elite. Both the Conservative PM and Mayor of London are nominally pro-bike, also The Times (centre-right paper for those from outside the UK) ran a major campaign on cycle safety, after one of their journalists was killed: [LINK] Although certainly their freedom to do something serious about the issue is restricted by the anti-metropolitan, anti-liberal vroom vroom demographic. > If cycling is to ever become safe, cities need to start embracing the bike like they do in the Netherlands, but it's no easy task. On that topic, this mini-documentary is worthwhile looking at: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6020086,"author":"gnosis","month":"2013-07","story":"Linux developer Seth Vidal killed in Hit and Run accident on his bike.","stance":"rec","why":"How the Dutch built safe cycle paths.","quote":"Here[1] is an interesting video on how the Dutch got their bicycle paths.","text":"Riding a bike does not have to be as dangerous as it is in most places in the United States. Here[1] is an interesting video on how the Dutch got their bicycle paths. From the intro: \"The Netherlands has the world's largest number of cyclists, but it is also the safest place in the world to cycle. That is largely because of the perfect cycling infrastructure that can be found throughout the country. How did the Dutch get this network of high quality cycle paths?\" [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":5540875,"author":"maurits","month":"2013-04","story":"Electric Velomobiles: fast, comfortable, and more efficient than cars","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Dutch-style bike lanes and intersection design.","quote":"In short, build bike lanes like the dutch.","text":"In short, build bike lanes like the dutch. [1] Big, physically separated, omnipresent bike lines. Ensure that bikes are first class citizens on intersections. Compare the 2-stage turn with the dutch design [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":32814413,"author":"snthd","month":"2022-09","story":"Bikes, not self driving cars, are the technological gateway to urban progress","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch bike infrastructure built around existing cities.","quote":"Change is possible.","text":"Any city old enough wasn't built around cars, because they didn't exist yet. There, car infrastructure was built on what existed - not on a blank slate. Likewise in the Netherlands bike infrastructure was built around what existed. Not on a blank slate. [VIDEO] Change is possible."},{"id":23760262,"author":"Luxonis-Brandon","month":"2020-07","story":"Luxonis wants to use computer vision to protect cyclists","stance":"reference","why":"Example of how Dutch design infrastructure.","quote":"A ton has been written/filmed/etc about this, but here's an example","text":"Yes, ultimately infrastructure is the real solution here. Take the Netherlands for example. We interviewed folks from nearly every country on this, and the response in the Netherlands was heartwarming: Every person we talked to had never even heard of someone being hit by a car while riding a bike (or walking). And after the interview, they interviewed a bunch of their friends/family/etc. and came back to us with those results: Still the same, not a single story of an accident. And this all goes back to the way the Dutch design infrastructure. A ton has been written/filmed/etc about this, but here's an example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21380597,"author":"Arnout","month":"2019-10","story":"After Spike in Deaths, New York to Get 250 Miles of Protected Bike Lanes","stance":"reference","why":"How cycling infrastructure became taken seriously in the Netherlands.","quote":"It's how cycling infrastructure finally got taken seriously in the Netherlands too","text":"Pretty much the case every single time. It's how cycling infrastructure finally got taken seriously in the Netherlands too - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18553042,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2018-11","story":"Boring Co. Drops LA Westside Tunnel Plan","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Amsterdam fighting for cycling-friendly streets.","quote":"Amsterdam fought like hell for it","text":"To the best of my knowledge, you are correct that every US city is horrible in this regard. This was not by accident. To your comment \"If we know it works how come no one has done it? \" - 1) We have done it. Amsterdam fought like hell for it [VIDEO] . Now they have one of the finest cities in the world in which to move about. Copenhagen at rush hour handles loads of people well because they have built systems that move people efficiently and safely. And pleasantly. Even driving there is nice, paradoxically. [LINK] 2) People fight it because they believe they should be able to drive anywhere they like, for free, and without interruption - and then even store their car for free as well! The ideal situation is to be the only driver, but of course that only works if driving is very expensive (something I support). In LA people are so furious at the idea of not being able to drive everywhere they shout down (almost) anything resembling progress. Things like Elon's tunnel are used as ammunition in these fights. After all, why should we build bike lanes when we're getting these kickass tunnels!?? This is part of why I left LA. As for your latter point - yes, it is very much my concern. We have similar views regarding Facebook and Co. but given that Elon's tunnels are unlikely to actually make life better (note my earlier comment regarding induced demand) this is taking resources from actual solutions we could do right now, cheaply, and that we know work."},{"id":16796950,"author":"calcifer","month":"2018-04","story":"Uber enters dockless bike wars with Jump acquisition","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Netherlands was car-first in the 70s.","quote":"The current cycling infrastructure is a very hard won victory, with thousands dead along the way","text":"Actually, in the 70s Netherlands was very much a car-first country. The current cycling infrastructure is a very hard won victory, with thousands dead along the way [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10379684,"author":"dalke","month":"2015-10","story":"Should bike helmets be compulsory? Lessons from Seattle and Amsterdam","stance":"reference","why":"Amsterdam was re-engineered after postwar car-centric era.","quote":"Amsterdam had similar problems to the US in the post-war era until the 1970s","text":"Two things make your objection less relevant. First, Amsterdam had similar problems to the US in the post-war era until the 1970s. That is, the cities were being rebuilt for cars, bicycling infrastructure became worse, and traffic deaths rose. The city had to be re-engineered to get where it is now. See [VIDEO] for a video. Second, and concerning topology, look to Trondheim, Norway as an example of a bike-friendly city with hills. And from what I've read, Portland, Oregon is also pretty bike friendly, with similar topology to Seattle. So, why can't Seattle be more like Portland in this respect?"},{"id":7930533,"author":"barrystaes","month":"2014-06","story":"A New Bike Lane That Could Save Lives and Make Cycling More Popular","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Dutch bike lanes that have worked for decades.","quote":"this is how all bike lanes are in the Netherlands, it works and already has for tens of years","text":"I dont know if its april fools, but this is how all bike lanes are in the Netherlands, it works and already has for tens of years.. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6617967,"author":"wsc981","month":"2013-10","story":"A Tour of a Whole Country That Has 'Transformed Itself for Cycling'","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the 1970s transformation toward cycling.","quote":"we actually did transform into a much more suitable country for cycling during the 70s","text":"From what I understand, we actually did transform into a much more suitable country for cycling during the 70s (~10 years before I was born). I'll try to look up the video that explains. Ah found it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5658323,"author":"CoreDumpling","month":"2013-05","story":"In 1897, a Bicycle Superhighway Was the Future of California Transit","stance":"reference","why":"History of how bike paths were brought back after postwar car boom.","quote":"it took a concerted effort of willful resistance and politically non-expedient measures to bring back the bike paths","text":"True story: I moved from southern California to Holland in no small part because of how amenable the country is to cycling. People like to think that cycling is popular here naturally because the country is flat and densely populated. However, it's easy to forget that during the postwar boom years, a major shift toward cars happened and it took a concerted effort of willful resistance and politically non-expedient measures to bring back the bike paths ( [VIDEO] ). Clearly, 1897 was not the last chance the Dutch had to fix their transit system, and with the groundwork they've laid over the years, it now costs only €30 per person in funding annually! ( [LINK] ) Yet I see very little chance of this happening in California, not because cyclists lack a voice, but because we need to create more dis incentives to driving. This obviously is a political no-go considering that motorists are by far the majority demographic. It's saddening, though, that there is so much willful ignorance (or apathy) with regard to how we've externalized the true cost of driving, whether it's in the form of: - damage to the environment - subsidy to cheap gas prices by the DoD budget (and body count) for controlling the world's oil flow - casualties from motor vehicle accidents - health problems resulting from sedentary lifestyles - zoning rules that require abundant free parking to be available in communities. We'd probably have to see gas hit $10/gallon and parking lots charge $50/day before Americans st"}],"r":[["Sx0J7dIlL7c",1],["Pzl1B7nB9Kc",1],["Q3uvzcY2Xug",1],["s9fByRmAHgU",1],["lJ3NC-R3gSI",1],["xSGx3HSjKDo",1],["2RQrKP9a0XE",1],["07o-TASvIxY",1],["y3qfeoqErtY",1],["vuEQixrBKCc",1]]},"WFCvkkDSfIU":{"wy":"HN recommends it for mind-blowing visuals that make cell biology tangible.","q":{"x":"I had a much greater appreciation for how our bodies are made up of trillions of tiny protein machines.","i":39552315,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39552315,"author":"benjismith","month":"2024-02","story":"Rendering protein structures inside cells at the atomic level with Unreal Engine","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Molecular animations showing the body as trillions of protein machines.","quote":"I had a much greater appreciation for how our bodies are made up of trillions of tiny protein machines.","text":"This reminds me of the amazing molecular animations of Drew Berry, which he showed in this TED talk: [VIDEO] Which was 12 years ago! After watching that video, I had a much greater appreciation for how our bodies are made up of trillions of tiny protein machines. Fascinating stuff!!"},{"id":15535213,"author":"joering2","month":"2017-10","story":"No human genome has ever been completely sequenced","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animation of DNA replication machinery inside the body.","quote":"blew my mind few months ago","text":"Slightly off-topic, but this semi-animation of DNA replication machine that is working right now in your body (in amounts of trillions of devices) blew my mind few months ago. from TED: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44634117,"author":"Zealotux","month":"2025-07","story":"Super-resolution microscopes reveal new details of cells and disease","stance":"rec","why":"Short video that helps understand cell biology.","quote":"","text":"Sorry for being a bit off-topic. Biology wasn't my forte in high school, I wish I had read this article back then [LINK] and seen that video (9:08 min) [VIDEO]"},{"id":32613271,"author":"Darkskynet","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Boring but important tech no one is working on?","stance":"rec","why":"TED talk on scientifically accurate animations of biological processes.","quote":"Here is a TEDTalk by Drew Berry: Animations of unseeable biology.","text":"On the topic of 'scientifically accurate animations of biological processes'. You may know of these videos, but I thought these might be worth sharing for others interested in this topic: Here is a TEDTalk by Drew Berry: Animations of unseeable biology. (uploaded in 2012) [VIDEO] Here is also a more recent video by Veritasium on YT that also talks about these animations. (uploaded in 2017) [LINK]"},{"id":16983757,"author":"discrisknbisque","month":"2018-05","story":"The life cycle of HIV in 3D [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Drew Berry on scientifically accurate biological animations, with history.","quote":"Interesting history in the beginning as well.","text":"Drew Berry creates similar animations and refers to them (in this talk) as \"accurate representations\", but doesn't go into much detail. Interesting history in the beginning as well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14595424,"author":"nom","month":"2017-06","story":"Close-Up View of DNA Replication","stance":"rec","why":"Drew Berry's TED talk on cell animations.","quote":"here's a nice TED talk by Drew Berry","text":"If you liked this video, also check out this one: Inner Life of the Cell [LINK] And here's a nice TED talk by Drew Berry: Animations of unseeable biology [VIDEO] These kind of visualizations are of course an idealized view on how it works. As others have already mentioned, the processes are hugely chaotic and much more probabilistic than deterministic. On this scale everything is just one huge wobbling soup of things crashing into each other, which makes it even more fascinating that it works as well as it does."},{"id":13621008,"author":"nol13","month":"2017-02","story":"Why Do DMT Users See Insects from a Parallel Universe?","stance":"rec","why":"Animated molecular biology that is compelling on its own.","quote":"also a really cool video even without the subtext","text":"[VIDEO] ^My favorite way I've seen it described, and also a really cool video even without the subtext. (but ya this is one rabbit hole that I've yet to travel down. guess i took the blue pill, but more pills just seemed unnecessary at the time)"}],"r":[["Dar8P3r7GYA",1],["xQ3oJlt4GrI",1],["iRkZcTg1JWU",1],["X_tYrnv_o6A",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1]]},"P-2P3MSZrBM":{"wy":"Praised as a long but rewarding conversation, with commenters especially liking Bach's emphasis on modelling as a skill and his bridge between mind and computation.","q":{"x":"If watching this isn't worth anyone's time, I'm not sure what would be.","i":23527696,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":29708453,"author":"meowface","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Favorite Podcast Episode of 2021?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite podcast episode of 2020, better in video form","quote":"His first interview with him was also my favorite podcast episode of 2020.","text":"Same for me. His first interview with him was also my favorite podcast episode of 2020. Also, you can watch it in video form. I'd argue it's better that way, especially with the way he emotes during certain parts of the discussion. Episode 1: [VIDEO] Episode 2: [LINK]"},{"id":24348527,"author":"miketery","month":"2020-09","story":"Mind Emulation Foundation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Joscha Bach bridges mind and computation","quote":"Also his 3 hour conversation with Lex Fridman is a whole other amazing rabbit hole [2].","text":"The best I've seen the topic of mind and computation bridged and well communicated has been by Joscha Bach. Recommend watching this video on Computational Meta-Psychology [1]. Also his 3 hour conversation with Lex Fridman is a whole other amazing rabbit hole [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23925295,"author":"alexshortt","month":"2020-07","story":"Joscha Bach: Artificial Consciousness and the Nature of Reality [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bach on modelling as a skill applied to all experience","quote":"I really love his emphasis on modelling as a skill.","text":"I really love his emphasis on modelling as a skill. Its obviously great applied to technical skills like computer science, but he seems to apply it to everything else in the human experience so well. \"everything that we sense is part of the same object ... we learn it all into one model ... we call this model the universe\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":23527696,"author":"reciprocity","month":"2020-06","story":"Emotional Blocks as Obstacles to Learning","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Joscha Bach conversation worth watching","quote":"If watching this isn't worth anyone's time, I'm not sure what would be.","text":"You might be interested in watching this: [VIDEO] If watching this isn't worth anyone's time, I'm not sure what would be."},{"id":24874784,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-10","story":"Aliens on 1k nearby stars could see us, new study suggests","stance":"rec","why":"Bach lays out ideas near the end of the podcast","quote":"Joscha Bach laid out a few interesting ideas around this at the end of a podcast.","text":"Totally agree. Joscha Bach laid out a few interesting ideas around this at the end of a podcast. If you have a few minutes start here for a broader context (starts of wholly unrelated but with some great concepts): [VIDEO] If not, he drills into it here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23522095,"author":"memexy","month":"2020-06","story":"Joscha Bach: Do we live in a simulation or in the base reality?","stance":"rec","why":"Philosophy of machine learning, models of thinking and consciousness","quote":"it was pretty good. It's a bit long but worthwhile.","text":"He recently did an interview with Lex Friedman ( [VIDEO] ) and it was pretty good. It's a bit long but worthwhile. Covers a bunch of topics about the philosophy of machine learning, models of thinking and consciousness, and some bits and pieces of future directions for AI."}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["DXYje2B04xE",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["JLb5hZLw44s",1]]},"1-dUkyn_fZA":{"wy":"Repeatedly recommended as the canonical demo of org-mode for literate programming and reproducible research.","q":{"x":"on Emacs + org-mode + python in reproducible research is worth a watch","i":9767343,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":7714293,"author":"cube_yellow","month":"2014-05","story":"Let’s Make a Bubble Map","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Must-watch on reproducible research with org-mode and Python.","quote":"For python specifically, this video is a must watch","text":"A lot of people are starting to use org-mode for that kind of thing. Fully reproducible research in a single file. To check the work, just export the file to pdf/html/etc., or \"C-c C-c\" the code blocks as you read through in org-mode. It's fantastic. For python specifically, this video is a must watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16842959,"author":"dahart","month":"2018-04","story":"Jupyter, Mathematica, and the Future of the Research Paper","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on using python in org-mode as a research notebook.","quote":"I remember seeing a great video a few years back on using python in org mode to make a research notebook.","text":"I Agree with all those points. I've used org mode for a few things, but not for python notebooks. I remember seeing a great video a few years back on using python in org mode to make a research notebook. It really made me want to try org mode for literate / notebook style programming. I think it was this one: [VIDEO] It sounds like org mode does not store images inline, since you don't like the b64 images in Jupyter? I imagine that could go both ways depending on what you want. Even for diffing, you might like to see that the image changed (though I generally agree that diffing images is not ideal). If you have to package images separately when sharing, then wouldn't inline images score a point for easier sharing? > use other languages that python Agreed, and that is powerful, but this also goes to my point. Jupyter is made for python and org mode isn't. Jupyter comes with python and libraries, where with Emacs you're on your own to figure out how to install and use different languages, and setup your environment so Emacs can see them. You're on your own to bring in images and plots, where Jupyter already understands commands that output images. > Plus, you get to use a proper text editor instead of editing on a browser textarea. Yes, but the editor is Emacs. :) For a lot of people the browser textarea is a bonus because it's the same thing you use everywhere. It's uncomplicated and non-technical and utterly consistent, even if lacking any power."},{"id":9767343,"author":"rndn","month":"2015-06","story":"Emacs for Data Science","stance":"rec","why":"Kitchin's SciPy presentation on emacs, org-mode and python research.","quote":"on Emacs + org-mode + python in reproducible research is worth a watch","text":"This SciPy presentation (Kitchin, John Carnegie Mellon University) on Emacs + org-mode + python in reproducible research is worth a watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8189689,"author":"taeric","month":"2014-08","story":"Show HN: Lit – a modern literate programming tool","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on org-mode for reproducible research and literate programming.","quote":"There was a really good talk given on this at a pypy convention a couple of years ago.[1]","text":"Do check out org-mode, then. It is surprising how far that tool has been extended. Many of these features are actually under the label \"reproducible research\" nowdays. There was a really good talk given on this at a pypy convention a couple of years ago.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8214972,"author":"taeric","month":"2014-08","story":"Typographic Programming Language","stance":"rec","why":"Shows combining TeX, lisp, markdown and python in one document.","quote":"want to combine TeX, lisp, a markdown like language, and python/whatever in a single document? Try org-mode.","text":"Seriously, try emacs. You want to see what color you have put in a css field?[1] Want to see the image you are referencing in a repl?[2] More amazingly, want to combine TeX, lisp, a markdown like language, and python/whatever in a single document? Try org-mode.[3][4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8026315,"author":"taeric","month":"2014-07","story":"Patoline: A modern digital typesetting system","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk showing org-mode used with Python.","quote":"See this great talk showing its use with python.","text":"I know it gets said a lot, but have you considered emacs and org-mode. Because, it goes a very long way for this. See this great talk showing its use with python. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7366559,"author":"hsitz","month":"2014-03","story":"IPython: Beginners’ Guide","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates using org-mode with Python for reproducible research.","quote":"Here's a video showing how one guy uses it with Python","text":"The Orgmode extension to Emacs has quite an elegant, flexible, and powerful way of doing what you want. Can be hard to wrap your mind around at the beginning but has good payoff once you're up to speed. Here's a video showing how one guy uses it with Python (though it integrates with multitude of languages, including R): [VIDEO] Here's paper (itself from LaTeX generated by Orgmode) describing use of Orgmode for this kind of \"reproducible research\": [LINK]"},{"id":17034289,"author":"dahart","month":"2018-05","story":"The Many Uses of Org Mode","stance":"reference","why":"Shows org-mode used much like a Jupyter notebook.","quote":"I remember watching a great video about someone who uses it a lot like a Jupyter notebook.","text":"I like to use org mode for cleaning up my hard drives... a little script to pipe du into something that sums the files in a directory and adds asterisks, and viola, you have a UI for finding all the large and delete-able files & directories. I haven't used org mode for all the other cool things it can do, but I remember watching a great video about someone who uses it a lot like a Jupyter notebook. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8272125,"author":"fensterbrett","month":"2014-09","story":"Designing a Personal Knowledgebase","stance":"reference","why":"Interesting emacs, org-mode and python research workflow.","quote":"The workflow proposed in the first link looks quite interesting","text":"The workflow proposed in the first link looks quite interesting: Emacs + org-mode + python in reproducible research; SciPy 2013 [VIDEO] And here are some more reviews, opinions, advices and experiences I just stumbled upon: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":8026338,"author":"taeric","month":"2014-07","story":"Patoline: A modern digital typesetting system","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as inspiration for how powerful existing editors already are.","quote":"","text":"I hate to be one of those that harps on \"why the downvotes,\" but I really would appreciate tips on how folks would plan on testing these claims. Just imagining that we could do better than some good pieces of software doesn't really do much to say that we can. For example, people have been imagining faster than light travel for a long time. I realize these don't compare directly, as we have reasons to believe upper bounds on speed. So, I am curious if we have reasons to believe that the upper bounds on editors and such is really that much higher. For inspiration, consider [1]. Same for typesetting. Take a look at most of the papers at [2] and tell me whether translating these to new languages is really necessary. (Though, note that I am not arguing against this. Just asking how we plan to know that \"things are better now.\") [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":7721640,"author":"cube_yellow","month":"2014-05","story":"Anki – Powerful, intelligent flashcards","stance":"reference","why":"Org-mode workflow keeping notes and flashcards together.","quote":"","text":"If you use emacs org-mode, check out org-drill [LINK] . It uses the same algorithms, allows you to tweak everything, and you can keep your flashcards alongside the relevant notes/code/files/issues/ToDos/bookmarks/contacts/feeds/calendar-entry/emails/thesis/spreadsheets/journals/diagrams/pictures/evernotes/etc. [VIDEO] , rather than in a totally separate program."}],"r":[["aircAruvnKk",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["WrxZ4AZPdOQ",1],["LUFp6sjKbkE",1],["0Rnq1NpHdmw",1],["hQVTIJBZook",1],["vT1JzLTH4G4",1],["l7rce6IQDWs",1],["BD6h-wDj7bw",1]]},"8fi7uSYlOdc":{"wy":"HN considers it one of the best talks for understanding how containers work from the ground up.","q":{"x":"Probably one of the most enlightening presentations I've seen in recent years, full-stop.","i":31250305,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31250305,"author":"smoldesu","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: Best educational courses/MOOCs/videos/blogs/books taught by a Female?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enlightening talk on containers from scratch","quote":"Probably one of the most enlightening presentations I've seen in recent years, full-stop.","text":"Indeed a bit of an odd request, but I'm partial to Liz Rice's \"Containers from Scratch\" talk. Probably one of the most enlightening presentations I've seen in recent years, full-stop. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43489246,"author":"pss314","month":"2025-03","story":"Building a Linux Container Runtime from Scratch","stance":"rec","why":"Hands-on live-coded talk building a container runtime from scratch","quote":"I loved this hands-on presentation Containers From Scratch by Liz Rice","text":"I loved this hands-on presentation Containers From Scratch by Liz Rice from few years ago [VIDEO] . Today, Linux containers in (less than) 100 lines of shell by Michael Kerrisk was published [LINK] ."},{"id":40577046,"author":"ashconnor","month":"2024-06","story":"Deep Diving into Docker and Containers: What It Is and How It Works","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how containers work from the ground up","quote":"Big fan of the following talk that explains how containers work from the ground up.","text":"Big fan of the following talk that explains how containers work from the ground up. Containers From Scratch • Liz Rice • GOTO 2018 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30624757,"author":"pdpi","month":"2022-03","story":"Linux containers in 500 lines of code (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Video presentation of building containers","quote":"Liz Rice gave a pretty good prsentation on this same topic in 2018","text":"This will be a fun read, thanks! If you'd prefer to see the same ideas in video form, Liz Rice gave a pretty good prsentation on this same topic in 2018: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22241841,"author":"sascha_sl","month":"2020-02","story":"Linux containers in 500 lines of code (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Live-coded tiny container runtime in Go","quote":"Liz Rice has done some awesome on-stage live coding, including a very small container runtime in go.","text":"Liz Rice has done some awesome on-stage live coding, including a very small container runtime in go. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18218625,"author":"komuW","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Building containers from scratch, in a list of best talks","quote":"Creating containers From Scratch - Liz Rice","text":"1. Why Do Keynote Speakers Keep Suggesting That Improving Security Is Possible? - James Mickens [LINK] 2. James Mickens on JavaScript - James Mickens [LINK] 3. Creating containers From Scratch - Liz Rice [VIDEO] 4. 2013 Isaac Asimov Memorial Debate: The Existence of Nothing - Panelists: J. Richard Gott, Jim Holt, Lawrence Krauss, Charles Seife, Eve Silverstein. Moderator: Neil deGrasse Tyson [LINK] 5. 2016 Isaac Asimov Memorial Debate: Is the Universe a Simulation? - Panelists: David Chalmers, Zohreh Davoudi, James Gates, Lisa Randall, Max Tegmark Moderator: Neil deGrasse Tyson [LINK] 6. Zig: A programming language designed for robustness, optimality, and clarity – Andrew Kelley [LINK] 7. Concurrency Is Not Parallelism - Rob Pike [LINK]"},{"id":30153479,"author":"jasode","month":"2022-01","story":"Gains I'm seeing from my second brain tool","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"these tutorials explain the verb (runtime) but not noun (artifacts of image file)","text":">I wish there were a tool that could just record everything [...] if you \"know\" everything, then notes serve no purpose. I guess the completeness of your proposed \"auto-memorize-everything\" solution depends on what style of notes a person writes. A lot of my notes are annotations or personal commentary that don't exist in the sources of the url text or video. Here's an example of that type of notes I often take... E.g. I found some links that explain how one can grok \"Docker\" by going through the learning exercise of re-inventing it from basic os features with homemade scripts. But I wrote my own personal notes (in \"##\")about the limitations of the explanations in the urls: ## these tutorials explain the verb (runtime) but not noun (artifacts of image file) [LINK] [VIDEO] Because content cannot meta-analyze itself and enumerate all the ideas and concepts that are not embedded in its own text, it means a hypothetical indexing engine that converted the 2 links above to searchable data still would not retrieve the extra meta information I wrote. A lot of good notes are generated from your own brain. EDIT add to categorize different types of \"notes\" to aid discussion: (1) notes as memorization aid such as copying facts : a user writes a note that says \"1 inch equals 25.4 millimeters\" after seeing it on a webpage or something. So the \"universal-record-and-index-everything\" would help make these type of notes obsolete because one could just skip writing that note down and just reca"}],"r":[["b9IkpUYlOx8",1],["UXZGCwAJpbM",1],["UBc7qBS1Ujo",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1],["D5xh0ZIEUOE",1],["wgSZA3NPpBs",1],["Z4oYSByyRak",1],["4KtotxNAwME",1],["8--5LwHRhjk",1]]},"p7bzE1E5PMY":{"wy":"HN commenters call it one of the best visual explanations of the wave, particle and measurement relationship, free of silly metaphors.","q":{"x":"Highly recommended for anyone trying to grok the relationship between a wave, a particle, and an observation.","i":14556233,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":18747101,"author":"erikpukinskis","month":"2018-12","story":"The Wavefunction Collapse Algorithm Explained","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualizations of wave function collapse with bite-sized iterative explanations.","quote":"one of my absolute favorite YouTube videos","text":"Somewhat off topic, but one of my absolute favorite YouTube videos is this [VIDEO] one by Udiprod that shows visualizations of wave function collapse, with bite sized, iterative explanation of all the concepts involved."},{"id":15106547,"author":"erikpukinskis","month":"2017-08","story":"Rumours swell over new kind of gravitational-wave sighting","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best explanation of quantum waves the commenter has seen.","quote":"my all time favorite physics videos on YouTube which explains quantum waves better than anything else","text":"I really wanted to like PBS SpaceTime. So many interesting subjects and I love physics videos on YouTube. But there's something about Matt O'Dowd that makes them unwatchable for me. Something about the pacing, I just can't stay focused. Another great channel is looking Glass Universe - [LINK] And Udiprod doesn't have a habit of making physics vids, but this is my all time favorite physics videos on YouTube which explains quantum waves better than anything else I've seen - [VIDEO]"},{"id":14556233,"author":"erikpukinskis","month":"2017-06","story":"The Largest Virtual Universe Ever Simulated","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear basics of waveform collapse without silly metaphors or historical detours.","quote":"Highly recommended for anyone trying to grok the relationship between a wave, a particle, and an observation.","text":"Side note: I recently came across this YouTube video which did a fantastic job showing just the basics of waveform collapse, without straying into silly metaphors and cartoons, or getting bogged down by historical narratives: [VIDEO] Highly recommended for anyone trying to grok the relationship between a wave, a particle, and an observation. I find I don't want to hear what quantum physics is \"like\" I just want to have things explained to me slowly and completely."},{"id":29524609,"author":"Isinlor","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Long-form info-dense videos on YouTube you would recommend to anyone?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"","text":"Everything from 3 blue 1 brown: [LINK] Visualization of Quantum Physics (Quantum Mechanics): [VIDEO] history of the entire world, i guess: [LINK] TIMELAPSE OF THE FUTURE: A Journey to the End of Time (4K): [LINK] Humanity was born way ahead of its time. The reason is grabby aliens. and Grabby aliens: when we'll meet them, how big they are, and other predictions [LINK] [LINK] Everyday Astronaut videos (he has 90 min documentary on Soviet rocket engine): [LINK] Cool Worlds - a lot of 25-35 min very nice astronomy explanations: [LINK] 'A Universe From Nothing' by Lawrence Krauss: [LINK]"},{"id":15501449,"author":"deusum","month":"2017-10","story":"Intel Delivers 17-Qubit Superconducting Chip","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful explanation of measurement collapsing a quantum state.","quote":"I found this video on quantum mechanics helpful.","text":"I found this video on quantum mechanics helpful. If you measure the qubit prematurely the function collapses. In classical computing it might be analogous to breaking out of a for loop before it has run its intended course and then you're left with an incorrect (or incomplete) answer. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TH9VCN6UkyQ",1],["r0plv_nIzsQ",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["uD4izuDMUQA",1],["oAVjF_7ensg",1],["xuCn8ux2gbs",1],["l3whaviTqqg",1],["7ImvlS8PLIo",1]]},"pQ0CvjAJXz4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a rare real-world look at a large fault-tolerant Erlang/Elixir system with no downtime in healthcare.","q":{"x":"an excellent presentation of design/architecture of a Real-World fault-tolerant and distributed System in Erlang/Elixir","i":38814668,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38904735,"author":"rramadass","month":"2024-01","story":"The Erlang Ecosystem (2018) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Complete distributed fault-tolerant Erlang/Elixir system architecture","quote":"You get to really see the mind-blowing power of the Erlang System in this","text":"After watching the above which gives one an idea of the Erlang System's capabilities, watch this presentation by Bryan Hunter where the design and architecture of a complete distributed and fault-tolerant Erlang/Elixir system is shown; You get to really see the mind-blowing power of the Erlang System in this - [VIDEO]"},{"id":38814668,"author":"rramadass","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: Good examples of fault-tolerant Erlang code?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Design of a real-world fault-tolerant distributed Erlang system","quote":"an excellent presentation of design/architecture of a Real-World fault-tolerant and distributed System in Erlang/Elixir","text":"Not code but an excellent presentation of design/architecture of a Real-World fault-tolerant and distributed System in Erlang/Elixir - [VIDEO]"},{"id":44547736,"author":"chantepierre","month":"2025-07","story":"Leveraging Elixir's hot code loading capabilities to modularize a monolithic app","stance":"rec","why":"Multi-DC cluster with parts progressively swapped at runtime","quote":"people who do not know about erlang's hot code loading should watch this talk","text":"To add to this topic, people who do not know about erlang's hot code loading should watch this talk : [VIDEO] A multi-DC running cluster where parts are progressively swapped at runtime. No database, only OTP."},{"id":42405648,"author":"GCUMstlyHarmls","month":"2024-12","story":"Elixir/Erlang Hot Swapping Code (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Large-scale resilient healthcare deployment with no downtime","quote":"the whole talk is quite interesting regarding availability","text":"This is a talk about a large scale, resilient elixir/erlang deployment in healthcare. Specifically they talk about running with no down time using hot code reloading here: [VIDEO] but the whole talk is quite interesting regarding availability. Warning: the video is quite quiet."},{"id":36362566,"author":"notpublic","month":"2023-06","story":"A LiveView Is a Process","stance":"rec","why":"Hunter explains how HCA healthcare uses processes","quote":"Bryan Hunter explains how they do it at HCA healthcare","text":"This video may be of interest to you: [VIDEO] Bryan Hunter explains how they do it at HCA healthcare (not specifically LiveView process, but processes in general)."},{"id":41325828,"author":"darkmarmot","month":"2024-08","story":"Constraining writers in distributed systems","stance":"reference","why":"Describes the commenter's production system for patient state in RAM","quote":"You can learn more about it here","text":"We use something like this at scale, similar to Riak's design. We use it to store things like the active state of millions of hospital patients in RAM for high availability (zero downtime when DCs or nodes fail). One copy per data center with a writer at one of the DCs. Our current cluster has 32 nodes (8 per DC -- we should have 5 DCs across the US but only have 4 at the moment). You can learn more about it here: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":37266515,"author":"darkmarmot","month":"2023-08","story":"Unpacking Elixir: Concurrency","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on running multiple versions in a cluster","quote":"","text":"We use it across multiple versions of our software running in the same cluster. As long as you dark launch API changes, it's not much of an issue. [VIDEO] (Doh, just realized I was replying to Mr. Elixir himself! And you're familiar with our project anyway :)"}],"r":[["n8Dd5aVXLCc",1],["144uOfr4SYA",1],["x5Efg42-Qn0",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["ffkIQrq-m34",1],["GcJxRqTs5nk",1],["yErKTVdETpw",1],["f7CW7S0zxv4",1],["A-fxij3zM7g",1]]},"arj7oStGLkU":{"wy":"Recommended as a funny, empathetic take on procrastination and the origin of the life calendar concept.","q":{"x":"absolutely legendary TedX talk that humanizes procrastination","i":35952165,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35952165,"author":"akhayam","month":"2023-05","story":"Taxonomy of Procrastination","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Humanizes procrastination and builds empathy for procrastinators.","quote":"absolutely legendary TedX talk that humanizes procrastination","text":"Nicely written. Before taxonomies and remediation, I would watch Tim Urban's absolutely legendary TedX talk that humanizes procrastination--despite all its ills--and how to empathize with procrastinators (inside and around you): [VIDEO]"},{"id":29967713,"author":"awb","month":"2022-01","story":"Effortless personal productivity (or how I learned to love my monkey mind)","stance":"rec","why":"Tim Urban's TED talk accompanying his illustrated guide.","quote":"","text":"In the same vein, here’s a fun illustrated guide to procrastination (and fixing it) by Tim Urban: [LINK] And accompanying TED Talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30001847,"author":"awb","month":"2022-01","story":"A simple math equation can transform your productivity","stance":"rec","why":"Tim Urban's TED talk on procrastination.","quote":"","text":"For those that aren’t resonating with this take on productivity (and procrastination), check out Tim Urban. Illustrated guide: [LINK] And accompanying TED Talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25831578,"author":"uppsalax","month":"2021-01","story":"The Reason We Procrastinate and How to Stop","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on procrastination shared from a psychology course.","quote":"","text":"Thank you for sharing, procrastination is something very addictive. A way to deal with it is fundamental. I'd like to share with you a TEDx Talk a professor of mine shown us along a Behavioural Psychology course: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15171383,"author":"foxhop","month":"2017-09","story":"Ask HN: How to be curious about last uninteresting 20% of a task?","stance":"rec","why":"Helps the commenter with procrastination.","quote":"Watch this video. It sometimes helps me.","text":"[VIDEO] Watch this video. It sometimes helps me. I've become a worst procrastinator as I have grown and have more and more responsibility. I like to get things done."},{"id":11930355,"author":"nu2ycombinator","month":"2016-06","story":"Imagine yourself starting, not finishing","stance":"rec","why":"Informative, entertaining talk ending with life calendar.","quote":"following video is very informational and entertaining","text":"On similar topic, following video is very informational and entertaining. Especially last part the life calendar. Sometimes this life calendar motivates me procrastinate the procrastination :) [VIDEO] ."},{"id":29991108,"author":"bloqs","month":"2022-01","story":"Show HN: Life calendar – use the concept of Memento mori to embrace every week","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the life calendar concept.","quote":"excellent TED talk about procrastination","text":"Might be useful for some readers. I originally saw the life calendar concept in Tim Urbans excellent TED talk about procrastination a few years back. I couldnt find one on his site so I simply used the ASCII square character on Word and printed out a box for every week from birth until I am 85. I decided I wanted the weeks bigger so I deleted the already passed weeks, and changed the end date to proposed retirement at 65. It's not fun. I've lost the file but easy enough The talk [VIDEO]"},{"id":29836399,"author":"cttet","month":"2022-01","story":"Procrastinatory Doom Loops","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"A lot of advices can be misleading too.","text":"I share the same problem for a very long time and the situation gets worse as I grow up. But recently at least I have more understanding of my problem on this. The most important thing I want to share with you is that: Not most of things online are not useful to people like me, because the people are different. A lot of advices can be misleading too.( [VIDEO] ) this one especially. Because for different people, the procrastination experience is very different. If you have the same issue as me, the core problem is not actually about planning and decision making. It is about my ability to do things. It's a long essay but I will put the essence here: To some of the people (about half the population as I estimate), there are two way to obtain knowledge, one is learning, the other is training. People tends to acquired knowledge by learning and lack of training will be highly likely to have my kind of procrastination, especially on something we are not really trained on. The common experience of me doing things is plan things, make time estimates and do it. This sounds familiar but it is extremely different experience among people. As a \"learner\" although I made the time estimates, I don't really know them well. But with something that I am more comfortable with, I have a special feeling about the time estimates, that will have a strong confidence on that I can finish in a certain time frame. I tend to have no decision hesitations on tasks like this. This is usually the reason that"}],"r":[["klaJqofCsu4",1],["yhO6jfHPFQU",1]]},"43XaZEn2aLc":{"wy":"Recommended as the approachable way into a precise definition of language expressiveness for language-design arguments.","q":{"x":"Shriram Krishnamurthi's talk from two years ago on this paper might be more approachable.","i":28308675,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":49310771,"author":"hayley-patton","month":"2026-08","story":"Gödel, Escher, Elisp: The Beauty of Macros","stance":"rec","why":"Useful walkthrough of what macros can and cannot express","quote":"might be a useful walkthrough of the ideas being presented","text":"> If macros can implement arbitrary language features Not really your question--others have provided examples of type systems as macros--but they can't, they can only do local rewrites. You couldn't, for example, express mutation or non-local control flow if you didn't have them already; On the expressive power of programming languages [0] is rather theoretical but does set out the boundary of what macros can and can't do. That they're local means everyone has to buy into your type system if you want it to be a macro, though. [0] [LINK] though [VIDEO] might be a useful walkthrough of the ideas being presented"},{"id":40309576,"author":"Hirrolot","month":"2024-05","story":"Algebraic Data Types for C99","stance":"rec","why":"Formal treatment of language power and local vs global transformation","quote":"There's also a great talk on the matter [1], if somebody is interested in formalities.","text":"In programming language design, we tend to distinguish between global and local analysis. While type checking and elaboration is an example of global analysis, desugaring is inherently local to some piece of code. Therefore, \"power\" or \"expressiveness\" usually mean that something cannot be syntactically \"expanded\"; e.g., while type classes elaborate into explicit dictionaries, they still require information from the type checker, and therefore considered a \"real\" feature of a programming language. On the other hand, nested pattern matching can be formulated as local syntax transformation, and therefore it doesn't bring anything fundamentally new to the type system or dynamic semantics. There's also a great talk on the matter [1], if somebody is interested in formalities. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35793835,"author":"fanf2","month":"2023-05","story":"Is Go an object-oriented programming language?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains expressiveness as needing global refactoring to emulate a feature","quote":"Worth reading Felleisen","text":"Worth reading Felleisen’s paper “on the expressive power of programming languages” (or watch this talk [VIDEO] ) which argues that if a language A has a feature that would require global refactoring to implement in language B, then A is more expressive than B."},{"id":28308675,"author":"Twisol","month":"2021-08","story":"All you need is call/cc (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"More approachable talk version of Felleisen's expressive power paper","quote":"Shriram Krishnamurthi's talk from two years ago on this paper might be more approachable.","text":"Certainly adding call/cc doesn't allow you to represent more computable functions by programs. But there are plenty of internal features of programs that we're interested in, that let us meaningfully distinguish amongst programs that otherwise have the exact same observable behavior. I like coroutines as an example here, because I have very direct experience writing programs with similar behavior in systems that either do or do not have coroutines. By far, I found the coroutine-based programs far easier to understand and extend. Mattias Felleisen wrote the seminal paper on comparing expressive power of Turing-complete languages. It's worth a read if it's not come across your radar before! [LINK] Although, Shriram Krishnamurthi's talk from two years ago on this paper might be more approachable. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26473752,"author":"opnitro","month":"2021-03","story":"Rust's type system is Turing-complete (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Formal basis for comparing expressive power of Turing-complete languages","quote":"a very good talk \"On the expressive power of programming languages\"[1][2]","text":"On this notion, a very good talk \"On the expressive power of programming languages\"[1][2] which attempts to provide a formal basis for distinguishing between expressive power of turning complete languages. [1] [VIDEO] [2] The paper it's based on [LINK]"},{"id":47378793,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2026-03","story":"Zig – Type Resolution Redesign and Language Changes","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for a mathematical definition of feature equivalence","quote":"There is one more mathematical definition of whether two features are equal","text":"> It is, for some definition of object-oriented. Sure, for some definition of red, green is red. E.g., colorblind people. I'm interested in more broadly accepted jargon. The problem is, Rust isn't really object-oriented either. I'm interested in a mostly consistent and hopefully majority definition. It's not message-passing sense (can't do cool fancy things* a la Ruby or Smalltalk); nor is it inheritance-based (can't do inheritance-based nor prototype-based OOP patterns). There is one more mathematical definition of whether two features are equal, but it involves languages, local macros, and Turing machines. See [VIDEO] * There was some kind of message recorder and playback in Ruby/Smalltalk, that I can't find. Basically send methods to objects and record them, then playback them at later date. Will update if I find it."},{"id":40560020,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2024-06","story":"in Rust, methods should be object safe","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for precise definitions of language feature power","quote":"","text":"> Python OOP != Rust OOP, but you naturally already knew that. No. Bold of you to assume that. I wasn't aware that one OOP isn't another OOP. I see, so OOP is a fuzzyish definition that you check more checkboxes to satisfy. If Rust is OOP, and Python is OOP and C is OOP, then the that circles back to my initial claims. A category that is so wide it's effectively useless. Compare this to sync and async. A precise definition, much greater informational content. You can prove something is pure or not[1]. Compare this to OOP, imprecise, less informational context, impossible to (dis)prove something is OOP or not. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32118638,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2022-07","story":"How I went about learning Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Used as framework to reason about adding inheritance to Rust","quote":"","text":"Post Scriptum: one way to prove this is that we could use [VIDEO] as a base. How to prove. Assume that we add struct inheritance to Rust (it looks similar to JS) - call it RustInh. Does adding inheritance to Rust increase expressive power? If yes, then right now Rust can't express OOP concept as inheritance. I.e. is there a C[RustInh] = C[Rust]. And way to prove it is to look at Deref anti-patterns [LINK] I'm still not sure that fully captures my intuition of easy to write."},{"id":22190526,"author":"pron","month":"2020-01","story":"C++20 Reference Card","stance":"reference","why":"Gives a precise definition of language expressive power","quote":"expressive power (which has a pretty good and precise definition","text":"> besides you don't want to go read the implementation of your functions every time do you ? IDEs show us only prototypes for a reason The IDE can be changed to show you assertions in addition to the signature, just as it also shows doc comments. That the signature is special isn't some law of nature, it's just habit (and users of untyped languages don't even have those). > How would you for instance specialize a generic function in Zig ? ... and properly dispatches between both ? No, you'd need a single definition with a comptime branch. > I'd be hard pressed to say that it is \"as powerful\" given what I'm seeing for now. What you're seeing is personal taste. In my taste, that Zig doesn't allow overloading and has clear dispatch is clearer is more preferable (in fact, it's touted as one of Zig's biggest strengths); in yours, it is less preferable. That's perfectly OK, but it's got nothing to do with expressive power (which has a pretty good and precise definition: [VIDEO] ). There is a reason why some languages are more complicated while others are simple -- some people aesthetically prefer the more complex languages while others prefer simpler ones. What is impressive about Zig is that despite being simple, it's not giving up power, at least in those aspects I mentioned. C, another simple language, cannot do those things."}],"r":[["dF_9YcehCZo",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["3HYw6vPR9qU",1],["5uljtqyBLxI",1]]},"7Nw6qyyrTeI":{"wy":"The go-to link in urban planning threads for the claim that suburban infrastructure costs more per resident than it pays back.","q":{"x":"Good overview of the research on this","i":33249413,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":47172204,"author":"gpvos","month":"2026-02","story":"The United States needs fewer bus stops","stance":"rec","why":"Compelling argument that suburban finances are unsound.","quote":"I find the arguments of Strong Towns and Not Just Bikes quite compelling","text":"Well, you're parking it in other places as well, I assume, unless you're only making pleasure rides with no stops at all. I am speaking more generally than just about your particular situation, which I obviously don't know. But in case you live in an American suburb: their entire financial setup is unsound, which arguably means even your own ground is subsidized. I find the arguments of Strong Towns and Not Just Bikes quite compelling: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":45323914,"author":"rglullis","month":"2025-09","story":"Why, as a responsible adult, SimCity 2000 hits differently","stance":"rec","why":"Shows suburbs cost more per capita and generate less tax revenue.","quote":"The whole series of videos from Strong Towns are good, you should take a look","text":"No, suburbs cost more per capita than people living in the inner city, generate less tax revenue and end up becoming a net negative to municipalities: [VIDEO] The whole series of videos from Strong Towns are good, you should take a look: [LINK]"},{"id":36480961,"author":"jwestbury","month":"2023-06","story":"U.S. pedestrian deaths reach a 40-year high","stance":"rec","why":"Great video showing suburbs are economically supported by cities, despite disliking the host.","quote":"he does have a great video on this","text":"Curious, have you considered the in-between here? Consider medium-density European cities, which still have massively higher density suburbs, but rarely the density -- combined employment and population densities -- of the urban core of most American cities. I hated living in Seattle, but I'm now an American finding I enjoy living in London. I walk around trees and birds every day, because I have good park access, and I can walk to them without having to worry about car traffic. For what it's worth, by the way, you almost certainly AREN'T willing to pay for the infrastructure that supports you: Suburbs are almost overwhelmingly economically supported by cities. I hate NotJustBikes (his self-righteous attitude is just awful), but he does have a great video on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34598083,"author":"uoaei","month":"2023-01","story":"CA Cities to Lose All Zoning Powers in 2 Days","stance":"rec","why":"Video summarizing studies showing suburbs drain city budgets.","quote":"Here's a video about some of the studies done to come to this conclusion","text":"If we taxed suburbs by how much they actually drain city budgets, no one would want to live in suburbs. It is by now a well-studied fact that suburbs are effectively subsidized by metropolitan cores. Makers and takers, indeed... Here's a video about some of the studies done to come to this conclusion: [VIDEO] The folks at Strong Towns also talk a lot about productive land use measured as property tax income per square foot. They say it is the gold standard way to make sure your budget remains solvent and you can provide the services people need to make your city thrive. Obviously this means building up."},{"id":33249413,"author":"downrightmike","month":"2022-10","story":"Office vacancy rate in San Francisco just hit a new high","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of research showing downtown tax revenue subsidizes suburban sprawl.","quote":"Good overview of the research on this","text":"Because \"Office Vacancy Rate in San Francisco Just Hit a New High\", That means property owners aren't making money. Workers aren't out spending to come in, stay and leave. All that is taxable, which is why government officials want people back in the office. In every city, the downtown core's tax revenue subsidizes the suburban sprawl all around it. Good overview of the research on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45204142,"author":"nehal3m","month":"2025-09","story":"Pontevedra, Spain declares its entire urban area a \"reduced traffic zone\"","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for low-density suburbs being subsidized by dense cities.","quote":"low density suburbs are subsidised by high density cities","text":"The current cost of owning and operating an automobile is around 12K USD assuming 15K miles driven yearly according to BTS [0]. This would push it up to a little over 14K USD. Then there's oil and gas subsidies that should be taken into account, since around 24% of oil consumption is from cars and light trucks. [1] Then there's some other factors that are hard to quantify but have a huge impact on taxes, like how low density suburbs are subsidised by high density cities [2] as an effect of car-first infrastructure. It's not as simple as just the cost of roads. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42242680,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2024-11","story":"The size of BYD's factory","stance":"reference","why":"Linked for evidence that dense centers subsidize sparse suburbs.","quote":"","text":"> There is just no way to economically justify bikelanes everywhere Roads pre-date cars. Cars muscled in and took over, forced humans off the roads onto sidewalks. Now car drivers say it cannot be economically justified for people to move around outside cars? This is \"car-brain\" thinking. If the cars were banned, people could walk and cycle and wheelchair and skate on the roads their taxes are already paying for . They aren't \"car roads\", they are just roads - cycles are allowed on them. Car drivers don't want to share, don't want to slow down, keep hitting and killing people, can't control their vehicles safely, so demand cyclists be moved somewhere else - then complain about the cost of doing that! People say cars have taken over, they want somewhere safe from the dangers of cars, car drivers say no it's too expensive to make yourself safe from me commuting through the places you live and work ! It's crazy land. As if the only reason Christchurch exists at all is for cars to drive through . Can you point to a report that has a cost/beneift analysis of each individual road in Christchurch? Because when Urban3 set out to find out that kind of thing in USA and Canadian cities[2] they found that the dense urban centers (\"poor\") were the parts of a city with enough tax revenue to cover their infrastructure costs, and the sparse suburbs (\"rich\") were being subsidised by them. The people in city center apartments, possibly without cars, possibly transit riders, pay for the sprawli"},{"id":39313365,"author":"fmobus","month":"2024-02","story":"Cyclist hit by driverless Waymo car in San Francisco, police say","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that suburban land use is subsidized like a pyramid scheme.","quote":"","text":"> The US is a continent spanning nation How is that relevant? No one commutes from NY to LA everyday, much as no one commutes from Berlin to Munich everyday. > and half the country does not live in a city Yes, and that's what I mean by \"dumb land-use patterns\". That people living in suburbs are not technically within a city's boundaries is a distinction without a difference. Land is wasted to highways and parking lots no one can cross without a two-ton machine, useless lawns no one really has time to enjoy or maintain, dumb McMansions that cost and arm and a leg and so on. And all of that is subsidised in what is effectively a [pyramid scheme]< [VIDEO] >."},{"id":36734807,"author":"fnimick","month":"2023-07","story":"S.F. says incidents by Cruise, Waymo driverless taxis are ‘skyrocketing.’","stance":"reference","why":"Backs claim that suburbs are going bankrupt from car infrastructure costs.","quote":"the suburbs are going bankrupt because car infrastructure is so expensive.","text":"> That money doesn't come from nowhere, the roads are paid for by the property and gas taxes of those burbs. Two points: many roads get federal and state funding from the general tax fund meaning we all pay for them whether we drive or not, and the suburbs are going bankrupt because car infrastructure is so expensive. [VIDEO] As for your other complaints, why is living in an apartment you own in a city with shared public spaces rather than owning private land such a downgrade?"},{"id":35396292,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2023-04","story":"Maps show how parking lots “eat” U.S. cities","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as one source that density subsidizes low-density areas.","quote":"","text":"> Who cares? People who want fiscally responsible government? \"The Many Costs of Too Much Parking\": * [LINK] And that's not even getting into high(er) density areas subsidizing low(er) density areas: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":33914112,"author":"mwest217","month":"2022-12","story":"Nearly six in 10 US young adults live within 10 miles of where they grew up","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among resources on suburban finances.","quote":"Lots of resources on the subject from Strong Towns[1], Not Just Bikes[2], and various consulting firms like Urban3[3]","text":"Lots of resources on the subject from Strong Towns[1], Not Just Bikes[2], and various consulting firms like Urban3[3] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":32501107,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-08","story":"Why American cities are broke (2021) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Math behind suburbs being subsidized by dense city cores.","quote":"","text":"> So why don't the cities expand their boundaries to include the suburbs […] Why would anyone want to absorb something that costs more to run to provide the same services? * [LINK] > […] instead of trying to kill the suburbs and pull the people back into the city (where they will generally be unhappy since they had chosen to live in suburbs in the first place)? Perhaps they want to kill them because they are wasteful of area (often paving over perfectly good agricultural land), and cause climate change due to their reliance on cars. Further, how much \"choice\" did suburban dwellers actually have? Post-WW2 most zoning has forced the creation of car-centric, low-density sprawl. Perhaps there are folks in the suburbs that want higher density neighbourhoods but because of the limited supply (due to lack of new build) the prices have gone up and they're priced out. Whose to say that walkable neighbourhoods wouldn't be popular if purchased in the \"suburbs: * [LINK] > † Side note: I have not found to be true, personally. I think this depends on the municipal boundaries involved. Depending on where the border is, on one side it could be that things built in the \"old\" / pre-WW2 way and on the other the \"new\" / post-WW2 way, and the taxes go to each municipality appropriately. In other places there could be the pre-WW2 Old Downtown and is walkable, but everything new is non-walkable. In those latter situations the more walkable parts are probably subsidizing the less walkable ones: * [V"},{"id":32594681,"author":"gernb","month":"2022-08","story":"Too many Americans live in places built for cars – not for human connection","stance":"reference","why":"Argues suburbs rely on subsidies from dense urban areas.","quote":"They just need to start paying for their externities and stop relying on being subsidized by the dense urban areas.","text":"That's fine. They just need to start paying for their externities and stop relying on being subsidized by the dense urban areas. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31884762,"author":"belligeront","month":"2022-06","story":"We need to make adulthood more desirable","stance":"reference","why":"Suburban infrastructure costlier per resident than urban","quote":"Suburban infrastructure is much more costly per resident than urban infrastructure.","text":"Suburban infrastructure is much more costly per resident than urban infrastructure. However, we massively subsidize suburban infrastructure and restrict urban environments with zoning. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30954049,"author":"flukus","month":"2022-04","story":"‘Housing in ‘07 wasn’t a bubble” is a true statement that almost no one believes","stance":"reference","why":"Sprawl unsustainable in the long term","quote":"","text":"> When the demand for houses rises, Flatland metropolitan areas, which don't really have traditional downtowns, just sprawl some more. And this is the driving force behind those cities going bankrupt, they're easy to build but not sustainable in the long term: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31047560,"author":"Hammershaft","month":"2022-04","story":"Tax the Land","stance":"reference","why":"Suburban infrastructure subsidized","quote":"","text":"> Of course it will distort choices and leave some people worse off. If the author can't see that, then they have a serious bias problem. The whole point of the land value tax is to tax land to encourage higher density and higher cost use. Under our current system we subsidize low value land uses by subsidizing the crap out of infrastructure to expansions of suburban areas zoned for single family homes. [VIDEO] The only revenue positive portion of nearly any modern american city/town are the few sparse walkable mixed use neighbourhoods and the dense urban core. Policies like a land value tax that push for higher value land uses are necessary to stave off the oncoming wave of municipal bankruptcies America faces. > Income tax would only provide a disincentive to work if the alternative (assistance programs) is more attractive. You are thinking in absolute terms and not on the margins. If the marginal income tax beyond a threshold jumps by %10, then even discounting the increasingly marginal value of each additional dollar earned I will value the return on work less when I hit that tax threshold."},{"id":31083822,"author":"kogepathic","month":"2022-04","story":"Canada bans foreign home buyers for two years","stance":"reference","why":"Urban sprawl unsustainable at current tax rates","quote":"","text":"> 1 house - tax breaks Why should society be subsidizing home ownership more than they already do? The real estate market already discriminates against people who cannot afford a deposit (an increasing proportion of people, since house prices are increasing so rapidly), and the design of North American cities encourages urban spawl which is unsustainable with current property tax rates. [1] > and I think to promote everyone's owning a house Why? Half of German households rent [2] and no one is wringing their hands about how people should be owning their home instead of renting. As someone who has lived in Europe and North America, there seems to be a borderline obsession with owning a house in anglophone countries, and I don't understand why. If the rental market is sane (e.g. rent is approximately equivalent to a mortgage) then it's up to the individual if they want to purchase a home (e.g. to renovate how they see fit) or is comfortable just renting. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":30843439,"author":"kd913","month":"2022-03","story":"We can do better than “same, but electric”","stance":"reference","why":"Suburbs as financial hole for cities","quote":"","text":">I sure do understand it, but I also understand that when it comes to comparing the environmental impact of upper middle class urbanites and suburbanites, we're splitting hairs Yea that is not at all true. You can literally compare the CO2 per capita for an average American/Canadian and someone from Europe and in general they produce 1/3 of the CO2. It is not a negligible difference. This is largely caused because of the urban designs which don't focus on gas dependent suburbia. Or the benefits from efficiency at scale which can only be achieved in high density living accommodations. [LINK] It is also a plain fact that downtown urban blocks are the profit generators for cities whilst suburbia is largely a financial hole. [VIDEO] You looking at a classist angle is I think an internal bias. There are poor/rich in both areas. It's just a straight fact though that suburban design is not sustainable both economically and ecologically."},{"id":30844901,"author":"karmelapple","month":"2022-03","story":"We can do better than “same, but electric”","stance":"reference","why":"True cost of suburbs to cities","quote":"","text":"> I'm not sure how we can hope to stop that inertia short of being limited by cheap energy or running out of actual land. Having the residents of the suburbs paying for their true cost seems like a great way to start: [VIDEO] As the video points out, would people be willing to start paying $9000 in property taxes instead of $1800 to stop operating the city at a loss every year?"},{"id":30854981,"author":"afiori","month":"2022-03","story":"The worst part of working from home is now haunting reopened offices","stance":"reference","why":"Suburban sprawl costs cities more than taxes bring in","quote":"Essentially urban sprawl/suburbia/single family homes cost more for the city","text":"To add a reference: Suburbia is Subsidized: Here's the Math -> [VIDEO] Essentially urban sprawl/suburbia/single family homes cost more for the city (road upkeep, water/gas pipes, etc.) than what they contribute in taxes, so many cities are in a positive feedback loop of building more houses to get quick money to do maintenance on the previous rounds of growth."}],"r":[["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["esQyYGezS7c",1],["B9ly7JjqEb0",1],["RQY6WGOoYis",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["y_SXXTBypIg",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"VQKMoT-6XSg":{"wy":"Cited as a landmark tech presentation, especially for the audience's astonished reaction to touch scrolling and demo moments like visual voicemail.","q":{"x":"I consider AlphaGo - The Movie [1] to be a timeless classic that will never feel outdated.","i":43714245,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43714245,"author":"redbell","month":"2025-04","story":"In Two Moves, AlphaGo and Lee Sedol Redefined the Future (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Greatest live tech event ever, crowd reacting to new gestures","quote":"the greatest live tech event ever captured on film","text":"I consider AlphaGo - The Movie [1] to be a timeless classic that will never feel outdated. In my opinion, it surpasses even Hollywood productions, despite being based on true events and filmed live with real people. I'm ranking it as #2, though, because I still believe Steve Jobs' 2007 iPhone presentation [2] is the greatest live tech event ever captured on film. Hearing the crowd screaming when seeing some tricks and techniques ( eg. slide to unlock , pinch to zoom and scrolling up ) on how to use the phone does really triggers some haptic feedback in my heart because we are now so used to these tricks that were pure magic back then. ______________________________ 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24279553,"author":"maneesh","month":"2020-08","story":"My startup validation process","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Original iPhone demo, especially visual voicemail, still impressive","quote":"That part of the initial demo still continues to blow my mind.","text":"Phone, music player, and internet communicator in a single device, that enabled worldwide communication reliably. Just the original phone solved voicemail (with visual voicemail) once and for all in one fell swoop. It was just echelons better. That part of the initial demo still continues to blow my mind. [VIDEO] Here’s the original introduction to the iphone: [LINK]"},{"id":39725887,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2024-03","story":"Vision Pro: What we got wrong at Oculus that Apple got right","stance":"rec","why":"Argues Vision Pro convincingly fools the mind into seeing it as real","quote":"A very interesting video take","text":"A very interesting video take: [VIDEO] tl;dw -- reviewers are understandably focusing on the technical specs, but ignoring the much more simple metric: conceptually, does the device fool the mind into accepting what it sees as \"real\"? And the answer for the Vision Pro is yes. It will get lighter, better, cheaper, but even now, it's important to realize that the AVP can transport you to another realistic reality in a way no other device has managed. I've only done the in-store demo, but I understand what the video is saying, and it resonates for me."},{"id":37362814,"author":"Seanambers","month":"2023-09","story":"Why the Apple phone will fail, and fail badly (2006)","stance":"rec","why":"Classic Jobs keynote introducing a major leap in phones","quote":"Steve Jobs Introducing at Macworld in 2007 is a classic for the ages.","text":"It's hard to overstate just how big of a leap the iPhone was. Critics only focused on the shortcomings but neglected to see what it really was. Steve Jobs Introducing at Macworld in 2007 is a classic for the ages. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44861355,"author":"jcheng","month":"2025-08","story":"1910: The year the modern world lost its mind","stance":"reference","why":"Audience reaction to scrolling a list on iPhone","quote":"Listen to the audience reaction when he shows how you scroll a list view","text":"Not at all. The iPod of the day had a click wheel as an input device. The iPhone introduced us to capacitive touch, multitouch, gesture recognition, full web browsing. Huge leap compared to not only the iPod but the PocketPCs of the day. Listen to the audience reaction when he shows how you scroll a list view: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43867258,"author":"jasode","month":"2025-05","story":"Judge rules Apple executive lied under oath, makes criminal contempt referral","stance":"reference","why":"Audience astonished at capacitive finger scrolling demo","quote":"the audience at Macworld 2007 gasped in astonishment when Steve Jobs demonstrated gentle finger scrolling on a capacitive screen","text":">, we had plenty touch screen smartphones in Europe, between Nokia, Siemens-Ericson and PocketPC/Windows CE. Those were older TFT resistive touchscreens and not the newer capacitive touchscreens that could detect multi-finger gestures like swipes and pinch-to-zoom. TFT touchscreens requiring finger pressure instead of finger swipes is not as intuitive a UI. That's why the audience at Macworld 2007 gasped in astonishment when Steve Jobs demonstrated gentle finger scrolling on a capacitive screen. Deep link to that demo: [VIDEO] Keep in mind that MacWorld tradeshow attendees are technology geeks who are aware of the latest gadgets and phones . Many of those in the audience would already have the latest 2006 Palm Treo 680 in their pocket that had a TFT touchscreen. ( [LINK] ) The iPhone's touchscreen capabilities made that Palm phone's TFT touchscreen technology obsolete. You can check the Europe news archives back in 2007 to see both the telecoms and customers there were also eagerly awaiting the iPhone. If Europe already had equivalent touchscreen smartphones with Nokia, Siemens-Ericsson etc, the iPhone would have been a non-event and flopped in sales. Nokia/Blackberry/WindowsCE/Android/etc switched to capacitive touchscreens to compete with the iPhone. Why didn't European devs collectively just ignore the Apple iPhone and instead, focus on Nokia Symbian OS? Devs did ignore platforms if they wanted to. E.g. the devs mostly ignored the Blackberry OS and Microsoft Windows CE Mob"},{"id":39614080,"author":"nomilk","month":"2024-03","story":"Alan Kay's talk at UCLA – Feb 2024 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I heard of Alan Kay via Steve Jobs's intro of the iPhone [1], but otherwise know little about him - can anyone recommend other Alan Kay talks/essays/books? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35818036,"author":"chrisco255","month":"2023-05","story":"Steve Jobs and the Usable Computer (6 October 2011 by Tim Berners-Lee)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the original iPhone demo was a huge hit","quote":"Steve Jobs original iPhone demo was a huge, huge hit","text":"> I do not recall iPhone in 2007 being that huge of a hit. I was working in mobile phone retail in 2007. It was absolutely an enormous hit, and was sold out everywhere. They couldn't ramp up production fast enough. Steve Jobs original iPhone demo was a huge, huge hit: [VIDEO] Apple already had a huge, established, fanatic base of iPod users by 2007. The presentation went viral, and people couldn't wait to get their hands on it."}],"r":[["5TkIe60y2GI",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["v3y8AIEX_dU",1],["Ds-v2-qyCc8",1],["sI-1YNAmWlk",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["pLPpl2ISKTg",1],["oqu5DjzOBF8",1],["WXuK6gekU1Y",1],["ExwqNreocpg",1],["MnrJzXM7a6o",1]]},"Y5K9HBiJpuk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a detailed, pilot's-eye summary of the whole MH370 story and the case for searching again.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend Mentour Pilots videos on the topic, for an indepth introduction to the whole story and the new search","i":46434120,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46434120,"author":"V__","month":"2025-12","story":"MH370 vanished in 2014.New search aims to find answers families desperately want","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth introduction to the whole MH370 story and the new search.","quote":"I highly recommend Mentour Pilots videos on the topic, for an indepth introduction to the whole story and the new search","text":"I highly recommend Mentour Pilots videos on the topic, for an indepth introduction to the whole story and the new search: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":43182300,"author":"ReptileMan","month":"2025-02","story":"MH370: Malaysia is resuming the search for missing 777 airliner, 11 years later","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best summary of MH370 and the case for resuming the search.","quote":"Probably the best summary and why it makes sense to actually search for it, courtesy of mentour pilot.","text":"Probably the best summary and why it makes sense to actually search for it, courtesy of mentour pilot. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41377469,"author":"mcbain","month":"2024-08","story":"Final two communications from MH370 support controlled descent scenario (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed pilot's look at MH370 with many new details.","quote":"I think Petter's (Mentour Pilot) delve into it is quite good - there's a lot of detail I hadn't known.","text":"[VIDEO] I think Petter's (Mentour Pilot) delve into it is quite good - there's a lot of detail I hadn't known."},{"id":41377553,"author":"sofixa","month":"2024-08","story":"Final two communications from MH370 support controlled descent scenario (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Recap of MH370 information, speculation and the most recent news.","quote":"If anyone is interested in recently made content that recaps the whole thing, all the available information, some of the speculation","text":"If anyone is interested in recently made content that recaps the whole thing, all the available information, some of the speculation, including the most recent news/information: * video by Mentour Pilot - [VIDEO] * great read, as usual, by Admiral Cloudberg, slightly older: [LINK]"},{"id":39739109,"author":"jkic47","month":"2024-03","story":"Ignore the Nutty Conspiracies, MH370 Was Likely a 'Zombie Flight'","stance":"rec","why":"Pilot's view of MH370, including WSPR radio tracking technique around 45 minutes in.","quote":"For those interested in a pilot's view of this incident, check out MentourPilot's latest video.","text":"For those interested in a pilot's view of this incident, check out MentourPilot's latest video. [VIDEO] About 45 min into the video, he quotes a technique called WSPR that can use radio transmissions to track flights. Super interesting, if validated. A less easily digested paper on WSPR technology is available under the video."},{"id":40432543,"author":"dsalfdslfdsa","month":"2024-05","story":"Underwater acoustic analysis reveals unique pressure signals – Revisiting MH370","stance":"reference","why":"Covers another sensing method for estimating MH370's last movements.","quote":"Mentour Pilot recently released a video with another sensing method for estimating the last movements of MH370","text":"Mentour Pilot recently released a video with another sensing method for estimating the last movements of MH370: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AW3gaelBypY",1],["7OpM_zKGE4o",1],["REni8Oi1QJQ",1],["2SuvO4Gi7uY",1],["XfQUOHlAocY",1],["lG4VkPoG3ko",1],["esQyYGezS7c",1],["LkH2r-sNjQs",1]]},"ZMByI4s-D-Y":{"wy":"Recommended in kilogram-redefinition threads as a clear, short explainer of the Avogadro and Planck approaches.","q":{"x":"this video is an imperative watch","i":6618343,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":8093058,"author":"gsteinb88","month":"2014-07","story":"The Meter: The Measure of a Man","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic video on the sphere for redefining the kilogram","quote":"Fantastic video by Vertiasium on this","text":"Fantastic video by Vertiasium on this: [VIDEO] (side note: that video is about a sphere, but the current standard kilogram and its copies are actually shaped like little bells)"},{"id":6618343,"author":"kunai","month":"2013-10","story":"The kilo is losing weight, changing all of science","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers the Planck and Avogadro projects to redefine the kilo","quote":"this video is an imperative watch","text":"For anyone interested in the Planck and Avogadro projects to redefine the kilo, this video is an imperative watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10502692,"author":"aexaey","month":"2015-11","story":"Why nuclear energy is our best option at the moment","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the gram was defined from water","quote":"actually the whole video is excellent","text":"One cubic centimetre of water at 4 degrees Celsius used to define one gram (or more precisely one litre -> one kilogram, which comes out to be the exact same thing) [1], [2]. Although 1 cm^3 of water is still pretty damn close to 1g. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] (around 1:50, and actually the whole video is excellent)"},{"id":8761520,"author":"ozh","month":"2014-12","story":"Why Won’t America Go Metric?","stance":"rec","why":"Short documentary on the metric system and the roundest object","quote":"Well worth 10 minutes of your break today.","text":"I stumbled upon a very interesting short documentary about the metric system and the world's roundest object. Well worth 10 minutes of your break today. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6617702,"author":"EGreg","month":"2013-10","story":"The kilo is losing weight, changing all of science","stance":"rec","why":"Nice video on the kilogram and its redefinition","quote":"there's a nice video on this","text":"\"Weirdly, it’s not even known if the IPK is getting lighter, or if the national prototypes are getting heavier — but either way, something is causing these kilos to change weight, by around 50 micrograms every 100 years.\" Well they COULD always do that thing with the water where they heat it to 4 degrees celcius and measure its volume and weight. Then they'd know which it is. That said, there's a nice video on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6618991,"author":"JamieLewis","month":"2013-10","story":"The kilo is losing weight, changing all of science","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the kilogram got its name","quote":"it is also very interesting in it's own right.","text":"Check out this veritasium video [VIDEO] - It goes into a little detail on how the kilogram got it's name, it is also very interesting in it's own right."},{"id":18473248,"author":"fengb","month":"2018-11","story":"International System of Units overhauled in historic vote","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"More info from an older Veritasium video","text":"Mole was just redefined as exactly 6.02214076e23 — the number of Si-28 atoms in a perfect 1 kg sphere. This was an intentionally check against the kibble balance measurement. More info from an older Veritasium video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14712200,"author":"DonbunEf7","month":"2017-07","story":"NIST to redefine the kilogram based on a fundamental universal constant","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"As Veritasium explains [0], all of the reference kilograms have drifted","text":"As Veritasium explains [0], all of the reference kilograms have drifted, and some appear to weigh less than they used to. So even if we know the precise mechanism of action of the drift, it doesn't help with the fact that our measurements are less reliable than they used to be. Of course, what's probably happened is that our measuring tools have gotten more accurate! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6619118,"author":"velodrome","month":"2013-10","story":"The kilo is losing weight, changing all of science","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The scientific community is working on the replacement: Known as the Avogadro Project, the plan is to bring together enough atoms of one substance – silicon – to make a kilo. Attention has focused on silicon because: - its characteristics are very well understood - a single crystal of the right size can be grown - its atomic structure is extremely uniform - its widespread use in the computer industry means it can be obtained with relative ease at high purity and resonable cost. A spherical shape was chosen because a sphere has no edges that might get damaged and only one dimension has to be measured in order to calculate its volume. --- [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["qOP2V_np2c0",1],["8q71hrwUcu0",1],["xoVJKj8lcNQ",1],["_fHfgU8oMSo",1],["ipRvjS7q1DI",1]]},"ph8LyNIT9sg":{"wy":"HN treats it as required viewing for understanding the PS5's SSD and architecture beyond raw specs.","q":{"x":"IMO required viewing for any dev to form a more informed opinion on the SSD tech.","i":23506604,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":36693461,"author":"xela79","month":"2023-07","story":"RPCS3 – The Open Source Playstation 3 Emulator","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lead architect on performance goals and optimization for PS5","quote":"I can really recommend this tech talk regarding PS4 and PS5 hardware by their lead hardware architect","text":"I can really recommend this tech talk regarding PS4 and PS5 hardware by their lead hardware architect [VIDEO] some really interesting topics related to what kind of performance they wanted as a baseline and how to optimize to make the PS5 do what it currently does... like no other platform. a $700 console that: - outputs 4k / HDR (upscaled from lower native res. ofc) - renders games steadily at 30 / 60fps - no hitches, framedrops - always records your gameplay - live video sharing of stream with PS friends - live streaming to youtube - updates of games being installed - downloads of games/data all simultaneous, instant game switches, quick loading times. the experience cannot be replicated with a 1k gaming PC, not even a $2000 machine. They really delivered a device which is imho, more than just \"an AMD gaming PC with a custom GUI and some DRM\" would have loved to have widescale keyboard/mouse support, as playing Far Cry with a controller is frustrating at best. And keyboard/mouse support is in hands of the game dev if they want to support it or not."},{"id":23506604,"author":"greyskull","month":"2020-06","story":"Storage Matters: Why Xbox and Playstation SSDs Usher in a New Era of Gaming","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cerny's practical explanation of PS5 SSD","quote":"IMO required viewing for any dev to form a more informed opinion on the SSD tech.","text":"IMO required viewing for any dev to form a more informed opinion on the SSD tech. Cerny talks about it fairly practically. Starting point on SSD: [VIDEO] for about 20 minutes"},{"id":23507659,"author":"slx26","month":"2020-06","story":"Storage Matters: Why Xbox and Playstation SSDs Usher in a New Era of Gaming","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Architecture over raw power: removing bottlenecks in PS5","quote":"a lot of people commenting here should really go watch the talk Mark Cerny gave","text":"Yes, a lot of people commenting here should really go watch the talk Mark Cerny gave [0]. It's clear there's no \"big technological breakthrough\". SSDs are great but have been around for a while already, we simply had to wait for the technology to mature enough. What we are seeing is that we can't just keep slapping better CPUs and GPUs to the machines, because the improvements nowaday are not that impressive anymore, so at least for the PS5 they have switched and started focusing on the architecture , removing bottlenecks, removing major pain points, and using additional hardware to handle tasks like compression and freeing resources for other purposes. Some people might say these improvements happen because they were lazy before and never focused properly on dev needs and bottlenecks and the specific needs of game development. I partially agree, but the SSDs really don't just improve disk speed, they allow a completely different set of architectural optimizations. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27250245,"author":"jonas21","month":"2021-05","story":"What’s your API’s “Time To 200”?","stance":"rec","why":"Cerny on time to triangle on consoles","quote":"if you have the time, it's definitely worth watching the entire talk","text":"I believe \"time to 200\" is a reference to \"time to triangle,\" which Playstation architect Mark Cerny and others have used to describe the amount of time it takes to get a game engine up and running on a new console to the point where it can render its first triangle [1]. You can see him talking about time to triangle on various Playstation consoles here [2] (and if you have the time, it's definitely worth watching the entire talk). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27304699,"author":"wincy","month":"2021-05","story":"Marvell Announces First PCIe 5.0 NVMe SSD Controllers: Up to 14 GB/S","stance":"rec","why":"Technical talk on PS5 OS and file packaging","quote":"There’s a good technical talk about it from one of Sony’s engineers.","text":"The PS5s operating system and even how they package game files had to be developed from the ground up to realize these gains. There’s a good technical talk about it from one of Sony’s engineers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22633845,"author":"Avamander","month":"2020-03","story":"PlayStation 5: the specs and the tech","stance":"rec","why":"PS5 architecture talk, interesting even for non-console gamers","quote":"I found it really quite interesting, even if I don't game on consoles primarily.","text":"The talk for those interested: [VIDEO] I found it really quite interesting, even if I don't game on consoles primarily."},{"id":31196985,"author":"WithinReason","month":"2022-04","story":"MIT researchers develop a paper thin loudspeaker","stance":"reference","why":"Sony discusses calibrating HRTF to the listener","quote":"A good HRTF implementation should be calibrated to your head","text":"Well, I tried with some one else who also didn't feel any spatial effect. I think it depends on whether the binaural recording was done with a head/ear shape similar to yours. So you'll just have to take my word for it that the audio positioning is very accurate. A good HRTF implementation should be calibrated to your head, Sony was talking about that when discussing the PS5's spatial audio: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25008563,"author":"Kipters","month":"2020-11","story":"Playstation 5 review: Not just a more-powerful PS4","stance":"reference","why":"PS5 3D audio uses preset HRTFs","quote":"","text":"No, they'll have a set of pre-made HRTFs and then use a listening test to pick the most appropriate one for the user [VIDEO]"},{"id":23506255,"author":"colesantiago","month":"2020-06","story":"Storage Matters: Why Xbox and Playstation SSDs Usher in a New Era of Gaming","stance":"reference","why":"Sony's internals presentation met with disinterest","quote":"","text":"Yes, gamers don't give a toss about the tech details [0], and they shouldn't, Sony gave a presentation about all of the internals of the PS5 and all they got in response was \"ZZzzZZZZZZ\". They only want the console for the games, man, nothing else. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22932365,"author":"ddoolin","month":"2020-04","story":"Playstation Architecture: A Practical Analysis","stance":"reference","why":"PS5 architecture highlights","quote":"(Some Playstation 5 architecture highlights).","text":"It's crazy to see just how far they've come from the first console to the latest and all the lessons they've learned along the way. [VIDEO] (Some Playstation 5 architecture highlights)."}],"r":[["YjjTPV2pXY0",1],["MFzDaBzBlL0",1],["Q13CishCKXY",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["ML4tw_UzqZE",1]]},"-4EDhdAHrOg":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as the quickest memorable illustration of listening versus problem-solving, and as shorthand in threads on empathy and support.","q":{"x":"This was the single most impactful thing I've ever been told to watch when it comes to 'solving' vs 'listening'.","i":46694143,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46694143,"author":"garciasn","month":"2026-01","story":"I'm Addicted to Being Useful","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Teaches the difference between solving and listening.","quote":"This was the single most impactful thing I've ever been told to watch when it comes to 'solving' vs 'listening'.","text":"[VIDEO] This was the single most impactful thing I've ever been told to watch when it comes to 'solving' vs 'listening'."},{"id":46693631,"author":"jpadkins","month":"2026-01","story":"I'm Addicted to Being Useful","stance":"rec","why":"Shows not every problem voiced needs a solution.","quote":"There is a great YT video on this topic","text":"> It took decades for my wife to finally get through and explain not every problem she voices is something that needs a solution. There is a great YT video on this topic [VIDEO] It's not about the nail!"},{"id":46267279,"author":"btilly","month":"2025-12","story":"Hashcards: A plain-text spaced repetition system","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates addressing emotions before solving the logical problem.","quote":"You may enjoy, It's not about the nail.","text":"You may enjoy, It's not about the nail. [VIDEO] What I was doing is very common. Trying to engage logically with what logic can engage with, while failing to recognize that the emotional challenge is what has to be dealt with first. And that once feelings are out of the way, the logical problem will be massively easier to solve."},{"id":30412915,"author":"MikeTheGreat","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: How do I develop skills to comfort people and offer compassion?","stance":"rec","why":"Memorable two-minute illustration of listening versus problem solving.","quote":"there's this 2 minute video to memorably highlight the idea","text":"> You can actually ask, if they talk about the same thing more than once: Is this something you want me to work on and try to fix, or you just want to talk about? Agreed! Asking helps me clarify to myself the possible goals (and make it explicit that \"just\" listening is a valid goal). Even if you don't ask thinking about the question is a nice way to remind yourself that you don't need to solve it for them. My experience has been that women appreciate this question - I think that most men tend to jump into problem-solving mode and actually being asked is a nice change (here in the U.S.) And of course there's this 2 minute video to memorably highlight the idea :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":28820394,"author":"dandotway","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: What can you do to help the grieving?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows why people want to be heard, not fixed.","quote":"Some insight into why can be gained by watching the short video","text":"It's counterintuitive to logical-thinking programmers, but you almost never want to tell a grieving person any of the following: 1. \"Things will get better.\" 2. \"I understand your loss.\" 3. \"Here's how we can fix the problem.\" Some insight into why can be gained by watching the short video, It's Not About the Nail : [VIDEO] In many (but not all) cases, if the grieving are willing to communicate at all, they want their cries of pain heard and listened to. They would rather that you cried with them instead of trying to tell them that there is light at the end of the tunnel or that there is a 'fix' for their 'problem' or that we must accept that life is hard and unfair and suck it up."},{"id":17478337,"author":"tboughen","month":"2018-07","story":"Research shows the difference between emotional and informational support","stance":"reference","why":"Short sketch about wanting empathy rather than solutions.","quote":"If my wife or I fail to communicate that we want emotional, rather than informational, support we just say","text":"If my wife or I fail to communicate that we want emotional, rather than informational, support we just say “It’s not about the nail” and the other instantly understands. The short video this idea is from: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10064326,"author":"rebootthesystem","month":"2015-08","story":"How California Is Winning the Drought","stance":"reference","why":"Comedy sketch used as an analogy.","quote":"HN is the person with the nail on her head.","text":"I don't know who \"we\" might be. Do you work for ycombinator? Are you an official moderator? Otherwise not sure why you would say \"we\". Just wondering. That said, if we are talking about antagonism and HN, well, when it comes to topics outside of tech it is almost a sport to down-vote or berate anyone who does not align with a certain ideology. In the over six years or so I've been reading HN I have yet to see any \"we\" coming down to say \"We'd appreciate it if you wouldn't post like this here\" in any of those cases in defense of being accepting of alternative points of view. I go back to USENET days. HN is definitely better than any of that, by far, however, there's an underlying accepted ideology that is promoted here and everything else is rejected and attacked in various overt and and not-so-overt ways. That aspect is not very different from the kinds of things that could happen on USENET. Granted, public discussion boards are horrendously difficult to run. I get that part. Discussions on HN are thoughtful when it comes to matters of technology. Outside of that HN is very intolerant of alternative points of view. People can find themselves defending from personal attacks instead of engaging in discussing the issues. Something like this: [VIDEO] HN is the person with the nail on her head. She is happy for the split second the guy comes in to alignment with her point of view. Outside of that, when an alternative point of view is offered, not happy at all. Before anyone says a"},{"id":7892648,"author":"gnl","month":"2014-06","story":"Founder Depression","stance":"reference","why":"Short comedy sketch about fixing problems versus listening.","quote":"","text":"Well said. [VIDEO] [1:41 \"It's not about the nail\"] The video is about women, because as men we like to take pride in the idea that we're \"fixers\", but if we're honest about it we'll have to admit that when it comes to the important things in life we're just as full of crap. No amount of talking, hand-holding, support groups and nods of understanding will amount to any kind of personal progress, until you are willing to answer the question \"What do you really want?\" You want to be productive? Have a successful startup? Million dollars? Improve the lives of others? Sure. But why? What's behind it? We like to look for an objective meaning in things, but what is meaning other than the belief in a cosmic points system that will bestow rewards upon us if we play our roles right? Whether you are a startup founder, career person, scientist, devout christian, philanthropist, volunteer or whatever makes no essential difference here - pretty much everyone chases some ill-defined ideal in the hope that somehow some ill-defined someone or something will save us and make everything alright when we reach some ill-defined goal. And when you do succeed, what do you really have to show for it? Years of sacrifice gone that you'll never get back, even more expectations than before, because now you're supposed to act like a successful person, even more anxiety that you'll disappoint the people who idolize you, eager to believe in the fairy-tale that you've made it and everything's happily-ever-a"},{"id":39999380,"author":"tstrimple","month":"2024-04","story":"For twenty years, PostSecret has broadcast suburban America’s hidden truths","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Someone should tell SNL that they can't be funny because it's scripted. Comedians all across the globe should be out of a job because scripted comedy isn't funny apparently. This video has no value because it's clearly scripted. No way she actually has a nail sticking out of her head. When will these creators stop trying to fool us!? [VIDEO]"},{"id":35033570,"author":"groffee","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: How do you turn off your problem solving brain with people you love?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"People don't want their problems solving, they want to whine.","text":"\"It's not about the nail\" [VIDEO] People don't want their problems solving, they want to whine. It helps to start a conversation like this with \"do you want me to listen or to offer help\""},{"id":33643172,"author":"thedanbob","month":"2022-11","story":"What not to say to someone who has just been laid off","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My wife and I use the phrase","text":"My wife and I use the phrase “it’s not about the nail”[0] as an in-joke/shorthand for communicating this. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30854141,"author":"SilasX","month":"2022-03","story":"The worst part of working from home is now haunting reopened offices","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Or, it’s that the dog does tell all his doggie friends about his predicament, but any time they’re about to give him actionable advice for solving the core problem, he cuts them off, more interested in emotional catharsis. “It’s not about the nail.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":30413317,"author":"tlrobinson","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: How do I develop skills to comfort people and offer compassion?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"For a humorous take on this, see","text":"> This isn't strictly true but men tend to want to offer solutions to problems For a humorous take on this, see \"It's Not About The Nail\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":21591730,"author":"jlawson","month":"2019-11","story":"Basic Social Skills Guide (2012)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The other side of this is expressed in the classic comedy video","text":"The other side of this is expressed in the classic comedy video, \"It's Not About the Nail\" (1:41). [VIDEO]"},{"id":11466116,"author":"sokoloff","month":"2016-04","story":"How to Avoid Empathy Burnout","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I can't help but link to","text":"> When you're dealing with people, though, that kind of response can be actively counterproductive. Anyone who's had a significant other \"vent\" and respond negatively when you try to turn to problem solving will probably recognize this dynamic. I can't help but link to \"It's not about the Nail\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":7633048,"author":"brazzy","month":"2014-04","story":"Do Men Suck At Friendship?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Or, in a satirical manner","text":"> when talking to them I often feel all they're doing is telling me how right I am to feel in such and such way, frequently highlighting the differences between \"us\" and \"them\", whoever \"them\" might be, whenever the opportunity arise. (I suppose you'd call that 'gossip'). It's called \"emotional validation\", and widely acknowledged as something that women are notoriously keen on and men are notoriously bad at providing. [LINK] Or, in a satirical manner: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21590285,"author":"jimmaswell","month":"2019-11","story":"Basic Social Skills Guide (2012)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"There's a youtube video about those kind of crazies","text":"I'd rather just not talk to someone like that so if they don't want to be friends anymore over being suggested a mechanic when having car issues or something then good. There's a youtube video about those kind of crazies: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["0xS68sl2D70",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["AF8d72mA41M",1],["u6XAPnuFjJc",1],["A9UmdY0E8hU",1]]},"2yDCox-qKbk":{"wy":"Tinnitus sufferers share it as a free trick that briefly stops the ringing, though not as a cure.","q":{"x":"this trick helped me be in silence for the first time in decades.","i":38499308,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38499308,"author":"hackernewds","month":"2023-12","story":"Tinnitus Linked to Undetected Auditory Nerve Damage – A Step Towards a Cure","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Trick that gave commenter silence for the first time in decades.","quote":"this trick helped me be in silence for the first time in decades.","text":"to add to my other comment: [VIDEO] this trick helped me be in silence for the first time in decades. it was wonderful, to say the least"},{"id":38499418,"author":"hackernewds","month":"2023-12","story":"Tinnitus Linked to Undetected Auditory Nerve Damage – A Step Towards a Cure","stance":"rec","why":"Low-risk exercise for temporary tinnitus suppression.","quote":"you can also try this exercise for very low risk temporary tinnitus suppression","text":"you can also try this exercise for very low risk temporary tinnitus suppression [VIDEO] it works reliably well"},{"id":37853845,"author":"k4runa","month":"2023-10","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone gotten complete, permanent relief from tinnitus?","stance":"rec","why":"Thumping technique that temporarily stops tinnitus.","quote":"Not permanent but whenever it bothers me now I just do this thumping and it disappears in a few seconds","text":"I had tinnitus for a while, and tried a few things that didn't work but this thomping technique I found on reddit one day stops it, and I just do it every couple of days and it disappears for a while. Not permanent but whenever it bothers me now I just do this thumping and it disappears in a few seconds: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14623563,"author":"conception","month":"2017-06","story":"A music-lover's guide to tinnitus","stance":"rec","why":"Head-thumping trick for temporary tinnitus relief.","quote":"Thump the back of your head like that with your ears covered well about 40 times and enjoy the silence.","text":"I've always had tinnitus since I was a child and just thought the world was like that. A trick popped up on Reddit to temporarily, and perhaps for some with regular application, cures it for a short time. [VIDEO] Thump the back of your head like that with your ears covered well about 40 times and enjoy the silence."},{"id":13140252,"author":"suby","month":"2016-12","story":"Bose Hearphones","stance":"rec","why":"Technique that briefly silences tinnitus.","quote":"There's a technique that has the potential to temporarily (around 1 minute) clear tinnitus.","text":"There's a technique that has the potential to temporarily (around 1 minute) clear tinnitus. [VIDEO] It's not much and doesn't seem to work for everyone, but if you've been suffering from tinnitus for years, one minute is better than nothing."}],"r":[["RjD1aLm4Thg",1]]},"Nrp_LZ-XGsY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best beginner-friendly introduction to monoids, monads and other FP vocabulary.","q":{"x":"This was by far the best introduction to monoid, monads and few other functional programming terms.","i":25610624,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25610624,"author":"__henil","month":"2021-01","story":"F# for fun and profit: Terms and Conditions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best introduction to monoids, monads and FP terms","quote":"This was by far the best introduction to monoid, monads and few other functional programming terms.","text":"On the same note, I recently shared a link[0] to his great beginner talk on functional programming languages. This was by far the best introduction to monoid, monads and few other functional programming terms. Direct youtube[1] for those who don't want to click twice :) [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27809463,"author":"loopz","month":"2021-07","story":"Haskell for Beginners","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why to use functional programming, first step to learn Haskell","quote":"WHY: The Functional Programmer's Toolkit - Scott Wlaschin","text":"Learn Haskell in 1-2-3: 1) WHY: The Functional Programmer's Toolkit - Scott Wlaschin : [VIDEO] 2) WHAT: Learn Haskell in one video : [LINK] 3) HOW: Haskell without the theory : [LINK] It'll teach you enough to be dangerous. Obviously your learning has just begun, but with a mental platform that lets you study other resources in time and practically test things out."},{"id":25262816,"author":"bern4444","month":"2020-12","story":"Write your own arbitrary-precision JavaScript math library","stance":"rec","why":"Among resources for diving into functional programming","quote":"A lot of the articles in conjunction with some other resources[1][2][3] are great for diving into functional programming","text":"More of a comment on the author, I've found his other articles enlightening, useful as both introductions to functional programming ideas and as comprehensive deep dives demonstrating their intuitive nature both in code and contextual application. A lot of the articles in conjunction with some other resources[1][2][3] are great for diving into functional programming (I've watched and read these many times). If you do front end development with React, I highly recommend the following two videos[4][5] with Ryan Florence and Micahel Jackson (devs behind react-router and Remix) on the compositional nature of React components and hooks. The last note I'll add is, once you get into Functional Programming, you start wanting to use `compose` or its counter part, `pipe` everywhere and coding becomes a lot more fun. I'll put a final plug for one of my own posts on how composition manifests in Javascript/React code[6]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK]"},{"id":21881750,"author":"lioeters","month":"2019-12","story":"Functional programming design patterns (2015) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Newer talk by the same speaker","quote":"Same speaker, newer presentations","text":"Same speaker, newer presentations: Functional Design Patterns - Scott Wlaschin (2017) from NDC Conferences [LINK] The Functional Programmer's Toolkit - Scott Wlaschin (2019) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["zrpUDuUtxPM",1],["S82hsEDY8Pc",1]]},"ZKC3hiyLeRc":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a research-heavy introduction to how food type and hormones affect fat storage.","q":{"x":"Here's a good talk by a doctor, heavy on research, that's a decent introduction","i":37512855,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":15761664,"author":"anindha","month":"2017-11","story":"Sugar industry withheld possible evidence of cancer link 50 years ago","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fung's explanations of sugar and carbs seen as most scientific.","quote":"Dr. Fung's book and videos are the most informative and most scientific explanations of these concepts","text":"Refined sugar is the worst and refined carbohydrates are not that much better. I found that Dr. Fung's book and videos are the most informative and most scientific explanations of these concepts [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37512855,"author":"user3939382","month":"2023-09","story":"You're Not Losing Fat Because You're Eating Too Damn Much","stance":"rec","why":"Research-heavy decent introduction to food type and hormones.","quote":"Here's a good talk by a doctor, heavy on research, that's a decent introduction","text":"People get very heated and religious about this topic so I'm hesitant to bring it up but there are strong arguments that this model is overly simplistic and that the types of food you eat are strongly related to hormonal changes that dramatically affect your metabolism and fat storage. Here's a good talk by a doctor, heavy on research, that's a decent introduction: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33976127,"author":"user3939382","month":"2022-12","story":"Effect of Low-Carb, High-Fat Diet versus High-Carb, Low-Fat Diet","stance":"rec","why":"Talk discussing research on hormones and metabolism beyond calories.","quote":"here's for example a pretty good talk that discusses some of the research","text":"Weight isn't the product of calories in vs out, that's an overly simplistic model that doesn't account for the effect of hormones on how we metabolize different compounds and nutrients. This area of nutrition science is nascent and unsettled, and fraught with research funded by parties with conflicts of interest, but here's for example a pretty good talk that discusses some of the research [VIDEO]"},{"id":27777543,"author":"JohnWhigham","month":"2021-07","story":"Yes, drinking more water may help you lose weight","stance":"rec","why":"Fung on how hormones regulate fat stores.","quote":"Dr. Jason Fung is a leading researcher on this","text":"Calories aren't equal, otherwise you'd feel sated for the day with 2000 calories of sugar (spoilers: you don't). Every year, we're finding out how much we still don't know about the body regulates our fat stores via hormones. Dr. Jason Fung is a leading researcher on this ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":27779683,"author":"user3939382","month":"2021-07","story":"Yes, drinking more water may help you lose weight","stance":"rec","why":"Research on insulin's role in weight loss.","quote":"I’d refer you the research of Dr. Jason Fung or Dr. Robert Lustig","text":"I’d refer you the research of Dr. Jason Fung or Dr. Robert Lustig, for example: [VIDEO] Here’s an article on keto from a medical journal [LINK] Even the experts debating here aren’t contesting the role of insulin in weight loss, but rather the secondary effects like those on LDL. Here in turn is a talk by a doctor from UC Berkeley explaining more about that: [LINK] From what I can tell, the sole focus on calories is the result of early misunderstandings and oversimplification of biochemistry in 1980s nutritional science. Anecdotally, I did a 3 month experiment where I increased my calories (lots of olive oil, avocado, nuts, butter, etc) but consuming almost no carbs (and mostly carbs from vegetables) and I lost 8 notches off my belt."}],"r":[["sGG-A80Tl5g",1]]},"_bP80DEAbuo":{"wy":"Cited in data center siting debates as evidence that noise and infrasound can seriously affect people living nearby.","q":{"x":"an insightful, scary 30 min video covering the datacenter infrasound noise","i":48482100,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47380806,"author":"soulofmischief","month":"2026-03","story":"Montana Leads the Nation with Groundbreaking Right to Compute Act","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Research on data center noise and its consequences.","quote":"I highly recommend it.","text":"This research presentation from Benn Jordan will hopefully change your mind on the noise issue and its consequences. I highly recommend it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48482100,"author":"code_biologist","month":"2026-06","story":"Meta steals a tactic from Tesla and builds data centers in tents","stance":"rec","why":"Explains datacenter infrasound noise and its harmful effects on people.","quote":"an insightful, scary 30 min video covering the datacenter infrasound noise","text":"Yes, the noise pollution is insane. Benn Jordan's YT video \"Datacenters Behaving Like Acoustic Weapons\" is an insightful, scary 30 min video covering the datacenter infrasound noise, and the nasty things infrasound does to people: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48216656,"author":"Torn","month":"2026-05","story":"Anthropic is expanding to Colossus2. Will use GB200","stance":"rec","why":"Covers infrasound from data centers including Colossus.","quote":"This is a really great watch from Benn Jordan on the topic","text":"This is a really great watch from Benn Jordan on the topic [VIDEO] Some wild things happening with those, and infrasound. Colossus is shown 4 mins in"},{"id":48243168,"author":"e40","month":"2026-05","story":"Why We've Filed a Referendum","stance":"rec","why":"Shows data center harms to nearby residents.","quote":"Watch these","text":"Watch these [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] Then, tell me if you want to live anywhere near those. Then, tell me of a nuclear power plant that has that bad a repo."},{"id":47755410,"author":"schainks","month":"2026-04","story":"Missouri town fires half its city council over data center deal","stance":"rec","why":"Explains infrasound pollution from data centers.","quote":"Plugging this video about infrasound, which I only recently learned was a thing","text":"Plugging this video about infrasound, which I only recently learned was a thing: [VIDEO] These datacenters can be built in ways to limit this kind of noise pollution, but it appears local leaders do not think about things like this that can truly harm their constituents."},{"id":48783216,"author":"ffsm8","month":"2026-07","story":"GLM5.2 on AMD MI355X at 2626 tok/s/node at over 2x lower cost than Blackwell","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence data center vibration can break glass.","quote":"The people having glass literally break from the vibrations would probably disagree with your opinion","text":"The people having glass literally break from the vibrations would probably disagree with your opinion [VIDEO] Yet here we are, discussing \"data center\" as if they're standardized and of similar (nose) isolation. There are no meaningful regulations in building them, and they can be incredibly polluting. So your experience with a potentially well isolated one is sadly not the norm going forward. And we don't even know how close you lived, if you're eg talking about \"within 5km/3miles\" then your experience would also have little value in this discussion in general."},{"id":48455122,"author":"bobsomers","month":"2026-06","story":"xAI is looking more like a datacentre REIT than a frontier lab","stance":"reference","why":"Shows local impact of data centers.","quote":"","text":"It's easy to deliver things fast when you don't care at all for the local communities or residents whose lives you ruin in the process. [VIDEO] (@ 4:08)"},{"id":48550760,"author":"budududuroiu","month":"2026-06","story":"Amazon Announces Multibillion-Dollar Data Center in Missouri","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for worries about living next to a data center.","quote":"","text":"I'm not an AI booster, nor an AI detractor. I like tech and progress. I'm mortified at the possibility of a data centre being built next to me at some point in the future [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48669802,"author":"one33seven","month":"2026-06","story":"Nvidia's 45°C cooling design cuts data center water use to near zero","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for infrasound pollution from AI data centers.","quote":"","text":"The value of an AI data center in your area is negative, not near zero. Pollution, water use, heat, infrasound ( [VIDEO] ) and so much more... But at least we may get some cheap heat in the future"},{"id":47941183,"author":"subscribed","month":"2026-04","story":"Utah greenlights 9GW AI campus using over twice state electricity","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence on data center noise.","quote":"Benn Jordan has captured quite an interesting evidence","text":"No, it will just burn GWs worth of natural gas. Benn Jordan has captured quite an interesting evidence: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["N4UFC0y1tY0",1],["SaadsrHBygc",1],["agxSclh27uo",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["jkrNMKz9pWU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["t-8TDOFqkQA",1],["Zd_UcjMusUA",1]]},"vuEQixrBKCc":{"wy":"HN commenters reach for it whenever a thread involves corporate or political euphemism, using it mostly as a reference.","q":{"x":"George Carlin made this point brilliantly","i":28094429,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28094429,"author":"gorwell","month":"2021-08","story":"Homelessness is about housing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Carlin on softened language reducing action.","quote":"George Carlin made this point brilliantly","text":"Did anyone else notice this euphemism treadmill update? \"for people experiencing homelessness\" \"the number of people experiencing homelessness\" \"of those experiencing homelessness\" This softer language with more syllables is not for the benefit of the homeless, it's for everyone else so they don't feel as bad about it. Which makes it less likely anything will be done. George Carlin made this point brilliantly: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41315641,"author":"bullfightonmars","month":"2024-08","story":"Euphemisms are best changed frequently (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin on euphemisms softening painful things into meaninglessness, e.g. shellshock to PTSD","quote":"George Carlin has a great bit on euphemism","text":"George Carlin has a great bit on euphemism where he makes the case that euphemisms are used to soften the meaning of things that are painful to the point of being meaningless, e.g. shellshock -> ptsd [VIDEO] The author of this article pointed our some similar examples: welfare -> cash assistance -> TANF."},{"id":31226025,"author":"ryandrake","month":"2022-05","story":"How to professionally say","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin's bit on softening language with euphemisms.","quote":"George Carlin had a great bit[1] about our continuous need to soften our language with euphemisms.","text":"George Carlin had a great bit[1] about our continuous need to soften our language with euphemisms. That, combined with a kind of forced optimism: needing to hide all negativity inside robotic passive aggression, is what communication has become in a lot of corporations. I don't imagine anyone actually likes having to do it, but we all seem to adopt these speech patterns eventually. Every time your boss tells you, \"Hey, can you tone it down next time? Fred told me he was very offended by your asking him to do his job!\"-- you're being asked to participate in the game. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8723895,"author":"DevX101","month":"2014-12","story":"US Senate Report on CIA Detention and Interrogation Program","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin dissects the language behind PTSD.","quote":"Here's an amusing and insightful video of Carlin dissecting the language behind Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder","text":"\"You can't be afraid of words that speak the truth. I don't like words that hide the truth. I don't like words that conceal reality. I don't like euphemisms or euphemistic language. And American english is loaded with euphemisms. Because Americans have a lot of trouble dealing with reality. Americans have trouble facing the truth, so they invent a kind of a soft language to protect themselves from it. And it gets worse with every generation. For some reason it just keeps getting worse.\" - George Carlin Here's an amusing and insightful video of Carlin dissecting the language behind Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder [VIDEO] The U.S. military seems particularly prone to this habit of using euphemisms to mask reality."},{"id":46031435,"author":"nsagent","month":"2025-11","story":"Git 3.0 will use main as the default branch","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin on Americans' soft euphemistic language","quote":"I don't like euphemisms, or euphemistic language.","text":"Because it goes hand-in-hand with the euphemism treadmill. I defer to the late great George Carlin's words on the subject: I don't like euphemisms, or euphemistic language. And American English is loaded with euphemisms. Cause Americans have a lot of trouble dealing with reality. Americans have trouble facing the truth, so they invent the kind of a soft language to protest themselves from it, and it gets worse with every generation. Video [VIDEO] or text form: [LINK]"},{"id":44888100,"author":"dig1","month":"2025-08","story":"Claude says “You're absolutely right!” about everything","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin discussing euphemization of American English","quote":"we don't die, we pass away\" or \"we are not broke, we have negative cash flow","text":"I believe this reflects the euphemization of the english language in US, a concept that George Carlin discussed many years ago [1]. As he put it, \"we don't die, we pass away\" or \"we are not broke, we have negative cash flow\". Many non-English speakers find these terms to be nonsensical. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39472571,"author":"esafak","month":"2024-02","story":"Our Company Is Doing So Well That You're All Fired","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's routine on euphemisms like fired and broke","quote":"","text":"\"Poor people used to live in slums. Now the economically disadvantaged occupy substandard housing in the inner cities. And they're broke! They're broke! They don't have a negative cash-flow position. They're ****ing broke! Cause a lot of them were fired. You know, fired. Management wanted to curtail redundancies in the human resources area, so many people are no longer viable members of the workforce.\" George Carlin. [VIDEO] *"},{"id":35006658,"author":"jjav","month":"2023-03","story":"The moral case against equity language","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin bit on euphemisms distancing us from reality","quote":"George Carlin bit on language euphemisms used to distance us from reality","text":"> I prefer the term blind because it is pretty descriptive and relatively short Always reminds me of the George Carlin bit on language euphemisms used to distance us from reality ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":35008920,"author":"civilized","month":"2023-03","story":"The moral case against equity language","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's rant on euphemistic language from decades ago","quote":"George Carlin did a rant about it over 30 years ago","text":"Contemporary word fixation is a distraction. People should get off the social media hamster wheel, take a walk, and dream up a more meaningful way to help their fellow man (human / hxmxn / whatever). It's blown up to the extent that we must now write essays about it, but it would have been better if we had never gone there in the first place. This stuff is very not new. George Carlin did a rant about it over 30 years ago [1]. Seems like the only real effect is class signaling; those who invest in staying \"with it\" signal their willingness to pay lip service to the latest ideological fads. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28942025,"author":"halotrope","month":"2021-10","story":"Stop Calling Everything AI, Machine-Learning Pioneer Says","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"captures really well what it means to dilute the language","text":"The George Carlin bit [1] about euphemisms comes to mind. While not quite in the lens of marketing hyperbole it captures really well what it means to dilute the language. The result is a loss of meaning and expressive power. If everything is something, nothing is. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24777588,"author":"MarcellusDrum","month":"2020-10","story":"PRISM – NSA surveillance program did not prevent a single terrorist attack","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of George Carlin's show about changing words to make them softer.[1]","text":"The \"Patriot Act\" is so maliciously named its more funny than sad. Reminds me of George Carlin's show about changing words to make them softer.[1] An good example he used to show this is the condition the fighters get from shock in combat. In WW1, it was called Shell Shock, a terrifying name. The same condition in WW1 was called Battle Fatigue, a nicer name. In the Korean War, the name changed to Operational Exhaustion, removing any humanity from it. After the Vietnam War, the name because Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder. When you compare the terms \"Shell Shock\" and \"PTSD\", it's obvious what the intent is. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16976602,"author":"stan_programmer","month":"2018-05","story":"The Peter Principle Isn't Just Real, It's Costly","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of George Carlin's bit on \"Shell Shock\"/\"Post Traumatic Stress Disorder\".","text":"I just have a hard time with this term. It feels.. disconnected... I don't know. It reminds me of George Carlin's bit on \"Shell Shock\"/\"Post Traumatic Stress Disorder\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":12643363,"author":"aq3cn","month":"2016-10","story":"The Art of Condolence","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"He made fun of euphemism and the way people mourn over or feel hurt because of choice of words.","text":"He made fun of euphemism and the way people mourn over or feel hurt because of choice of words. [VIDEO] This article asks us to avoid the d word (death, dead). This is bit too extreme for me. English is my third language and I have hurt people at multiple occasions because of poor choice of words. I wish it goes away, so that my life can be easier. It is not easy to live in society as an autistic person and I have no interest in learning manners of society when I believe I have more meaningful interests to pursue. If you are a social person, you will not like my opinion and it's fine. I am used to that. Check what Linus has to say about it in his TEDx talk. [LINK]"},{"id":10556547,"author":"Nadya","month":"2015-11","story":"Replace suicide as a verb – Issue #3721 – Node.js/node","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"at least it provides relevant context to shoehorn Carlin in.","text":"Do people realize that euphemisms do not change the meaning of something? `voluntaryExit`? What do they think suicide actually is? Oh well, at least it provides relevant context to shoehorn Carlin in. [0] [1] It amazes me so many people are willing to receive their sticks from the stick people. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":9536887,"author":"itistoday2","month":"2015-05","story":"How the DEA took a young man’s life savings without charging him with a crime","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"George Carlin observed this as a dangerous trend in our language","text":"I am concerned that by calling it \"civil forfeiture\" this article is using language to mask a very basic crime. What was described in this article is called stealing or theft. To call it anything else is to mask and downplay what was done to this man. It is to enable the very act that was committed. George Carlin observed this as a dangerous trend in our language: [VIDEO] Call it \"civil forfeiture\" once if you have to (perhaps as a footnote). But to repeatedly use such \"soft language\" is to delay an end to such injustice."},{"id":8429239,"author":"jimbobimbo","month":"2014-10","story":"The Horror of a 'Secure Golden Key'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"That reminded me George Carlin's \"Euphemisms\"","text":"Good observation on \"golden key\" == \"backdoor\". That reminded me George Carlin's \"Euphemisms\": [VIDEO] (May have strong language)"},{"id":40854055,"author":"kragen","month":"2024-07","story":"Pattern of brain damage is pervasive in Navy SEALs who died by suicide","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"george carlin would be so proud","text":"> Much of what is categorized as post-traumatic stress disorder may actually be caused by repeated exposure to blasts. that is to say, it's actually shell shock? as in, actual physical shock from actual shells? george carlin would be so proud [VIDEO] the article also mentions that they'd all trained extensively in diving deep underwater, which is also known to cause brain damage, but the 'interface astroglial scarring' pathology lab results sound pretty specific to big shock waves i'm skeptical that firing a rifle produces shock waves that induce cavitation in brain tissue, though"},{"id":31513346,"author":"drdaeman","month":"2022-05","story":"Bolt announces layoffs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ha, I'm surprised they still say \"layoff\" at all, and not mask it behind some softer euphemism. Reminds me of this George Carlin's performance: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26201421,"author":"ublaze","month":"2021-02","story":"Sequoia Capital says it was hacked","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"reminds me of Euphemisms by George Carlin","text":"Not to throw shade on Sequoia, but the statement \"We recently experienced a cybersecurity incident\" reminds me of Euphemisms by George Carlin ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":9681950,"author":"sarciszewski","month":"2015-06","story":"How to Fight Unconscious Bias and Become a Better Ally","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Slight exaggeration for the sake of humor.","text":"> Feminism is a women's movement for women by women. I am not a woman, so I cannot be a feminist, but I can be a feminist ally. > What would you prefer to call it? I'd prefer the use of the compound noun \"feminist ally\" over the generic and ambiguous noun \"ally\". > I fail to see how this is a torture of the English language. [VIDEO] Slight exaggeration for the sake of humor."}],"r":[["fPrvnlvnu-k",1],["wMFPe-DwULM",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["XuBdf9jYj7o",1]]},"xzYgiOC9cj4":{"wy":"HN commenters share it in union threads as a concise statement of the free-market economist's case against unions.","q":{"x":"Great short explanation from Milton Friedman","i":24577790,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":27302256,"author":"whitepaint","month":"2021-05","story":"Uber recognises a union for the first time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Friedman explains unions' real-world effects; a starting point for his economics.","quote":"He explains this (and many more questions regarding the economy) extremely well. Begin with this video","text":"Listen to Milton Friedman. He explains this (and many more questions regarding the economy) extremely well. Begin with this video: [VIDEO] (The Real World Effects Of Unions)"},{"id":24577790,"author":"blueterminal","month":"2020-09","story":"Mark in the Middle","stance":"rec","why":"Short Friedman explanation of union effects.","quote":"Great short explanation from Milton Friedman","text":"Unions are absolutely detrimental to the society. Thank god we'll never have one for Software Engineers. Great short explanation from Milton Friedman: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20968784,"author":"Consultant32452","month":"2019-09","story":"The Myth of the Wealthy Welder","stance":"rec","why":"Friedman explains how unions raise pay for high earners and lower it for low earners.","quote":"If you watch to the end he fully explains how the net effect of unions is to raise the pay of high paid workers","text":"Nobel Economist Milton Friedman, for one. [VIDEO] If you watch to the end he fully explains how the net effect of unions is to raise the pay of high paid workers and lower the pay of low paid workers. You might also like \"Basic Economics\" by Thomas Sowell which talks about this phenomenon also."},{"id":15812384,"author":"conanbatt","month":"2017-11","story":"American Airlines Accidentally Let Too Many Pilots Take the Holidays Off","stance":"rec","why":"Friedman on real-world effects of unions.","quote":"For anyone at home that wants to listen to someone that knows more about economy than any of us","text":"Because its true. Unions can only increase wages by reducing the amount of workers. Unions dont get wage increases by reducing the profits of capital in the long term. If uber drivers unionized and made uber pay them more, then less capital would go to uber and eventually have less workers. Ultimately the return on capital will end up being the same, because capital looks for returns and will move. So in effect, it would only increase wages of drivers as long as it can prevent other drivers from showing up. For anyone at home that wants to listen to someone that knows more about economy than any of us: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34248955,"author":"PathOfEclipse","month":"2023-01","story":"Microsoft Workers to Form Company’s First Union in the U.S.","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as supporting the argument that pro-union laws add injustices.","quote":"","text":"It really is, though, about correcting cosmic wrongs, because it is the world view you must first hold to believe something \"unjust\" is occurring when someone voluntarily bargains to work long hours for lower pay to be able to work their dream job. The next step is to pass legislation or push for judicial or executive action to try to \"correct\" for the injustice. The U.S. already has several very counter-productive pro-union labor laws it has accumulated over the years, and, under democrat presidents like Obama and Biden, the NLRB rules heavily in favor of unions: [LINK] [LINK] These laws and actions don't make the world more just. On the contrary, they just add new injustices on top of the old ones: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21039949,"author":"Consultant32452","month":"2019-09","story":"Anti-union activity is heating up ahead of Google contractor's vote to unionize","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman discusses how unions restrict labor supply to raise wages for some.","quote":"Here's Nobel economist Milton Friedman discussing it in more detail.","text":"The primary mechanism of power used by unions to keep wages high is by reducing the available work force. This is done by lobbying for things like requiring certifications to be legally allowed to perform work and lobbying/negotiating for disallowing non-union members from accepting work. This has the net effect of increasing the wages of the high paid workers while reducing the wages of the low paid workers who could do the higher paid work but aren't allowed to. Here's Nobel economist Milton Friedman discussing it in more detail. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["leFuF-zoVzA",1],["d-7o9xYp7eE",1],["5HbYScltf1c",1]]},"2IT2oAzTcvU":{"wy":"HN points to it as an entertaining and informative explanation of the economics of reshipping and stolen-card resale scams.","q":{"x":"here's an excellent defcon talk on what these some groups do","i":38169177,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42608085,"author":"h3xadecima1","month":"2025-01","story":"I made $100K from a dick joke","stance":"rec","why":"Scammers selling coffee on eBay with stolen cards","quote":"Nina Kollars gave an interesting talk on scammers selling coffee through eBay using stolen credit cards.","text":"Nina Kollars gave an interesting talk on scammers selling coffee through eBay using stolen credit cards. Signature way of knowing is they're shipped direct from nespresso and the purchase price is 40-50% cheaper than retail. I ordered some keurig pods and experienced the same direct shipping and cheap prices. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38169177,"author":"clutchdude","month":"2023-11","story":"Who's Behind the Swat USA Reshipping Service?","stance":"rec","why":"How reshipping scams use stolen credentials and who profits","quote":"here's an excellent defcon talk on what these some groups do","text":"In addition to the other commenters, here's an excellent defcon talk on what these some groups do: Nina Kollars - Confessions of an Nespresso Money Mule - DEF CON 27 Conference [VIDEO] Essentially, they wait for a buyer of a product to come along to a listing and order it - they'll then buy it using stolen credentials, have it drop-shipped or reshipped to the end user. Everyone but the person with the original card comes out ahead, which is then pushed to all of us via higher fees if they notice and report the fraud."},{"id":24413367,"author":"Mountain_Skies","month":"2020-09","story":"The 'brushing' scam that's behind mystery parcels","stance":"rec","why":"Nespresso resale scam using stolen credit cards, experienced firsthand","quote":"Nina Kollars had a nice related talk during Defcon","text":"Nina Kollars had a nice related talk during Defcon on a similar scam buying what she thought was gray market Nespresso pods whose \"seller\" bought for her using a stolen credit card. They also gave her a free Nespresso machine, presumably to give her motive to keep quiet. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23268091,"author":"rozab","month":"2020-05","story":"G2A pays Factorio developer $40k over illegally obtained game keys","stance":"rec","why":"DEF CON talk on economics of stolen-card resale scams","quote":"Here's a tangentially related def con about the strange economics of that whole scam.","text":"Here's a tangentially related def con about the strange economics of that whole scam. Basically, everyone in the chain apart from the cardholder benefits. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34142921,"author":"ValueAddedRS","month":"2022-12","story":"eBay, Etsy and Other Markets Now Have to Disclose Seller Details with Inform Act","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See also Confessions of a Nespresso Money Mule by Nina Kollars.","text":"3P mule accounts have been a thing on eBay for years and eBay has been very much aware of it since at least 2015 when Brian Krebs wrote about it. [LINK] See also Confessions of a Nespresso Money Mule by Nina Kollars. [VIDEO] In 2020, I personally reported 150+ hijacked eBay accounts used to commit $160k+ in triangulation fraud against a company I worked for at the time - eBay did very little to help. In February 2022, I collected about 80 reports from people who received 1099-Ks from eBay even though they had never knowingly created an account on the site - their PII (names, addresses, and in some cases even correct SSNs) were used to create fraudulent accounts that sold tens of thousands of dollars each with no vetting or verification of any kind by eBay. And all of that is a tiny drop in the bucket. I place a lot of the blame for where we are, and why this legislation ever came to be, squarely on the marketplaces. They've known about rampant fraud and scams for years, could have put in stricter vetting and verification procedures, invested more in highly trained human powered Trust & Safety to deal with counterfeits and fraud etc. but for the most part they have done the minimum required to cover their own \"assets\" and nothing more...which still left room for a lot of consumer harm to be done. While I don't think this will curb all of the fraud, like you said they will likely just jump to new accounts, having to produce actual documents to match the stolen PII may at least "},{"id":31309618,"author":"numpad0","month":"2022-05","story":"Tell HN: I was permanently banned from eBay in one hour","stance":"reference","why":"DEF CON talk on dropshipping with stolen cards","quote":"there's a DEF CON talk[1] on that topic","text":"ID could be faked or stolen credit cards can be used. In fact, dropshipping(ordering items on the fly, automatically, from a backend webstore - e.g. Amazon, Aliexpress, taobao) on stolen credit cards, or even pretending to do that without shipping part, is so prevalent there's a DEF CON talk[1] on that topic. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22960805,"author":"sgillen","month":"2020-04","story":"Jeff Bezos has returned to day-to-day management of Amazon","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You may be a money mule! And you may want to report this","text":"You may be a money mule! And you may want to report this, someone vulnerable could be racking up massive debt paying for free stuff. see : [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["_o7qjN3KF8U",1],["wBBnfu8N_J0",1],["qEV9qoup2mQ",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1]]},"3GObi93tjZI":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a real-world case study of moving from React to htmx, especially in threads on boring web development and HTML over the wire.","q":{"x":"This recent talk from Djangocon \"React to htmx on a real-world SaaS product\" might be of interest","i":33571508,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":38245335,"author":"Xeamek","month":"2023-11","story":"HTML First – Six principles for building simple, maintainable, web software","stance":"rec","why":"Author is very happy after switching to htmx stack.","quote":"the author expressed being verry happy with switching to 'stack' you described","text":"I think you might find that relevant. (But in short, the author expressed being verry happy with switching to 'stack' you described) [VIDEO]"},{"id":37147297,"author":"dvtkrlbs","month":"2023-08","story":"Htmx is part of the GitHub Accelerator","stance":"rec","why":"Covers faceted search implemented with htmx.","quote":"in the video they specifically talk about faceted search and how they did it with htmx.","text":"You can check this talk out [VIDEO] I was thinking the same but in the video they specifically talk about faceted search and how they did it with htmx. For the second part you probably go with your own javascript or get away with hyperscript"},{"id":34745743,"author":"dragonshed","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: What companies are embracing “HTML over the wire”?","stance":"rec","why":"SaaS product moving from React to htmx.","quote":"Check out this djangocon talk where a SaaS product moved from react to htmx.","text":"It depends on the usecases & application reqs. There are times when server-rendered content can have enormous benefits over a SPA. Check out this djangocon talk where a SaaS product moved from react to htmx. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33571508,"author":"grose","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Where can one learn about boring web development?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on moving a SaaS from React to htmx.","quote":"This recent talk from Djangocon \"React to htmx on a real-world SaaS product\" might be of interest","text":"I'm not aware of exactly what you're looking for, but I think such a resource would be very valuable. Unfortunately, modern web development has almost completely eschewed simplicity. I can offer some leads, though. This is more of a manifesto but there is a small movement around simplicity at [LINK] which is written by the guy behind [LINK] -- a nice little way to add the bare minimum JS needed for a 'modern' app. You can find a community of likeminded people at HTMX-adjacent places like their Discord and Twitter. I've also been an advocate of this kind of 'primitive' dev style and it's great to see it gain some popularity. This recent talk from Djangocon \"React to htmx on a real-world SaaS product\" might be of interest: [VIDEO] Ironically, the ultra-modern serverless platforms such as Cloudflare Workers have strict size constraints which is leading to a kind of back-to-basics approach that minimizes dependencies and bundle size. You can find a lot of small libraries for this at [LINK] . The latest JS framework to buzz here, Deno's Fresh, even touts \"no JS is shipped to the client by default\". We can see there is a resurgence of interest in server-side rendering. The classics such as Rails are still alive and kicking. Phoenix seems to be a promising candidate for \"the modern Rails\". As someone who experienced the Good Old Days of web development, I would recommend at least trying out the managed cloud services for things like databases and cron jobs and deployments. IMO, it's"},{"id":46958318,"author":"dev_l1x_be","month":"2026-02","story":"Another GitHub outage in the same day","stance":"reference","why":"Shows partial page updates without React.","quote":"Are you implying that this only doable with React?","text":"> to do great stuff in the name of efficiency; for example, when browsing through some parts of the UI, it's now much more capable of just updating the part of the page that's changed Are you implying that this only doable with React? I mean just for the fun of it you can look at this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38956308,"author":"dpc_01234","month":"2024-01","story":"Is htmx Just Another JavaScript Framework?","stance":"reference","why":"React to htmx rewrite changed team composition.","quote":"the result of HTMX rewrite was \"JS guy left\".","text":"I think there's something to it. More important HTMX is just new and different enough that it needs time to be picked up by the industry, BUT indeed it does largely eliminate what is commonly considered strictly front-end work, and thus forces teams to change their composition and division of responsibilities. At least where I worked there was often a frontend vs backend divide where some devs would write backend logic, and some others would worry only about consuming backend APIs to get the UI to do certain thing. The APIs is the contract and interface between the two sides and a natural place to divide responsibilities. Now, with HTMX, the divide is gone. The backend generates and swaps the parts of the UI, effectively driving the UI/UX directly. This cuts tons of JS out of the picture and couples (not necessarily in a bad way) backend and frontend. If you look at a popular YT video: [VIDEO] , the result of HTMX rewrite was \"JS guy left\". So in larger teams switching from something like React to HTMX largely turns everything upside down, not just on technical level but on how people work and collaborate. It would be far easier for new teams and projects to go HTMX route, where they can start and keep hiring people who are fine in a new model."},{"id":38100893,"author":"naasking","month":"2023-11","story":"Htmx, Rust and Shuttle: A New Rapid Prototyping Stack","stance":"reference","why":"Testimonial of a real-world htmx migration.","quote":"Maybe you should actually see some testimonials before judging, like","text":"> And is the second one some weird DSL What's weird about standard CSS selectors? Maybe you should actually see some testimonials before judging, like: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38255244,"author":"Xeamek","month":"2023-11","story":"HTML Web Components","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a non-trivial htmx app.","quote":"Would this example be considered \"trivial\"?","text":"Would this example be considered \"trivial\"? [VIDEO] But also, you can mix things togheter. You can just use react based app 'embeded' into mpa for when you need that heavy lifting, and the same keep the content part of your app simple. \"But why would i do that, if i'd already use react for some parts, why not every where\". Becuase then it leads to abominations like reddit or facebook, where websites that are basically glorified text forums will use gigabytes of memory, have terrible UX, be slow and will crash your browser if you leave them open for too long."}],"r":[["HxaD_trXwRE",1],["8XUutFBbUrg",1]]},"6AeWu1fZ7bY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a balanced, eloquent take on the systemd debate that even skeptics find persuasive.","q":{"x":"One of the better videos on this subject","i":19960363,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21314510,"author":"kbumsik","month":"2019-10","story":"Why Did ArchLinux Embrace Systemd? (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on systemd shared for those who have not seen it","quote":"This is a really good talk.","text":"> After listening to the BSD Canada talk on systemd and BSD For someone who don't know, here is the video \"The tragedy of systemd\". This is a really good talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20861369,"author":"znpy","month":"2019-09","story":"Knoppix 8.6 abandons systemd","stance":"rec","why":"BSD conference talk acknowledging systemd has a good reason to exist","quote":"A nice talk that might change your view of the subject is the following","text":"A nice talk that might change your view of the subject is the following: [VIDEO] . It's a video from BSDCan, the Canadian BSD conference. Even some BSD people are realizing that systemd has a good point to exist!"},{"id":19960363,"author":"sjwright","month":"2019-05","story":"Linux distros without systemd","stance":"rec","why":"Balanced talk on why distributions adopted systemd and whether it is bad","quote":"One of the better videos on this subject","text":"One of the better videos on this subject: [VIDEO] BSDCan 2018—Benno Rice: The Tragedy of systemd \"systemd is, to put it mildly, controversial. Depending on who you ask it's either a complete violation of the UNIX philosophy, a bloated pile of bugs, a complete violation of the elegant simplicity it replaced or, it most cases, some or all of the above. So why have so many Linux distributions taken to it? Is it as bad as people say?"},{"id":18544712,"author":"ernst_klim","month":"2018-11","story":"BSDs or Illumos may be good alternatives to Linux (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Eloquent explanation of the real problems with rc init scripts","quote":"the real issues with rc are much more serious, and eloquently described here","text":">Personally, I never found startup time to be much of a problem, on any OS. Well, start up problem is a matter of convenience, it's not a fatal problem, but I just like that my laptop with arch+systemd+systemd-boot starts instantly (3 seconds + 6 seconds for firmware), while bsd on my other laptop starts soo damn slow. (btw slow shutdown pisses me off even more) Though the real issues with rc are much more serious, and eloquently described here (aka you don't have proper login management, service management, device and policy management, and better in a unified way): [VIDEO]"},{"id":41186174,"author":"pxc","month":"2024-08","story":"Systemd, 10 years later: a historical and technical retrospective (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk covers systemd as a system layer, used to support the commenter's point","quote":"Benno says literally all of this in his talk. He even has a diagram with the phrase 'system layer' up on a slide","text":"> Tacitly: most people don’t like launchd, but they don’t interact with it either. Find me a person who writes or debugs launchd plists and you’ll find that: > [...] > 2. they don’t like launchd This is true in my case, although the main thing on my mind when I work with launchd my main complaint often boils down to 'I wish this were more like systemd'. IME, systemd has much nicer CLIs, more capabilities, clearer and more comprehensive documentation, more abundant examples, and more conveniences/less privileges required for per-user stuff. The simple INI format for systemd units is also a lot easier and nicer to deal with than the XML format that plists use, even though there's a lot of tooling around plists. > even if it weren’t there is a built in backup/restore system that literally restores the machine to its entire previous state. I get this for free on NixOS and it's really easy to get on other distros by plugging /etc/systemd into etckeeper, even without a snapshotting filesystem. (Plus many distros have backups via snapshots of CoW filesystems built in just like macOS does.) > launchd and systemd aren’t even comparable, one is part of a monolithic proprietary operating system and the other is an ecosystem that is semi-modular which lends itself strongly to being an entire layer of your computing experience (dubbed: “the system layer” by Lennart Poettering). Benno says literally all of this in his talk. He even has a diagram with the phrase 'system layer' up on a slide"}],"r":[["iSmkqocn0oQ",1],["yJDv-zdhzMY",1],["yQ16_YxLbB8",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1],["ON0A1dsQOV0",1],["Zpnncakrelk",1]]},"KqFNbCcyFkk":{"wy":"HN treats it as the definitive explanation of the Simpsons decline, recommended in every thread asking when the golden age ended.","q":{"x":"For an actual exploration of what happened, I highly recommend this video.","i":15579241,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":18284445,"author":"lostgame","month":"2018-10","story":"Why did the golden age of the Simpsons end?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear explanation that the decline came down to bad writing","quote":"which is one of my favourite videos on the internet","text":"Nobody here seems to be mentioning the death of Phil Hartman[1] in 1998. The article, as well, staggeringly, doesn't mention his name. There's a video[2], 'The Fall of the Simpsons', which is one of my favourite videos on the internet. It explains and describes with utmost clarity the issues that led to the series 'demise', which, ultimately, boils down to 'bad writing'. However, in interviews, and comments from the showrunners and staff, it seems like Phil Hartman's death, who voiced the characters of Troy McCleur, and Lionel Hutz, as well as the voice of Lyle Lanley, from the fantastic classic episode 'Marge vs. the Monorail'. When Hartman died, they retired Troy's character. His absence alone is strongly felt when viewing the first several seasons against the others. His voice is certainly missed. Said Groening of Hartman: \"[I] took [Hartman] for granted because he nailed the joke every time,\" and that his voice acting could produce \"the maximum amount of humor\" with any line he was given. I mean, this was 1998. Most folks consider season 8-9 to have some of the last 'great' episodes of the series, or at least the last 'great' seasons. There's absolutely no way this is a coincidence, and the affect of his death on the series has been verified by countless staff. I often think of Hartman's death as equivalent to Brian Epstein's death in '67. Similar to the Simpsons, there are a million reasons why the Beatles went to shit (even if their output for three years after that was"},{"id":15579241,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2017-10","story":"When did the golden age of The Simpsons end?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Actual exploration of why The Simpsons declined","quote":"For an actual exploration of what happened, I highly recommend this video.","text":"For an actual exploration of what happened, I highly recommend this video. The Fall of The Simpsons: How it Happened [VIDEO]"},{"id":15579299,"author":"brudgers","month":"2017-10","story":"When did the golden age of The Simpsons end?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite take on the decline of The Simpsons","quote":"My favorite take on","text":"My favorite take on \"The Decline and Fall of the Simpsons Empire\": The Fall of the Simpsons [VIDEO]"},{"id":47063627,"author":"yndoendo","month":"2026-02","story":"After 800 episodes, 'The Simpsons' creators look back – and ahead","stance":"rec","why":"How Simpsons comedy went from sharp and witty to laugh track","quote":"I recommend watching _The Fall of The Simpsons: How it Happened_ [0].","text":"I recommend watching _The Fall of The Simpsons: How it Happened_ [0]. It goes though how the comedy went from sharp an witty to laugh track quality. Personally, the only show that was also focused on sharp and witty was _Futurama_. That show was able to retain it better through out the years. Every series finally episode was a masterpiece. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18283313,"author":"saganus","month":"2018-10","story":"Why did the golden age of the Simpsons end?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Simpsons quality shifted after season 9-10","quote":"Could never put it into words why it felt like that, until I watched this.","text":"There's a very interesting YouTube video that has a bit more of an explanation on why The Simpsons had a decline. I liked how they analyze the show and IMO it does a great job explaining why lots of people loved the show up until season 9-10. Personally I think I enjoyed up to season 12 or so, but definitely after 10 I noticed the shift in style/quality/whathaveyou. Could never put it into words why it felt like that, until I watched this. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18283399,"author":"j45","month":"2018-10","story":"Why did the golden age of the Simpsons end?","stance":"rec","why":"Attributes decline to writer changes and comedy style shift","quote":"attributes the decline of the Simpsons to the changes in writers and how the type of comedy itself changed","text":"This reminds me of this video which attributes the decline of the Simpsons to the changes in writers and how the type of comedy itself changed. I still catch episodes when possible, the old ones definitely have a different depth than the current ones. The fall of the Simpsons: How it happened: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18283898,"author":"nervousvarun","month":"2018-10","story":"Why did the golden age of the Simpsons end?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains decline through joke complexity rather than nostalgia","quote":"there are explanations out there that aren't based on nostalgia but actually joke complexity among other things","text":"Interesting it's possible, but there are explanations out there that aren't based on nostalgia but actually joke complexity among other things: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18284554,"author":"richardboegli","month":"2018-10","story":"Why did the golden age of the Simpsons end?","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended viewing on the Simpsons decline","quote":"I would suggest watching these videos on YouTube.","text":"Skim read the article and I would suggest watching these videos on YouTube. The Fall of The Simpsons: How it Happened [VIDEO] The Day The Simpsons Died [LINK]"},{"id":15579343,"author":"brownbat","month":"2017-10","story":"When did the golden age of The Simpsons end?","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis of Simpsons going from counterculture to absurdity","quote":"There is a video with great analysis on this.","text":"There is a video with great analysis on this. Ultimately argues that Simpsons was a great piece of counter culture, then it just won and became culture, losing its ability to skewer mainstream comedy. Along with an upheaval in the writer's room, it just went absurd all the time. That's a sometimes brilliant move, but dull when used in place of any substance. It also forgot how to stay true to its characters. Much more here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15579461,"author":"lobut","month":"2017-10","story":"When did the golden age of The Simpsons end?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the pattern behind weaker later jokes","quote":"The video everyone is referencing in this thread","text":"I think nostalgia-based makes sense ... but I just decided to watch later Simpson episodes and their jokes just suck. I watched a few episodes and didn't laugh once. The video everyone is referencing in this thread has an example of the possible \"pattern\" that you reference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15512795,"author":"kombucha2","month":"2017-10","story":"Goodbye Uncanny Valley [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"case in point this Youtube Video that's been making the rounds.","text":">we often see with think-pieces that want to take on an air of authority. It's very simple to convince people right now with a reference to an article every other word, regardless of how reliable or even relevant each of the references actually is. The effect though is that the piece attains a state of legitimacy [case in point this Youtube Video that's been making the rounds.]( [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["2LyzBoHo5EI",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["Hatav_Rdnno",1],["jqjacHSTd48",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["0Rnq1NpHdmw",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["CZIINXhGDcs",1],["jtMWEiCdsfc",1],["dljNabciEGg",1],["Tl9kD693ie4",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["2u6HTG8LuXQ",1],["agZ0xISi40E",1],["5cWck_xcH64",1],["qSfdtmcZ4d0",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["r9rGX91rq5I",1],["MZvmYaFkNJI",1]]},"Qi1Yry33TQE":{"wy":"HN points to it as the sharpest, most-quoted critique of deep learning practice, and cites it whenever ML reliability or understanding is debated.","q":{"x":"For a more cutting and insightful critique, watch Ali Rahimi's short talk at NIPS 2017","i":16348678,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16348678,"author":"ssivark","month":"2018-02","story":"Greedy, Brittle, Opaque, and Shallow: The Downsides to Deep Learning","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A cutting, insightful critique of deep learning practice, received a standing ovation.","quote":"For a more cutting and insightful critique, watch Ali Rahimi's short talk at NIPS 2017","text":"This article is a little too glib in my opinion, preferring citations and statements to substance and explanations. For a more cutting and insightful critique, watch Ali Rahimi's short talk at NIPS 2017 (where he was presenting for a paper that won the \"Test of time\" award, for standing out in value a decade after publication). The standing ovation he received at the end indicate that his comments resonated with a significant fraction of the attendees. [VIDEO] Here's a teaser from the talk: \"How many of you have devised a deep neural net from scratch, architecture and all, and trained it from the ground up, and when it didn't work, felt bad about yourself, like you did something wrong? This happens to me about every three months, and let me tell you, I don't think it's you [...] I think it's gradient descent's fault. I'll illustrate...\" [Addendum] Ben Recht and Ali Rahimi published an adendum to the talk, elaborating on the direction they envision -- [LINK] Ali also has a post taking a stab at organizing some puzzling basic observations about deep learning, and motivating that with analogous historical progress in optics -- [LINK] ----- PS: The first 11 minutes, on the idea of using random features (the main idea in the research he presented) are also interesting."},{"id":16046669,"author":"vowelless","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite 2017 talk calling modern machine learning alchemy.","quote":"Ali Rahimi's NIPS 2017 Test of Time award talk.","text":"Ali Rahimi's NIPS 2017 Test of Time award talk. \"Artificial intelligence is not like electricity (quoting Andrew Ng), but alchemy\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":34786186,"author":"ShredKazoo","month":"2023-02","story":"Only You Can Stop an AI Apocalypse","stance":"rec","why":"Compares modern AI work to alchemy: works unreliably, not always understood.","quote":"See this talk which compares modern AI work to alchemy","text":">In no way can I believe that the corporation doesn’t have a good understanding of exactly how it can manipulate the AI systems it leverages to achieve its goals. See this talk which compares modern AI work to alchemy (i.e. it works, unreliably, and we don't always know why): [VIDEO]"},{"id":43446991,"author":"nsagent","month":"2025-03","story":"The Humans Building AI Scientists","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as still resonant in saying ML is alchemy.","quote":"Ali Rahimi's 2017 NIPS Test of Time [1] talk still resonates today","text":"I'm also an AI researcher and I'm not hopeful that the hype will die down anytime soon. People have been touting insignificant results through shoddy science for a long time now. They've noticed it works well enough because current ML is still pretty much alchemy (Ali Rahimi's 2017 NIPS Test of Time [1] talk still resonates today) so people rarely spend the effort to effectively refute the bogus claims. As a result, I've opted out of the system and I'm working on trying ambitious ideas I have to try to upend the current paradigm of training on big data, which is truly insane (by age 4 the most erudite of children have probably only been exposed to 45 million words [2] and yet exhibit vastly more understanding and fluency than any language model trained on a similar amount of data). [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["-Ht4qiDRZE8",1],["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["uGlzRt-FYto",1],["kc9HwsxE1OY",1],["39iKLwlUqBo",1]]},"VO_1AdYRGW8":{"wy":"Cited in threads on cars, streets and urbanism as evidence of how mixed traffic worked before cars took over.","q":{"x":"You can see pedestrians, horse-drawn carriages, and early automobiles all \"flowing\" together.","i":32122201,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":38014656,"author":"carapace","month":"2023-10","story":"CA DMV suspends Cruise deployment and driverless testing permits","stance":"rec","why":"Extraordinary pre-earthquake film of horses, pedestrians and early cars mixing","quote":"You can see the origin of the situation from this extraordinary film shot just before the 1906 Earthquake","text":"The two things are orthogonal. Item: humans driving huge machines on every street is and always has been totally insane. The reason we do it is because of a deliberate domestic propaganda campaign. No really. See: \"The Real Reason Jaywalking Is A Crime (Adam Ruins Everything)\" [LINK] You can see the origin of the situation from this extraordinary film shot just before the 1906 Earthquake: \"San Francisco, a Trip down Market Street, April 14, 1906\" [VIDEO] You can see that horses, horse-drawn wagon and carts, and pedestrians could and did mix together in the public way. There are also early automobiles, and the problem is obvious: cars accelerate faster than horses. Already we can see reckless driving by cars that is only possible due to their ability to accelerate quickly! BTW, just for the record, cars themselves are generally awesome. It must be said. Cars are cool. It's only when we use them as mass-transportation and/or mix them with non-car-driving people (bikes, pedestrians, etc.) that it becomes mad. - - - - Ergo, safe automatic automobiles can't come soon enough! - - - - However, none of the above justifies using random people without their consent as test dummies in an open-air testing facility for massive, fast robots. That's also insane. - - - - To me it seems obvious that you do two things: 1) Start with slow light robots! Build a nerf foam golf cart that is limited to 5mph and get that working reliably. Going straight for Knight Industries Two Thousand right out o"},{"id":32122201,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-07","story":"When coal first arrived, Americans said 'no thanks'","stance":"rec","why":"Upscaled 1906 film showing pedestrians, carriages and automobiles flowing together","quote":"You can see pedestrians, horse-drawn carriages, and early automobiles all \"flowing\" together.","text":"FWIW, check out \"A Trip Down Market Street\", filmed in 1906 in SF just before the earthquake. [LINK] Upscaled and colorized: [VIDEO] You can see pedestrians, horse-drawn carriages, and early automobiles all \"flowing\" together."},{"id":28888461,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2021-10","story":"The Hindenburg disaster denoised, upscaled, and colorized using ML [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Upscaled early 1900s city footage","quote":"I've really come to enjoy these sorts of upscaled videos that allow a view of various cities in the early 1900's.","text":"I've really come to enjoy these sorts of upscaled videos that allow a view of various cities in the early 1900's. New York: [LINK] San Francisco: [VIDEO] Paris: [LINK] Wuppertal, Germany: [LINK]"},{"id":24417270,"author":"sarora27","month":"2020-09","story":"A Brief Cultural History of the Parking Lot","stance":"rec","why":"1906 San Francisco street with cars, horses and pedestrians sharing the road","quote":"This reminds me of a cool video i saw the other day of San Francisco's Market Street in 1906","text":"This reminds me of a cool video i saw the other day of San Francisco's Market Street in 1906: [VIDEO] It's so interesting to see how people, street cars, horse & carts, and cars all seemed to share the road in what seemed like a controlled chaos manner"},{"id":36726704,"author":"carapace","month":"2023-07","story":"S.F. says incidents by Cruise, Waymo driverless taxis are ‘skyrocketing.’","stance":"reference","why":"Shows cars outpacing horses on shared streets","quote":"The source of the problem is clear: cars accelerate much faster than horses.","text":"First, where is the data? Second, yeah, it's bananas to test large, fast, heavy robots on public streets. The only reason we let it happen is because the robots look like cars. Third, it's bananas to have large, fast, heavy vehicles everywhere mixed in with all the other traffic. The only reason we let it happen is a mass-marketing campaign (see \"The Real Reason Jaywalking Is A Crime\" (Adam Ruins Everything) [LINK] ) Here is a film \"a Trip down Market Street\" recorded in San Francisco on April 14, 1906, just before the Great Earthquake. The source of the problem is clear: cars accelerate much faster than horses. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35937688,"author":"carapace","month":"2023-05","story":"How to Survive a Car Crash in 10 Easy Steps","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the transition period when cars appeared on shared streets","quote":"","text":"I just want to take a moment to remind everybody just how insane car travel is as a mode of transportation. Sure, it's convenient , but at what cost!? Air pollution, tire particle pollution, covering the Earth with asphalt, etc. Also, and this is gruesome so brace yourself, car travel causes incredible amounts of human suffering. Car collisions cause something like 40,000 fatalities per year just in the USA. In fact, more Americans have died in car crashes than in all the wars we've fought. I call it the \"Mayhem Lottery\". Anyone can play, little kids, old people, pregnant women, everybody. And the tickets cost nothing, all you have to do is be near a street. (Sometimes cars will even crash into buildings. People have been killed by cars while sitting in their own homes watching TV!) Now the weird, bizarre thing about this state of affairs is that it is the result of a successful mass-marketing campaign! When cars were first introduced people complained of reckless drivers (\"Speed Demons\"), the car companies and the government got together and created a deliberate plan to change public attitude (about being run down in the street by car drivers), they actually coined the term \"jaywalker\" to describe people using the street in the traditional fashion (pedestrians.) It sounds nuts, because it is, yet it really happened: cf. \"The Real Reason Jaywalking Is A Crime\" (Adam Ruins Everything) [LINK] - - - - Edit to add: You can see the trasition period in the \"a Trip down Market Stree"},{"id":32598422,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-08","story":"Too many Americans live in places built for cars – not for human connection","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of traffic mixing before cars dominated streets","quote":"You can see that, at low speeds and modest density, carriages and pedestrians flow together just fine.","text":"If it helps, it's the result of a deliberate domestic propaganda campaign. I usually point to \"The Real Reason Jaywalking Is A Crime\" (Adam Ruins Everything) [LINK] for the story. We can see what traffic was like just as motorized carriages began to mix with pedestrians and horses. Here is \"San Francisco, a Trip down Market Street, April 14, 1906\" upscaled and colorized: [VIDEO] You can see that, at low speeds and modest density, carriages and pedestrians flow together just fine. Although I'm sure there were still lots of accidents, everyone is more-or-less sharing the space. You can already see the problem brewing: horseless carriages can accelerate much faster than horse-drawn carriages."},{"id":25302979,"author":"DominikPeters","month":"2020-12","story":"Paris to ‘get rid of 70k parking spaces’","stance":"reference","why":"Old film footage showing chaotic street use","quote":"","text":"Old film footage suggests to me that it was just more \"lanes\" and in general more chaotic, e.g.: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24153615,"author":"dmix","month":"2020-08","story":"Street View of 1940s New York","stance":"reference","why":"1906 Market Street footage showing busy mixed traffic","quote":"Plenty of cars, bikes, and people.","text":"Regardless still looks like morning hours on a snowy/winter day in most. Look at Market Street, San Francisco in 1906: [VIDEO] Plenty of cars, bikes, and people. I doubt NYC was much different in 1940 at higher trafficked hours."}],"r":[["gkj_91IJVBk",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["rk6z1vP4Eo8",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["NHWjlCaIrQo",1],["eNliOm9NtCM",1],["Mc_4Z1oiXhY",1],["vxopfjXkArM",1],["_IgqJr8jG8M",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["Z-9rLlFgcm0",1],["-8nqnOcoLqE",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1],["vwCdKU9uYnE",1],["Gk-9Fd2mEnI",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["43CMV8KIs3E",1],["s0ukXDnWlTY",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1]]},"hMKy52Intac":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as the author's own short explanation of Xanadu's core ideas, usually in threads on hypertext, linking and decentralizing the web.","q":{"x":"Ted Nelson recently did a series of videos on the important ideas in Xanadu. Start here","i":19721468,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":23491701,"author":"dang","month":"2020-06","story":"Xanaflight: Three Pages","stance":"rec","why":"Beautiful examples of visible connection in text","quote":"For more on that idea, see , which contains beautiful examples.","text":"This is software implementing one version of Ted Nelson's idea of visible connection in text. For more on that idea, see [VIDEO] , which contains beautiful examples."},{"id":21442225,"author":"pratio","month":"2019-11","story":"Roam – Tool for Thinking","stance":"rec","why":"Nelson's explanation of the linking concept","quote":"This video is better","text":"That's the first thought that came to my mind. I love hearing ted nelson explain it h [LINK] It is a good effort to bring that concept to life. This video is better [VIDEO]"},{"id":19721468,"author":"shalabhc","month":"2019-04","story":"Project Xanadu","stance":"rec","why":"Starting point for Nelson's Xanadu video series","quote":"Ted Nelson recently did a series of videos on the important ideas in Xanadu. Start here","text":"Ted Nelson recently did a series of videos on the important ideas in Xanadu. Start here: [VIDEO] Also you can buy his classic 'Computer Lib' book here: [LINK]"},{"id":17446985,"author":"shalabhc","month":"2018-07","story":"Tim Berners-Lee’s Solid is a platform designed to re-decentralize the web","stance":"rec","why":"Ted Nelson on the important ideas of Xanadu","quote":"Ted Nelson recently posted a series of videos describing the important ideas on Xanadu, the original hypertext project","text":"Related, Ted Nelson recently posted a series of videos describing the important ideas on Xanadu, the original hypertext project: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29275743,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-11","story":"Forth vs Lisp","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thank you for your kind words, and for asking a great question! My secret system that has gotten me through the coronavirus pandemic is simply an investment in an automatic coffee machine that grind beans and foams milk for me. ;) I use HN search and google site search, copy and paste and clean up old postings, and then check through all the links (since HN abbreviates long links with \"...\" so I have to copy and paste the full links manually), and I update the broken and walled links with Internet Archive links. I realize some of my posts get pretty long, and I apologize if that overwhelms some people, but it's a double edged sword. One important goal is to save other people's time and effort, since there are many more readers than writers, and I have to balance how long it takes for somebody who's interested to read, versus how long it takes for somebody who's not interested to skip. The new HN \"prev\" and \"next\" buttons that were recently added to help make HN posts more accessible to people with screen readers are helpful to everyone else too. (Accessibility helps everybody, not just blind people!) And as the internet has gotten faster and storage cheaper, while the user interface quality, usability, and smooth flow and interactivity of browsers has stagnated (especially on mobile), the cost of skipping over long post gets lower, while the cost of jumping back and forth between many different links and contexts stays ridiculously and unjustifiably expensive (just ask Ted Ne"},{"id":17696310,"author":"robertkrahn01","month":"2018-08","story":"Freeing the Web from the Browser","stance":"reference","why":"Ted Nelson's link representation ideas","quote":"Ted Nelsons ideas around Xanadu and its link representations aren't even mentioned.","text":"Great to see those ideas discussed! It is a little strange, however, that Ted Nelsons ideas around Xanadu and its link representations aren't even mentioned. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["HrVtA-ue-x0",1],["_ul7X5js1vE",1]]},"K3lP3BhvnSo":{"wy":"HN users point to it to correct the 'money printer go brrr' view and as a clear explanation of how banks create money through loans.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend this video to understand and this channel in general","i":24329822,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24329822,"author":"italicbold","month":"2020-08","story":"Banks create money, but it's less impressive than it sounds","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains modern banking without fractional reserve lending","quote":"Highly recommend this video to understand and this channel in general","text":"The modern banking system does not use fractional reserve lending. Highly recommend this video to understand and this channel in general: [VIDEO] Skip to 1:14."},{"id":27026060,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-05","story":"Negative interest: Danske Bank changes threshold for personal customers","stance":"rec","why":"Twenty minute summary of how banks create money via loans","quote":"A twenty minute video summarizing the above paper","text":"> Savings in a bank account will be loaned by the bank to another business. This has not been how banks have worked for decades: * [LINK] Banks create loans typing something into a computer screen and money shows up in the businesss' account. No savings required. 99% of money created is not by the Fed \"printing money\", but rather by private bank loans. A twenty minute video summarizing the above paper: * [VIDEO] Interview with the paper's author: * [LINK]"},{"id":25537333,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-12","story":"2020 had some significant silver linings","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of central bank money printing","quote":"Good video by Ben Felix on the topic","text":"Strictly speaking, in the modern era, it is not \"a government\" that decides to print money, but rather the central bank, which are considered independent on government and do (should) not follow the orders of politicians. The central bankers are hired for a fixed-term (though renewable) and are given a mandate: > The Federal Reserve works to promote a strong U.S. economy. Specifically, the Congress has assigned the Fed to conduct the nation’s monetary policy to support the goals of maximum employment, stable prices, and moderate long-term interest rates. When prices are stable, long-term interest rates remain at moderate levels, so the goals of price stability and moderate long-term interest rates go together. As a result, the goals of maximum employment and stable prices are often referred to as the Fed’s “dual mandate.” * [LINK] If 'printing money' helps to accomplish that mandate they will do so, but they will (theoretically/ideally) not print money just because it would be convenient for political purposes. It should also be noted that in most/may modern economies, private banks actually create the money the supply through issuing loans: * PDF: [LINK] Good video by Ben Felix on the topic: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":24022567,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-08","story":"Ask HN: I have $450K cash, what should I do to maximize my return?","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the idea that the Fed prints money without restraint","quote":"just released a video explaining why this is completely wrong","text":"> the willingness of the Federal reserve to print money (create debt). Without restraint nowadays. Ben Felix, a portfolio manager at PWL Capital in Canada, just released a video explaining why this is completely wrong: * [VIDEO] > Quantitative easing is a monetary policy whereby a central bank buys government bonds or other financial assets in order to inject money into the economy to expand economic activity. But what exactly does that mean? In today’s episode, Benjamin and Cameron are going to address this topic, avoiding highly politicized aspects, like whether or not central banks should be involved in the economy in the first place, and focusing purely on the operational perspective of quantitative easing – what is it, how it works, and what the intended transmission mechanisms are. * [LINK] > For instance, house flipping or starting a business. You can get better than the 5% or whatever you get after inflation in stocks. If you're really good at what you do, your odds of getting a better return go up. That's nice if you want a different/second career or something. But there are those of us who are happy with our careers/jobs, and simply want something to do with our retirement savings… ain't nobody got time for that. Of course the half-life on any new business is quite abysmal, so I'm not crazy/ambitious enough to take on that kind of risk."},{"id":42271441,"author":"simula67","month":"2024-11","story":"Engineers do not get to make startup mistakes when they build ledgers","stance":"reference","why":"Source for bank lending creating new money","quote":"","text":"> The bank is not printing money new money, no matter how many times this idea gets repeated by people who are, ironically, pumping crypto coins that were printed out of thin air. It is now widely accepted that bank lending produces new money[1][2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24022795,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-08","story":"Ask HN: I have $450K cash, what should I do to maximize my return?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Is the landscape of 1991 the same as the landscape of 2020, with its 10% of the s&p in just a few companies Yes, it is the same: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] That's the definition of the S&P 500: the largest five hundred companies publicly traded companies. > with money printing, I do not think this actually does what you thinks it does: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] > […] Talk shows about stocks and news algorithms pumping stocks to the masses? Please see the 1990s and \"irrational exuberance\". I live in Canada: ask anyone here above a certain age (40s) about Nortel and Bre-X. > Are fund managers (who have clients) compared to the likes of you and me ? Fund managers have more resources than you and me, and thus probably have better information to base decisions on. Unless, of course, you are using satellite imagery in your investing workflow: > Currie’s prediction proved correct. As word spread that satellite images were a reliable predictor of corporate profits, a range of investment funds began buying retail-traffic data from RS Metrics. In the following years, the company expanded, tracking not just parked cars but solar-panel installations, lumber inventory at sawmills, and the mining of metals worldwide. * [LINK]"},{"id":24212917,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-08","story":"Apple becomes first U.S. company to reach a $2T market cap","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Fed paper questioning QE's effect on stocks","quote":"The video cites an US Fed paper on the subject","text":"> Yep! S&P 500 reaches pre-COVID level record high again recently all thanks to U.S. Fed's UNLIMITED Quantitative easing (QE) policy. I question the validity of this hypothesis, at least in so far that it has a predominant or even major effect: * [VIDEO] The video cites an US Fed paper on the subject (see Section 3): * [LINK] It's mostly \"policy shocks\" / surprises that cause changes in bond rates, which may have knock-on effects to other asset classes. Indexes have gone up and up and hit all-time highs regularly over the decades on their own: there's no need to throw QE magic pixie dust as a cause. Further, in the past, a much smaller percentage of the population probably had equity ownership: as pensions have given way to private retirement funds (401(k) in the US), and so you have people buying the S&P 500 in their Vanguard accounts. On the flip side, you have a large population cohort (Baby Boomers) entering retirement age, and they want safer asset classes so are going after bonds, driving down yields. With yields getting lower, anyone who wants returns is stuck with equities. So you have one group of people bidding up equities for growth towards retirement, and another group bidding down bonds for safety in retirement."}],"r":[["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1],["NRoUC9P1PmA",1]]},"SC2eSujzrUY":{"wy":"Recommended as a good, accessible explanation of PFAS, its low safe limits and why regulation matters.","q":{"x":"If you want a good explanation of PFAs, Veritasium did a video on it.","i":44365273,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44125138,"author":"chiph","month":"2025-05","story":"Derek Muller confronts PFAS \"forever chemicals\" in his own blood","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convinced commenter to give up microwave popcorn over PFAS in bags.","quote":"Based on his video, I have given up eating microwave popcorn and have gone back to stove-top made.","text":"Based on his video, I have given up eating microwave popcorn and have gone back to stove-top made. If his numbers are to be believed (and I do), the coating on the inside of a microwave popcorn bag that is used to keep the cooking oils from soaking into the paper emit PFAS chemicals. Which leach into the popcorn oils - later to be consumed by you. In the chart comparing them with other coated papers such as fast food burger wrappers, they are far and away the leading source of PFAS. I still have questions - do the PFAS chemicals leach into the oils while sitting on the store shelf, or are they emitted when they get heated up in the microwave and exceed some temperature (like getting a Teflon pan too hot)? Not that this makes a difference in the end result, but I am curious. Video link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45503351,"author":"starwatch","month":"2025-10","story":"3M May Escape Toxic Chemical, PFAS Manufacturing Legacy","stance":"rec","why":"Nuances PFAS badness, non-stick cookware likely fine, covers manufacturing damage.","quote":"If you want more context on PFAS, I recommend this Veritasium video","text":"If you want more context on PFAS, I recommend this Veritasium video [0]. It expanded on my usual thought of \"PFAS = bad,\" explaining why non-stick cookware is probably fine while other forms of PFAS are problematic. The video also covers the environmental damage caused by PFAS manufacturing. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44365273,"author":"ourmandave","month":"2025-06","story":"Microplastics shed by food packaging are contaminating our food, study finds","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of PFAS and its very low safe limits.","quote":"If you want a good explanation of PFAs, Veritasium did a video on it.","text":"If you want a good explanation of PFAs, Veritasium did a video on it. [VIDEO] The safe allowable ppm counts are insanely low for this shit."},{"id":48841997,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2026-07","story":"GAO: DOE Is Prematurely Excluding Less Expensive Options for Nuclear Cleanup","stance":"reference","why":"Example of PFAS in the host's blood.","quote":"PFAS in the Veritasium hosts blood.","text":"The toxicology of soluble heavy-metal bioavailable material is a well understood area of research, and can bioaccumulate in humans several times higher than background if a food chain allows biomagnification of a contaminant. The publication assumes the reader has first year biology, and will understand test subject mortality data implications. I would recommend studying the story of Polychlorinated biphenyls in whales, Mercury levels in salmon, and PFAS in the Veritasium hosts blood. [VIDEO] \"Particle Accelerator vs Egg\" is a great demonstration of purely irradiated cell decomposition, and is great entertainment for all ages. =3 [LINK]"},{"id":47730721,"author":"alt227","month":"2026-04","story":"How to breathe in fewer microplastics in your home","stance":"reference","why":"Says PFAS from pans passes through digestion unless heated above 300 C.","quote":"Even in the recent Veritasium video about it they said","text":"Fully agree with you, however eating small bits of PFAS from pans seems to be pretty non toxic. Even in the recent Veritasium video about it they said that unless the chemical was heated to above ~300 degress C if will pass through the human digestive system without causing any harm. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45240366,"author":"vincnetas","month":"2025-09","story":"EPA Seeks to Eliminate Critical PFAS Drinking Water Protections","stance":"reference","why":"Explains PFAS and why environmental protections are needed.","quote":"Veritasium had video explaining about PFAS and environment protections","text":"Veritasium had video explaining about PFAS and environment protections that were needed to keep people/animals from being sick. Somewhere around minute 23 in video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44642199,"author":"sakesun","month":"2025-07","story":"If writing is thinking then what happens if AI is doing the writing and reading?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows convenience inventions becoming decades-later health concerns.","quote":"Inventions created for convenience decades ago have now become health concerns.","text":"Just watched Veritasium's \"How One Company Secretly Poisoned The Planet\" [VIDEO] Inventions created for convenience decades ago have now become health concerns. I wonder how AI might affect our intellectual well-being in the decades to come."},{"id":44156655,"author":"aptj","month":"2025-06","story":"Ask HN: How are parents who program teaching their kids today?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a title in a general parenting message.","quote":"How One Company Secretly Poisoned The Planet","text":"@all Don't forget to help your kids learn to communicate, cooperate, stand for the healthy life on this wonderful planet (our common habitat) and resist being fooled and bullied. > Veritasium: How One Company Secretly Poisoned The Planet [VIDEO] Edits: minor typos."}],"r":[["4Wrc4fHSCpw",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["5eW6Eagr9XA",1],["nmgFG7PUHfo",1],["8AHCfZTRGiI",1],["MesrrYyuoa4",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["lI7T650RTT8",1],["Hhy7JUinlu0",1]]},"Y4Gt3Xjd7G8":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as the best way to understand what async machinery actually does under the covers.","q":{"x":"an amazing resource to understand async at a deep level","i":23296660,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23166878,"author":"devxpy","month":"2020-05","story":"Linux containers in a few lines of code","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows what async actually does under the covers","quote":"to successfully show what the hell its actually doing under the covers, just conveys much more information","text":"Yes, something like that. Like here's David's \"Build Your Own Async\" [1], which I prefer over Philip's (still extremely good) \"What the heck is the event loop anyway?\" [2]. It's one thing to tell how something works, but to successfully show what the hell its actually doing under the covers, just conveys much more information. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23296660,"author":"migueloller","month":"2020-05","story":"Guide to Concurrency in Python with Asyncio","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing low-level screencast for deeply understanding Python async","quote":"an amazing resource to understand async at a deep level","text":"I've found this [1] screencast on the low-level principles of async in Python an amazing resource to understand async at a deep level. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37396428,"author":"mananaysiempre","month":"2023-09","story":"Philosophy of Coroutines","stance":"rec","why":"Walks through implementing an async runtime, showing how it fits together","quote":"David Beazley has a talk[2] where he walks you through implementing an async runtime","text":"> I talk about generators as if they were the coroutine system in Python, but in fact, they’re one of two: Python also has a completely different kind of thing that it actually uses the name ‘coroutine’ for, in which function definitions start with async def and suspend with await . The async and await system of coroutines in Python is in fact layered on top of the yield system, via an intermediate construct called yield from —which isn’t even technically required, it’s just that implementing it as a loop passing values through from and to the subcoroutine is exceptionally tedious if done correctly[1] and can’t be abstracted as a function call. As a first approximation, await x in Python is equivalent to yield from x.__await__() . It should have been completely equivalent, arguably, but history got in the way. David Beazley has a talk[2] where he walks you through implementing an async runtime (to put it pompously) and you can see how the whole thing hangs together. It’s really quite simple, and in my experience it’s much better than trying to wrap your head around the asyncio docs, because while the language-level foundations are extremely nice and simple, asyncio the stdlib module just kind of sucks. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30857472,"author":"neoncontrails","month":"2022-03","story":"Pre-Allocated Lists in Python","stance":"rec","why":"Beazley keynote among enlightening dives into Python's dark side","quote":"","text":"I highly recommend Victor Skvortsov's exhaustive, 12-part series, \"Python Behind the Scenes,\" a deep dive into the implementation details of just about every Python language feature one could possibly take for granted. There isn't a single one I haven't learned something from, but some of my personal favorites include: * Part 2, How the CPython compiler works: [LINK] * Part 6, How Python's object system works: [LINK] * Part 10, How Python dictionaries work: [LINK] * Part 13: The GIL and its effects on Python multithreading: [LINK] The whole series can be found here: [LINK] For a slightly higher-level (i.e., no macabre, low-level C code), but still incredibly enlightening dive into the \"dark side\" of Python, in particular various abuses and hexes for manipulating control flow, have a look at David Beazley's smorgasboard of keynote presentations like \"Generators: The Final Frontier\" ( [LINK] ), \"Python Concurrency From The Ground Up\" ( [LINK] ), and \"Build Your Own Async\" ( [VIDEO] )."}],"r":[["cCOL7MC4Pl0",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["8aGhZQkoFbQ",1],["Obt-vMVdM8s",1]]},"nAbCKa0FzjQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a detailed, entertaining look at the crazy optimizations compilers do.","q":{"x":"Both are great presentations going into details on some of the crazy optimizations compilers will do.","i":20915513,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20915513,"author":"snailmailman","month":"2019-09","story":"Popcount CPU instruction","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed tour of surprising compiler optimizations","quote":"Both are great presentations going into details on some of the crazy optimizations compilers will do.","text":"I highly recommend Matt Godbolt's talk \"What has my compiler done for me lately?\"[0] and its follow-up[1]. Both are great presentations going into details on some of the crazy optimizations compilers will do. As someone who knows very little about the inner workings of compilers my mind was blown when I saw some them. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22981369,"author":"manaskarekar","month":"2020-04","story":"I translated a simple C program to x86_64 and it was slower","stance":"rec","why":"Follow-up Godbolt talk on what compilers do","quote":"this reminds me of this great talk by Matt Godbolt","text":"Tangent, but this reminds me of this great talk by Matt Godbolt from CppCon 2017: “What Has My Compiler Done for Me Lately? Unbolting the Compiler's Lid” [LINK] . And the follow up [VIDEO] ."},{"id":22268741,"author":"drej","month":"2020-02","story":"Rust: Zero-Cost Abstraction in Action","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on clever compiler optimizations like closed-form sums and popcount","quote":"Compilers are awesome, check out Matt Godbolt's talk on this very topic","text":"This is not Rust specific, this is a compiler thing, both LLVM and GCC can detect sums and generate closed form formulas instead. There are other fun algorithm detections - e.g. if you try to do bitcounts yourself, LLVM will use popcnt instead. Compilers are awesome, check out Matt Godbolt's talk on this very topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18680342,"author":"foxrob92","month":"2018-12","story":"Compiler Explorer","stance":"reference","why":"Matt Godbolt discusses Compiler Explorer architecture and security","quote":"Matt Godbolt has spoken a bit about the architecture of Compiler Explorer.","text":"Doesn't look like it works any more. In any case, Matt Godbolt has spoken a bit about the architecture of Compiler Explorer. Seems like he's given some thought to security. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bSkpMdDe4g4",3],["0mCsluv5FXA",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["MO8hZlgK5zc",1],["T5RmMNDR5XI",1]]},"tOMiAeRwpPA":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as a clear explanation of encrypted messaging challenges and of the thinking behind Signal.","q":{"x":"I got excited about signal after watching this video, which explains pretty well the challenges of encrypted communication and how Signal addresses them","i":10665445,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":25522117,"author":"1cvmask","month":"2020-12","story":"No, Cellebrite Cannot “Break Signal Encryption”","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moxie ties American action movie narratives to secure communications","quote":"Here is another great one where he ties American action movies as narrative with secure communications","text":"Here is another great one where he ties American action movies as narrative with secure communications: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27276443,"author":"sig-reduction","month":"2021-05","story":"I Was Robbed in San Francisco While the Cameras Rolled","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis of American action movies that may change how you see Batman","quote":"It may change the way you see Batman and other characters that may have influenced your thinking.","text":"Check out this analysis of American action movies. It may change the way you see Batman and other characters that may have influenced your thinking. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10665445,"author":"dates","month":"2015-12","story":"Signal Desktop","stance":"rec","why":"Explains challenges of encrypted communication and how Signal addresses them","quote":"I got excited about signal after watching this video, which explains pretty well the challenges of encrypted communication and how Signal addresses them","text":"I got excited about signal after watching this video, which explains pretty well the challenges of encrypted communication and how Signal addresses them (it might be basic stuff for people super familiar with the topic though): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9WfZuNceFDM",1]]},"xNjI03CGkb4":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it critically, as an insider's blunt playbook for psychologically exploiting players in F2P and loot box games.","q":{"x":"Must see video for anyone interested in the topic, from someone explaining how to do these monetizations tactics","i":37659948,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37659948,"author":"grotorea","month":"2023-09","story":"The Casino in Your Pocket","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insider explains how mobile monetization tactics work.","quote":"Must see video for anyone interested in the topic, from someone explaining how to do these monetizations tactics","text":"Must see video for anyone interested in the topic, from someone explaining how to do these monetizations tactics: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37660006,"author":"grotorea","month":"2023-09","story":"The Casino in Your Pocket","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insider explains how mobile monetization tactics work.","quote":"Reminds me of this must see video for anyone interested in the topic, from someone explaining how to do these monetizations tactics","text":"Reminds me of this must see video for anyone interested in the topic, from someone explaining how to do these monetizations tactics: [VIDEO] Sometimes I wonder what is is about specifically mobile gaming that makes this sort of thing so much more prevalent. Like it is very far from absent from PC or consoles, but it's still a bit different."},{"id":43097723,"author":"layman51","month":"2025-02","story":"Among top researchers 10% publish at unrealistic levels, analysis finds","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting 2016 talk of tips for game developers.","quote":"It is very interesting because it is from 2016","text":"Gamification as a field of study also reminds me a lot of this video[1] of a talk about tips for game developers. It is very interesting because it is from 2016 which is close to the time period you mentioned. I’m pretty sure this video has been shared on Hacker News a bunch. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41081742,"author":"jonathanyc","month":"2024-07","story":"How Mihoyo's monetization works","stance":"rec","why":"More informative industry talk on monetization than the article.","quote":"I think this industry talk about monetization ( )","text":"I’m not too familiar with gacha games, so I was intrigued. I thought the criticism of EA’s former CEO was funny. But the rest of the article felt like a lot of term-dropping and examples that aren't linked together into a cohesive theory, so I’m kind of disappointed. I think this industry talk about monetization ( [VIDEO] ) and Tynan Sylvester’s book “Designing Games” were a lot more informative. For example, the blog post says this but then doesn't really elaborate on what any of the clauses mean (\"repeatable design levers\", \"without compromising the core gameplay experience\"): > A healthy long-term monetization system should therefore have repeatable design levers that can be used to reliably generate demand without compromising the core gameplay experience. In contrast \"Designing Games\" has a whole section on fixed/variable reward schedules, e.g. (pg. 210): > We can avoid such shelf moments by superimposing several fixed ratio schedules. Consider what happens when the player can get a dollar for every 10th chest, a diamond for every 10th rock mined, and an arrow for every 10th goblin killed. ... The player shifts focus back and forth between activities, never missing a dopamine-driven beat. I asked some friends who play gacha games and they mentioned “stamina” mechanics as something that makes these game addictive. I’d never heard of it before, but apparently you’re limited to perform X number of actions per day. You can purchase some additional stamina/actions, but this c"},{"id":46661927,"author":"constantcrying","month":"2026-01","story":"Italy investigates Activision Blizzard for pushing in-game purchases","stance":"reference","why":"Cited regarding whale-targeted monetization and proposed spending limits.","quote":"","text":"To be honst I think state run lotteries are a pretty good idea, if they would replace other forms of gambling, which apparently is becoming less and less true, especially with the rise of the completely under regulated prediction markets. Regarding the subject at hand, I think a very obvious and necessary first step is banning children from participation. Obviously this is easily circumventable, but at will at least be some form of harm reduction. I think another possibility as a first step is a forced limit on how much an account is allowed to spend in a given time, e.g. the company is only allowed to add digital goods not exceeding X euros to the owners account, together with restrictions on how these items are traded, this will make whaling impossible, which is apparently the most important demographic for developers. [ [VIDEO] ]"},{"id":20948908,"author":"lozenge","month":"2019-09","story":"Ban kids from loot box gambling in games","stance":"reference","why":"Describes player profiles in game monetization.","quote":"You just described two of the four player profiles in this talk on monetizing games","text":"That isn't generational, it is part of your personality though. You just described two of the four player profiles in this talk on monetizing games: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45953694,"author":"redbell","month":"2025-11","story":"Dark Pattern Games","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"You might be interested to watch this video entitled","text":"These patterns do really take away the feeling of joy, fun and sense of accomplishment of playing games and replace them with pain, stress, fear and probably, every negative feeling you might think of. It's sad to witness the tragic shift from \"pay to play\" to \"pay to win\". You might be interested to watch this video entitled Let’s go whaling: Tricks for monetising mobile game players with free-to-play : [VIDEO] , which has been referenced by many YouTube analysis, and for good reason. Also, this paper was a nice read for me: Predatory monetization schemes in video games and internet gaming disorder ( [LINK] )"},{"id":45530026,"author":"redbell","month":"2025-10","story":"Predatory monetization schemes in video games and internet gaming disorder","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It perfectly captures the sad reality of modern gaming culture and how deeply monetization has replaced passion.","text":"> These types of purchases are common in mobile game revenue models where the base game is ‘free-to- play’, but the player is encouraged to spend money to make unimpeded progress in the game I miss the old 90's days at the arcade where we were stacked horizontally in a queue waiting for our turn, holding coins in our hands. We've been forced to trade the \"pay to play\" purity of skill and community for the predatory economics of \"pay to win.\" This evolution is well-explained in this video ( [VIDEO] ), entitled \"Let’s go whaling: Tricks for monetizing mobile game players with free-to-play.\" which has been cited in countless YouTube analyses, and for good reason . It perfectly captures the sad reality of modern gaming culture and how deeply monetization has replaced passion."},{"id":37494308,"author":"Hammershaft","month":"2023-09","story":"Apple iPhone 15 Pro and iPhone 15 Pro Max","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Watch a GDC talk on growing mobile F2P revenue and you will be exposed to a slew of deceptive methods for psychologically manipulating players","text":"> All the \"ethical problems\" usually are just older (usually) men in IT not understanding that people want to pay money to have fun. Fucking absurd, to be frank. Watch a GDC talk on growing mobile F2P revenue and you will be exposed to a slew of deceptive methods for psychologically manipulating players (who are often kids) into addictive spending habits, often literally through gambling mechanisms. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34480435,"author":"rng_civ","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Why do games (as media) make so much money?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Only bit I disagree with is that the big spenders are doing it purely because of addiction or gambling. People spend big in games because: * The spending is very stimulating visually and audibly (think lootbox openings) * The gains from their spending translates directly to game-social prestige, game power, or both (i.e. an Advantage) * This Advantage allows them to lord over the players who have spent less (or 0 in the case for F2P players) Whether that manifests into addiction depends entirely on the rest of the game's design (but you know, games that introduce the Advantage tend to want to make a lot of money by getting you hooked on spending...) > Second, games provide a sense of community. A lot of game revenue is monetizing people's desire to not be alone. Calling these players addicted is I think reductive. Yes, these games do provide a sense of community because they are purposefully designed to do so. Without an incentive to play while getting lorded over by whales, the fish will leave. Without a bunch of fish to lord over, the whales will leave. Again, this depends on the game's design, but the vast majority of them encourage addiction to the Advantage and its use against others. Maybe you can extend this analysis to IRL stuff. I don't know because I don't participate in any of it. Source: [Let’s go whaling: Tricks for monetising mobile game players with free-to-play]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":33537200,"author":"xphx","month":"2022-11","story":"TikTok’s greatest asset isn’t its algorithm, it’s your phone","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Ominously delivered with smug smirk, met by smug laughter","text":"A 2016 talk by Tribeflame CEO Torulf Jernström about the monetization of mobile games called \"Let's Go Whaling!\" also caused a bit of a ruffle a while ago. Ominously delivered with smug smirk, met by smug laughter: \"I'll leave the morality of it out of the talk. We can discuss it, if we have time, later.\" [VIDEO] Turns out they would have had time later but for some reason opted not to."},{"id":32673377,"author":"Pmop","month":"2022-09","story":"Sony is ready to admit that mobile gaming is too big to ignore","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"so they'll happily drop tens of thousands of dollars on gacha gambling crap.","text":"Yep. Been following this for a while. Mobile games are basically built for \"hunting whales\": people with deep pockets and nothing else to spend on other than games, so they'll happily drop tens of thousands of dollars on gacha gambling crap. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25397903,"author":"d3nj4l","month":"2020-12","story":"Gamers allege EA uses dynamic difficulty adjustment so players spend more money","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"especially the section where he quotes from a talk by one such developer [2].","text":"It's sickening, really. Jim Sterling's video on lootboxes [1] really hammered it home for me, especially the section where he quotes from a talk by one such developer [2]. The talk is literally titled \"Let's go whaling\", a reference to getting people to \"whale\" the game by paying tons of money. Fate Grand Order, a gacha game, even lampshades it: > What’s interesting about Fate/Grand Order is that the game’s marketing features a vicious caricature of dedicated players. She’s a slobbering, lecherous, gambling-addicted little monster. > Fans love her. [From [3]] 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO] 3: [LINK]"},{"id":25438946,"author":"GreenWatermelon","month":"2020-12","story":"Should There Be Limits on Persuasive Technologies?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this is a tried and true method which you can learn about from the whaler himself[1].","text":"People who are addicted to gacha are psychologically manipulated, not to mention the gambling aspects. Gambling is already regulated across the world. Just like laws protect the physically weak from exploitation, they should protect the psychologically weak from psychological exploitation. You wouldn't accept someone holding you at gunpoint demanding your money, and you would have no choice but to give up you belongings. Even though it was your choice to give up your stuff, you were coerced into it. gacha/loot boxes and similar addictive phenomena pry on your psych. This isn't some conspiracy, this is a tried and true method which you can learn about from the whaler himself[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20949184,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-09","story":"Ban kids from loot box gambling in games","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He discusses strategies for building the perfect Skinner Box, and how to design your game around operant conditioning","text":"They know exactly what they are doing. If anyone wants proof, see Torulf Jernström's talk \"Let’s go whaling: Tricks for monetising mobile game players with free-to-play\"[1]. He discusses strategies for building the perfect Skinner Box, and how to design your game around operant conditioning and other psychological tricks that help people become addicted. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20445816,"author":"en4bz","month":"2019-07","story":"'My son spent £3,160 in one game'","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They're not even hiding the fact the these are the type of people Free2Play/Pay2Win games prey on anymore.","text":"If you haven't seen it all ready just take a look at the infamous \"Let's Go Whaling!\" talk here [1]. They're not even hiding the fact the these are the type of people Free2Play/Pay2Win games prey on anymore. [1]. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20494216,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2019-07","story":"'My son spent £3,160 in one game'","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"But there is a certain set of games that relies on these tricks. See","text":"> If I understand correctly, you're saying that AAA game companies are at fault for producing video games that influence people through addictive psychological tricks to the point of ruining their lives. No, not AAA exclusively. But there is a certain set of games that relies on these tricks. See [VIDEO] > Are McDonald's commercials to blame for obesity and heart disease? Did McDonalds create a \"Diabetes increases sales\" video? Because AAA and mobile games did."}]},"G4hL5Om4IJ4":{"wy":"Recommended as a stellar long-form interview with someone both very smart and wise, mined for CPU design insights.","q":{"x":"Investing time to listen to the guy seems to be not worse.","i":34908359,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35692202,"author":"WestCoastJustin","month":"2023-04","story":"Second IC – Homemade 1000 transistor array chip","stance":"rec","why":"Second Keller appearance on Lex Fridman","quote":"Jim Keller was on the Lex Fridman podcast twice [1, 2] and it was awesome.","text":"I didn't make the connection until now, but Jim Keller was on the Lex Fridman podcast twice [1, 2] and it was awesome. In terms of founders this is pretty great team. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34908359,"author":"comboy","month":"2023-02","story":"Sam Zeloof and Jim Keller start a new semiconductor fab","stance":"rec","why":"Keller is very smart and wise","quote":"Lex has good interviews with Jim[1], he's not just very smart, he is wise.","text":"K8 seems pretty small after he made the fucking Zen architecture (and Tesla AI). Lex has good interviews with Jim[1], he's not just very smart, he is wise. He started Tenstorrent some time ago with Ljubisa Bajic so I'm assuming this fab is related. Here is youtube channel for Tenstorrent[2], and last time I checked it was getting under 100 views per video... Ian also interviewed him[3] some time ago. Long story short if I had money that would be relevant in this context I would invest really hard into Tenstorrent. Investing time to listen to the guy seems to be not worse. 1a. [LINK] 1b. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK]"},{"id":33419084,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Have you stopped reading most news?","stance":"rec","why":"Stellar long-form interview worth listening to","quote":"he's got some stellar interviews like the one with Jim Killer[1]","text":"Something I have taken to doing these days is playing some mindless but fun game while listening to long-form interviews or talks. As a random example, these days I'm playing Slipways[1], but Diablo-style games or factory/builder games are also suitable. Meanwhile I like to listen to fantastically insightful interviews with people like Julia Ioffe. You can watch a thousand 1-minute news clips and not gain as much understanding about Putin and the war in Ukraine as from a one-hour interview with her. Similarly, the Telegraph's \"Ukraine the latest\" is a roughly one-hour podcast I listen to every morning while taking the kid out for a walk. Perun's analysis of the logistics situation also tends to be spot on, and very insightful. I used to listen to interesting characters on Joe Rogan, but Joe turned a bit... right wing nutjob. Lex Fridman is less crazy but also a less skilled interviewer. Still, he's got some stellar interviews like the one with Jim Killer[1]. Do you want to watch a soundbite from a politician in another country talking about something that will never affect you, or do you want to hear from the horse's mouth how your everyday life will change because of new technology? I used to feel I didn't have time for this kind of thing, but overlapping it with gameplay makes it seem like zero time \"spent\" on it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35793164,"author":"ricopags","month":"2023-05","story":"Mojo – a new programming language for AI developers","stance":"reference","why":"Keller's callout predicted the Mojo announcement","quote":"I've been waiting for this announcement since Jim Keller's callout on Lex Fridman podcast two years ago[0]","text":"Congratulations on the launch preview! This is exciting, as I've been waiting for this announcement since Jim Keller's callout on Lex Fridman podcast two years ago[0]. I'm curious about the teasing around open source. Obviously the amount of money and the slick product launch dictate a need to capitalize on this pooled expertise. Don't want to give away the game to the hyperscalers. I wonder what the revenue and license models are going to end up looking like. A cloud of their own, professional services to HW manufacturers to optimize their performance, professional services to hyperscalers? Curious if anybody has any ideas beyond the obvious. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28010635,"author":"anonymouse008","month":"2021-07","story":"B-tree Path Hints","stance":"reference","why":"Second Keller interview, cited for prediction speedup","quote":"","text":"I'm not qualified to truly understand this, but when Lex first interviewed Jim Keller, Jim basically said regarding processor design -- 'yeah, if you just guess the same result as last time, you get a 50% speed increase.' First Interview (where that poorly paraphrased quote resides): [LINK] Second Interview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27043027,"author":"joakleaf","month":"2021-05","story":"Efficiency Is the Enemy","stance":"reference","why":"Keller's graph of productivity versus order","quote":"So there is a graph. Y-axis is productivity. X-axis at 0 is chaos and infinity is complete order.","text":"I heard Jim Keller (the chip-designer) say this (or something similar) recently in a podcast with Lex Fridman. It is at the 24.40 mark in \"Jim Keller: The Future of Computing, AI, Life, and Consciousness | Lex Fridman Podcast\" [VIDEO] \"So there is a graph. Y-axis is productivity. X-axis at 0 is chaos and infinity is complete order. As you improve order, you increase productivity. And at some point productivity peaks, and it goes down again. Too much order -- Nothing can happen... Once you start moving towards order, the force vector that drives you towards order is unstoppable.\""},{"id":26340530,"author":"limaoscarjuliet","month":"2021-03","story":"Accidentally quadratic: When Python is faster than C++","stance":"reference","why":"Keller on branch and data prediction driving CPU speed","quote":"Per Jim Keller, current source of speed in CPU is better branch prediction and data fetching prediction.","text":"Per Jim Keller, current source of speed in CPU is better branch prediction and data fetching prediction. It is possible C++ (or some other lang) may have more met information to drive these better, in which case it will be faster than C/assembler. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26199773,"author":"AlchemistCamp","month":"2021-02","story":"Jim Keller: The Future of Computing, AI, Life, and Consciousness – Lex Pod #162","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamped highlights from Keller interview","quote":"A part about \"bad\" designs regularly winning both in software and in hardware","text":"A part about \"bad\" designs regularly winning both in software and in hardware: [VIDEO] Jim Keller's thoughts of the relationship between productivity and chaos: [VIDEO] His father's later years: [VIDEO] The fast and slow versions of Moore's Law: [VIDEO] On Chris Lattner and Swift: [VIDEO] Do you dream? [VIDEO] Advice to young people: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1]]},"H62hZJVqs2o":{"wy":"HN recommends it in Voyager threads as a good talk on how these old computers have proved durable and adaptable.","q":{"x":"this talk was really good","i":40698720,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40698720,"author":"ForHackernews","month":"2024-06","story":"Voyager Spacecraft and Fortran 5","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about Voyager probe computers","quote":"this talk was really good","text":"If you're interested in the computers on the Voyager probes, this talk was really good: \"Uptime 15,364 days\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":37995178,"author":"throwaway888abc","month":"2023-10","story":"NASA wants the Voyagers to age gracefully, so it's time for a software patch","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the Voyager computers","quote":"","text":"Love the Voyagers story. Just get me thinking: Why there is not more missions like this ? It takes significant time to cross \"somewhere there\", every year one launch could not hurt ? Edit: *Obligatory video: \"Uptime 15,364 days - The Computers of Voyager\" by Aaron Cummings [VIDEO] Also here on HN: [LINK]"},{"id":37012118,"author":"retrac","month":"2023-08","story":"NASA has reestablished full communications with Voyager 2","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on the Voyager computers","quote":"There's a good talk about the computers on the Voyagers available online here.","text":"There's a book on the computers used at NASA from the beginning through to when it was written - late 80s, I think. Whole thing is available online [1] It has a chapter titled \"Voyager - The Flying Computer Center\". [2] It gives a high-level overview of the computers and software. Three different processors, each dual redundant. 18 and 16 bit machines. Comparable to early 1970s minicomputers. There's a good talk about the computers on the Voyagers available online here. [3] As far as I know, beyond what's available between those two sources, very little otherwise is available publicly on the computer hardware itself - no detailed architecture descriptions, instruction sets, electronics details, etc. And no software listings. Though if I had to guess the 18-bit machines are a lot like - but not the same as - the OBP/AOP/NSSC series [4]. A bit of Voyager trivia: the computers were reprogrammed in-flight to give new abilities the Voyagers didn't have at launch, such as new image compression algorithms to allow more images to be returned than originally anticipated. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":25683641,"author":"bullen","month":"2021-01","story":"Nuclear lighthouses built by the Soviets in the Arctic [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions RTG elements used in space probes","quote":"","text":"In Sweden between 1930-1970 we produced building elements from concrete where oilshale was used to burn the chalk and the ashes of the fuel was mixed into the concrete! That shale ash was so rich in uranium that the concrete turned blue! Since then thousands have died by passively breathing the radon evaporation from the walls in some Swedish houses. On the subject RTG elements are also used in space probes: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8vJT8AW0wYw",1],["3BJU2drrtCM",1]]},"PtKhbbcc1Rc":{"wy":"HN recommends it in map projection and coastline threads as a fun look at how measurement choices change area figures.","q":{"x":"Matt Parker from Standup Maths made a great video on exactly this subject","i":25109593,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":35483800,"author":"rdlw","month":"2023-04","story":"The longest straight line in Great Britain (without crossing a public road)","stance":"rec","why":"Area measurement depends on elevation data resolution.","quote":"Great video on this from Matt Parker","text":"Then it becomes a coastline problem, with different answers depending on the resolution of your elevation data. Great video on this from Matt Parker (focusing on area in this case): [VIDEO]"},{"id":25109593,"author":"jobigoud","month":"2020-11","story":"The True Size Of Countries: Mercator projection","stance":"rec","why":"Matt Parker on whether land area assumes flat countries.","quote":"Matt Parker from Standup Maths made a great video on exactly this subject","text":"Matt Parker from Standup Maths made a great video on exactly this subject a few weeks ago. \"Does land area assume a country is perfectly flat?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":24438775,"author":"swilliamsio","month":"2020-09","story":"Coastline Paradox","stance":"rec","why":"Explores whether national land area accounts for elevation.","quote":"StandUpMaths did a great video on this recently","text":">But then realized that the coastline paradox must also exist for 3D surfaces (TINs or DEMs or whatnot) so asking “what is the area of a surface on earth” can be a very complex question if dealing with elevation. StandUpMaths did a great video on this recently, asking whether national land area includes elevation and which nations would gain/lose the most land area if it were/weren't: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28312535,"author":"jobigoud","month":"2021-08","story":"Concrete: The most destructive material on Earth","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the topography and area measurement rabbit hole.","quote":"Cf. Stand-up Maths","text":"> over every hill, dale, nook and cranny in England That would take topography into account which another rabbit hole like calculating the perimeter of Great Britain. Cf. Stand-up Maths, Does \"land area\" assume a country is perfectly flat? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bPhUhypV27w",1],["rgaTLrZGlk0",1],["sR8M4zARBXY",1],["xOCurBYI_gY",1],["t_rzYnXEQlE",1]]},"X9RYuvPCQUA":{"wy":"Pointed to in copyright and AI debates as a compelling case that all culture is remix.","q":{"x":"It highlights how essentially everything in culture is a remix of some previous version of the culture","i":40705849,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":47723969,"author":"supern0va","month":"2026-04","story":"AI assistance when contributing to the Linux kernel","stance":"rec","why":"Argues creativity is copying, transforming, and combining ideas","quote":"Kirby Ferguson's video on this is pretty great","text":">LLM-creation (\"training\") involves detecting/compressing patterns of the input. There's a pretty compelling argument that this is essentially what we do, and that what we think of as creativity is just copying, transforming, and combining ideas. LLMs are interesting because that compression forces distilling the world down into its constituent parts and learning about the relationships between ideas. While it's absolutely possible (or even likely for certain prompts) that models can regurgitate text very similar to their inputs, that is not usually what seems to be happening. They actually appear to be little remix engines that can fit the pieces together to solve the thing you're asking for, and we do have some evidence that the models are able to accomplish things that are not represented in their training sets. Kirby Ferguson's video on this is pretty great: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40705849,"author":"alexpotato","month":"2024-06","story":"Pop culture has become an oligopoly (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows everything in culture is a remix of earlier work","quote":"It highlights how essentially everything in culture is a remix of some previous version of the culture","text":"Anytime these discussions pop up, it's good to link to the documentary \"Everything is a Remix\" (here is the 2023 updated version: [VIDEO] \" ) It highlights how essentially everything in culture is a remix of some previous version of the culture (or some aspect of it). E.g. even language is largely a remix of earlier languages with some occasional creation of new words. Also, while true that there is definitely an oligopoly, it's also clear that the Long Tail is both huge AND easy to access. In the past, you could only see movies by going to the movie theater and there were only a limited number of theaters. Now, you can both create and consume media in niches as small (or combined) as you want."},{"id":38815882,"author":"tsumnia","month":"2023-12","story":"Things are about to get worse for generative AI","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary arguing culture is built on copying and remixing","quote":"It's a great video if you have an hour.","text":"When I cover generative AI in my Ethics in AI lecture, one of few soapbox opinions I give is that GenAI is doing essentially what people do - copy others. Picasso has a quote about \"Good Artists copy, Great Artists steal\", which doesn't mean try to pass Lario and Muigi off as your own, but rather that great artists are able to take aspects from other works (also called 'inspiration') without being caught. My personality is a combination of elements taken from Jim Carrey, Robin Williams, and King of the Deathmatch Mick Foley. I like making vector graphics based on pictures. I have a folder on my computer called \"Website Ideas\" that's just screenshots of UIs that I've come across that I really like. I also point to a YouTube video by Kirby Ferguson \"Everything is a Remix\" [1] which talks about how so much of our collective culture stems from copy. It's a great video if you have an hour. When Little Timmy crayons a copy of Mario, we congratulate him for his creativity. Is it unique, one of a kind art? Well Timmy made it, but he didn't think up the original idea of a video game plumber. I give this view to GenAI right now - it's not capable of achieving that \"next step\" in \"original design\", but its performing like a novice artist/musician, it's mimicking what it sees. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48601734,"author":"timschmidt","month":"2026-06","story":"Amateur may have cracked Linear A, a 120-year-old puzzle","stance":"reference","why":"Used to argue information is either all stolen or none is","quote":"Either all information is stolen, or none is.","text":"Everything is a Remix: [VIDEO] Either all information is stolen, or none is. Can't have it both ways."},{"id":41466843,"author":"ranger_danger","month":"2024-09","story":"AI Training is Copyright Infringement (under European law)","stance":"reference","why":"Argues derivative work is inspiration, as in remix culture","quote":"Since the derived work is merely inspired by the original, just like a human being uses inspiration to create new things","text":"From the article: > parts of the training data can be memorized in whole or in part by current generative models Humans do the same thing... >can therefore be generated again with suitable prompts by end users and thus reproduced Personal copies (even verbatim) are not disallowed in most countries AFAIK, the concern is regarding distribution which is a separate issue. I would have thought it would be fair use: [VIDEO] Since the derived work is merely inspired by the original, just like a human being uses inspiration to create new things, whether consciously or not (hence the video \"everything is a remix\"). The only debate IMO is just how much is being \"copied\" exactly, and how obvious it is."},{"id":40736192,"author":"timschmidt","month":"2024-06","story":"Swapping GNU coreutils for uutils coreutils on Gentoo Linux","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Nothing in permissive licenses prevents you from adding a useful feature and licensing the result under the GPL. If your fork is better for users, it'll catch on. I really appreciate the permissive licensing in the Rust ecosystem as it greatly eases the task of writing code for pay. While the finished product may have a commercial license, I often find bits to improve in the permissively licensed parts and contribute them back upstream. Customers seem perfectly fine with this arrangement. Tough to do the same with the GPL - even LGPL'd libraries complicate contract terms and distribution a little by comparison. With the huge productivity increase LLMs provide for writing code, it seems to me that we're rapidly entering an era in which libraries and tools for everything are available in every language, and under every license, which seems like a good thing. It is nice not to feel limited by one's language choice or work environment. I did a fair amount of work on the RepRap project, which is mostly GPL'd, and that worked out OK, but there have definitely been opportunities lost over the last 15 years or so due to license constraints which more permissive licenses would have allowed. Finding a balance which helps developers put food on the table while writing open source code also seems like a good thing. The GPL is great. I think there are important projects which really benefit from the strong incentive it provides to share. But there's definitely room for more than one way t"},{"id":42867266,"author":"ranger_danger","month":"2025-01","story":"OpenAI says it has evidence DeepSeek used its model to train competitor","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of Steve Jobs complaining to Bill Gates about MS \"stealing\" the GUI concept from them, which they in turn had stolen from Xerox. Obligatory \"Everything is a Remix\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rufnWLVQcKg",1],["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["wfYbgdo8e-8",1],["8dhp_20j0Ys",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["HUEvRyemKSg",1]]},"ZerUbHmuY04":{"wy":"Used as vivid evidence of crow intelligence and animal physical reasoning in discussions of cognition.","q":{"x":"This video is a startling verification.of the crows mental capabilities.","i":24587342,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24587342,"author":"boltefnovor","month":"2020-09","story":"Crows possess higher intelligence long thought a primarily human attribute","stance":"rec","why":"Footage verifying crows' causal reasoning with water displacement.","quote":"This video is a startling verification.of the crows mental capabilities.","text":"This video is a startling verification.of the crows mental capabilities. “Causal understanding of water displacement by a crow” [VIDEO]"},{"id":21772251,"author":"danmostudco","month":"2019-12","story":"Crows could be the smartest animal other than primates","stance":"rec","why":"Crow solving water displacement puzzle to reach food.","quote":"Crows are astoundingly intelligent.","text":"Crows are astoundingly intelligent. If you're having a slow day, a quick youtube of \"crows solving puzzles\" yields some great videos of them solving complicated tasks like water displacement to reach food[0]. I believe raccoons share this achievement. Additionally, there was a thread on here some time ago[1] by a Dutch Startup[2] about trying to teach crows to exchange cigarette butts for treats inside a tiny device place in parks, I always thought that was a funny idea if you could get the crows to teach one another. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11300970,"author":"lr4444lr","month":"2016-03","story":"Why Neuroscientists Need to Study the Crow","stance":"rec","why":"Crow demonstrating reasoning by raising water level to reach food.","quote":"The reasoning ability and executive function of birds, crows in particular, is astonishing.","text":"The reasoning ability and executive function of birds, crows in particular, is astonishing. Intelligence (certain kinds, at least) may be just as reliant on brain:body mass ratio as it is on overall brain size and complexity. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36702770,"author":"squarefoot","month":"2023-07","story":"She Steals Surfboards by the Seashore. She’s a Sea Otter","stance":"reference","why":"Example of crows using tools to solve problems.","quote":"Some animals learn to use tools to solve problems, for example crows [0].","text":"Some animals learn to use tools to solve problems, for example crows [0]. What if that otter was just using the board to travel longer distances (=> finding more food) by keeping nearby a safer place to rest on? 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34302488,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2023-01","story":"Causal understanding of water displacement by a crow (video, silent, 2014)","stance":"reference","why":"YouTube copy of the silent crow video, with paper abstract.","quote":"Video also available on YouTube (possibly with ads)","text":"Video also available on YouTube (possibly with ads): [VIDEO] Paper available here: [LINK] \"Using the Aesop's Fable Paradigm to Investigate Causal Understanding of Water Displacement by New Caledonian Crows\" Abstract: Understanding causal regularities in the world is a key feature of human cognition. However, the extent to which non-human animals are capable of causal understanding is not well understood. Here, we used the Aesop's fable paradigm – in which subjects drop stones into water to raise the water level and obtain an out of reach reward – to assess New Caledonian crows' causal understanding of water displacement. We found that crows preferentially dropped stones into a water-filled tube instead of a sand-filled tube; they dropped sinking objects rather than floating objects; solid objects rather than hollow objects, and they dropped objects into a tube with a high water level rather than a low one. However, they failed two more challenging tasks which required them to attend to the width of the tube, and to counter-intuitive causal cues in a U-shaped apparatus. Our results indicate that New Caledonian crows possess a sophisticated, but incomplete, understanding of the causal properties of displacement, rivalling that of 5–7 year old children."},{"id":24587858,"author":"sobani","month":"2020-09","story":"Crows possess higher intelligence long thought a primarily human attribute","stance":"reference","why":"Related experiment on crows and water displacement.","quote":"@boltefnovor just commented about a related experiment [0]","text":"@boltefnovor just commented about a related experiment [0] > “Causal understanding of water displacement by a crow” > [VIDEO] [0] [LINK]"},{"id":23427402,"author":"stared","month":"2020-06","story":"Why Is the Human Brain So Efficient? (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Compare and contrast with the brain of rats or Corvidae","text":"I wouldn't say that human brain is that efficient (per volume). Compare and contrast with the brain of rats or Corvidae: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":21677852,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Do animals grasp the concept of solid-liquid-gas?","stance":"reference","why":"Crow understanding water displacement as evidence of animal physical reasoning.","quote":"the first thing that came to me mind was","text":"It's a very interesting question. I guess big apes can, but I have no proof at all. Also, the extension of the question to the ice-water transition in the other comment is interesting too. Anyway, the first thing that came to me mind was \" Causal understanding of water displacement by a crow \" [VIDEO]"},{"id":17739461,"author":"throwawaymath","month":"2018-08","story":"'Intelligent' crows to pick up litter at French theme park","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I read somewhere about training crows to pickup cigarette butts in exchange food. I’ve always wondered if it would be possible to train a crow to pick up loose coins for food. Probably not. It probably seems like I'm shilling this researcher's blog in this thread at this point but this commentary [1] gives a short explanation of why. One of my hobbies is actually bird watching, particularly with crows. I love the animals, they're very fascinating creatures. I've had the opportunity to interact with many of them because I set out peanuts for them near my neighborhood. They've very gradually become acclimated to this routine, and they reliably arrive to pick up food if I make a particular noise and they're in the area. But I'm very skeptical that I could \"train\" them to start exchanging items for food. In captivity a crow's intelligence can be especially fostered and trained because there's no other source of food to distract it. If you want it to proceed through a puzzle [2] to get food it has to do so. But wild crows are very, very cautious and (like most animals) very economically rational in their feeding habits. To date none of the wild crows who give people items have been trained to do so, it's been a happy accident (it's never happened to me, unfortunately). If you start imposing an exchange system on the food dispensary, you might find that the crows simply go elsewhere for easier meals. _____________ 1. I read somewhere about training crows to pickup cigarette butts"},{"id":16984587,"author":"ozy","month":"2018-05","story":"Technically, what is Understanding?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Some animals clearly show understanding up to a level","text":"Some animals clearly show understanding up to a level [VIDEO] But they can not explain it to us, so \"can explain\" is a unnecessary high bar."},{"id":10121474,"author":"acqq","month":"2015-08","story":"A new Stephen Hawking presentation about black holes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"When you formulate it so, the problem is the question itself, as, except if we are religious believers, we don't have any reason to consider a \"soul\" (or a \"consciousness\") to be a \"nonphysical entity.\" Look at the etymology of the \"psyche:\" it's \"the breath.\" At the time people explained their environment unscientifically they considered that the existence of \"breath\" makes the difference between the dead and the living person but also between the conscious and the unconscious bodies. Animals do breathe too. But humans saw themselves as \"special\" in their religious setup. As a kid would ask \"does my dog go to heaven too when he dies?\" See, we go deeper and deeper in religious explanations which just postulate something non-existing (once you accept nonphysical soul, it's easier to believe in heaven or reincarnation or whatever). Now go back and ask, if you believe that the only thing we can \"see\" around us that doesn't have the \"physical entity\" is the \"soul\" of humans why do you do so? Are humans by any way \"the chosen ones\" in the animal world, looking at their physical bodies and the processes in them? The scientific view doesn't see them so. The human \"consciousness\" is, so observed, more complex than the \"consciousness\" of the crow on the lawn due to the more complex brain, but both of these can be explained as the function of their physical \"hardware\" (the body and the brain) and the interaction with the environment. The \"consciousness\" as a \"neurological process by wh"}]},"egEraZP9yXQ":{"wy":"Pointed to as a fun, light introduction to topology and to answer the human-holes question.","q":{"x":"If you'd like a fun, light, video introduction to this topic, this Vsauce video is worth a watch","i":25929876,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39555949,"author":"davkan","month":"2024-02","story":"'Entropy bagels' and other complex structures emerge from simple rules","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining, somewhat rudimentary introduction to topology","quote":"Vsauce has an interesting video regarding topology[0]. Probably a bit rudimentary for anyone already familiar with the math, but still entertaining","text":"Vsauce has an interesting video regarding topology[0]. Probably a bit rudimentary for anyone already familiar with the math, but still entertaining in the typical vsauce way. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27231453,"author":"dsamarin","month":"2021-05","story":"The anus is an evolutionary marvel","stance":"rec","why":"Explores how many holes a human has depending on what counts as a hole","quote":"Check out the video from VSauce on YouTube about how many holes a human has.","text":"It depends on the minimum size of the what you will consider a hole. Check out the video from VSauce on YouTube about how many holes a human has. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25929876,"author":"thearrow","month":"2021-01","story":"Topology 101: How Mathematicians Study Holes","stance":"rec","why":"Fun, light video introduction to topology and holes","quote":"If you'd like a fun, light, video introduction to this topic, this Vsauce video is worth a watch","text":"If you'd like a fun, light, video introduction to this topic, this Vsauce video is worth a watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42301564,"author":"725686","month":"2024-12","story":"Fish have a brain microbiome – could humans have one too?","stance":"reference","why":"Answers the question of how many holes a human has","quote":"Michael Stevens of VSauce fame has you covered with his \"How Many Holes Does a Human Have?\" video","text":"Michael Stevens of VSauce fame has you covered with his \"How Many Holes Does a Human Have?\" video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29879484,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2022-01","story":"Let's Settle This","stance":"reference","why":"Animations of rings morphing into a T-shaped pipe at given timestamps","quote":"The Vsauce video in sibling comment at 12:59 shows an animation from the two-rings to the T-shaped pipe","text":"The Vsauce video in sibling comment at 12:59 shows an animation from the two-rings to the T-shaped pipe, or even a better animation at 15:59. 12:59 [VIDEO] 15:59 [VIDEO]"},{"id":29134325,"author":"ravi-delia","month":"2021-11","story":"The Math of Hunting Lions","stance":"reference","why":"Shows humans have more holes than a donut topologically","quote":"As it turns out we humans have quite a few more holes than fleshy donuts would.","text":"This vsauce video is about humans, but I imagine pretty much every mammal will be just about topologically equivalent. As it turns out we humans have quite a few more holes than fleshy donuts would. [VIDEO]"}]},"f5SE47Xjx2Q":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear, compact explainer on China's empty homes, ghost cities and property troubles.","q":{"x":"it explains clearly (for a complex situation) what's been going on in China's real estate, the 'ghost cities' and the demolitions.","i":32647107,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32647107,"author":"8bitsrule","month":"2022-08","story":"Loan woes: China's troubled property market hits banks","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of China's property situation and ghost cities.","quote":"it explains clearly (for a complex situation) what's been going on in China's real estate, the 'ghost cities' and the demolitions.","text":"I was befuddled by what's been going on there. So earlier today I watched this [0], it explains clearly (for a complex situation) what's been going on in China's real estate, the 'ghost cities' and the demolitions. Below that,[1] a recent look at a 'ghost city' by two western bikers. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":22219222,"author":"Fjolsvith","month":"2020-02","story":"China to inject $174B of liquidity on Monday as markets reopen","stance":"rec","why":"Explains cultural reasons behind excess Chinese housing.","quote":"You might be surprised to learn why there's excess housing.","text":"You might be surprised to learn why there's excess housing. Young men who are courting a lady need two or three houses to have any chance of winning her hand: Why 50 Million Chinese Homes are Empty - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18936198,"author":"itchyjunk","month":"2019-01","story":"A Fifth of China’s Homes Are Empty (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Nice 12-minute explainer on China's empty homes.","quote":"I watched a really nice 12 mins video[0] on this topic only yesterday.","text":"I watched a really nice 12 mins video[0] on this topic only yesterday. I haven't fact checked it though. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18688967,"author":"kristofferR","month":"2018-12","story":"China’s Economy Slows Sharply","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why China builds empty housing.","quote":"","text":"China builds useless buildings/cities because it raises the GDP and because it's often the only viable way to raise funds for local governments. The Chinese people invest in useless buildings because it is often the only viable way to invest your savings. Some of the useless buildings built for buildings sake will potentially be filled as people move from rural areas, but a lot won't. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["DZTIbHIsIYw",1],["xQx-tuW9A4Q",1],["oaDTiWaYfcM",1]]},"ghtpJnrdgbo":{"wy":"Recommended for the SQLite author's reasoned case for a different approach to version control, and as an introduction to Fossil.","q":{"x":"If the author of SQLite advances some arguments around the proper way of doing version management, I would advise to error on the side of caution and listen at least a second time.","i":27738825,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27738825,"author":"belter","month":"2021-07","story":"Git vs. Fossil: what you should have done vs. what you did","stance":"rec","why":"SQLite author's arguments about proper version management","quote":"If the author of SQLite advances some arguments around the proper way of doing version management, I would advise to error on the side of caution and listen at least a second time.","text":"Here is a (somewhat old) talk by Richard Hipp, the author of SQLite about Git: \"Richard Hipp - Git: Just Say No\" [VIDEO] I use Git every day, but I am curious about Fossil and have been researching it for a while. I am not planning on changing, as I need to get to the bottom of some data corruption events I have seen in their forums. Not saying it was the fault of Fossil, just that it requires more research on my side. We should not forget Version Management did not start with Git but has a vibrant history that began with SCCS, RCS, CVS, ClearCase, Perforce, SVN, etc... As I used all the systems above except for SCCS and RCS I would advise for some caution here on thinking Git core flows are the one true way. I know the authors of ClearCase though the same way :-) If the author of SQLite advances some arguments around the proper way of doing version management, I would advise to error on the side of caution and listen at least a second time. Do not throw away your own experience and insight, but at least listen carefully. Don't just dismiss the arguments as quickly as some are doing here. I found this recent thread very useful not only as a features comparison but also as a core reminder of what the main use case for Fossil is: \"Lessons from 13+ Years of Fossil\" : [LINK] \"Fossil's reason for existence, plain and simple, is managing the source code for sqlite3. That's the scope of project it was initially envisioned for, and that size of project seems to be quite average for t"},{"id":22491629,"author":"brigandish","month":"2020-03","story":"Please stop recommending Gitflow","stance":"rec","why":"Compares Fossil with Git, keeping history without rebasing","quote":"It sounded nice - refreshing, even.","text":"> Take a look at this image and tell me it’s immediately intuitive I'm using Git, there's nothing intuitive to be found here. On a possibly related note, I watched a talk by D. Richard Hipp (I don't remember which one - edit: probably \"Richard Hipp - Git: Just Say No\"[1]) where he compared it with Git (a lot) and explained how Fossil keeps everything, there's no throwing away of history, no need for rebasing (at least as a workflow). It sounded nice - refreshing, even. I'm going to start using it for some projects and see how I get on, because frankly, I'm tired of Git and this need to strategise saving copies of my work , because that's basically what it does. Sometimes I even pine for the days I used to version zip files… [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22300842,"author":"asix66","month":"2020-02","story":"The History of Git","stance":"rec","why":"Hipp's git enhancements list and an introduction to Fossil","quote":"It's a good talk, and caused me to discover Fossil SCM.","text":"This reminds be of a talk I watched by Richard Hipp, \"Git: Just Say No\" [0] where he discusses a \"top 10 git enhancements\" list, as of the time (4 years ago.) It's a good talk, and caused me to discover Fossil SCM. Sadly I've yet to try/use Fossil, and my teams still use git. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["Wp7GAKLSGnI",1],["HaEPXoXVf2k",1],["QpAhX-gsHMs",1],["3LopI4YeC4I",1],["hMtu7vV_HmY",1],["cbSu2PXOTOc",1],["75Ju0eM5T2c",1],["48sCx-wBs34",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["d_zt3kGW1NM",1],["okg7uq_HrhQ",1]]},"pnv5iKB2hl4":{"wy":"The canonical reference on flag and logo design principles, cited in branding and design threads.","q":{"x":"one must watch the short talk on state and country flags","i":22319522,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":30206395,"author":"misterS","month":"2022-02","story":"Country Flags in SVG","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Seminal TED talk on flag design principles.","quote":"Roman taught me in his seminal TED talk","text":"> kids in first year of school should know how to draw flag from memory Roman Mars taught me that – well Ted Kaye, but Roman taught me in his seminal TED talk [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22319522,"author":"alpb","month":"2020-02","story":"State of Oklahoma's New Brand Identity / Logo","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short talk on good state and country flag design.","quote":"one must watch the short talk on state and country flags","text":"This is painful to see. It looks like a shopping mall or apartment building logo, compared to other states with proper flags. And it goes without saying, on this topic, one must watch the short talk on state and country flags: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22319606,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2020-02","story":"State of Oklahoma's New Brand Identity / Logo","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what makes a good flag, citing Chicago, DC and Amsterdam.","quote":"99% Invisible does a great episode on what makes a good flag","text":"Their flag is terrible: [LINK] 99% Invisible does a great episode on what makes a good flag, and why cities like Chicago, DC and Amsterdam have iconic, memorable flags: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":34890326,"author":"surprisetalk","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: How to protect blog from AI search?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is an extremely difficult question that I've been grappling with myself. My first strategy is to create writings that can't be summarized. I try to make essays that are dripping with idiosyncrasy. I can't win in an information war, but I can stay competitive for ~10 years in a creative clash: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] I myself draw lots of my inspiration from my \"sages of style\", in the same way that AIs will try to replicate my voice. None of these great thinkers can be summarized or replicated in any meaningful way: [6] David Foster Wallace: [LINK] [7] George Carlin: [LINK] [8] Derek Sivers: [LINK] [9] John Green: [LINK] [10] Roman Mars: [VIDEO] [11] Alan Kay: [LINK] [12] Alan Watts: [LINK] [13] Richard Feynman: [LINK] [14] Raymond Smullyan: [LINK] But style only takes you so far. My second strategy is to socialize IRL. This year, I'm starting a tech conference called \"Outland\" to foster community in an age where community might be all that matters. [15] [LINK]"},{"id":34327778,"author":"Someone","month":"2023-01","story":"New American City Flags Survey (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Basic flag design principles.","quote":"would think that’s “flag design 101","text":"American flag design continues to amaze with its ugliness. I’ve read [LINK] and watched [VIDEO] years ago, and would think that’s “flag design 101”, but it doesn’t look like few designers know that in the USA."},{"id":13425840,"author":"spaceribs","month":"2017-01","story":"Mozilla launches new brand identity","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on design philosophy and branding.","quote":"","text":"Rebranding, when done correctly, can be a great way to regroup and focus on what's important about your business. You really need to stop looking at it as just a new design, and look at what philosophy it exudes. This constant visual reminder of what the company's mission is resonates downward much better than a mission statement, and how clients/customers interact with you. This is of course if your rebrand was a success, and that's a very very difficult task dependent on all sorts of internal factors, that even the largest companies get wrong (see Gap, Uber, Pepsi). Don't listen to me though: [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1],["uI1eRy0uBI8",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1],["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["-HFwok9SlQQ",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1],["1rCrfQUcXDI",1],["FzRP1qdwPKw",1]]},"0GzzFeS5cMc":{"wy":"Recommended as a great talk on live, functional-style game programming.","q":{"x":"here is his presentation on this subject at the last Conj (great talk)","i":8940734,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":8940734,"author":"zach","month":"2015-01","story":"Code, Eval, Play, Loop – Common Lisp OpenGL Environment","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk on game programming in a functional style.","quote":"here is his presentation on this subject at the last Conj (great talk)","text":"For Clojure enthusiasts, I highly recomment Zach Oakes' environment in the same vein, Nightmod (his Nightcode IDE specialized with his play-clj library). It's an experimental platform that is a tidy, simple way to experience game programming in a functional style. [LINK] Also, here is his presentation on this subject at the last Conj (great talk): [VIDEO]"},{"id":10949523,"author":"ronjouch","month":"2016-01","story":"Clojure is for Type B personalities","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on making games at runtime with Clojure.","quote":"his \"Making Games at Runtime with Clojure\" talk [2] is great.","text":"If you're interested in Clojure, the gist author (Zach Oakes) maintains a few cool projects [1]: - play-clj, a Clojure game library - Nightcode, an IDE for Clojure - Nightweb, an anonymous P2P social network in Clojure and (non-Clojure) - SolidOak, an IDE for Rust Also, his \"Making Games at Runtime with Clojure\" talk [2] is great. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14323661,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2017-05","story":"Ask HN: What are some tips for making my first tech talk a success?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Assuming your audience are beginners (as it is an intro talk), I find it immensely useful when tech talks have slides that follow this kind of pattern: - Problem/Feature/Reason why it's useful - Code - Demo of the problem/feature/reason working (whether recorded or live) Doing a bunch of live demos is real risky for a first tech talk, but if you practice (I mean really practice, with network access turned off for example), and know your stuff, it's possible. If you're game to do a demo, I think one of the most important things you can demo is going 0-100 with a small kotlin app on Android. This will require you to make some choices that your audience would normally make themselves (like how to develop the app, not everyone uses android studio), but if Kotlin for Android Development is actually valuable, it should be self-evident from watching it used (you may have to throw in some problems that you solve by writing idiomatic Kotlin, and explain why they would look worse in Java or whatever else). Also you might want to include these slides (probably just one so people don't lose focus): - Options for native android development (to set up the next slide), you might briefly explain things like React Native, Go for android, Java, Scala, Clojure. Hard to keep this to one slide but it is valuable because it makes the person watching feel like they know the landscape, at least at a high level, and motivates the talk. - What/Who/When/Why for Kotlin on Android Here's an excellent tal"}],"r":[["HaEPXoXVf2k",1]]},"0w_xEUoK79o":{"wy":"Cited as a showcase of what fits in 4k, in discussions of code size limits and the demoscene.","q":{"x":"So then I'm going to post my favorite 4k [1], which even if you ignore the file size, is just a very cool use of fractals.","i":39123963,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39123963,"author":"12_throw_away","month":"2024-01","story":"We made an animated movie in 8kB","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite 4k demo, a cool use of fractals.","quote":"So then I'm going to post my favorite 4k [1], which even if you ignore the file size, is just a very cool use of fractals.","text":"So then I'm going to post my favorite 4k [1], which even if you ignore the file size, is just a very cool use of fractals. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5509737,"author":"xal","month":"2013-04","story":"Hello from a libc-free world","stance":"rec","why":"Stunning recent 4k demoscene productions.","quote":"The most recent 4ks are nothing short of stunning","text":"Sadly, I don't have any of my own work anymore, although I'm sure I could write out a few procedural texture generators from top of my head. Luckily, the demo scene has collectively decided to go back and open source much of it's best work recently: [LINK] The most recent 4ks are nothing short of stunning: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30265695,"author":"Bayart","month":"2022-02","story":"MadMax: Analyzing the Out-of-Gas World of Smart Contracts (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Demoscene as parallel to blockchain size constraints.","quote":"Blockchain constraints are eerily reminiscent of the demoscene","text":"Related to that, people have started using old procedural generation tricks to store \"large\" amounts of data on-chain. For example, as far as I understand it, Aavegotchi [1] stores base layer SVGs, metadata and functions to use these and render the actual end-product at view-time. Blockchain constraints are eerily reminiscent of the demoscene [2][3][4] (just using the opportunity to share stuff I like, don't mind it). [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13618089,"author":"titzer","month":"2017-02","story":"From Philosophy to Program Size (2003)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows what fits in 4k, countering claims about megabyte pages.","quote":"Have you seen 4k demo contests, btw?","text":"I understand your argument, but even then I don't think it's quite right; there is lots of room to improve on the size of a webpage vs its rendered pixels on the screen, even with the fancy fonts and gradients and animations. I still don't believe it's really megabytes. Have you seen 4k demo contests, btw? [VIDEO]"},{"id":11744892,"author":"userbinator","month":"2016-05","story":"Rendering a Buddhabrot at 4K and Other Bad Ideas","stance":"reference","why":"Example of realtime fractal rendering in a 4k demo.","quote":"realtime fractal rendering is a pretty common effect in the demoscene and definitely doable in that size category","text":"I almost read the title as about rendering it in a 4KB binary - realtime fractal rendering is a pretty common effect in the demoscene and definitely doable in that size category. Here are some examples: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":3958787,"author":"sp332","month":"2012-05","story":"Infinite complexity in 4096 bytes","stance":"reference","why":"A 4k intro demo, uploaded in high resolution.","quote":"It was uploaded in 2048x1526 resolution","text":"This one? [VIDEO] It was uploaded in 2048x1526 resolution, so anything less than that will be downsampled by Youtube."}]},"DXYje2B04xE":{"wy":"Recommended as part of a channel with deep teardowns of extreme test equipment, valuable for anyone interested in electronics or RF.","q":{"x":"If you even have a passing interest in electronics or radio, I can't recommend his channel enough.","i":24673704,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24673704,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-10","story":"Tearing into a $1.3M Oscilloscope","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Teardowns of complex, expensive test equipment with a 128GHz FFT demo.","quote":"If you even have a passing interest in electronics or radio, I can't recommend his channel enough.","text":"I knew this was going to be Shahriar from TheSignalPath. If you even have a passing interest in electronics or radio, I can't recommend his channel enough. He's regularly doing teardowns and even repairs on remarkably complex (and expensive!) test equipment from the past few decades. RF is weird and you'll see some complex and alien physical configurations in the equipment that many times look like dark arts. He puts a lot of effort into his videos and the scenarios he creates to demonstrate the power that this technology brings to the table. This 128GHz FFT is insane. I'm honestly a little surprised that this doesn't have ITAR regulations on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21430408,"author":"0j","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: Favorite YouTube channels under a million subscribers","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite small channel on RF electronics.","quote":"The Signal Path - RF electronics - 66k","text":"BPS.space - Model rockets - 133k - [LINK] OskarPuzzle - 3D printed puzzles - 110k - [LINK] The Signal Path - RF electronics - 66k - [VIDEO]"},{"id":32429191,"author":"tverbeure","month":"2022-08","story":"From Oscilloscope to Wireshark: A UDP Story","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown of 110GHz Keysight UXR oscilloscope.","quote":"The Signal Path has a teardown of the Keysight UXR oscilloscope, which has a BW of 110GHz","text":"The Signal Path has a teardown of the Keysight UXR oscilloscope, which has a BW of 110GHz and samples 4 channels at 256Gbps/channel, with 10bit resolution: [VIDEO] ."}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1]]},"HlAXp0-M6SY":{"wy":"HN recommends it for Hightower's clear mental models, such as comparing the scheduler to Tetris and explaining why resource limits matter.","q":{"x":"Kelsey Hightower comparing the kubernetes scheduler to Tetris was awesome for cementing a mental model.","i":31784912,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31784912,"author":"leetrout","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best dev tool pitches of all time?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hightower compares the Kubernetes scheduler to Tetris, building a mental model.","quote":"Kelsey Hightower comparing the kubernetes scheduler to Tetris was awesome for cementing a mental model.","text":"Kelsey Hightower comparing the kubernetes scheduler to Tetris was awesome for cementing a mental model. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15954681,"author":"org3432","month":"2017-12","story":"Kubernetes 1.9: Apps Workloads GA and Expanded Ecosystem","stance":"rec","why":"Hightower explains Kubernetes models and how they differ from traditional approaches.","quote":"Watch tutorials by Kelsey Hightower, he's been helping folks understand the differences and the models.","text":"Watch tutorials by Kelsey Hightower, he's been helping folks understand the differences and the models. e.g. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22098365,"author":"deadbunny","month":"2020-01","story":"Technical Debt Is Like a Tetris Game","stance":"reference","why":"Hightower's Tetris analogy for resource allocation.","quote":"Kelsy Hightower has used a similar analogy in regards to resource allocation","text":"Kelsy Hightower has used a similar analogy in regards to resource allocation which covers a little on the tech debt side of things. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14454208,"author":"jcastro","month":"2017-05","story":"Kubernetes by Example","stance":"reference","why":"Kelsey Hightower's advice on why setting resource limits is a good thing.","quote":"There's a great example on why setting limits is a good thing from one of Kelsey Hightower's talks","text":"There's a great example on why setting limits is a good thing from one of Kelsey Hightower's talks, for some reason your initial comment triggered remembering this advice: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["B1J2RMorJXM",1],["lJ8ydIuPFeU",1]]},"QLUIExAnNcE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear explanation of a mind-blowingly simple power-split hybrid design.","q":{"x":"Some of these designs are mind-blowingly simple. My favorite is Honda's E-drive","i":39760566,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39760566,"author":"kube-system","month":"2024-03","story":"Dual-Clutch Transmission: Wet or Dry?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains Honda's simple e-drive power-split hybrid design.","quote":"Some of these designs are mind-blowingly simple. My favorite is Honda's E-drive","text":"I wish more people knew how the power-split devices in modern hybrids worked. The 'eCVT' and similar terminology they often get just doesn't do it justice. They're a beautiful design, and entirely different than 'CVTs' as most people know them. Some of these designs are mind-blowingly simple. My favorite is Honda's E-drive: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44670086,"author":"graton","month":"2025-07","story":"Electric cars produce less brake dust pollution than combustion-engine cars","stance":"rec","why":"More detail on how Honda hybrids drive the wheels.","quote":"has more info.","text":"Current Honda hybrids are like that. The only time the Internal Combustion Engine (ICE) is directly connected to the drive train is when the speed gets to above around 60MPH. The rest of the time the ICE is used to power a generator which then sends power to the electric motor and/or the battery pack. [VIDEO] has more info."},{"id":42315008,"author":"CRConrad","month":"2024-12","story":"First Geared CVT [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Another WeberAuto video on the similar Honda hybrid system.","quote":"Another one from WeberAuto like in my parent comment, this time on the (apparently very similar) Honda system","text":"More YouTube videos about the eCVT... 1) Better -- much simpler, and above all shorter -- video explanation of the Toyota system: [LINK] . 2) Another, a little longer, but very pedagogical and beautifully animated explanation: [LINK] . Eight years old, but apparently the \"power split device\" worked the same even then. Bonus -- short conversation in the comments, arriving at the same conclusion I did above: @TimpBizkit 3 weeks ago I thought the prius had a cvt 1 reply @aspecreviews 3 weeks ago It doesn't. The \"CVT\" is electrically simulated. Heh, yeah. 3) Titled something about \"Launch Gear\", but actually on the whole CVT thing (from a channel I semi-regularly watch): [LINK] . 4) A good basic look-through of the various types of CVT and their history: [LINK] . 5) Long and thorough explanation of the Prius powertrain: [LINK] . Uses a cutaway of the actual transmission, so good for getting a more concrete picture than e.g. #2 above. But... Long. 6) Another one from WeberAuto like in my parent comment, this time on the (apparently very similar) Honda system: [VIDEO] . So likely to be about as good as the previous one, but... 25 minutes more of this? Naah, I've had enough. There were also some from Scotty Kilmer et al, but... The less said, the better. The ones I'd most recommend are #4 for general CVT stuff and #2 for the Prius e-CVT's operating principle specifically. Well well, all in all that was quite the rabbit hole."}],"r":[["thqbjA2DC-E",1]]},"RNsEsZbXE-4":{"wy":"HN points to it as a fun, technically deep talk that makes memory-safety bugs vivid through a beloved game.","q":{"x":"I really enjoyed the RustConf 2020 Closing Keynote","i":25537492,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":31896093,"author":"Meekro","month":"2022-06","story":"Super Mario Bros warp zones were intended to work slightly differently [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth technical discussion of Pokemon Missingno glitch.","quote":"I highly recommend this pokemon/missingno discussion","text":"I loved the in-depth technical discussion in this video. If you're into this sort of thing, I highly recommend this pokemon/missingno discussion: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25537492,"author":"jtdressel","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Missingno glitches and how memory-safe languages prevent them.","quote":"I really enjoyed the RustConf 2020 Closing Keynote","text":"I really enjoyed the RustConf 2020 Closing Keynote: [VIDEO] It details how the Poikemon missingo glitches work. (In the context of how memory safe languages would prevent them)"},{"id":24458054,"author":"xiaodai","month":"2020-09","story":"Super Mario Bros. 3 in 3 Minutes – World Record Speedrun Explained [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote on Pokemon Blue glitches.","quote":"reminds of this keynote on how to get the best pokemon and cheat in pokemon blue!","text":"one of the best nerd food of all time. reminds of this keynote on how to get the best pokemon and cheat in pokemon blue! [VIDEO]"}]},"pqQSwQLDIK8":{"wy":"Cited in urbanism and self-driving threads as a vivid example of dense mixed traffic working through infrastructure and culture.","q":{"x":"Watching that video sooths my mind and warms my heart","i":49065278,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49065278,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-07","story":"Park by Robot at London Gatwick Airport","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nine minutes of calming mixed traffic proving harmonious shared space.","quote":"Watching that video sooths my mind and warms my heart","text":"The therapy that Juleiie needs begins by watching nine minutes of mixed car, bike, and pedestrian rush hour traffic at the intersection of Korte Prinsengracht and Haarlemmerstraat in Amsterdam. Unbelievably busy bicycle crossing in Amsterdam: [VIDEO] Watching that video sooths my mind and warms my heart by proving how well a society of people who have not literally driven themselves mentally ill by viciously driving around in environmentally and economically destructive metal tanks can harmoniously and heathily get along together without fearing and murdering one another, by simply looking each other in the eyes and acknowledging that they all exist in the same shared space together. The famous Dutch traffic engineer Hans Monderman believed drivers become more alert and cautious when there’s more uncertainty on the road. Want fewer car accidents? Remove traffic signals and road signs: [LINK] >Hans Monderman believed that societies could make roads safer by making drivers more uncertain, and therefore alert. >“When you treat people like idiots, they’ll behave like idiots.” >That was the philosophy of Hans Monderman, the Dutch traffic engineer who became famous not for what he added to road design and urban planning, but for what he removed: curbs, traffic signals, signs. He believed drivers become more alert and cautious when there’s more uncertainty on the road. >“The trouble with traffic engineers is that when there’s a problem with a road, they always try to add something,”"},{"id":26510904,"author":"sjaak","month":"2021-03","story":"It's Never a Bicycle Accident","stance":"rec","why":"Shows cyclists dominating roads in the Netherlands.","quote":"Cyclists own the roads here, see for yourself","text":"To all the outlawed cyclists I'd like to say: show your fellow countrymen how it's done in the salvation state of the Netherlands. Cyclists own the roads here, see for yourself: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46013698,"author":"Mawr","month":"2025-11","story":"California DMV approves map increase in Waymo driverless operations","stance":"reference","why":"Existing bike-friendly traffic that already works without red lights.","quote":"Here's a 7 year old video","text":"> There will be no red lights, no parking lots, less congestion, fewer accidents. In 20 years? Here's a 7 year old video: [VIDEO] Also, you forgot to mention the silence, nearly zero cost infrastructure, nearly zero environmental impact, and immense population-wide health benefits—and therefore healthcare cost savings."},{"id":46089452,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2025-11","story":"Copenhagenize Index 2025: The Global Ranking of Bicycle-Friendly Cities","stance":"reference","why":"Intersection showing how local culture shapes traffic safety.","quote":"showing how much the local culture affects the traffic safety and bicycle friendliness","text":"On my first visit to Amsterdam, my friend picked me up on a bike at Centraal Station, and I rode to his apartment in the traditional Dutch style sitting on the back rack. On the way said \"only three more mountains to go till we're home\"! I asked \"WTF?\" and he explained that's what they call the bridges over the canals. This is one of the intersections we went through right after one of the mountains, showing how much the local culture affects the traffic safety and bicycle friendliness as much as the geography: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43696211,"author":"Mawr","month":"2025-04","story":"Air pollution fell substantially as Paris restricted car traffic","stance":"reference","why":"Meme-famous footage of Dutch cycling in busy city.","quote":"A meme you say?","text":"A meme you say? [VIDEO] As for the excuses: > they are a small What does a size of a country have to do with how their cities are designed? > dense Same as above, unless you mean city density in which case there's nothing special about Dutch cities. > flat Yes, and? > an oceanic weather Yeah, it rains all the time and yet they cycle."},{"id":38271468,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-11","story":"Vehicles with higher, more vertical front ends pose greater risk to pedestrians","stance":"reference","why":"Shows background noise levels on a very busy Amsterdam street.","quote":"A very busy mixed-use street in Amsterdam","text":"You're comparing living in a car dependant city vs living in car dependant suburbs. It's not going to be meaningfully different. You mention the constant noise, but what do you think causes it? To see what everyone means by car dependency, you'd need to see the few places that aren't. Here's what a 100% car-free island looks like: [LINK] Here are some progressive European cities that have started to undo the damage that's been done by cars: • Freiburg, Germany: [LINK] • Haarlem, Netherlands: [LINK] • Paris, France: [LINK] Check out these background noise levels: • A very busy mixed-use street in Amsterdam: [VIDEO] • Exiting the train station in Delft, Netherlands: [LINK] • Cycling streets in the Netherlands: [LINK] . You can even hear birds chirping at 4:29."},{"id":38057294,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-10","story":"Cruise pauses driverless operations across all cities","stance":"reference","why":"Shows bicycles work when infrastructure supports them.","quote":"Entirely dependent on the infrastructure","text":"> A bicycle is trash (fun to ride, not that useful). Entirely dependent on the infrastructure: [VIDEO] > What are you going to carry on your bike? Five bags? Seems like a lot of groceries to buy at a time, but if you really want to: [LINK] > I don’t want to go 5 miles away, I want to go 200. You cover 200 miles every day? [LINK] Regardless, you can own a car for those longer journeys just fine and still make most trips by bicycle. > With bikes and walking, your world is small. Yeah, that's why public transport is important too. Cars as well, although deprioritized. > Electric cars powered by renewable energy get rid of most of the negative externalities. They get rid of some of the noise and almost all of the harmful exhaust. That's about it. They're still 2 ton+ massive steel cages that are incredibly dangerous to everyone else, take up a ridiculous amount of space, and require extremely expensive infrastructure. As for the rest, sure, I'm not really criticising your particular choices, it sounds like you live in a rural area where a car is necessary anyway. However, there's no way for everybody to be able to live like you do. There are just too many people. We need to live with long-term sustainability in mind."},{"id":36942961,"author":"screye","month":"2023-07","story":"The New York Times attacks e-bikes while ignoring the real danger all around us","stance":"reference","why":"Example of traffic flowing without waits.","quote":"avoid waits as much as possible, let actions happen async","text":"> subtractive solutions and don’t “grow the GDP” like brand new EVs Maybe GDP is a bad metric. In any other field, a metric this naïve would be laughed out the door. If GDP is trying to capture net effective productivity, then let's stop using a metric that's 2 degrees of aggregated abstraction away from this metric of interest. Cars are middlemen. They allow labor to reach a location that performs a productive activity. They produce no productivity of their own. IE. Cars are network IO. Whichever transport method maximizes throughput and minimizes latency for the least cost is ideal. Any decent engineer knows that the key to better network IO is to: * improve locality - (keep things near each other) * batch things together - (buses and trains) * reduce size of individual packets - (cycles instead of huge cars) * no lossy transmission - (maintain worker productivity, stave off obesity) * avoid waits as much as possible, let actions happen async ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":35238721,"author":"em500","month":"2023-03","story":"Cyclists Now Outnumber Motorists in City of London","stance":"reference","why":"Shows few helmets among Amsterdam cyclists.","quote":"Try to count the number of helmets you can spot","text":"Try to count the number of helmets you can spot: [VIDEO] [LINK] In Amsterdam if we see adult cyclists with a helmet we'd guess it's probably a tourist or new expat. For children most parents only bother with helmets until they're six years or so."},{"id":32577701,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-08","story":"Things people blamed on bicycles","stance":"reference","why":"Busy Amsterdam crossing cited in Monderman shared space list.","quote":"Unbelievably busy bicycle crossing in Amsterdam: The Netherlands, Amsterdam, May 8, 2018","text":"Hans Monderman was the world's most famous traffic engineer! ;) [LINK] >Hans Monderman (19 November 1945 – 7 January 2008) was a Dutch road traffic engineer and innovator. He was recognised for radically challenging the criteria used to evaluate engineering solutions for street design. His work compelled transportation planners and highway engineers to look afresh at the way people and technology relate to each other. [LINK] >de Kaden shared space.wmv: Shared space traffic intersection in Drachten, The Netherlands. Traffic signals removed in 2002. The junction handles around 17,000 vehicles per day. One of many projects led by the late Hans Monderman. [LINK] >Shared space intersection, Drachten - The Netherlands 3,824 views Apr 29, 2009 Drachten, Drift/De Kaden, shared-space-intersection. From the centre of expertise on bicycle policy: [LINK] . The intersection Drift and De Kaden is an example of shared space in the purest sense. Despite a quite high traffic volume hardly any measures have been taken to regulate traffic. [VIDEO] >Unbelievably busy bicycle crossing in Amsterdam: The Netherlands, Amsterdam, May 8, 2018 Bicycle and car traffic at the crossing Korte Prinsengracht and Haarlemmerstraat. [LINK] >Hans Monderman: Rethinking the design of streets and public space. >An Urban Design London and Urban Design Group Lecture Hans Monderman has been one of the most significant influences on the current debate about the design of streets and spaces in the UK. A traffic engineer"}],"r":[["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["helScS3coAE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["8Gv0H-vPoDc",1],["wpOxbesRNBc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["1iAJPoc23-M",1],["Jd3-eiid-Uw",1],["ZSRHeXYDLko",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1]]},"6Af6b_wyiwI":{"wy":"During COVID-19 HN commenters cited it as a prescient pandemic warning.","q":{"x":"It's impressive how the details are similar to coronavirus.","i":22603488,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22748825,"author":"dgritsko","month":"2020-04","story":"Bill Gates calls for nationwide shutdown","stance":"rec","why":"Prescient talk on global pandemic risk and preparedness.","quote":"Gates gave a prescient TED talk in 2015","text":"Gates gave a prescient TED talk in 2015 [1] in which he describes the risk of a global pandemic and the general lack of preparedness. The Gates foundation has also been working for years to help eradicate diseases like polio and malaria [2][3]. Given his expertise in this area, it would seem worthwhile to listen to what he has to say rather than dismissing him as just a \"billionaire industrialist.\" [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":22603488,"author":"digaozao","month":"2020-03","story":"Climate change: The rich are to blame","stance":"rec","why":"2015 warning about pandemic unpreparedness.","quote":"It's impressive how the details are similar to coronavirus.","text":"We are not prepared, but there was people warning about it. See this Ted talk from Bill Gates, 5 years ago. It's impressive how the details are similar to coronavirus. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26791987,"author":"nindalf","month":"2021-04","story":"Just Be Rich","stance":"reference","why":"Sarcastically cites Gates's 2015 pandemic warning.","quote":"","text":"Yeah Bill Gates - terrible guy. Spending money trying to eradicate diseases that kill poor people. What's worse, here's him in 2015 ( [VIDEO] ) wasting money and faffing on about some pandemic. As if that's going to happen, and even if it does the pandemic won't affect rich countries in any way."},{"id":24045945,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2020-08","story":"One-third of New York’s small businesses may be gone forever","stance":"reference","why":"Example of executive concern about pandemics years ago.","quote":"Even software executives have suggested concern, this example from 5 years ago","text":"\"Global Catastrophic Risk Survey\", 2008 [pdf] [LINK] Includes discussion of a \"biggest natural pandemic\". Posted to HN, if not discussed, on Oct 2, 2016. [LINK] The question of GCR has been raised numerous other times on HN, though admittedly more recently of late: [LINK] Infectious disease features prominantly among discussions of GCR: [LINK] Even software executives have suggested concern, this example from 5 years ago: [VIDEO] As has the US military, from 2017: [LINK] Economic impacts of pandemic have seen significant scholarly treatment, here looking at 1990-2018: [LINK] Less black swan than head-buried ostrich, it might appear."},{"id":22748726,"author":"Cantbekhan","month":"2020-04","story":"Bill Gates calls for nationwide shutdown","stance":"reference","why":"Gates warned of pandemic years earlier.","quote":"He totally saw this coming from a mile away and actively tried to warn the world about it.","text":"He totally saw this coming from a mile away and actively tried to warn the world about it. Years ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22647794,"author":"Reedx","month":"2020-03","story":"I’m Bill Gates. AMA about Covid-19","stance":"reference","why":"Gates warned of pandemics in 2015.","quote":"See his TED talk from 2015","text":"It's not really about wealth, though. No one is asking or cares what the Walton heirs would have to say. It's the fact that Bill is smarter than the people in charge of our institutions. And he has a lot of experience in this domain. And has been warning about exactly this problem for many years. Indeed he warned that pandemics were our biggest blindspot and threat. E.g., See his TED talk from 2015: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22698026,"author":"Reedx","month":"2020-03","story":"How H-E-B planned for the pandemic","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of advance warning on pandemic preparedness.","quote":"","text":"The failure goes well beyond just the federal government too. One example being NYC leaders and even health officials encouraging New Yorkers go participate in parades and other large gatherings through Feb and even into March, with messages of defiance against Coronavirus concerns: [LINK] Edit: Removed ventilator comment as that specific claim seems to be misleading, and it's not something particular to NY. Many governments weren't prepared for a surge. The U.S. as a whole had plenty of advanced warning about ventilator[1] and pandemic preparedness[2]. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22724323,"author":"manicdee","month":"2020-03","story":"California once had mobile hospitals, ventilator stockpile but dismantled them","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as warning that pandemics were likely.","quote":"","text":"We’ve known that pandemics were going to get more likely as international travel got faster and cheaper, and population density rose and absolute population numbers rose. These factors have been part of the horror stories that epidemiologists tell each other late at night when the lights are out: “so it was a normal year, and airlines reduced the cost of India to USA flights by 20%,” horrified gasps (the audience knows where this story is going) Pandemic might have seemed improbable to you as a layperson, but the medical community has been bracing for this for decades. The less seriously you take it, the less seriously your politicians take it and the less seriously pandemic preparation gets funded. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["f84n5oFoZBc",1],["v0YEaeIClKY",1]]},"6K8NkJpUei4":{"wy":"Shared as an early long-form Musk interview, notably for his answer about what motivates him.","q":{"x":"His answer on motivation was striking to me","i":4026642,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4430951,"author":"MikeCapone","month":"2012-08","story":"Elon Musk Wins 2012 Popular Mechanics Breakthrough Leadership Award","stance":"rec","why":"Another Musk video worth catching","quote":"A couple more Musk videos for those who might have missed them when they first came out","text":"A couple more Musk videos for those who might have missed them when they first came out: Bloomberg profile that gives a good overview of his companies: [LINK] Reddit AMA: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4026642,"author":"6ren","month":"2012-05","story":"Space Station grabs SpaceX Dragon ship","stance":"rec","why":"Musk's striking answer on motivation","quote":"His answer on motivation was striking to me","text":"It's pretty amazing that the (possibly) most accomplished human did a reddit AMA: [LINK] His answer on motivation was striking to me: [VIDEO] My drive to get it done is somewhat disconnected from hope, enthusiasm or anything else. I just... I... I actually don't care about hope or enthusiasm... motivation. I just give it all I've got irrespective of what the circumstances may be. [...] Yeah... You just keep going and get it done."},{"id":3069656,"author":"Empact","month":"2011-10","story":"First Ride in Tesla Model S","stance":"reference","why":"Musk on plans for a low-price car","quote":"","text":"In his reddit IAMA he says the plan has always been for the 3rd-generation (post-S) car to be a high-volume, low-price car. Based on the time between the Roadster and S releases, it will probably be a few years before they get around to that. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["k2h2lvhzMDc",1],["QgbLb6QCK88",1],["AZR0UKxNPh8",1]]},"Bz2-49q6DOI":{"wy":"HN people share it as a funny and apt take whenever job postings or recruiters demand passion.","q":{"x":"David Mitchell had a very good take on it.","i":13587425,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13587425,"author":"lacampbell","month":"2017-02","story":"You don't have to be a passionate programmer","stance":"rec","why":"Good take on the passion demand in jobs","quote":"David Mitchell had a very good take on it.","text":"The \"passion\" thing is not unique to the software industry. David Mitchell had a very good take on it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7628209,"author":"jmagoon","month":"2014-04","story":"How to be a great software developer","stance":"rec","why":"Satirical take on recruiters demanding passion","quote":"David Mitchell's take","text":"Can we also add \"Ninja\" and \"Genius\" to the list of abused recruitment buzzwords? And don't even get me started on \"passion\". (David Mitchell's take: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":8599571,"author":"solarmist","month":"2014-11","story":"Rejected for a Job at the Container Store","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as supporting commentary on fake enthusiasm at work","quote":"all this fake enthusiasm is annoying. Most people are there for one reason to make money","text":"I call bullshit. Especially because this is a temporary position, either way in a few months she would be gone. And if there's an attitude problem she could be fired. I've had plenty of jobs that were beneath me and I hated (I even worked at Taco Bell for a season as a graduate student), I did them and did them well then left when I felt it was time to move on, no harm no foul. And this includes computer science and programming jobs. Yes I enjoy programming and engineering and generally like my job, but all this fake enthusiasm is annoying. Most people are there for one reason to make money, either now or in the future. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8902924,"author":"Terr_","month":"2015-01","story":"What Doesn't Seem Like Work?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant satire clip","text":"> \"we're looking for people who are passionate about [thing that literally no-one has ever been passionate about]\" Relevant satire clip: [VIDEO]"}]},"DoA4Txr4GUs":{"wy":"The standard pointer for anyone asking how to organize larger Elm codebases.","q":{"x":"Just watch rfeldman's talks about how to structure large Elm projects","i":30825654,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30825654,"author":"hombre_fatal","month":"2022-03","story":"Tao of Node – Design, Architecture and Best Practices","stance":"rec","why":"How to structure large Elm projects.","quote":"Just watch rfeldman's talks about how to structure large Elm projects","text":"You can write this sort of guide for literally any tech, even the ones you think are better. I've been using Elm since it came out and I use it for everything, but you could write a \"Tao of Elm\" that's just as in-depth as this. Just watch rfeldman's talks about how to structure large Elm projects ( [VIDEO] ) or watch the community chat about how to do it, it's an open question. For example, the Tao of Elm would give pointers on what you should send over ports, how granular you should even make ports, how to pick Msg types (LogIn User | LogOut vs. SetUser User), how to build a good Model, when to nest models, (a big one:) how to build parent/child communication, what should be implemented on the JS side instead of Elm side, how to represent fetch/wait/response loop when making http requests, etc. and then the trade-offs of all of those suggestions. Being able to compile a list of best practices doesn't mean something sucks. Though the comparison to Elm isn't all that great because it doesn't even compete with Node, and if you do use it on the server with Elm.worker, you could write a whole other guide of best practices around that. Anyone who has built a massive Elm client is shimmering with trail/error knowledge that they could share in this kind of format. Anyways, be wary of developing a knee-jerk \"JS sucks\" response."},{"id":19301155,"author":"z5h","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: Anyone Using Elm in Production?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on structuring and scaling Elm apps.","quote":"You'll probably want to watch Richard Feldman's","text":"You'll probably want to watch Richard Feldman's [Scaling Elm Apps]( [VIDEO] ). I built [Component Result]( [LINK] ) to help with a pattern that occurs when scaling up apps. I'm not clear what you mean by or expect to be problematic with by cross-cutting concerns. Feel free to ping me, z5h, on the Elm slack."},{"id":25095821,"author":"rtfeldman","month":"2020-11","story":"How I write Elm applications","stance":"reference","why":"Author's talk on when to nest Elm messages.","quote":"For example, I gave a whole talk that could have been titled","text":"From early in the article: > This [flat Msg design] does work, but at some point it becomes cumbersome to support a large number of constructors. As guessed, I'd personally stick with the simpler flat approach the article acknowledges and then tries to improve on. Nesting that data structure is (according to the article) an ergonomics improvement, but then the rest of the article is about how to solve ergonomics problems caused by the very nesting that was supposed to make things nicer! Given that, surely it's reasonable to raise the question of whether this nesting was actually an improvement after all. My conclusion at the end of the article is that in retrospect nesting did more harm than good, and knowing that, I would have happily left it flat. Incidentally, \"leave it flat\" is not my stance in all cases. For example, I gave a whole talk that could have been titled \"when to nest\" - [VIDEO] - and my most popular Elm project uses nesting in multiple places - [LINK] But indeed, in this case, I'd happily leave Msg flat and not bother with all the lens stuff!"}],"r":[["aMcjxSThD54",1],["LZPKTaVB1IU",1],["LQRAfJyEsko",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["XpDsk374LDE",1]]},"EpRrD58Ah3Q":{"wy":"HN commenters share it as a helpful lecture on plant-based weight loss and which foods to experiment with.","q":{"x":"I also find this lecture helpful for thinking about foods to experiment with","i":32964535,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32964535,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2022-09","story":"‘I’d rather eat an actual burger’: plant-based meat’s sizzle fizzled in the US","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful lecture for thinking about foods to experiment with","quote":"I also find this lecture helpful for thinking about foods to experiment with","text":"This is a good question, but I don't work well with cookbooks. They tend to sit on my shelf closed. I learned by experimentation. I do know of one cookbook because I heard an interview with the author on the radio, called \"Decolonize your diet\" about traditional indigenous recipes from Mexico before the Spanish arrived and made everything about meat. The authors deserve support but for the curious it is also on library genesis. But I do a combination of looking online at vegan recipes and experimenting with stuff on my own. I make a lentil sweet potato curry that is really filling and delicious. Here is my recipe copied from a tweet I made about it: Large onion, chopped. ‘Sautee’ in water until partially clear. Add minced garlic and ginger paste. Sauté 2 more mins. Add curry powder, turmeric, any other spices, salt, pepper. Add can chopped tomatoes, 2-3 cups oat milk, 1.5 cup washed lentils, Add chopped large sweet potato, add a can of garbanzo beans, add a small can of chopped mushrooms (drained). Serve with brown rice. Can add raisins and nutritional yeast. Can get a sweet crunch adding candied nuts. I also find this lecture helpful for thinking about foods to experiment with: [VIDEO] You can also look for vegan or vegetarian restaurants in your area and try them, see what you like, and try to work with those foods. And look for different cultures like Indian food that focus on more vegetarian meals. Bon appetit!"},{"id":31118033,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2022-04","story":"My diet of almost only red meat","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on weight loss with a plant-based diet","quote":"Great lecture on weight loss with a plant based diet","text":"Congratulations! The latest studies suggest that a plant based whole food diet is equally if not more effective at weight loss than a keto diet. The most important thing is paying attention to what you eat! In a head to head study between keto and plant based, participants in both categories lost weight. It seems that a controlled reasonably healthy diet is the key. That said there are many anti-cancer fighting properties of a plant based diet. Consumption of meat and animal products seem to lead to higher cancer risks. I’m currently losing weight on a whole food plant based diet combined with modest daily fasting (10 hours eating, 14 hours fasting per day). Vegan vs keto study: [LINK] Great lecture on weight loss with a plant based diet: [VIDEO] Health concerns with a keto diet: [LINK] That said I’m just a vegan who has been listening to these folks talk about this stuff, but I’m not an expert. I’m glad you found something that works!"},{"id":33767280,"author":"myshpa","month":"2022-11","story":"South Asian people undergo type 2 diabetes remission with low calorie diets","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among resources on reversing diabetes with plant-based eating","quote":"Evidence-Based Weight Loss: Dr. Michael Greger","text":"Low calorie diet is not the only way how to reverse diabetes. One can eat to satiety and lose weight & reverse diabetes at the same time. [LINK] Neal Barnard, MD | A Nutritional Approach for Reversing Diabetes [LINK] Diabetes Reversal and Weight-loss with Neal Barnard, M.D. [VIDEO] Evidence-Based Weight Loss: Dr. Michael Greger [LINK] How Not To Die : Dr. Michael Greger"},{"id":26328605,"author":"dqv","month":"2021-03","story":"Meat consumption and risk of 25 conditions: outcome-wide analyses of 475K people","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that water-rich foods increase satiety","quote":"","text":">Now enter plant based / animal free diets, how are people gonna get to satiety without all that dense meat / fish / chicken? There was a time when animal products were a lot more expensive and scarce. When you add more foods with water in them (provided you're not overloading those foods with fat and oils) you end up feeling more satiated. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uxykI30fS54",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1],["zb7Bs98KmnY",1],["-s5szfPYKY4",1],["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["hx7BXih7zx8",1],["tTBWfkE7BXU",1]]},"FegjLbCnoBw":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the definitive explanation of XFS metadata performance fixes and how XFS was developed and made robust on Linux.","q":{"x":"Please check this talk from Dave Chinner, where he explains how XFS was developed and ported to Linux","i":38556143,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38556143,"author":"noigai","month":"2023-12","story":"In OpenZFS and Btrfs, everyone was just guessing","stance":"rec","why":"How XFS was developed, ported to Linux and made robust","quote":"Please check this talk from Dave Chinner, where he explains how XFS was developed and ported to Linux","text":"> XFS is probably the best bet on Linux Yes, agree. However, I also lost files on it (had files overwritten with 0s, as other people already commented here). Also, it's your best bet of you don't need the extra features provided by ZFS. Thanks to its checksum validation, I detected bad SATA cables three times already (on different systems), instead of getting corrupted data. >it was beautifully designed and implemented by SGI back in the day. Hopefully the ChatGPT generation don't start modifying that code too much. Except it's not what happened. Please check this talk from Dave Chinner, where he explains how XFS was developed and ported to Linux and then made robust and stable by him and other contributors. The disk format has changed many times, now it's robust and resilient against metadata corruption. XFS Development talk by Dave Chinner [VIDEO] History of Linux FS, also by Dave Chinner: [LINK]"},{"id":7876317,"author":"justin66","month":"2014-06","story":"Red Hat Unveils Red Hat Enterprise Linux 7","stance":"rec","why":"Talk addressing and fixing XFS metadata performance","quote":"I wonder if you know about Dave Chinner's talk, which addresses the metadata performance issue.","text":"I wonder if you know about Dave Chinner's talk, which addresses the metadata performance issue. (they fixed it) [VIDEO]"},{"id":3543149,"author":"Tuna-Fish","month":"2012-02","story":"XFS: the filesystem of the future?","stance":"reference","why":"Dave Chinner presenting XFS metadata performance improvements","quote":"Video of Dave Chinner presenting this at linux.conf.au","text":"> A while ago, I read somewhere that XFS is good for a small number of large files, whereas ReiserFS is good for a large number of small files. I don't know whether that's true anymore if it ever was It used to be true. XFS has for a long time been the very fastest real filesystem available when it comes to moving data, and one of the slowest ones in real-world use when it comes to changing metadata. Untarring a kernel tarball a second time (on top of the first) is pretty much the canonical benchmark for metadata operations, and the one where XFS used to be at it's absolute worst. XFS is in the news right now exactly because they have fixed that deficiency. It's still faster than ext*, zfs and btrfs in pretty much everything else, but now it's comparable to them when modifying metadata. So this is exactly the right benchmark to use, because it's the one where there has been recent change. Video of Dave Chinner presenting this at linux.conf.au: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_fHfgU8oMSo",1]]},"Gzj723LkRJY":{"wy":"HN treats it as the model for a compelling developer demo and a benchmark for how simple web development once felt.","q":{"x":"I think a good example to follow for your project is the original Rails announcement video by DHH","i":18167209,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":37888684,"author":"girishso","month":"2023-10","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"Blog with comments built in 15 minutes","quote":"I was blown away that he created a blog application with comments in 15 minutes","text":"Ruby on Rails demo by DHH back in 2005/6. I was blown away that he created a blog application with comments in 15 minutes, I was working on Asp.NET back then and it would have taken me about a week to do that. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18167209,"author":"grblovrflowerrr","month":"2018-10","story":"Show HN: Alan – a low-code application platform","stance":"rec","why":"Model for a compelling demo: a full app in minutes","quote":"I think a good example to follow for your project is the original Rails announcement video by DHH","text":"I think a good example to follow for your project is the original Rails announcement video by DHH: [VIDEO] Maybe you can do a similar app in way less time!"},{"id":35928075,"author":"avg_dev","month":"2023-05","story":"Rails Cheat Sheet","stance":"reference","why":"Rails momentum started from a quick blog-building video","quote":"the initial momentum behind rails was sparked by a video about how to create a blog using rails without any setup in just a few minutes.","text":"i find this critique interesting, because i believe the initial momentum behind rails was sparked by a video about how to create a blog using rails without any setup in just a few minutes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35972292,"author":"dmix","month":"2023-05","story":"Smol Developer","stance":"reference","why":"DHH's blog-in-15-minutes video as model for AI scaffolding","quote":"","text":"code2prompt.py has some interesting implications for \"forking\" github projects into the base of your own side project. Once this stuff matures it will be fascinating to see how the 2030 version of 2010 Rails scaffolding looks. What will the DHH \"make a blog in 15min\" video look like? (Edit: which apparently is 17yrs old now, aka 2006) [VIDEO]"},{"id":36128081,"author":"sph","month":"2023-05","story":"Dynamic Forms with LiveView Streams","stance":"reference","why":"The original Rails demo from 17 years ago","quote":"Rails, it was this one 17 years ago","text":"The Live View demo or the Rails one? :-) Rails, it was this one 17 years ago: [VIDEO] Live View, I'm not sure which is the first demo. There's \"Build a real-time Twitter clone in 15 minutes with LiveView and Phoenix 1.5\" ( [LINK] )"},{"id":29963213,"author":"sergiomattei","month":"2022-01","story":"DOS on Dope (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Form text matches DHH's 2005 Rails introduction","quote":"the text in the forms is exactly what DHH used in 2005 when famously introducing Rails at a conference.","text":"Love the little details: the text in the forms is exactly what DHH used in 2005 when famously introducing Rails at a conference. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29603460,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2021-12","story":"Remix: On Rails","stance":"reference","why":"Original Rails blog-in-15-minutes video from 2005","quote":"I've yet to see a single video like that from any of the new remixes","text":"The original \"How to build a blog in 15 minutes with Rails\" from 2005: [VIDEO] I've yet to see a single video like that from any of the new remixes/redwoods/nexts/nuxts/<god there are so many of them> And one that doesn't require 270 KB of Javascript to display a static page like Remix's landing page."},{"id":28412387,"author":"BoumTAC","month":"2021-09","story":"I completely ignored the front end development scene for 6 months. It was fine","stance":"reference","why":"Blog in 15 minutes contrasted with modern frontend complexity","quote":"In 15 years we came from 15 minutes to create a blog with this old tech to a few days of work.","text":"When I started learning to code few years ago, tutorials were always, \"how to create a blog ?\" because it shows everything (authentication, crud, nested view, rights). Then javascript framework came out. It was so hard that they move to just a todolist tutorial because even the creator didn't know how to create a blog with their new cool tech. Some blogger were still able to understand how to use it and release todolist tutorial with authentication to demonstrate authentication, session management and rights. Then new cool javascript framework came out and now it was even harder to code, creators weren't able to develop a todolist because it was too hard so their tutorial was just a basic counter application. Now with nextjs we are even a step further. The developers don't know how to use it (is it even possible ?) so the tutorials show just how to \"start\" an application, load css and how to create a route. In just a few years we came from create a full application to not even know how to do a todolist. (Imagine form validation, it must be so hard !!!). Everybody seems happy and very productive. It must be myself. In 15 years we came from 15 minutes to create a blog with this old tech to a few days of work. That's a lot of progress we don't have to reload the page ! [VIDEO]"},{"id":28208695,"author":"edu","month":"2021-08","story":"Show HN: Imba – I have spent 7 years creating a programming language for the web","stance":"reference","why":"Original Rails demo as benchmark for an impressive demo","quote":"The video gave me the same feeling I had when I watched the original rails demo video","text":"Wow, congrats. It looks great, need to try it. The video gave me the same feeling I had when I watched the original rails demo video [1]. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22217728,"author":"grappler","month":"2020-02","story":"Lenovo now shipping Ubuntu on high end workstations in the US","stance":"reference","why":"DHH's Rails intro on TextMate as origin of the Mac developer tradition","quote":"dhh's intro to rails development using textmate","text":"I know you're at least half joking, but for me it actually is the year of linux on the desktop. As a developer who does server-side stuff, a mac was an obvious easy choice for a long time -- a tradition that has been widespread going at least as far back as dhh's intro to rails development using textmate [1]. What does that open source crowd want now? They want to build OCI/docker containers and deploy them to kubernetes clusters. You can do that on mac, but these containers need to run on a linux kernel, so the setup winds up as containers on a VM on the host OS. If your dev machine is linux, you can remove the VM layer in between and the setup is simpler and more efficient. I think that might push a lot of developers from mac to linux. I just got a new dev machine recently and it's a linux laptop. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13476891,"author":"mavelikara","month":"2017-01","story":"DHH answers: What makes Rails a framework worth learning in 2017?","stance":"reference","why":"DHH building a blog in 15 minutes with Rails","quote":"you have DHH creating a blog in 15 minutes using RoR.","text":"> There are a lot of vocal people who make it seem like you should be doing everything in Go or Angular or what have you It might be hard to believe now, but Rails also went through this stage of vocal supporters advocating for it over alternatives. If you go back and watch the introduction video [1], you have DHH creating a blog in 15 minutes using RoR. I remember being impressed by it and vocally pushing for it where I worked then. :) [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9300677,"author":"chadkruse","month":"2015-03","story":"Meteor 1.1 Released – Now Supporting Microsoft Windows and MongoDB 3.0","stance":"reference","why":"Rails scaffolding demo as precedent for rapid prototyping","quote":"Meteor is the 2015 version.","text":"Here's a reasonably recent list of production Meteor apps: [LINK] IMHO, one of the often overlooked use cases for Meteor is rapid-prototyping. If anyone remembers DHH's video 10 years ago demonstrating Rails' scaffolding [0], Meteor is the 2015 version. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5253257,"author":"jules","month":"2013-02","story":"Rails Has Turned into Java","stance":"reference","why":"Original Rails demo once amazing, now seems boring as competition caught up","quote":"Back then when I saw the first Rails video I remember finding it amazing. Now it's just boring.","text":"Rails is now like Java EE because it is no longer better than the competition. It's not that Rails got worse. Perhaps in simplicity it did get worse a bit, but overall Rails now is much better than Rails then. The thing is that the competition got a lot better too. The baseline changed. I don't think the thing that will be to Rails as Rails was to Java EE exists yet though. The competition has merely caught up, perhaps a little ahead on some fronts and a little behind on others. But there is nothing that is clearly much much better. Things like Meteor and Opa are a step in the right direction, but they don't go far enough (yet). Does anybody remember this video: [VIDEO] This is not the original Rails video, it's the second one I think, but they're fairly similar (couldn't find the original). Back then when I saw the first Rails video I remember finding it amazing. Now it's just boring."},{"id":43967078,"author":"Lio","month":"2025-05","story":"Ruby 3.5 Feature: Namespace on read","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The original DHH demo video[1], from 19 years ago, was a blog in 15 minutes.","text":"20 minutes? Who’s got time for that? The original DHH demo video[1], from 19 years ago, was a blog in 15 minutes. AI is clearly rubbish! :P 1. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8X69_42Mj-g",1]]},"J7GY1Xg6X20":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as inspirational and relevant whenever power, technology and humanity are in tension.","q":{"x":"I think Charlie Chaplin's speech at the end of The Great Dictator is relevant and inspirational","i":48415204,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48415204,"author":"UtopiaPunk","month":"2026-06","story":"India's surprise baby bust is a warning to the world","stance":"rec","why":"Chaplin's speech seen as relevant against concentrated power.","quote":"I think Charlie Chaplin's speech at the end of The Great Dictator is relevant and inspirational","text":"Is the concern that we'd marginalize parents and kids? As a parent of little kids, that's a concern that wasn't even on my radar. I had no idea that that was a major concern of people. Wild. I'm not saying it is a fabricated or unfounded fear, I just don't have that concern at all for myself. As a parent of little kids, I worry much more about them living fulfilling lives as they grow up in the future. I'm concerned about climate change, wars, and an economic system that will allow them to live self-actualized lives. I have no doubt that the population number plays some factor in that, creating problems that must be solved. But ultimately, humans have created amazing technologies and the Earth is bountiful. We can support whatever number of people is on the horizon (whether that number is larger or smaller), but society must choose to do so and adapt. My greatest fears are that governments and corporations consolidate their wealth and power to only an elite few, bending society to serve that elite. That is a fear exists regardless of the fertility rate. I think Charlie Chaplin's speech at the end of The Great Dictator is relevant and inspirational: [VIDEO] Thank you for sharing your article. It's from a view so unlike my own, and it's been eye opening."},{"id":32232124,"author":"qbasic_forever","month":"2022-07","story":"Cinema’s greatest scene: ‘Casablanca’ and ‘La Marseillaise’ (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Chaplin's closing speech from a 1940 film mocking Hitler.","quote":"Its monologue is an equally unforgettable scene","text":"Watch Chaplin's The Great Dictator and it's really amazing when you realize it came out in 1940 and was created in the late 30's, right when Hitler was seizing power and starting WW2. Chaplin was a genius and realized the most powerful way to hurt Hitler was to make him look like a fool and mock him. Its monologue is an equally unforgettable scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38724609,"author":"neogimp","month":"2023-12","story":"We think too much and feel too little","stance":"reference","why":"Speech about dictators and liberty, tied to billionaire longevity worries.","quote":"gave me a chill","text":"[VIDEO] the line \"The hate of men will pass, and dictators die, and the power they took from the people will return to the people. And so long as men die, liberty will never perish…\" gave me a chill. i believe in science, progress, and long for a day where everyone is treated with equality and empathy. but- once billionaires start living longer with longevity regimes / giant piles of money, how long until we are subjected to the whims of a maniac who will not die like musk, bezos, or gates?"}],"r":[["qSfdtmcZ4d0",1],["j6VlT_jUVPc",1],["OUNXFHpUhu8",1]]},"JQymn5flcek":{"wy":"Recommended as a fun and illuminating watch on early mobile computing, especially in threads about Bill Atkinson and Telescript.","q":{"x":"check out the documentary[0] on General Magic which Bill co-founded in 1990","i":44211299,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44211299,"author":"gavmor","month":"2025-06","story":"Bill Atkinson has died","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on General Magic, which Bill Atkinson co-founded; early vision of mobile email","quote":"check out the documentary[0] on General Magic which Bill co-founded in 1990","text":"If you haven't, check out the documentary[0] on General Magic which Bill co-founded in 1990. Among the more remarkable scenes in there is when a member of the public seems perplexed by the thought that they would even want to \"check email from Times Square.\" An unthinkable future, but they thought it. And yet, most folks have never heard of General Magic. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44211512,"author":"JKCalhoun","month":"2025-06","story":"Bill Atkinson has died","stance":"rec","why":"General Magic documentary is a fun watch","quote":"perhaps the General Magic documentary is a fun watch too","text":"Plenty of interviews will Bill on YouTube. Example: [LINK] Also, perhaps the General Magic documentary is a fun watch too: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37446149,"author":"tapoxi","month":"2023-09","story":"The Telescript Programming Language","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on General Magic, free on YouTube","quote":"For anyone else amazed by General Magic, the recently released documentary is available for free","text":"For anyone else amazed by General Magic, the recently released documentary is available for free (legally) on YouTube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48703001,"author":"wmf","month":"2026-06","story":"We almost had a smartphone in the 90s. Why did it fail?","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on General Magic where Fadell once worked","quote":"There's also a documentary about General Magic","text":"This headline isn't great. It's an interview with Tony Fadell who invented the iPod, iPhone, and Nest thermostat. Before the iPod, Fadell worked at failed PDA maker General Magic. The interview doesn't get into other 90s attempts like the IBM Simon, Palm Pilot, or Apple Newton. There's also a documentary about General Magic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44214429,"author":"JKCalhoun","month":"2025-06","story":"Bill Atkinson has died","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp of Atkinson and anticipation of the smartphone","quote":"someone expresses the excitement anticipating the smart phone","text":"Atkinson at this timestamp: [VIDEO] Also, it's here in the documentary that someone expresses the excitement anticipating the smart phone. It's hard to watch for me now and not shake my head, \"Oh, it's not quite as wonderful as you imagined.\""},{"id":34992106,"author":"AlbertCory","month":"2023-03","story":"50 Years Later, We’re Still Living in the Xerox Alto’s World","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Take it all with a grain of salt.","text":"Well, the Macintosh team certainly thought they were. There's a YT video about General Magic that tries to paint them that way, and they thought of themselves that way at the time. [VIDEO] Take it all with a grain of salt."}],"r":[["ihkRwArnc1k",1]]},"Ks0SOn8hjG8":{"wy":"HN points to it as a good overview of Chip and PIN attacks and the consumer-liability downsides.","q":{"x":"This is a good overview by some researchers in the different ways Chip and Pin can be hacked.","i":10414980,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":17902866,"author":"Crosseye_Jack","month":"2018-09","story":"Fear the reaper: characterization and fast detection of card skimmers","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses observed attacks on Chip and Pin.","quote":"still worth a watch discussing the attacks they have seen on Chip and Pin","text":"One trick (which I believe has been fixed) is not so much a replay attack but a multiple transaction attack. A modified terminal makes multiple transactions on the card while the card is still in the terminal, coupled with another attack that modified what was presented on the screen of the terminal could turn a single $5 transaction to 3 $500 transactions. The video is 3 years old now but still worth a watch discussing the attacks they have seen on Chip and Pin - [VIDEO]"},{"id":10414980,"author":"bardworx","month":"2015-10","story":"X-Ray Scans Expose Chip-And-Pin Card Hack","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of ways Chip and Pin can be hacked.","quote":"This is a good overview by some researchers in the different ways Chip and Pin can be hacked.","text":"This is a good overview by some researchers in the different ways Chip and Pin can be hacked. This explains this hack as well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11836562,"author":"Matt3o12_","month":"2016-06","story":"‘Stop using paper checks,’ says agency in charge of them","stance":"reference","why":"Explains downsides of PIN cards for consumer liability.","quote":"If you are interested in the downsides of the new pin card, checkout this video","text":"I want to have a system which protects me from money loss (through my own fault, a malicious third party, or my bank's fault). I don't care how as long as I'm protected. With Chip-and-PIn cards, I'm not protected because I have to proof that it was not my fault (which can be incredible difficult to do). So, I personal prefer cards that require a signature. It doesn't matter to me if it has a chip or not (the chip has nothing to do with the pin, there are also chip-and-signature cards and cards that support both. The chip makes it hard to physically clone the card). If you are interested in the downsides of the new pin card, checkout this video: [VIDEO] So, if zero authentication means that I am not liable for financial damages, I'm happy with it but I wouldn't bother to use a pin if I had the same liability. Unfortunately, the pin adds laughable authentication which makes me liable for many types of fraud (see the video)."}],"r":[["N65DpT2nqEI",1]]},"OTVE5iPMKLg":{"wy":"HN links it in caffeine threads as a quick, clear explanation of caffeine's effects.","q":{"x":"Nice explanatory video on the matter","i":15015047,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15015047,"author":"askvictor","month":"2017-08","story":"Quitting Caffeine","stance":"rec","why":"Explains caffeine's benefits and few negatives.","quote":"Nice explanatory video on the matter","text":"I occasionally quit caffeine as a way to recalibrate the tolerance that develops over time. But, within certain limits (my general consumption is 1-2 coffees a day, sometimes 3-4 if super tired or when exercising), there's very few negatives for most people, and quite a number of benefits. Nice explanatory video on the matter: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12512472,"author":"jimmytidey","month":"2016-09","story":"Show HN: I invented a caffeinated toothpaste","stance":"rec","why":"Explains caffeine's effects and health implications.","quote":"There is a great cgp grey video on caffeine","text":"I don't think caffeine has many negative health implications. Although it has certainly wasted my time by leaving me too jittery to work. There is a great cgp grey video on caffeine: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6192513,"author":"Dewie","month":"2013-08","story":"How Your Brain Becomes Addicted to Caffeine","stance":"reference","why":"Video suggesting coffee addiction may be harmless or beneficial.","quote":"","text":"The article doesn't seem to mention whether coffee is healthy or not. One thing is if it is addictive, but if it is healthy (or even if it just isn't unhealthy), I've got to ask... so what if it is addictive? [VIDEO]"},{"id":5317230,"author":"lcc_tn","month":"2013-03","story":"Silicon Valley is high on innovation. And pot","stance":"reference","why":"Video on whether coffee consumption is good for you.","quote":"You've reminded me of this video talking about coffee now","text":"You've reminded me of this video talking about coffee now: [VIDEO] Is there really any good reason why I shouldn't be drinking a lot of coffee? It seems I see as many researches claiming I shouldn't drink much coffee as I see researches claiming I should. I'm not sure what to think about coffee at this point."}]},"WjTrfoiB0MQ":{"wy":"Pointed to in threads on defer, scope_exit and RAII as the clearest treatment of scope guards.","q":{"x":"Andrei Alexandrescu has a fun talk about implementing scope_exit","i":16324582,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16324582,"author":"uep","month":"2018-02","story":"Scope exit in C++11 (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Fun talk on implementing scope_exit with std::uncaught_exceptions","quote":"Andrei Alexandrescu has a fun talk about implementing scope_exit","text":"Andrei Alexandrescu has a fun talk about implementing scope_exit using a new C++17 function: std::uncaught_exceptions (which is a tweak to std::uncaught_exception). Fundamentally, it doesn't really solve the destructor throwing exceptions issue though. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12119914,"author":"Ace17","month":"2016-07","story":"Memory management in C programs (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Alexandrescu on declarative control flow for convoluted resource cleanup","quote":"Andrei Alexandrescu - Declarative Control Flow","text":"Google is an example that writing production code that doesn't use exceptions is possible, not that this is the right thing to do. They said that their decision was primarily legacy-motivated (in short, they were fearing that introducing exceptions in a non-RAII codebase would lead us to a long period of instability). I'll add that in some situations (e.g when freeing resources) the control flow gets very convoluted, and you don't want to deal with directly. See: Andrei Alexandrescu - Declarative Control Flow ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":33397132,"author":"einpoklum","month":"2022-10","story":"A Tour of C++ (Third edition)","stance":"reference","why":"Video about scope guard motivating the uncaught_exceptions change","quote":"YouTube video about scope guard","text":"A count of the number of exceptions in-flight was added, replacing the boolean indication of whether an exception is in-flight. This allows the scope guard construct proposed by Andrei Alexandrescu to distinguish between a scope being left on success and on an exception being thrown. YouTube video about scope guard: [VIDEO] Description of the change to the standard: [LINK]"},{"id":31623568,"author":"watmough","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: What should have been the term for RAII?","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk on scope guards","quote":"","text":"I think scope or similar is good. Also, see related video here CppCon 2015: Andrei Alexandrescu “Declarative Control Flow\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":25418831,"author":"dnautics","month":"2020-12","story":"A Defer Mechanism for C","stance":"reference","why":"Explains scope guard with success and failure variants","quote":"","text":"yes, you always run defer. I don't see it in this proposal, but Most languages/frameworks that implement a defer also implement a \"error defer\" which only gets triggered on some sort of labeled early exit scenario, and some implement \"success defer\": (i believe this is scopeguard: [VIDEO] )"}]},"aNR4n0i2ZlM":{"wy":"HN commenters call it a great beginner-friendly introduction and enjoy watching Quilez work through graphics.","q":{"x":"is a great introduction for beginners and younger people","i":15188425,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":22318795,"author":"isoprophlex","month":"2020-02","story":"Wolfram Alpha: {y = Re(sqrt(abs(x)(1-abs(x)))), y = Re(-sqrt(1-sqrt(abs(x))))}","stance":"rec","why":"Iquilez hacking away at graphics","quote":"Watching the guy hack away at graphics is super enjoyable","text":"Highly recommended video by Iquilez, in a similar vein: [VIDEO] Watching the guy hack away at graphics is super enjoyable"},{"id":15188425,"author":"eggy","month":"2017-09","story":"Articles on fractals, computer graphics, mathematics, demoscene and more","stance":"rec","why":"Beating heart composited on live video, explained step by step","quote":"is a great introduction for beginners and younger people","text":"His YouTube tutorial where he creates the beating heart composited on live video as he explains it is a great introduction for beginners and younger people [1]. I've learned so much from his sharing of his techniques and knowledge. Thanks Inigo! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19161526,"author":"userbinator","month":"2019-02","story":"Plotting hearts with Python and C","stance":"reference","why":"Procedurally generating a 3D heart","quote":"there is this video on how to procedurally generate a 3D heart","text":"I was expecting it to be the same algorithm in two different languages, but the Python version is a mathematical description whereas the C version is RLE-compressed. Relatedly, there is this video on how to procedurally generate a 3D heart: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tCRPUv8V22o",1],["My4RA5I0FKs",1],["lMcRBdSdO_U",1],["tQpqsmwlfY0",1],["z8MVKianh54",1]]},"frnLiXclAMo":{"wy":"Recommended whenever ZFS encryption comes up as the authoritative explanation of how it was added and its trade-offs at scale.","q":{"x":"check out this presentation on how encryption was bolted on ZFS","i":14388931,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":33926524,"author":"drbawb","month":"2022-12","story":"Apple advances user security with powerful new data protections","stance":"rec","why":"Covers trade-offs of encryption at scale in ZFS","quote":"The presentation about ZFS' native encryption[1] covers many of these sorts of trade-offs necessary to do full-disk encryption at scale.","text":"The problem is that a stream cipher is going to have some per-object uniqueness (a salt, IV, etc.), so by design even if you feed it related input blocks you will get different output blocks. This is, of course, antithetical to deduplication: so you need to check/store the hash of the input before it goes through the cipher. The presentation about ZFS' native encryption[1] covers many of these sorts of trade-offs necessary to do full-disk encryption at scale. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14388931,"author":"dchest","month":"2017-05","story":"TFS: A file system built for performance, space efficiency, and scalability","stance":"rec","why":"How encryption was added to ZFS, with authentication via checksum fields","quote":"check out this presentation on how encryption was bolted on ZFS","text":"On the contrary, it makes it suitable for file systems! File systems are not block devices, they are data structures on top of block devices — it's the job of these data structures to keep stuff, such as data, inodes, and... keys, and IVs, and MACs, checksums, etc. TFS uses Speck in XEX mode (edit: I just realized you're the author, so you already know that :). tptacek wrote a nice post about it: [LINK] and I quote: \"If you’re encrypting a filesystem and not disk blocks, still don’t use XTS! Filesystems have format-awareness and flexibility. Filesystems can do a much better job of encrypting a disk than simulated hardware encryption can.\" Edit: check out this presentation on how encryption was bolted on ZFS: [VIDEO] (slides: [LINK] ) It's not perfect, but it provides data authentication by reusing checksum fields for storing MACs. Edit 2: also check out bcachefs encryption design doc: [LINK] (also not perfect, but uses proper AEAD — ChaCha20-Poly1305. I sent some questions and suggestions to the author, but received no reply :/)"},{"id":14619067,"author":"wspeirs","month":"2017-06","story":"Playing with ZFS encryption on Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Caputi's design decisions for ZFS encryption","quote":"He presented all of his design decisions at the OpenZFS developer conference here","text":"I work with Tom at Datto. He presented all of his design decisions at the OpenZFS developer conference here: [LINK] Slides: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13852917,"author":"zxv","month":"2017-03","story":"Silent Data Corruption Is Real","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation of upcoming native ZFS encryption","quote":"There was a presentation of this by Tom Caputi at the most recent OpenZFS Developer Summit.","text":"Native ZFS encryption may become available soon. It is pending code review: [LINK] There was a presentation of this by Tom Caputi at the most recent OpenZFS Developer Summit. Slides: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO] Conference: [LINK]"},{"id":12680453,"author":"RJIb8RBYxzAMX9u","month":"2016-10","story":"FreeBSD 11.0 Now Available","stance":"reference","why":"Native ZFS encryption presentation","quote":"not native zfs encryption that was recently presented in OpenZFS Developer Summit","text":"This is still geli+zfs, and not native zfs encryption that was recently presented in OpenZFS Developer Summit [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HxaD_trXwRE",1]]},"iSmkqocn0oQ":{"wy":"HN cites it constantly in language debates for his useful-versus-safe diagram and the convergence of languages, with commenters clarifying that his remark predates how Haskell handles effects.","q":{"x":"I'm hoping that Rust will fit in the \"useful and safe\" sweet spot in Simon Peyton Jones's diagram here","i":9552084,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":40666486,"author":"ReleaseCandidat","month":"2024-06","story":"Gerald Sussman: Programming is (should be) fun (2022) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"SPJ's enthusiasm makes even boring topics interesting","quote":"his enthusiasm can even make the mseemingly most boring topic interesting","text":"That must of course be \"Haskell is useless\" [VIDEO] But apart from that, his enthusiasm can even make the mseemingly most boring topic interesting. I'd personally start either at \"the story of Haskell\" Escape from the Ivory Tower [LINK] Or something more recent, about Epic's Verse [LINK]"},{"id":38027315,"author":"ernst_klim","month":"2023-10","story":"Was Rust Worth It?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on soundness versus completeness","quote":"A good talk on topic [1]","text":"These are two issues, which a theoretically orthogonal but in practice not so much. These are known as soundness and completeness. A good talk on topic [1] Rust will reject a lot of sound programs, and that's a huge performance hit. You are hitting the incompleteness wall with multiple mutable borrowing, closures in structures are a huge source of pain as well. And usually the answer from the community is \"use handlers instead of pointers\", but this gives you, surprise surprise, manual resource management alike that in C, and the compiler won't help much. Of course this is all subjective but for me the ergonomics of Rust is far too bad. It's a good step in right direction along with ATS, but I really hope we could do better than this. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39423982,"author":"breadwinner","month":"2024-02","story":"Haskell is Useless (2011) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Clarifies SPJ's remark about effect-free programs being useless","quote":"He is talking about Haskell before they figured out how to add effects.","text":"His comment is being taken out of context. He is talking about Haskell before they figured out how to add effects. As he says [1], a program without effects is useless, as it doesn't do anything except make the computer hot. Then they figured out how to combine — in a single language — effectful computations, and effect-free ones, without making them pollute each other. The type-system keeps them apart. This makes Haskell useful. So in summary, he is not saying modern-day Haskell (which has effects) is useless. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23250878,"author":"kungato","month":"2020-05","story":"Welcome to C# 9.0","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ on convergence of programming language features","quote":"I always remember this chat by Simon PJ, the \"father\" of Haskell about convergence of languages in features","text":"Well F# obviosly never gained enough momentum and C# has to grow somewhere so why not in this direction. All \"big\" languages are gaining more and more functional and declarative features each version. I always remember this chat by Simon PJ, the \"father\" of Haskell about convergence of languages in features [VIDEO]"},{"id":19656534,"author":"Eli_P","month":"2019-04","story":"Learn more programming languages, even if you won't use them","stance":"reference","why":"Haskell's creator on how Haskell changes how you code","quote":"The creator of Haskell confirmed and explained","text":"> learning Haskell has changed the way I write code in any language I think that's the bottom line. The creator of Haskell confirmed and explained: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17484356,"author":"tchaffee","month":"2018-07","story":"Why not program right?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I did a little digging and even started reading \"System design from provably correct constructs: the beginnings of true software engineering\". From what I can see, it's mostly garbage used to sell the products of the HOS company. James Martin was on the board Higher Order Software, Inc. A company that despite claiming being able to provide \"bug free software\" went out of business. Here's Edsger Dijkstra debunking the books written about HOS. [LINK] An evaluation by the US Navy concluded. \"the HOS literature tends to advertise their ideas and products more than making a contribution in substance to the field of Computer Science. The author recommends that USEIT not be used in the TRIDENT program or any program development at NSWC. Even for a high level system specification, USEIT is not seen as a good choice. A mathematical functional notation or a PROLOG-like notation appears better suited for that purpose. The examples in the Appendices of this report, especially Appendices C, D, and E, show that a LISP-style mathematical notation is more compact and normally easier to read than the control map notation of USEIT. On a more positive note, the author considers the functional approach to system development and programing very promising. Systems so conceived and programs so constructed are more amenable to analysis and therefore, in principle, more reliable and better manageable.\" [LINK] So are we just talking about functional programming when we say HOS? If so, the pros and con"},{"id":17583067,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2018-07","story":"The Wrong Abstraction (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ on programs needing state to be useful","quote":"At some point, you need to modify some state, otherwise your program/language is useless.","text":"At some point, you need to modify some state, otherwise your program/language is useless. And that's not me saying that, I am just quoting, or at least paraphrasing, Simon Peyton Jones: [VIDEO] And of course a lot of so-called \"functional\" programs just outsource their state management to some sort of relational database. And the people talking about their creation will praise the state-less-ness of their creation. Unironically. What can you do? ¯\\_(ツ)_/¯ Anyway, more practically, the vast majority of workloads do not have computation as their primary function. They store, load and move data around. Computers, generally, don't compute. Much. For those workloads, a paradigm that tries to eliminate the very thing that the problem domain is about, and can only get it back by jumping through hoops, is arguably not ideal. [LINK]"},{"id":16656141,"author":"dwenzek","month":"2018-03","story":"Alan Kay on Why is FP seen as the opposite of OOP rather than an addition to it","stance":"reference","why":"FP and OO ideas can mutually improve","quote":"an idea well developed in this short video","text":"I find this post a bit wordy but interesting. It notably points out well how the two approaches to deal with the state of a system, using versions or overwriting it, are in competition since a long time and in various domain of computer sciences. Alan also cites a paper about [Worlds: Controlling the Scope of Side Effects]( [LINK] ). A hot topic in the functional programming world. That paper makes me remember that ideas from FP and OO can mutually improve (an idea well developed in this short video [VIDEO] ."},{"id":13671187,"author":"klodolph","month":"2017-02","story":"Rust Sucks If I Fail to Write X","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Let's get this out of the way: Rust is great. Rust apologia like this article is not so great. > As an aside, remember that the only difference to C/c++ is that if you write a “basic linked list” in them, all of your code will be unsafe. There's a bit of mental gymnastics going on here. The word \"unsafe\" is performing double duty, since it means \"memory safety not guaranteed by the compiler\" in Rust and it means something else entirely when you are talking about C++, since memory safety was never guaranteed by the compiler in the first place. The other problem with this statement is that linked lists in C or C++ aren’t really that hard to get right, in fact, they’re easy. Maybe you draw out a diagram on pen and paper before you write the code, but you’re unlikely to be facing segfaults. I admit I’m biased here, because I’ve been using Haskell for something like 15 years now, but I feel like the Haskell community acknowledges that Haskell’s type system gets in the way and prevents you from doing useful, interesting work, and that even a great library ecosystem isn’t enough to overcome this. That’s how safety generally works. It’s harder to write programs that do useful things, but in exchange, it’s also harder to write programs that behave unpredictably or do dangerous things. Because Rust and Haskell put you in such restrictive type systems, sometimes you have to break out to get real work done. Haskell’s pitch, in my mind, is, “Let’s make it easy to reason about side effects"},{"id":13350423,"author":"zengid","month":"2017-01","story":"Challenging Clojure in Common Lisp (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ on STM ideas moving between languages","quote":"Simon Peyton Jones describes [1] how they took the ideas around STM from 'unsafe' languages and put it into Haskell","text":"I feel like the author didn't do this as a way to bash Clojure but to try and progress Common Lisp. Anecdotally, I remember Hickey saying something about a stagnation in the CL community, so if Clojure inspires cross-pollination of ideas then that's a good thing. For instance, Simon Peyton Jones describes [1] how they took the ideas around STM from 'unsafe' languages and put it into Haskell, developed it further, and then later the 'unsafe' languages took the Haskell innovations and put it back into their languages. Clojure isn't perfect, and Common Lisp isn't perfect, but hey, they're better than programming in assembly. We should be stoked about someone trying to innovate; why do these posts always turn into language wars? [1] [VIDEO] around 3:50, but the video is short so it's worth watching the whole thing."},{"id":11471388,"author":"aninhumer","month":"2016-04","story":"Strategic Scala Style: Principle of Least Power","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See also SPJ's \"Haskell is Useless\" video","text":">Maybe 'aninhumer has a point - restricting the language is kind of the opposite goal of making it expressive/powerful. My point was more that safety is a separate, important metric of language quality. It often conflicts with expressiveness, but not necessarily. See also SPJ's \"Haskell is Useless\" video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9552084,"author":"Arnor","month":"2015-05","story":"Announcing Rust 1.0","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ's useful versus safe diagram","quote":"I'm hoping that Rust will fit in the \"useful and safe\" sweet spot in Simon Peyton Jones's diagram here","text":"Awesome! I assume you intentionally released this on a Friday just to eat up everyone's weekends :) Thanks for the hard work. As I dig in, I'm hoping that Rust will fit in the \"useful and safe\" sweet spot in Simon Peyton Jones's diagram here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9269013,"author":"tempodox","month":"2015-03","story":"Kronos Haskell","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Simon Peyton Jones himself does not count Haskell among the","text":"Simon Peyton Jones himself does not count Haskell among the “useful” languages (Simon Peyton Jones - Haskell is useless): [VIDEO]"},{"id":8275169,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2014-09","story":"ML Family Workshop","stance":"reference","why":"Context that Haskell was nearly useless before monads","quote":"SPJ is not saying that Haskell is useless now, but it was close to useless for creating programs for a number of years.","text":"\"A good example is the notorious video interview [VIDEO] in which Simon Peyton Jones laments the fact that pure functional programming languages just make the CPU heat up and nothing else (i.e. they have no useful side effects.)\" You're not the first person I've seen that's misunderstood this video, I can only guess at why... One thing it's probably worth remembering is that Haskell has been a research platform for many years, and some of the Haskell features that people take for granted today did not exist in earlier versions. SPJ is not saying that Haskell is useless now, but it was close to useless for creating programs for a number of years. As a case in point, try to imagine Haskell without monads. Hopefully knowing that Haskell did not have monads at one point helps you understand the historical context of this discussion."},{"id":6718455,"author":"lukaseder","month":"2013-11","story":"What's wrong with Object-Oriented Programming and Functional Programming","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ and Meijer discussing Haskell usefulness","quote":"Guys, listen to Simon Peyton Jones himself: \"Haskell is useless\".","text":"Sign. Yet another discussion full of trolling pros and cons of FP vs. OO Guys, listen to Simon Peyton Jones himself: \"Haskell is useless\". He's discussing stuff with Erik Meijer in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4705991,"author":"Raphael_Amiard","month":"2012-10","story":"Rob Pike: Go at Google","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ's view that Haskell is useless but safe","quote":"I think Simon Peyton Jones says it best about Haskell","text":"I think Simon Peyton Jones says it best about Haskell : [VIDEO] He says Haskell is useless. Without even debating that (it can certainly be useful for certain classes of programming problems), you can see his point : The goal of Haskell was to start from an ideal view (pure functional programming without side effects) and try and see how to make that eventually practical for everyday programming, without sacrificing the pure core of the language . You have languages, like F# (or even C# !) that takes lessons from Haskell, with at the same time being practical for pretty much every programming task (their platform , .Net, makes them useless for a lot of use cases, but that is another problem) And on the other side (in my opinion), you have languages like Go, being pretty dismissive about pretty much everything learned from languages like Haskell. I respect the point of view of Go's creator, i just disagree with it, and don't see any project i could do in Go that i wouldn't prefer doing in Scala/OCaml/C#. But more power to people who want to do great things in Go ! Go fills a niche (AOT compiled without a VM) that only OCaml fills in the list of languages i have listed, and OCaml is probably much more complicated to learn, even if it is way simpler than Haskell, so even if i don't agree with the design choices, i can see why Go could gain traction (apart from the fact that it is Google powered)"},{"id":3591525,"author":"tikhonj","month":"2012-02","story":".NET: So long and thanks for all the fish","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ on idea exchange between Haskell and .NET","quote":"Here is a random video I found earlier where SPJ talks both about this in particular","text":"I was particularly thinking about things like order and group in list comprehensions[1]. Turns out the paper introducing them was actually from 2007, so maybe it's not that recent :). (I thought it was newer than that.) [1]: [LINK] Here is a random video I found earlier where SPJ talks both about this in particular and the exchange of ideas between Haskell and .NET in general: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31193358,"author":"eklavya","month":"2022-04","story":"I want off Mr. Golang’s Wild Ride (2020)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"WTF is going on in this thread? How did we become so sensitive, Haskell has had a lot of criticisms here at HN, never saw any flags/downvotes. People argued vehemently but without any animosity. Maybe something to be learned here [VIDEO]"},{"id":11604421,"author":"eternalban","month":"2016-05","story":"Simon Peyton Jones Elected into the Royal Society Fellowship","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> .. role model .. And don't forget, the superior way to project confidence [1] in your 'precious'. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8274803,"author":"theoh","month":"2014-09","story":"ML Family Workshop","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"A good example is the notorious video interview","text":"Disappointed that this wasn't actually a workshop for families. Cambridge used to use (still uses?) ML as a first language for CS undergrads, making its obscurity a strength (it is a new experience for most students). I see one of the abstracts talks about a \"classless society\" as a pun relating to object orientation. This makes me groan a bit, as there seems to be a general tendency in FP to make superficial quips, puns or observations like this while the community of FP programmers remains totally divorced from reality. A good example is the notorious video interview [VIDEO] in which Simon Peyton Jones laments the fact that pure functional programming languages just make the CPU heat up and nothing else (i.e. they have no useful side effects.) He probably wasn't being completely ingenuous but it comes across as a lack of self-awareness: FP is all about calculation and expressions, the program is an input and the value it evaluates to is the output... That's all you can expect. You need some other paradigm to \"do\" things, IO monads notwithstanding. Conan Elliot has a fun blog post on the c preprocessor which dances around this issue a bit: [LINK]"}],"r":[["gd5uJ7Nlvvo",1],["OJv8rFap0Nw",1],["6AeWu1fZ7bY",1]]},"ssob-7sGVWs":{"wy":"Cited as a deep, entertaining look at firmware-level software and vendors abusing pre-boot access.","q":{"x":"a brief history of quick boot operating systems and UEFI","i":39963195,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":47137507,"author":"nhubbard","month":"2026-02","story":"I Ported Coreboot to the ThinkPad X270","stance":"rec","why":"Examples of BIOS writers abusing firmware access.","quote":"Multiple examples from the excellent Cathode Ray Dude","text":"> Hopefully the BIOS writers do not abuse this Unfortunately, they have. Multiple examples from the excellent Cathode Ray Dude: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39963195,"author":"jwoglom","month":"2024-04","story":"An IRC client in your motherboard","stance":"rec","why":"Brief history of quick boot operating systems and UEFI.","quote":"a brief history of quick boot operating systems and UEFI","text":"I think this might be the video you're talking about, in case anyone is also interested in a brief history of quick boot operating systems and UEFI: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41976973,"author":"prettyStandard","month":"2024-10","story":"New iMac with M4","stance":"reference","why":"Covers HP's offline email client built on EFI.","quote":"the offline email client HP built on EFI","text":"That would be amazing. This reminds me of the offline email client HP built on EFI. Cathode Ray Dude - [VIDEO]"},{"id":41235316,"author":"voidUpdate","month":"2024-08","story":"Negative Rings in Intel Architecture","stance":"reference","why":"Shows HP Daystarter using ring -1 to hijack Windows boot.","quote":"HPs Daystarter, which uses ring -1 to hijack the windows boot process","text":"I found out about these through CRD's video about the horrors of HPs Daystarter, which uses ring -1 to hijack the windows boot process and display its own stuff instead of the boot screen. ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":40793979,"author":"kmeisthax","month":"2024-06","story":"Microsoft breached antitrust rules by bundling Teams and Office, EU says","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a footnote for a 'Bob needs a bonus' feature.","quote":"","text":"\"Public API\" doesn't mean you can't change the API, nor does it limit how quickly extensions or new versions can be added to that API. It just means you have to actually inform people of what you're changing and when. If a client application refuses to implement functionality, that's on them, not the original developer. If I want the new feature, I'll switch. These days however, new features nowadays are usually things I don't want. Not strictly outright anti-features, but usually completely pointless \"Bob needs a bonus[0]\" changes that lets a middle manager put something good in their promo packet. The whole reason why people want compatible file formats and third-party clients is specifically so we can dictate to the originator of those formats and protocols how and how fast they can iterate on their products and limit how bad they can deliberately make them to increase profits. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39947406,"author":"Latty","month":"2024-04","story":"An IRC client in your motherboard","stance":"reference","why":"Covers HP QuickLook, an email client built into firmware, and weirder stunts.","quote":"also goes into some even weirder stuff they did","text":"This instantly made me think of a recent video by the Cathode Ray Dude, talking about HP's 'email client' (read: Outlook Plugin) \"QuickLook\" which was a shipped product implemented this way: [VIDEO] (also goes into some even weirder stuff they did). This does the hard bit of networking that QuickLook skipped though."}]},"ts4BD8AqD9g":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a detailed RCT documentary in threads on the game's optimization and design legacy.","q":{"x":"Folks may also like the noclip documentary on RollerCoaster Tycoon","i":42347266,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":47481850,"author":"HelloUsername","month":"2026-03","story":"The gold standard of optimization: A look under the hood of RollerCoaster Tycoon","stance":"rec","why":"Further RCT material list","quote":"also recommend more about RCT","text":"Fun read, thx! I'd also recommend more about RCT: \"Interview with RollerCoaster Tycoon's Creator, Chris Sawyer (2024)\" [LINK] \"Rollercoaster Tycoon (Or, MicroProse's Last Hurrah)\" [LINK] \"RollerCoaster Tycoon at 25: 'It's mind-blowing how it inspired me'\" [LINK] \"RollerCoaster Tycoon was the last of its kind [video]\" [LINK] \"The Story of RollerCoaster Tycoon\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":42347266,"author":"DavidPiper","month":"2024-12","story":"RollerCoaster Tycoon was the last of its kind [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed RCT documentary including post-RCT2 and OpenRCT2 perspectives","quote":"Folks may also like the noclip documentary on RollerCoaster Tycoon","text":"Folks may also like the noclip documentary on RollerCoaster Tycoon: [VIDEO] . It goes into more detail, particularly about what happened after RCT2, and has some perspectives from people in the OpenRCT2 community."},{"id":28626869,"author":"markedathome","month":"2021-09","story":"Sid Meier and the meaning of ‘Civilization’","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary featuring legacy of RCT and Chris Sawyer's response","quote":"Noclip did a documentary","text":"Noclip did a documentary[1] on RCT and successor games, and tried to interview Chris. The response passed through his representative is stated in the 2nd to last segment (The Legacy of RCT at 30m55) in the video. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bRcu-ysocX4",1]]},"u7TwqpWiY5s":{"wy":"HN mentions it mainly for Conan's roasting of the interviewer and Google's leadership, not for any informational content.","q":{"x":"This interview of Conan, where Vic was the interviewer, was hilarious.","i":35090625,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46728803,"author":"mrsvanwinkle","month":"2026-01","story":"Douglas Adams on the English–American cultural divide over \"heroes\"","stance":"rec","why":"Conan roasting Google's CEO and employees","quote":"Here is Conan's best (natural) performance roasting the Google CEO and gOogLers","text":"As an American I'm a huge fan of HHGG and Rowan Atkinson (not just his Bean character). I'm also a huge fan of Conan O'Brien and his Harvard generation's work for The Simpsons. Would be far ahead of Adams' time. Though while I find them _okay_ I never laughed so hard that my face and stomach hurt from laughter when watching Monty Python anything, not even Black Adder. For other American comedy what made me really, really laugh so hard was Kenan & Kel and The Lonely Island's work for SNL. I've tried to find something as funny as HHGG for so long that I've read P.G. Wodehouse as Adam's main inspiration. Also watched Fry & Laurey. I guess Sacha Cohen as British humour? Since he's Cambridge Highlights alum after all. Found his works extremely hilarious though the parody of racism was disgusting. Here is Conan's best (natural) performance roasting the Google CEO and gOogLers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35090625,"author":"dralley","month":"2023-03","story":"Google dusts off the failed Google+ playbook to fight ChatGPT","stance":"rec","why":"Conan roasting the interviewer on stage","quote":"This interview of Conan, where Vic was the interviewer, was hilarious.","text":"This interview of Conan, where Vic was the interviewer, was hilarious. Conan spent 20 minutes skewering him and then kicked him off the stage. I imagine a lot of Googlers enjoyed that. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1331638,"author":"noisedom","month":"2010-05","story":"Conan O'Brien at Google: The World Has Completely Changed","stance":"critical","why":"Criticizes an interviewer question","quote":"that was a seriously stupid question.","text":"[VIDEO] Who else was astonished at the hair-ball question? I mean, I know it's a casual atmosphere, but that was a seriously stupid question."}]},"vXBfwgwT1nQ":{"wy":"Cited in threads on rigged arcade machines and gambling-like games as the clearest demonstration that skill games can be tuned to be unwinnable.","q":{"x":"Rober built a machine to play the game perfectly, and it still lost","i":27841623,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":19482072,"author":"pavel_lishin","month":"2019-03","story":"The Elaborate, Dying Art of Hustling for Money at Dave and Buster's","stance":"rec","why":"Software influencing outcomes of arcade games.","quote":"Mark Rober covered this in a pretty decent YouTube video","text":"> You aren't just using skills and competing with physics. You are competing with software that is affecting the outcome. Mark Rober covered this in a pretty decent YouTube video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18824815,"author":"gowld","month":"2019-01","story":"YouTube Stars Are Pushing a Shady Gambling Site","stance":"rec","why":"Physics and electronic tricks behind scam arcade games.","quote":"Mark Rober did some nice videos about the analog/physic and electronic tricks of scam games","text":"Back in the legacy world of arcades and carnivals, Mark Rober did some nice videos about the analog/physic and electronic tricks of scam games: [LINK] [VIDEO] It's quite a problem that all these gambling scams are (to more or lesser extent) regulated in geographic jurisdictions, but Internet-bsaed systems bypass all local sovereignty. How long before the governments step up and demand that all Internet businesses get import licenses and traffic must pass through \"customs\" at each ISP \"port of entry\" for their goods and services?"},{"id":37661569,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2023-09","story":"The Casino in Your Pocket","stance":"reference","why":"Example of supposedly skill-based Cyclone game.","quote":"Also applies to other supposedly skill based games at arcades","text":"Also applies to other supposedly skill based games at arcades, like \"Cyclone\"[1]. Interesting to contrast this to how pinball machines were considered gambling for a while, even though they were not[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27839339,"author":"sethammons","month":"2021-07","story":"Sega sued for ‘rigged’ arcade machine","stance":"reference","why":"Robot timing shows game is random, not skill.","quote":"Guy builds a robot to time pressing the button to get the light to land on the winning sections","text":"Reminds me of this one: [VIDEO] Guy builds a robot to time pressing the button to get the light to land on the winning sections and demonstrates it is not skill but a random number generator."},{"id":27841623,"author":"ericpruitt","month":"2021-07","story":"Sega sued for ‘rigged’ arcade machine","stance":"reference","why":"A perfect-play machine still loses the game.","quote":"Rober built a machine to play the game perfectly, and it still lost","text":"Mark Rober also made a video about this for a machine that works similarly. Rober built a machine to play the game perfectly, and it still lost: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28004114,"author":"kbenson","month":"2021-07","story":"One Tenth of a Second","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing Cyclone arcade game is a scam.","quote":"","text":"Yeah, that's also along what I was thinking (as noted later), but also I think you can also get close or achieve if you can prime yourself because you know the event is coming, and get the timing down. Sort of like the cyclone arcade game (if it wasn't a scam[1]). I think there's a huge difference between hitting a random 100ms time and hitting a 100ms time that's coming at a set time that you can see and judge. That's what I meant by \"planned\", even if it poorly encapsulates that. 1: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1],["z71h9XZbAWY",1],["EMKLeS-Uq_8",1]]},"1RLdSvQ-OF0":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a thoughtful and nuanced take on the philosophy of mathematics.","q":{"x":"The whole interview is actually quite nuanced on the question of whether math is discovered or invented and well worth a watch","i":28942240,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28942240,"author":"skoodge","month":"2021-10","story":"Platonism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nuanced Chaitin interview on math as invented versus discovered.","quote":"The whole interview is actually quite nuanced on the question of whether math is discovered or invented and well worth a watch","text":"Gregory Chaitin has an interesting take on this: \"The only place where medieval theology survives is pure math.\" [VIDEO] (The whole interview is actually quite nuanced on the question of whether math is discovered or invented and well worth a watch. There are also interviews with other mathematicians and physicists in the series, for example with Roger Penrose, but most other researchers seem to hold a rather dogmatic and platonist view, which is why I found Chaitin's perspective refreshing.)"},{"id":24650913,"author":"seg_lol","month":"2020-10","story":"Programming language notation is a barrier to entry","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among thought-provoking interviews.","quote":"","text":"You got me to chuckle at, \"thought terminating thought experiment\". I will file that in my cliché attaché. Are you saying that I am asking for a universal perfect compressor, like I am winning the Hutter Prize [1] ? I don't believe I said that. To add to the tooling, what if the forum itself \"learned you\", and took that into account when I respond. It would know everything you wrote, potentially everything you read (on this site), and you in turn would know me. It might help mediate a discussion or prevent folks from talking past each other. What if our systems, could allow us to contextualize communication so that it was grounded in the same basis of knowledge as the receiver? Might some of those signaling mechanisms be the norms and affectations of each branch of study? You have given me some things to think about [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] Looks like they have some thought provoking books and interviews [LINK] Is Mathematics Invented or Discovered? [VIDEO] [4] A physicist looks at linguistics through a complexity lens [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":22899911,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-04","story":"Roger Penrose – Is Mathematics Invented or Discovered? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Chaitin interview on whether math is invented or discovered.","quote":"Gregory Chaitin was interesting as well","text":"Entire playlist: * [LINK] Gregory Chaitin was interesting as well: * [VIDEO] The (apocryphal?) story about Godel not believing in empirical science and only in a priori truths was interesting."}]},"UKxQTvqcpSg":{"wy":"Recommended as a good explanation of what happens when gravity acts on an object.","q":{"x":"Here's a really good explanation of what's actually going on when gravity influences an object.","i":30624789,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30624789,"author":"simonh","month":"2022-03","story":"Four years on, new experiment sees no sign of ‘cosmic dawn’","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains what actually happens when gravity influences an object","quote":"Here's a really good explanation of what's actually going on when gravity influences an object.","text":"One problem is that a lot of simplifications get layered on in pop science explanations of relativity, or actually deep science of any kind. We often talk about the force of gravity, but the thing is it's actually not a force, or even a field, in the same way as other forces and fields in physics. This is why Einstein is so revered in Physics. He didn't just explain a force, otherwise why would he be given pre-eminence over Maxwell who explained electromagnetism? Relativity is something else completely. Here's a really good explanation of what's actually going on when gravity influences an object. Hold on to your chair. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35728101,"author":"moffkalast","month":"2023-04","story":"Why Gravity Is Not Like the Other Forces","stance":"reference","why":"Video on the theory that time gradients cause gravity","quote":"Found the video on this theory","text":"Well maybe not literally, just in practical terms. If it's a time gradient that causes an object to accelerate, then if you can apply a reverse gradient that would just cancel out the forces. But yes, one would need to first figure out how to do that without an equivalent amount of mass on the other side, if it's even physically possible. Here's a stupid idea that's based on my definitely flawed understanding of relativity: If having high relative velocity causes time dilation, then wouldn't spinning a disc at relativistic speeds cause that same time gradient? Maybe we just need 99.9% c rad/s fidget spinners to float in the air just like bricks don't. Found the video on this theory: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26728928,"author":"Strilanc","month":"2021-04","story":"Particle mystery: physicists confirm the muon is more magnetic than predicted","stance":"reference","why":"Gravity as arising from time gradients","quote":"Apparently you can think of the gravitational force as arising from time gradients [1].","text":"It's space time curvature. This is an important distinction, because although you can zero out the spatial component of your 4-vector you can't also zero out the time component. Apparently you can think of the gravitational force as arising from time gradients [1]. Time flows slower closer to the planet, so if your arm is pointing towards the planet then your arm is advancing slightly slower in a particular way and this creates a situation where your arm wants to pull away from you; an apparent force. 1: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["cIQ36Kt7UVg",1],["o1dgrvlWML4",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["viJt_DXTfwA",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1],["kF9KretXqJw",1]]},"hWLjYJ4BzvI":{"wy":"Cited as an example of media consolidation and coordinated messaging, mostly in threads about distrust of news, though it is also posted as a plain source.","q":{"x":"I found it pretty eye opening","i":28168532,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28168532,"author":"mrfusion","month":"2021-08","story":"Illinois Is the First State to Have High Schools Teach News Literacy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dozens of newscasters reading the exact same script","quote":"I found it pretty eye opening","text":"Make sure you begin your class by playing this clip? I found it pretty eye opening: [VIDEO] Description: dozens/hundreds of newscasters reading the exact same script."},{"id":29521923,"author":"publiush","month":"2021-12","story":"Show HN: Decentralized, Mutable, Serverless Torrent Swarm Websites","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of power struggle and information asymmetry","quote":"there is a massive power struggle and a lack of information on our side","text":"I don't think that Facebook and Twitter are a bad thing at all. I think there is confusion as there is a massive power struggle and a lack of information on our side ( [VIDEO] ). Also, I don't know what you mean by \"average Joes.\" I don't think any person is less competent than another, and I would rather help to give access to information to the people, frankly, if I thought someone did have less information. I'm a little confused by your position on people, and afraid of your position on freedom of information and equality."},{"id":29242965,"author":"rasengan","month":"2021-11","story":"Five fold increase in Cardiac deaths of FIFA athletes","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of media being conquered by few corporations","quote":"the news media has been all but completely conquered","text":"This is a result of vaccination. France[1], Taiwan[2] among others are already stopping the distribution of Moderna and Pfizer vaccines. mRNA vaccines actually inject \"code\" into a cell, much like reverse engineering software. Unfortunately, small surface hacks can lead to detrimental disaster in overall systems without a full understanding of what that hack affects within said overall system. We rushed to fight COVID19, and it makes sense. That said, we shouldn't have panicked leaders who aren't able to take a scientific approach. Now, the news media has been all but completely conquered [3]. The internet is being censored on the guise of misinformation. Freedoms are being lost. It's a tremendous leap that the system of government has made within the past couple years. This is not the result of evil actors, but simply, the system itself has so many forces acting upon it which has led it to become evil itself. Finally, and moreover, as the article implies, the mRNA vaccination experiment is failing, and humanity itself is now at risk. People talk about 'robot machines' taking over people as in the Matrix movie, but its much more likely 'complex systems of legislation' will rule and dominate people. Welcome to the Matrix. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28787049,"author":"mrfusion","month":"2021-10","story":"Americans' Trust in Media Dips to Second Lowest on Record","stance":"reference","why":"Shows examples of media manipulation behind the commenter's distrust of news","quote":"here are some highlights from my journey to 0% trust in the media","text":"If you’re interested here are some highlights from my journey to 0% trust in the media: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] I’d be curious to hear yours."},{"id":16735109,"author":"Ajedi32","month":"2018-04","story":"Sinclair Made Dozens of Local News Anchors Recite the Same Script","stance":"reference","why":"Original video of anchors reading same script","quote":"This is the original","text":"FYI, the article seems to be linking to a reupload of the original video for some reason. This is the original: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28913726,"author":"nickthemagicman","month":"2021-10","story":"Covid increases unrelated to vaccination rates","stance":"critical","why":"Exposes Sinclair stations reading identical scripts, as example of media inaccuracy","quote":"Shocked that the media is inaccurate? You should see Sinclair broadcasting.","text":"Shocked that the media is inaccurate? You should see Sinclair broadcasting. One of the 4 or so corporations that own the media. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24083965,"author":"paulmd","month":"2020-08","story":"One-third of American renters expected to miss their August payment","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Cause right now this is your local news station","text":"> I'm starting to think that the outcome of our political systems in the West produces suboptimal compromises engineered by politicians who are quite frankly ignorant, incompetent, corrupt, and really just not good specimens of the species. It's like the system is designed to be mediocre. the problem is that one of the political parties has it as an ideological point that government doesn't and can't work, and sets out to sabotage everything to prove that they're right. Like how florida deliberately makes it impossible to get your unemployment. [LINK] There is, unfortunately, not a good fix for this, as similar parties exist worldwide. But banning Murdoch media and de-consolidating media control in general (returning local stations to local management, etc) would be a good start. Cause right now this is your local news station: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23939823,"author":"nickthemagicman","month":"2020-07","story":"Editorial board of Index and more than 70 staff members resign","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"In the U.S. 90% of the news media is held by 5 conglomerates. You get creepy stuff like this.","text":"Another way to put this..how much does the news media influence people? In the U.S. 90% of the news media is held by 5 conglomerates. You get creepy stuff like this. [VIDEO] Makes you wonder how reliable the news media is in the U.S. I can't even imagine how bad it is in countries where oligarchs control things."},{"id":23940303,"author":"rozab","month":"2020-07","story":"Editorial board of Index and more than 70 staff members resign","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I'm not convinced US media is all that different.","text":"Looks a whole lot like Sinclair's script for stations: [VIDEO] I'm not convinced US media is all that different."},{"id":20596484,"author":"rotred","month":"2019-08","story":"Hong Kong protests: 'I'm in Australia but I feel censored by Chinese students'","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The media is a oligarchy yet it's still \"independent","text":"> I wouldn’t, but that’s because of something deeper than anything we experience in America or Australia. I'm sick of this holier than thou attitude that people have here. There's this patronizing attitude that if only the brain washed Chinese knew what was really happening they would rise up! Yet you know about the Nayirah testimony, MKUltra, Snowden, Cambridge Analytica, migrant camps... why aren't you out protesting, storming congress, why isn't Bush in prison, why isn't Trump impeached? It's not a \"small minority\"; it's the majority. Most Americans don't care enough to find out and if they did they wouldn't care enough to do anything. I'd argue that the propaganda is much deeper and subtle in the West. The media is a oligarchy yet it's still \"independent\" [VIDEO] You believe are the good guys despite history despite everything."},{"id":20184703,"author":"mrguyorama","month":"2019-06","story":"Newspapers don’t need a special law to help compete with Google and Facebook","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"See this for a chilling example","text":"It's worse than that. A significant portion of of American \"local\" news is actually just parroting Sinclair Broadcast Group which is a national group trying to push right wing opinion. See this for a chilling example [VIDEO]"},{"id":19568026,"author":"bduerst","month":"2019-04","story":"Zuckerberg’s Rules Would Hurt Everyone but Facebook","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Facebook would need to be pushing a narrative to it's most popular users to promote on their channels, the same way that Sinclair group does","text":"Not quite. I think for it to be a more apt comparison, Facebook would need to be pushing a narrative to it's most popular users to promote on their channels, the same way that Sinclair group does: [VIDEO] I don't think there's evidence of that right now with Facebook."},{"id":17471667,"author":"exikyut","month":"2018-07","story":"The BeOS file system, an OS geek retrospective","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The news media is of course pure bias and content/distribution/agenda masquerading as fact-based objectivity","text":"I find it sad this is being downvoted. I think this rings true. Perhaps too true? HN has its bents and focuses, but it's at least more conversationally balanced than reddit (perhaps [LINK] partially explains some of the reasons why). The news media is of course pure bias and content/distribution/agenda masquerading as fact-based objectivity (eg, [VIDEO] ). Where else can I look for... the kind of apolitical, discussion-focused information sharing that eg used to happen on blogs circa 2007, but in discussion community form, and within a community framework that is bias-resistant?"},{"id":16741640,"author":"throwaway080383","month":"2018-04","story":"UN: Facebook has turned into a beast in Myanmar","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Exhibit A","text":"I'm not sure I would paint traditional media companies, like say local news stations, as paragons of integrity, autonomy, and having a strong conscience. Exhibit A: [VIDEO]"}]},"1k4x9FrD5k4":{"wy":"HN cites it as the debunking of the Norway salmon sushi story.","q":{"x":"This myth was disproven by Andong on YouTube.","i":34217481,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34217481,"author":"throwaway2037","month":"2023-01","story":"Up to the 1990s, raw salmon sushi was not eaten in Japan","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the Norway salmon sushi story using a 1975 NYTimes article and the Nichirei deal.","quote":"This myth was disproven by Andong on YouTube.","text":"This myth was disproven by Andong on YouTube. He found a NYTimes article from 1975 about sushi in Japan that included salmon. Watch here: [VIDEO] Edit: Andong also specifically talks about the Nichirei deal."},{"id":35401681,"author":"franciscop","month":"2023-04","story":"The surprising roots of Japan's Indian curry","stance":"reference","why":"Shows salmon was popular in Japan before Norway's supposed marketing.","quote":"apparently salmon was popular way before Norway theoretically started marketing it","text":"Salmon for sushi seems not to be the famous story that was going around. Like, it has all the tenets of a nice fake story, talking about Japan and how a foreign more familiar power outsmarted them, but apparently salmon was popular way before Norway theoretically started marketing it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31149098,"author":"gruez","month":"2022-04","story":"Norway convinced Japan to love salmon sushi (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of salmon sushi existing outside Japan before the Norwegian campaign.","quote":"there's plenty of evidence of salmon sushi being a thing outside of japan","text":">How The Desperate Norwegian Salmon Industry Created A Sushi Staple This is slightly misleading. Salmon sushi in japan might not have been a thing prior to norway's marketing campaign, but there's plenty of evidence of salmon sushi being a thing outside of japan[1] and being at least somewhat popular. You could argue that if it weren't for acceptance in japan it wouldn't have been a \"staple\", but that seems a bit tenuous when it was already on magazine covers. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26909136,"author":"pedalpete","month":"2021-04","story":"Wildtype: Sushi-grade salmon grown from Pacific Salmon cells","stance":"reference","why":"Debunks the Norwegian salmon sushi origin story.","quote":"This video says that the Norwegian sushi story is a bit of a myth, or over stated due to poor journalism.","text":"I'm not sure who to trust here. This video says that the Norwegian sushi story is a bit of a myth, or over stated due to poor journalism. [VIDEO] But he definitely has evidence of salmon sushi being eaten before the Norwegians claim to have invented it."}],"r":[["XZGiVzIr8Qg",1],["UE-k4hYHIDE",1]]},"6yqi0FabHHs":{"wy":"Linked as a worked look at the maths and assumptions of orbital sunshades, which concludes they are impractical.","q":{"x":"Here's a video of someone going through the maths and their various assumptions","i":25910217,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38709606,"author":"laweijfmvo","month":"2023-12","story":"Scientists want to put a 'sunshade' in orbit to fight climate change","stance":"rec","why":"Covers fun details such as how big a sunshade would need to be.","quote":"talks about how big it would need to be, among other things.","text":"Since the article doesn't talk about the fun details: [VIDEO] , talks about how big it would need to be, among other things."},{"id":25910217,"author":"flukus","month":"2021-01","story":"Earth is losing ice faster today than in the mid-1990s, study suggests","stance":"reference","why":"Works through the maths and assumptions of space sunshades; concludes impractical.","quote":"Here's a video of someone going through the maths and their various assumptions","text":"Here's a video of someone going through the maths and their various assumptions: [VIDEO] TLDR: It's preposterous in just about every way, from the number of shades needed to the costs to the number of launches required."},{"id":24576730,"author":"pas","month":"2020-09","story":"Mars Problems vs. Venus Problems","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Solar shades the expensive way (as in right now, launch from Earth)","text":"Solar shades the expensive way (as in right now, launch from Earth): [VIDEO] After kickstarting a Lunar economy so you can manufacture and launch them well outside Earth's gravity well: [LINK]"}]},"9BnLbv6QYcA":{"wy":"HN uses it as a recurring punchline whenever a device removes keys or buttons in favor of touch or novelty controls.","q":{"x":"Two of my favourites","i":17485827,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17485827,"author":"dessant","month":"2018-07","story":"No Joke: The Onion Faces Layoffs by Univision, Says Report","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favourite Onion videos","quote":"Two of my favourites","text":"Two of my favourites: Apple Introduces Revolutionary New Laptop With No Keyboard - [VIDEO] Anonymous Hero Donates Hospital 200 Human Kidneys - [LINK]"},{"id":45190578,"author":"ayaros","month":"2025-09","story":"iPhone Air","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"as seen in this classic from The Onion","text":"Meanwhile my 2019 i9 16\" MacBook Pro gets battery life on par with the MacBook Wheel (as seen in this classic from The Onion: [VIDEO] ) I kid you not, the hummingbird battery is real . At least I have something to look forward to when I upgrade."},{"id":45315259,"author":"whartung","month":"2025-09","story":"Are touchscreens in cars dangerous?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's very reminiscent of The Onions MacBook Wheel","text":"The reason they're dangerous is not because you have to interact with them, per se, its simply because they provide to much information that takes a lot of cognitive processing to interpret. Specifically, text. Reading is \"hard\". Even things as simple as the title of the song on the radio. Especially when the text changes. I have a modern LCD on my motorcycle, a BMW, that uses a WonderWheel (rotate to scroll up/down, and push or pull for right/left click) as an interface. It's very reminiscent of The Onions MacBook Wheel[0]. It is absolutely dangerous to use while riding. It's a cognitive black hole. Obviously, the LCD is not alone in this case, the interaction pushes it all up to eleven. But the old school car interface was numbers and small words, and, eventually icons. Consider changing the temperature in a car, for me, I'd shove the hot/cold slider around until the air coming the from the vent was comfortable vs clicking up and down and deciding \"do I want 72 or 73?\". And, yea, maybe it's just me. Perhaps I alone am a hazard when interacting with these things. So, maybe it's not fair for me to project my experiences to the population at large. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44082056,"author":"spicybright","month":"2025-05","story":"Show HN: Rotary Phone Dial Linux Kernel Driver","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"That would have been a hilarious timeline if they just upgraded ipods with cell networking, at least for one model. Then you can use this for typing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42043654,"author":"vel0city","month":"2024-11","story":"Touchscreens are out, and tactile controls are back","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's like you're arguing for the MacBook Wheel","text":"So a knob that doesn't even have the feedback of knowing when you've gotten to the next selection at all, you have to actually stare at it as it goes through the different choices. That doesn't seem better to me at all. Personally, in this idea of a dial I'd like one that can actually give some haptic feedback. Or even better yet just be able to actually tap on the option instead of needing to turn a dial to move a selector on the screen to choose it. At least with an old-fashioned radio knob you got the feedback of if you were tuning into the station by hearing it. But moving a selector on a screen? It's like you're arguing for the MacBook Wheel, as if a knob is the most optimal way to input arbitrary choices on a computer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41288560,"author":"lelanthran","month":"2024-08","story":"Leaving Neovim for Zed","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The average developer averages 10 lines of finished code per day. And even with a raw 10x that amount to account for variance, changes, debugging etc, you'd be at 12 lines PER HOUR. Being generous at a full 80 chars per line, you have a maximum of 1000 keypresses per hour, or an average of one every 3 seconds. Even doubling your typing speed or keyboard response time would have no discernible effect. Just my personal experience, it's not about how fast I can get my ideas down in writing (whether code or not), it's about how often I am broken out of the flow when typing. At one extreme, using your stats above of \"10 finished lines of code per day\", you could argue that using this keyboard ( [VIDEO] ) should have no effect on your productivity, and yet no developer would make or support this argument. Small frictions add up; there's a joelonsoftware post that I read a long time ago about this[1], and it stuck for 24 years! The best editor/environment is the one that gets out of my way. One where I don't need to look things up on google. One in which I have already learned all the shortcuts, and don't need to relearn them to make the software useful. Emacs `M-x sql-connect` is much easier for me to do than to look up, in VS Code, what shortcut has been assigned to the extension I use that does the same thing, and then look it up again in a few years when that extension is no longer supported. Same for Vim commands. Using VS Code, or a full-fledged IDE[2] removes the whole comm"},{"id":40167061,"author":"askl","month":"2024-04","story":"Jeff Lawson buys The Onion","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Apple Introduces Revolutionary New Laptop With No Keyboard\" [VIDEO] \"Markets In Turmoil As Price Of Money Skyrockets To $90 A Dollar\" [LINK]"},{"id":38963034,"author":"tempestn","month":"2024-01","story":"Hertz to sell 20k EVs in shift back to gas-powered cars","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oy, I wouldn't hold up a 2008 BMW as a paragon of usability. That early iDrive system was awful. Three different ways to differently manage climate temperature? Navigation straight out of the Macbook Wheel[1]? I could go on. The auto wipers were definitely better than the Tesla though. I do largely agree with you re the Tesla, although the steering wheel controls I could argue both sides. They're multi-function, which allows you to customize their operation and do a lot more than you can with dedicated controls. Edit: though I guess it's 2024 now. IIRC the 2009s had slightly less bad idrive. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37872307,"author":"Mumps","month":"2023-10","story":"Scrollbars Are Becoming a Problem","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Makes me think of the","text":"Makes me think of the \"MacBook Wheel\" [0] from ages past. It's really a shame how user-hostile design can be. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32757189,"author":"type0","month":"2022-09","story":"Apple Event Live Blog: iPhone 14, Apple Watch, AirPods Pro, and More Expected","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm still waiting for the revolutionary laptop Macbook Wheel","text":"I'm still waiting for the revolutionary laptop Macbook Wheel , hopefully it will run the latest IpadOS [VIDEO] - 2009 video"},{"id":32356964,"author":"rasz","month":"2022-08","story":"My favorite iPhone feature was removed, long live its subpar replacement","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">Human Machine Interface hall of fame along with the old click wheel iPod Classic Apple Introduces Revolutionary New Laptop With No Keyboard [VIDEO] \"just few hundred clicks away\""},{"id":32557036,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2022-08","story":"Apple expands Self Service Repair to Mac notebooks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I thought you were talking about the Apple MacBook Wheel.","text":"You mentioned a wheel in the context of Apple and I thought you were talking about the Apple MacBook Wheel. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28914226,"author":"apostacy","month":"2021-10","story":"MacBook Pro 14-inch and MacBook Pro 16-inch","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of this video from awhile ago by The Onion","text":"I think that many people only decided that they didn't need these features because Apple removed them. It reminds me of this video from awhile ago by The Onion: Apple Introduces Revolutionary New Laptop With No Keyboard[1] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17315355,"author":"bitwize","month":"2018-06","story":"Ask HN: Does anyone know if Apple plans to address the MacBook keyboard issue?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think they'll address it by eliminating the keyboard entirely à la the MacBook Wheel[0], albeit perhaps with a touch screen. From an absolute, ergonomic standpoint, it will suck. But soft keys won't fail or get crud in them like hard keys do, and Gruber will soon be writing thinkpieces about how the keyboard is the most failure-prone part of a computer, and the complaints about recent MBP keyboards are inherent failings of hardware keyboards themselves rather than Apple skimping on quality, and Apple is taking the lead in something the industry should have done a long time ago by eliminating the hardware keyboard. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14686041,"author":"orbitingpluto","month":"2017-07","story":"Differentiating laptops: How about a decent keyboard? (2015)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Do you even vi? Remove everything! Here's a link to the pic of the keyboard for which vi was originally designed on: [LINK] I completely agree with your post. Removing the home/end & function keys is such a waste given the amount of free space for keys on every single notebook ever made. edit: I realized I hadn't taken my initial argument to complete absurdity, \"Apple Introduces Revolutionary New Laptop With No Keyboard\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":14155684,"author":"erikb","month":"2017-04","story":"A two-button mechanical keyboard that types ASCII values, one bit at a time","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That reminds me of this fake ultimate apple ad.","text":"That reminds me of this fake ultimate apple ad. It's not exactly the following video, but maybe it helps remembering it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12791839,"author":"mattbillenstein","month":"2016-10","story":"Apple giveth, and Apple taketh away – the Escape key","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This stuff always brings me back to \"there's nothing more simple than one giant button\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":12816079,"author":"landhar","month":"2016-10","story":"Macbook Pro Touch Bar runs watchOS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"reminded me of this onion video from 2009","text":"Watching the apple event and the various demos of the touch bar reminded me of this onion video from 2009: [VIDEO] I really needed a macbook upgrade (I've been using the same machine since 2008), but I have a feeling that the touch bar will fail and be removed in the next generation of macbooks. But buying the macbook without the touch bar means settling for lesser specs (and given that I'm thinking about buying a machine that would last me another 8 years it feels like a bad choice)."},{"id":3782762,"author":"Xlythe","month":"2012-04","story":"Introducing Gmail Tap","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm surprised. I thought Apple would have been the first to bring something like this to mobile since they already have a similar concept for laptops. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3503237,"author":"thwarted","month":"2012-01","story":"Google+ relaxes real-name policy, allows pseudonyms","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It would also integrate nicely with a no-keyboard computer.","text":"Perhaps G+ should substitute all comment boxes with dropdown menus It would help with data hygiene. It would also integrate nicely with a no-keyboard computer. [VIDEO] \"The aardvark admitted its fault.\""}],"r":[["cK8i6aMG9VM",1]]},"AXlyMrv8_dQ":{"wy":"Mentioned in Nelson and Engelbart threads as a curiosity with amusing cameos by computing pioneers.","q":{"x":"they make amusing cameos in the film by Ted Nelson","i":23559442,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23559442,"author":"raleighm","month":"2020-06","story":"Doug Engelbart’s 1968 demo","stance":"rec","why":"Amusing cameos by Brand and Engelbart in a Nelson short film","quote":"they make amusing cameos in the film by Ted Nelson","text":"On the topic of videos involving Brand and Engelbart, they make amusing cameos in the film by Ted Nelson, which I recently stumbled upon. \"Silicon Valley Story -- A Very Short Comedy\" [VIDEO] [note: brief NSFW moment from 2:10 to 2:25]"},{"id":22524066,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-03","story":"The Call of the Ocean: Hypertext, Universal and Open (1988)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ted Nelson: Computer Lib / Dream Machines (1975) [pdf] (worrydream.com) [LINK] [LINK] For what it's worth, YC is helping Ted Nelson sell his \"Computer Lib / Dream Machines\" book: [LINK] This book is a truly unique and is worth owning in hardcopy format. [LINK] Ted versus The Media Lab [video] (youtube.com) [LINK] [LINK] Interview with Ted Nelson (notion.so) [LINK] [LINK] Ted Nelson on What Modern Programmers Can Learn from the Past [video] (ieee.org) [LINK] [LINK] Ted Nelson struggles with uncomprehending radio interviewer (1979) [audio] (youtube.com) [LINK] [LINK] Ted Nelson’s published papers on computers and interaction, 1965 to 1977 (archive.org) [LINK] [LINK] Ask HN: What is the best resource for understanding Ted Nelson's ZigZag? [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Alan Kay's tribute to Ted Nelson at \"Intertwingled\" Fest (how the script of Tron was the first movie script to ever be edited by a word processing program, on the Alto computer) [LINK] \"Silicon Valley Story\" — a Very Short Romantic Comedy by Ted Nelson A playful story about the microcircuitry of love, with Ted Nelson as an absentminded genius, featuring Doug Engelbart as Ted's father and Stewart Brand as the villainous CEO. Closing song: \"Information Flow\", sung by Donna Spitzer and the auteur. With Timothy Leary as the Good Venture Capitalist! [VIDEO] Ted Nelson's Channel [LINK]"},{"id":15271564,"author":"tosh","month":"2017-09","story":"Here I Stand, at Age 80","stance":"reference","why":"Short romantic comedy featuring Engelbart and Brand","quote":"A playful story about the microcircuitry of love, with Ted Nelson as an absentminded genius","text":"\"\"\" \"Silicon Valley Story\" — a Very Short Romantic Comedy by Ted Nelson A playful story about the microcircuitry of love, with Ted Nelson as an absentminded genius, featuring Doug Engelbart as Ted's father and Stewart Brand as the villainous CEO. Closing song: \"Information Flow\", sung by Donna Spitzer and the auteur. \"\"\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xtpgkX588nM",1]]},"Bch5B23_pu0":{"wy":"Cited in threads on camless, digital valve and free-piston engines as a look at Koenigsegg's freevalve work.","q":{"x":"For those of you interested in seeing some more details on Koenigsegg's work with their freevalve engine","i":17748381,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17748381,"author":"nounaut","month":"2018-08","story":"Fully digital valves open up engine possibilities","stance":"rec","why":"More details on Koenigsegg's freevalve engine work.","quote":"For those of you interested in seeing some more details on Koenigsegg's work with their freevalve engine","text":"For those of you interested in seeing some more details on Koenigsegg's work with their freevalve engine: [VIDEO] (2013) [LINK] (2016)"},{"id":11464744,"author":"andrewdb","month":"2016-04","story":"How a Car Engine Works","stance":"reference","why":"Koenigsegg's work on camless valve technology.","quote":"Koenigsegg (among others, I assume) has been working on this tech [0][1].","text":"The timing chain and the camshaft seems really inefficient; I'm looking forward to when camless designs become more mainstream. Koenigsegg (among others, I assume) has been working on this tech [0][1]. 0: [LINK] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10717758,"author":"Already__Taken","month":"2015-12","story":"Car Engines: The incredible shrinking machine","stance":"reference","why":"Example of 3D printing a whole engine.","quote":"3d printing a whole engine must surely enable some impressive things.","text":"There is a lot more to come from the ICE when it comes to manufacturing processes and the electronics. Work like this: [VIDEO] 3d printing a whole engine must surely enable some impressive things. I remember watching a little demonstration using machine learning for the engine timing once you can get the belts out."},{"id":7977270,"author":"Stolpe","month":"2014-07","story":"No crankshaft, no problem: Toyota's free piston engine is brilliant","stance":"reference","why":"Koenigsegg documentary segment on a similar engine concept.","quote":"I remember seeing something similar to this a year ago in a documentary series concerning the small Swedish supercar manufacturer Koenigsegg.","text":"I remember seeing something similar to this a year ago in a documentary series concerning the small Swedish supercar manufacturer Koenigsegg. See it here: [VIDEO] . It sounds really interesting."}]},"EtsXgODHMWk":{"wy":"HN cites it as the original and still-wow reference whenever newer ultra-high-speed light imaging comes up.","q":{"x":"I think you are gonna love this","i":31303675,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31303675,"author":"bXVsbGVy","month":"2022-05","story":"Exporting Data from Old Gear Through LCD Sniffing","stance":"rec","why":"Trillion-fps imaging of light","quote":"I think you are gonna love this","text":"Since you are finding about femto-photo today, I think you are gonna love this: [LINK] [VIDEO] By the way. Femto laser are somewhat common nowadays. They are used in eye surgery and microscopy. I even seem papers talking about using them in finger print scanners! See Optical coherence tomography for more info."},{"id":45651242,"author":"jval43","month":"2025-10","story":"A laser pointer at 2B FPS [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Original MIT video that inspired the project","quote":"The original MIT video from 2011","text":"The original MIT video from 2011: \"Visualizing video at the speed of light — one trillion frames per second\" [VIDEO] (project site: [LINK] ) He mentions this as the inspiration in his previous video ( [LINK] )."},{"id":45775881,"author":"westurner","month":"2025-10","story":"Man Builds Two Billion FPS Camera That Records at the Speed of Light","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier trillion-fps MIT work","quote":"","text":"\"Visualizing video at the speed of light — one trillion frames per second\" (2011; MIT) [VIDEO] \"Single-shot real-time femtosecond imaging of temporal focusing\" (2018) [LINK] \"Filming the Speed of Light at 10 Trillion FPS\" (2019; The Slow Mo Guys) [LINK]"},{"id":44804378,"author":"getpost","month":"2025-08","story":"Los Alamos is capturing images of explosions at 7 millionths of a second","stance":"reference","why":"Video explaining 1.7 ps exposure imaging","quote":"Video with explanation.","text":"It's an apples-to-oranges comparison, but I'm reminded of the ~10^12 fps (1.7 ps exposures) demonstrated in work at MIT[0], which was for a completely different application. [0] [LINK] EDIT: Video with explanation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44102673,"author":"westurner","month":"2025-05","story":"'Strange metals' point to a whole new way to understand electricity","stance":"reference","why":"High-speed imaging of light pulses","quote":"","text":"Photons certainly appear to have a real physical location with 1e9 FPS imaging capabilities: \"Visualizing video at the speed of light — one trillion frames per second\" (2012) [VIDEO] But is there an identity function for a photon(s), and is \"time-polarization\" necessary for defining an identity function for photons?"},{"id":42198117,"author":"soulofmischief","month":"2024-11","story":"U of T computational imaging researchers harness AI to fly with light in motion","stance":"reference","why":"Prior art capturing light in a coke bottle","quote":"One of the coolest things I'd ever seen.","text":"For those curious, here is prior art from 12 years ago, capturing light in a coke bottle with a single streak camera. [VIDEO] [LINK] I remember when this dropped and where I was when I watched it and read the paper. One of the coolest things I'd ever seen. This seems like a great extension of the work. I'm okay with trading accuracy for crude usefulness as a model. Making this interactive and putting it in the hands of curious minds is the next step."},{"id":42200283,"author":"itishappy","month":"2024-11","story":"U of T computational imaging researchers harness AI to fly with light in motion","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier video used a blank red label","quote":"","text":"> There's zero purpose for having a bottle with any label. Light interacts quite differently with the label than the material of the bottle. We get to observe scattering, diffusion, color... y'know, science stuff. It is rather interesting that the first video (from 12 years ago) used a blank red label instead. (See how it differs in from the rest of the bottle? I particularly like how it obscures the pulse itself and highlights the wavefront on the surface.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":42200450,"author":"itishappy","month":"2024-11","story":"U of T computational imaging researchers harness AI to fly with light in motion","stance":"reference","why":"Same effect captured without AI","quote":"It's quite realistic, here's the same thing without AI","text":"It's quite realistic, here's the same thing without AI: [VIDEO] I still share your concern, however, particularly because they seem to be avoid moving the camera without time moving as well. I was expecting bullet-time!"},{"id":3347130,"author":"jcr","month":"2011-12","story":"Trillion-frame-per-second video","stance":"reference","why":"MIT camera capturing light at a trillion fps","quote":"MIT researchers have created a new imaging system that can acquire visual data at a rate of one trillion exposures per second.","text":"The relevant video from MIT Media Lab is here: [VIDEO] > MIT researchers have created a new imaging system that can acquire visual data at a rate of one trillion exposures per second. That's fast enough to produce a slow-motion video of light traveling through objects. > [LINK]"}],"r":[["c0O6UXrOZJo",1]]},"FBOLk9s9Ci4":{"wy":"HN points founders to YC's content like this for practical startup advice on product-market fit.","q":{"x":"There's great content about that at the YC YouTube channel.","i":31872335,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31872335,"author":"jaxr","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: How to raise a seed round in a down market?","stance":"rec","why":"YC advice on product market fit","quote":"There's great content about that at the YC YouTube channel.","text":"I don't think you hit product market fit, though. It should be pretty obvious. If you had, and the market was as big as you say, then people would be flocking in just by word of mouth. I would suggest to iterate on product, talk to your users, get some good metrics on usage, etc. There's great content about that at the YC YouTube channel. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":41797016,"author":"BrandiATMuhkuh","month":"2024-10","story":"Typical Startup Mistakes – A YC-Collection","stance":"reference","why":"YC startup advice cited among mistakes collection","quote":"","text":"Hi HN, I was the technical co-founder of a couple of startups that showed early promise but didn’t ultimately succeed. After my last attempt, I started digging into the reasons why, and found that YC pretty much has all the advice I wish I had from the start. One thing that really stood out was their list of “What not to do as a startup” (aka “Typical Startup Mistakes”). It’s a goldmine of advice that could have saved me a lot of headaches. I’ve put these insights together in a presentation and thought this would be the right place to share them. --- For convenience, I’ve added the presentation content as text below. --- Y Combinator Advice Authors - Michael Seibel (YC Partner and Managing Director) - Paul Graham (YC Co-Founder) - Jessica Livingston (YC Co-Founder) - Dalton Caldwell (YC Partner) - Kat Manalac (YC Partner) Definition of a Startup - \"A startup is a temporary organization in search of a scalable, repeatable, profitable business model.\" [The Startup Owner's Manual, 2012]. Definition of Product/Market Fit - \"The customers are buying the product just as fast as you can make it -- or usage is growing just as fast as you can add more servers. Money from customers is piling up in your company checking account. You're hiring sales and customer support staff as fast as you can.\" [Marc Andreessen] Working on the wrong thing - You don't personally know 10 people with the problem - You solve a problem you don't care about - You solve a problem for people you don't care abo"},{"id":21692089,"author":"james_impliu","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: What are successful projects that started as prototyped hypotheses?","stance":"reference","why":"Twitch origin as example of rapid prototyping","quote":"","text":"Two examples of rapid prototyping spring to mind: * Twitch, started as one guy streaming his life then they realised lots of gamers were watching, and that they'd like to be able to stream [VIDEO] * Segment, started as a thumbs up/down tool for professors in lectures to work out when students are getting confused. They realised everyone just went to Facebook instead, then they wondered why they couldn't tell this when they were remote! [LINK]"}],"r":[["C27RVio2rOs",1]]},"Mri66Uz6-8Y":{"wy":"Cited in init-system and Unix debates as FreeBSD's stance on launchd versus systemd, and recommended for its view of FreeBSD's future.","q":{"x":"Check out JKH's talk about FreeBSD and potential future directions","i":8649203,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8649203,"author":"peatmoss","month":"2014-11","story":"Ask HN: Can we talk about Linux vs. OS/X?","stance":"rec","why":"Hubbard on FreeBSD's future, informed by his Apple Unix experience.","quote":"Check out JKH's talk about FreeBSD and potential future directions","text":"> compensates for a mish-mash of inefficient and insecure technology on the backend side of things. . . no matter how optimized and clean the back end is (relative to OS X) I'm not sure that's really an accurate characterization of OSX. In terms of security, I don't see where there have been any great number of exploits attacking the kernel (XNU) or userland (mostly FreeBSD) layers. Recent high-profile vulnerabilities like heartbleed were hardly OSX specific. I'm also not sure that those layers can really be called inefficient relative to Linux. Mach was a great deal more modular in terms of separation of concerns as compared to Linux (good for cleanliness/security, but never had great performance). However, with their XNU kernel, they seem to have made some of the same tradeoffs as Linux with respect to pure microkernel separation of concerns. OSX has also done, in my opinion, a good job of rethinking parts of the traditional Unix infrastructure that needed rethinking. Check out JKH's talk about FreeBSD and potential future directions, and see how much that is informed by his experience leading the Unix technologies group at Apple (for the record, JKH was also one of the cofounders of FreeBSD, so he's not exactly a slouch): [VIDEO] I'd say the biggest reasons OSX never took off as a server OS are 1) licensing 2) coupling to Apple hardware 3) most of Apple's value-add is for desktops, laptops, and smaller portables--in other words, not compellingly better than other free nixe"},{"id":10868226,"author":"Someone","month":"2016-01","story":"FreeBSD Jails the hard way","stance":"reference","why":"Hubbard talk source for slides on rc.d and systemd, 49 minutes.","quote":"Talk is by Jordan Hubbard, FreeBSD co-founder","text":"[LINK] , slides 32 and 33: \"etc/rc.d is quite sophisticated for what it does, but the paint on /etc/rc is obvious\" \"Too many things need to know explicitly about dependencies\" \"Even the Linux die-hards have essentially grasped the necessity of systemd\" Talk is by Jordan Hubbard, FreeBSD co-founder ( [LINK] ) Discussion at [LINK] , video at [VIDEO] (49 minutes)"},{"id":10497139,"author":"2trill2spill","month":"2015-11","story":"nosh: Linux/BSD init, with systemd compat","stance":"reference","why":"Explains reasoning for bringing launchd to FreeBSD.","quote":"FreeBSD next 10 years","text":"> Instead, you're ignoring all of the flaws I listed in favor of a red herring dismissal by focusing on my criticism of your question. Not everything revolves around your feelings. I'm not ignoring the issue you have pointed out, rather It would be nice to hear about nosh. There's significantly more information about launchd than nosh, for example launchd has two videos, explaining the reasoning and methodology for bringing in launchd and the other apple libraries. FreeBSD next 10 years: [VIDEO] NextBSD intro: [LINK] A quick search on google and their are no videos about nosh. The nosh website doesn't contain much information about the design of nosh. Looking around the source of nosh the various html files look promising and i'm going through those. Unfortunately my c++ skills are inadequate to judge the code quality of nosh effectively. Which on a side note I think will hurt nosh chances of being adopted, because the open source os world seems biased to using C. I't just harder to find stuff out about nosh and you seemed knowledgeable on the subject, hence the original question. So is there any good documentation on nosh besides the source it's self?"},{"id":9985808,"author":"cjsthompson","month":"2015-08","story":"The Systemd Chronicles","stance":"reference","why":"Cited at 27:23 as FreeBSD's stance on launchd-style init.","quote":"And as far as FreeBSD is concerned : (At 27:23)","text":"It is perplexing that someone who you deem to be \"historically and technically illiterate\" and who you accuse of not \"actually reading Haiku's scheme\" actually has been reading the Haiku commit logs and can point to this : [LINK] \"* This will be heavily inspired by Apple's launchd, as well as systemd -- for now it really doesn't do a whole lot, though.\" Note the \"as well as\" here. [LINK] \"* This follows systemd design; since those jobs are fixed, it doesn't matter where you maintain them.\" And as far as FreeBSD is concerned : [VIDEO] (At 27:23) I'm not sure who appears to be more historically and technically-challenged here. In the scope of Linux it is appropriate to call systemd revolutionary. Criticizing the choice of this term only shows how you are reduced to nitpicking word choices. Just like you are reduced to making assumptions about my historical and technical literacy. The part where you feel the need to show that you know some things like some teacher's pet is entertaining too. We'll let others judge whether the linked article is a based on an argument to moderation or not. Which it most definitely is. Just because you say it isn't doesn't make it so. I am enjoying making a fool out of you."}],"r":[["_ul7X5js1vE",1]]},"QGw-cy0ylCc":{"wy":"HN points to the Mill talks as an excellent, deep look at an unconventional CPU design, especially in threads on registers and instruction sets.","q":{"x":"the Mill was described in an excellent series of talks","i":38598968,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38598968,"author":"wood_spirit","month":"2023-12","story":"Clockhands: Rename-Free Instruction Set Architecture for Out-of-Order Processors","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent talk series on temporal addressing and the belt.","quote":"the Mill was described in an excellent series of talks","text":"This is reminiscent of the Mill CPU which had temporal addressing and called the history a “belt”. the Mill was described in an excellent series of talks: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10108282,"author":"w0000t","month":"2015-08","story":"What's new in CPUs since the 80s and how does it affect programmers?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the belt: FIFO of operands addressed by relative offset.","quote":"Instead you have a FIFO queue( belt ), where every element( register ) is accessible and has a relative address( offset ).","text":"No ordinary registers. Instead you have a FIFO queue( belt ), where every element( register ) is accessible and has a relative address( offset ). [VIDEO]"},{"id":10108408,"author":"w0000t","month":"2015-08","story":"What's new in CPUs since the 80s and how does it affect programmers?","stance":"reference","why":"Designer explains how Mill avoids register renaming.","quote":"Here is a talk from one of the designers explaining how it is done","text":"False. Mill does not do register renaming. ( Or use \"lies\". I failed to comprehend you second sentence. ) Here is a talk from one of the designers explaining how it is done: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9054410,"author":"runeks","month":"2015-02","story":"64-bit Linux Return-Oriented Programming","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This video[1] mentions that \"nothing with pins on the bottom\" has been made yet.","text":"How far has development come with the Mill CPU? This video[1] mentions that \"nothing with pins on the bottom\" has been made yet. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6535801,"author":"brigade","month":"2013-10","story":"Why Registers Are Fast and RAM Is Slow","stance":"reference","why":"Presentations on register limits in modern CPU design.","quote":"see the presentations by the mill CPU guy [1]","text":"The studies would vary over time because CPU design and bottle necks have changed. Early designs were of course limited by transistor count, now we have OoOe and physical registers are limited by muxers and latency (see the presentations by the mill CPU guy [1] Saving registers in functions is mostly irrelevant - you only save what you'd use, so saving more means fewer spills within the function. Saving on context switches (interrupts alone aren't a big deal) was indeed a problem back when AltiVec was designed, thus it has a special register to keep track of which registers need to be saved. In modern designs this is less of a problem, between higher frequencies, multiple cores, and the other effects of a context switch dominating (effective flush of l1 cache and predictors). The interesting bits nowadays are that load/store is expensive power-wise, which was what ARM identified as the major motivation behind having 32 registers (fewer spills in functions) and OoOe designs. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6217090,"author":"mtrimpe","month":"2013-08","story":"Smalltalk runtime in the browser","stance":"reference","why":"Mill CPU cited as good architecture fit for Lisp.","quote":"The Mill CPU [1] that was discussed a while ago is quite close to being the perfect architecture for a Lisp though","text":"The Mill CPU [1] that was discussed a while ago is quite close to being the perfect architecture for a Lisp though, so hopefully many of other parts of these ideas will also come back in even more impressive forms. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13384044,"author":"riazrizvi","month":"2017-01","story":"Mill Computing in 2017","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"According to the CTO, 80% of software operations are loops","text":"According to the CTO, 80% of software operations are loops ( [VIDEO] )? I need convincing of that before I watch the rest of the talk. I suspect it would be a fundamental premise for the architecture of the chip. If it's not true, the stats are probably skewed for an unusual benchmark."}]},"STKCRSUsyP0":{"wy":"Cited as a useful taxonomy for pinning down what an event-driven design actually is.","q":{"x":"This was interesting as far as I remember but not what I was looking for","i":22521503,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22521503,"author":"dathinab","month":"2020-03","story":"Data-Oriented Architecture","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on event-driven architecture.","quote":"I think that one was good","text":"I think that one was good: - [VIDEO] I wasn't able to find any other talk I watched back then or any of the stuff I did read, but I then last time I looked into talks and reading material about this was ~2.5 Years ago and while a lot of new software and tooling was done since then the principles didn't change. Try some of the other GOTO; talks about it if you like listening to talks, they tend to be quite good. This was interesting as far as I remember but not what I was looking for: - [LINK]"},{"id":32448595,"author":"nivertech","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Are any new systems *not* event sourced and microservices?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You're right about microservices, but IMO you're mistaken Event Sourcing (CQRS/ES)[1,2] with EDA (Event-Driven Architectures)[3] in general. There are many different types of EDAs. There are at least 3 relevant dimensions when choosing a Software Architecture: 1. complexity of the domain 2. scalability requirements 3. company stage / number of developers working on the same codebase Microservices are mostly solving #3, and to a lesser extent #2. They mostly solve the problem of too many developers blocking each other on a monolithic codebase (NOTE: monolithic doesn't mean non-modular). Event Sourcing and friends (DDD, CQRS, EventStorming, etc.) are mostly solving #1. It's also technology/vendor-agnostic, so makes migration to other tech (including microservices) easier, and most importantly possible at all. So for a complex domain in a small company I would choose a CQRS/ES monolith, but for a simple domain and high scalability in a large company maybe CRUD-based microservices are making more sense. -- [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] The Many Meanings of Event-Driven Architecture • Martin Fowler • GOTO 2017 [VIDEO] What do you mean by “Event-Driven”? [LINK] tagged by: event architectures [LINK]"},{"id":16627331,"author":"leoc","month":"2018-03","story":"Message-Oriented Programming (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Martin Fowler on implementing message-oriented design as a pattern or framework.","quote":"And here's a Martin Fowler talk about the practise of implementing it informally as a pattern or framework","text":"As a first-class language feature this goes back at least to 1985 and Linda [LINK] and tuplespaces. (You'd know about Linda already if you'd read Concepts, Techniques, and Models of Computer Programming . Don't delay, read CTM today: [LINK] ) And here's a Martin Fowler talk about the practise of implementing it informally as a pattern or framework: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16631816,"author":"leoc","month":"2018-03","story":"Message-Oriented Programming (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Fowler's taxonomy of event-driven patterns.","quote":"I think Fowler would describe the model in the article as more like \"Event-carried State Transfer\"","text":"I think Fowler would describe the model in the article as more like \"Event-carried State Transfer\": [VIDEO] ."}]},"VVvnTByzTmA":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on college admissions and affirmative action as Sowell's argument about unintended consequences.","q":{"x":"here's a great interview from Sowell about the unintended consequences","i":17447121,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17447121,"author":"ericdykstra","month":"2018-07","story":"Why Asian-Americans feel powerless in New York’s elite high schools","stance":"rec","why":"Sowell on unintended consequences of affirmative action","quote":"here's a great interview from Sowell about the unintended consequences","text":"The entire doctrine of Disparate Impact, and Affirmative Action which is borne from it, is one of the most illiberal policies in America. I really hope a constitutionalist Supreme Court can reverse this madness. On Affirmative Action in specific, here's a great interview from Sowell about the unintended consequences that arise from the awful policy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24727756,"author":"sebmellen","month":"2020-10","story":"Justice Department sues Yale for illegal practices in admissions","stance":"reference","why":"Sowell discusses mismatch and dropout dynamics","quote":"It's something Thomas Sowell documented in one of his books.","text":"No, I'm not moving the goalposts! Sowell's point is exactly that - being the worst in your class, and sharing that with all your peers of the same race, is tantamount to constant demoralization and disenfranchisement. This dynamic leads to higher drop out rates. Either way, it's really not my theory. It's something Thomas Sowell documented in one of his books. Here he is talking about it: \"Thomas Sowell: affirmative action creates academic failure & resentment\" - [VIDEO] . Sowell is black, if it matters. [LINK] ."},{"id":18804334,"author":"asabjorn","month":"2019-01","story":"IQ is largely a pseudoscientific swindle","stance":"reference","why":"Sowell video on mismatch effects of admissions","quote":"or if prefer a video [2].","text":"If the SAT test wasn't selective in some meaningful way then people admitted through affirmative action should be expected to do approximately as well as the people that was admitted purely through merit. The outcome says otherwise [1], or if prefer a video [2]. Mismatching people to schools based upon an ideal hurts those people when they come unprepared relative to their peers. Many diverse people admitted through affirmative action with a dream to do the sciences abandon them for other less rigorous disciplines, and it doesn't have to be so if they were matched with a school without the social engineering attempts of ideologues. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17879054,"author":"risto1","month":"2018-08","story":"Asian-American Students Suing Harvard Over Admissions Win Justice Dept. Support","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Why do people think affirmative action is a good idea? It's not meritocratic. People should be judged based on their skill, not based on their gender, race, or ethnicity. We should be striving for a fair society, not equality of outcome It's insulting to those minorities, it implies that they can't be smart enough to make it without this kind of leg up, and they will never know the extent to which this played a role in their success even if they're absolutely solid at what they do. And I think there's definitely a negative psychological effect to minorities by doing these kinds of things If a student gets into a school which is above their skill level or general academic interest, they could burn out and loose interest in a profession that otherwise would've been a good fit for them. A similar lack of motivation can also happen for someone who can only get into a school that's below their true skill level because they don't feel that their being valued fairly It isn't all encompassing -- it's not just gender and race, there are ethnicities that are widely discriminated against and they don't get a similar leg up. I don't think AA should be applied at all, just pointing this out There are problems in some minority communities struggle with for sure, but this isn't the right way to solve it at all One of big reasons why asian and jewish communities do better here is just culture -- their cultures highly value intellectual pursuits. I knew an asian guy that went to Harvard and t"}]},"ayh0qebfD2g":{"wy":"HN points to it as the original proposal that explains the vision behind the Commander X16.","q":{"x":"His original proposal video[0] explains it well","i":35400945,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35400945,"author":"colejohnson66","month":"2023-04","story":"Commander X16 2023 update [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Original proposal explains the X16 vision well.","quote":"His original proposal video[0] explains it well","text":"His original proposal video[0] explains it well: it's not supposed to be powerful; he is aware of the Raspberry Pi but doesn't see it fitting his vision. It's supposed to be something like the C64 and other computers of the era where you could understand how all the various chips interacted and worked. Things like DDR memory abstract all that stuff away. Computers today don't \"put an address on the bus and read the data\" like parallel SRAM/DRAM; they send a command packet to the memory chips and wait for the data packet to come back. Audio interfaces, especially over PCIe, aren't as simple anymore either. You can't write direct to the screen anymore like you could with VGA cards on old IBM PCs. All these abstractions have helped computers become so complex that one person can't possibly understand it all at once. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20975127,"author":"kgwxd","month":"2019-09","story":"Build a 6502 Computer","stance":"reference","why":"One of the videos about the X16 project.","quote":"He did some videos about the project [2] [3].","text":"I really can't wait for that to happen. I'm going to be throwing money at that project like crazy. There's already an emulator for the OS [1]. I had never done any C64 programming, but I was able to do some BASIC tutorials using it with the exception of any video stuff, since that will be different on the X16. He did some videos about the project [2] [3]. I absolutely hate that he formed the group on Facebook. I'll be missing so much. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":20742462,"author":"tcbawo","month":"2019-08","story":"Pico-8 Virtual Fantasy Console is an idealized constrained modern day game maker","stance":"reference","why":"Introduces the 8-Bit Guy's expandable C64-like computer project.","quote":"David Murray (The 8-bit Guy) and others are building an expandable, C64ish computer with sourceable parts and modern I/O connectivity","text":"David Murray (The 8-bit Guy) and others are building an expandable, C64ish computer with sourceable parts and modern I/O connectivity (eg. VGA). They're calling it Commander X16. It's very early, but it looks promising as a retro gaming/educational platform. Due to hardware limitations (eg 6502-based CPU), it will probably be fairly limited to Basic and assembly programming and w/o networking capability. But, it will be expandable, so who knows. I think they will eventually have a Kickstarter. Should be a lot of fun! [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cIQ9IXSUzuM",1],["p8u_k2LIZyo",1],["w2A8q3XIhu0",1]]},"cPNkH-7PRTk":{"wy":"Referenced for Hickey's reasoning on design choices such as macros and not building on SBCL.","q":{"x":"this is not only a very good introduction to Clojure and the design decisions made by Rich Hickey","i":20337667,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20337667,"author":"tosh","month":"2019-07","story":"Clojure for Lisp Programmers (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to Clojure and its design decisions by Rich Hickey","quote":"this is not only a very good introduction to Clojure and the design decisions made by Rich Hickey","text":"Context: this is not only a very good introduction to Clojure and the design decisions made by Rich Hickey it also is a video that isn't very widely known. The video was uploaded to Youtube after the migration from Blip.tv but somehow got broken ( [VIDEO] ) and lived in a parallel universe in Youtube. I remember I found the video a few years ago but was unable to watch it. Last year someone re-uploaded the video to Youtube and it seems to works now. Transcript: [LINK]"},{"id":19041380,"author":"aidenn0","month":"2019-01","story":"Scripting in Common Lisp","stance":"reference","why":"Rich Hickey on handling macro symbol issues in Clojure","quote":"In particular, CL style macros with out some first-class concept of symbols are a footgun[1]","text":"Hy may have changed since I last looked at it, but when I had looked at it, Hy was more of an s-expression syntax bolted onto python. Some random thoughts on why this matters: Clojure is very much not a thin s-expression layer for java; that would have been terrible (Python is more like lisp than java which is why Hy is tenable whatsoever). In particular, CL style macros with out some first-class concept of symbols are a footgun[1], but I'm sure I could find other issues. Another item that can't be ignored is that Hy selected the clojure syntax, which is going to be a turn-off for anyone coming from Lisp or Scheme. Finally, there are hundreds of thin s-expression layers on top of existing languages, and most of them are terrible; Hy has done nothing to stand out from this crowd, so most lispers will not look closely at it. 1: See [VIDEO] for Rich's discussion of how he managed this issue in Clojure."},{"id":15598682,"author":"joe-user","month":"2017-11","story":"Effective Programs: 10 Years of Clojure","stance":"reference","why":"Rich Hickey's reasons for not using SBCL","quote":"He was certainly aware of SBCL. He had a number of reasons for not wanting to use it.","text":"> Pity he hadn't heard of Armed Bear Common Lisp ( [LINK] ), which runs on the JVM. He was certainly aware of SBCL. He had a number of reasons for not wanting to use it. [1] > I don't mind mutable state, since the real world is not functional: I hear this argument often, but the \"real world\" is not linked lists or hash maps or mutable or 0s and 1s either, but they are all useful representations. > real programs are all about side effects Clojure has plenty of facilities for side effects, and without having to resort to monads (though you could if you wanted). [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rI8tNMsozo0",1],["udlMSe5-zP8",1],["SrKj4hYic5A",1],["lKXe3HUG2l4",1],["9Q6sLbz37gk",1],["7jiPeIFXb6U",1]]},"ceCOhdgRBoc":{"wy":"HN commenters mostly cite it as an investigative documentary on lobbying and influence operations in UK politics, with some presenting it as a critical exposure of the lobby's tactics.","q":{"x":"There's a very interesting investigative documentary by Al Jazeera that shows how Israel funds and manages the Israeli lobby in the UK.","i":14283032,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14283032,"author":"Cyph0n","month":"2017-05","story":"On Campuses Far From China, Still Under Beijing’s Watchful Eye","stance":"rec","why":"Al Jazeera undercover documentary on Israeli lobby in the UK","quote":"There's a very interesting investigative documentary by Al Jazeera that shows how Israel funds and manages the Israeli lobby in the UK.","text":"There's a very interesting investigative documentary by Al Jazeera that shows how Israel funds and manages the Israeli lobby in the UK. I'll look for the link and edit this comment. Edit : Here is a link to part 1 of the series: [VIDEO] . You can follow the \"Up next\" link to watch the other parts; there are 4 parts in total."},{"id":31077488,"author":"l33tbro","month":"2022-04","story":"UK Government Officials Infected with Pegasus","stance":"reference","why":"Undercover journalist recording a group trying to subvert UK Labour Party","quote":"Anyone ever see the investigative journalist piece of the Zionist group trying to subvert the UK Labour Party?","text":"Israel seems to have a relative degree of immunity when it comes to subverting UK pol. Anyone ever see the investigative journalist piece of the Zionist group trying to subvert the UK Labour Party? They had a journalist go undercover for 6 months recording all kinds of things they weren't meant to: [VIDEO] There was only minor fallout from this incident, and I imagine it will be the same here."},{"id":19847162,"author":"aptidude187","month":"2019-05","story":"Ex-NSA man slams Israel for strike on alleged Hamas cyber attackers","stance":"critical","why":"Cited as exposing smearing tactics of the Israeli lobby","quote":"The Lobby\" - Documentary exposing the vile and venomous smearing tactics of the Israeli lobby","text":"Poor Israel, can't even dehumanise, colonise a people, drive them out of their homes and kill them without being criticised. You are dishing out the same old washed out propaganda and people know it's utter bullshit. Israel is the coloniser and the oppressor no matter how hard you push your propaganda, people can see the evidence from too many sources now, even secular jewish ones. Israeli Solider testimony [ [LINK] ] \"The Lobby\" - Documentary exposing the vile and venomous smearing tactics of the Israeli lobby (undercover reporter is jewish) [ [VIDEO] ] The Jewish State (Der Judenstaat), (1896) [ [LINK] ] On Palestine - Noam Chomsky [ [LINK] ] The Ethnic Cleansing of Palestine - Ilan Pappe (jewish historian) [ [LINK] ]"}],"r":[["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["le71yVPh4uk",1]]},"fKSSY1gzCEs":{"wy":"Cited in caliper threads as a teardown showing how cheap digital calipers work and that they are better than expected.","q":{"x":"It's a fascinating device.","i":32890047,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32890047,"author":"djmips","month":"2022-09","story":"Precision DIY calipers? That’s a moiré","stance":"rec","why":"Explores how an inexpensive caliper senses position with PCBs.","quote":"It's a fascinating device.","text":"That's interesting. Big Clive explored a set of inexpensive calipers that seemed to work on an interference pattern but sensed with two PCBs passing over each other. He mentions in his theory of operation moire pattern measuring devices. It's a fascinating device. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40009649,"author":"RA2lover","month":"2024-04","story":"Method for making a holographic linear position encoder","stance":"reference","why":"Example of capacitive vernier encoder in cheap calipers.","quote":"","text":"Haven't looked into this deeply, but isn't this basically a vernier pattern printed into a hologram? How would this be better for mass manufacture compared to a vernier pattern capacitive encoder as seen in cheap calipers such as [1] ? I'm pretty sure photodetectors would be more expensive than capacitive sensors. AFAIK these are also based on an expired patent which i believe to be US4743902 [2], that was mainly used by Mitutoyo. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":34693240,"author":"adolph","month":"2023-02","story":"How cheap digital verniers work (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown emphasizing the schematic, with comments linking findings.","quote":"Big Clive also covered this a couple years later.","text":"Big Clive also covered this a couple years later. Has more of an emphasis on the teardown and schematic. The comments are also great for links to other people's findings. This article is great for more details about how the electrons move. [VIDEO] I'd like to adapt the concept using flexible pcbs to make a largish Nest-style rotary encoder. To understand Vernier measurement conceptually, the Wikipedia article is actually not bad: [LINK]"},{"id":32806134,"author":"stacktrust","month":"2022-09","story":"Dial Caliper Repair (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown of cheap calipers.","quote":"Teardown of cheap calipers","text":"Teardown of cheap calipers: [VIDEO] Power consumption analysis: [LINK] > The Mitutoyo caliper draws about 1/4 the power of the chinese caliper when on, and about 1/8 the power when off. SR44 batteries should last longer than LR44. For infrequently used cheap calipers, one workaround for short battery life is to remove the battery between uses, [LINK] > cheap calipers never actually turn off.. because the encoder they use is not an \"absolute\" encoder. You push the caliper all the way to the closed position, push \"zero\", and then the circuitry simply counts the number of pulses that goes by and figures out how far the caliper moved. If the caliper fully turned off, it would lose its position and you'd have to re-zero it every time you turned it on. > Mitutoyo Digimatics have what they call an \"absolute encoder\". The sensor doesn't just count the number of pulses. They've put a special reference pattern on their encoder such that the caliper's circuitry can figure out where it is at any given time. With a Mitutoyo caliper, you can turn it off, take the battery out, slide it to a new position, put the battery back in, turn it on, and it will know exactly where it is. This \"absolute encoder\" allows the caliper to fully shut down when not in use, greatly increasing battery life. Hacking a switch onto cheap calipers, [LINK] & [LINK] & [LINK]"},{"id":26537913,"author":"spuz","month":"2021-03","story":"Counterfeit Mitutoyo digital calipers (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown finding cheap calipers drain battery even when off.","quote":"Big Clive did a tear down of some that he found and found they were pretty good","text":"You can buy cheap digital calipers for just £5 on ebay these days. Big Clive did a tear down of some that he found and found they were pretty good except for the fact that the digital circuit drains the battery even when it's off: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["rEoc0YoALt0",1],["3MghYhf-GhU",1],["0ifChJ0nJfM",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1]]},"gzgyksS5pX8":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a thoughtful talk from a legendary chip architect, both for the s-curves view of progress and for concrete technical details on semiconductor scaling.","q":{"x":"If you are fan of the stack of s-curves, you will probably like this talk by Jim Keller.","i":34768785,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34768785,"author":"sitkack","month":"2023-02","story":"“A little bit of slope makes up for a lot of y-intercept”","stance":"rec","why":"Jim Keller talk on change, fitting the s-curves idea.","quote":"If you are fan of the stack of s-curves, you will probably like this talk by Jim Keller.","text":"If you are fan of the stack of s-curves, you will probably like this talk by Jim Keller. Jim Keller on Change [VIDEO]"},{"id":37525878,"author":"Voultapher","month":"2023-09","story":"Intel plans to overtake Samsung and TSMC with its 1.8nm chips by 2025","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the actual physical size of a TSMC N5 transistor.","quote":"a TSMC N5 transistor is roughly 100x100x100nm in size as per","text":"Transistor measurements in nanometers has been more or less meaningless since the introduction of FinFETs over a decade ago, and IIRC it was TSMC that started the let's play funny business with nanometers affair. Today they are marketing terms and the different fabs try to align them somewhat, but have inherent conflicts of interest when it comes to overselling their tech. For example a TSMC N5 transistor is roughly 100x100x100nm in size as per [VIDEO] ."},{"id":36928841,"author":"ly3xqhl8g9","month":"2023-07","story":"ASML EUV Lithography Machine Could Keep Moore’s Law on Track","stance":"reference","why":"Keller talk cited for technical detail on how far computing is from limits.","quote":"For more technical details see Jim Keller","text":"We are ridiculously far from physical limits in our current artificial computers (both theoretical [1], and practical [2]). For more technical details see Jim Keller: [3] [4] [5]. [1] [LINK] [2] The ~12 watts computer inside each living human adult skull (and perhaps each eukaryote cell [6]) is still the state-of-the-art, for quite some time. [3] 2021, Jim Keller: The Secret to Moore's Law , [LINK] [4] 2019, Jim Keller: Moore’s Law is Not Dead , [LINK] [5] 2023, Change w/ Jim Keller , [VIDEO] [6] Our computers aren't yet able of polycomputation, where the computation topology, data, and functions depend on the observer, instead of computation in a passive implementation, once done forever set in s̶t̶o̶n̶e̶ silicon, 2023, Michael Levin, Agency, Attractors, & Observer-Dependent Computation in Biology & Beyond , [LINK]"},{"id":36516729,"author":"ly3xqhl8g9","month":"2023-06","story":"Future CSS: State Container Queries","stance":"reference","why":"Keller on DNA origami, cited for factory smaller than the product idea.","quote":"Jim Keller talking about DNA Origami at 19:18,","text":"\"Is this really the way forward?\" No. The way forward for scale-free systems which implement a hierarchy of competence is to have \"the factory smaller than the product\" [1]. Instead of the Chrome Canary team and the various Working Groups dictating from above what passes as valid, let the rules be sent at the same time with the interpretation of the rules [2]. In the same manner DNA encodes proteins with no mention of the underlying morphology, but once the RNA starts building the morphology arises. [1] Jim Keller talking about DNA Origami at 19:18, [VIDEO] [2] Not even such a new idea, \"Building a Better Web Browser - James Mickens - Harvard CS Colloquium 2015\", on rendering at 04:20, [LINK]"},{"id":34908359,"author":"comboy","month":"2023-02","story":"Sam Zeloof and Jim Keller start a new semiconductor fab","stance":"reference","why":"Tenstorrent channel listed as source to hear Keller.","quote":"Investing time to listen to the guy seems to be not worse.","text":"K8 seems pretty small after he made the fucking Zen architecture (and Tesla AI). Lex has good interviews with Jim[1], he's not just very smart, he is wise. He started Tenstorrent some time ago with Ljubisa Bajic so I'm assuming this fab is related. Here is youtube channel for Tenstorrent[2], and last time I checked it was getting under 100 views per video... Ian also interviewed him[3] some time ago. Long story short if I had money that would be relevant in this context I would invest really hard into Tenstorrent. Investing time to listen to the guy seems to be not worse. 1a. [LINK] 1b. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] 3. [LINK]"}],"r":[["EL7Au1tzNxE",1],["1OEu9C51K2A",1],["v3pK0dccQ38",1],["97t7Xj_iBv0",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["RMWNvwLiXIQ",1]]},"hcGgaa2mFc4":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible, well-delivered lecture on high-intensity laser physics.","q":{"x":"Here's a great Royal Institution Lecture by Kate Lancaster talking about Ultra Intense Lasers from 2017","i":16331095,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16331095,"author":"tudorw","month":"2018-02","story":"Intense laser experiments provide evidence that light can stop electrons","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on ultra intense lasers.","quote":"Here's a great Royal Institution Lecture by Kate Lancaster talking about Ultra Intense Lasers from 2017","text":"Here's a great Royal Institution Lecture by Kate Lancaster talking about Ultra Intense Lasers from 2017 :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":23132007,"author":"raverbashing","month":"2020-05","story":"Finding new physics will require a new particle collider","stance":"reference","why":"Laser-driven acceleration as a promising alternative to colliders.","quote":"There are promising works with high-powered lasers","text":"What physics needs is a revolutionary (not evolutionary like the SSC or the LHC) particle accelerator. There are promising works with high-powered lasers, for example: [VIDEO] But it seems the \"spin particles really fast in a very long circular tube\" is reaching the end of its life."},{"id":21923236,"author":"ccvannorman","month":"2019-12","story":"Electric Cars Threaten the Heart of Germany’s Economy","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on using lasers for fusion and its costs.","quote":"Just saw a talk [0] where they talk about using lasers to create fusion.","text":"Fusion may be difficult but math and physics say we should be able to do it. Just because it's difficult and with a long time horizon doesn't mean you should throw it in the bin with \"perpetual motion\". (Cold Fusion however, you may throw in the bin) Just saw a talk [0] where they talk about using lasers to create fusion. The current puck used to kick start the process costs $1M, (paraphrased) \"it needs to cost 20 cents before Fusion is viable. But hey, we're in the research phase!\" [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["1xQeXOz0Ncs",1],["MNXypBxNGMo",1]]},"nsjDnYxJ0bo":{"wy":"HN cites it as the landmark brain-decoding demo and compares newer reconstruction work against it.","q":{"x":"Reconstructing what a person is seeing out of their fMRI scan (2011)","i":34505063,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":6270967,"author":"Houshalter","month":"2013-08","story":"Computer can read letters directly from the brain","stance":"rec","why":"Reconstructs seen video from brain activity","quote":"This is a much cooler example","text":"This is a much cooler example [VIDEO] If I'm understanding the description correctly, they are just training it to recognize what the image is closest to and taking an slice of a youtube video that most closely matches it. I imagine if they used a more efficient method or trained it more, they could do way better. It seems like most of the data to build an accurate picture of what they are seeing is already there."},{"id":42464362,"author":"jazzyjackson","month":"2024-12","story":"Show HN: Movie Iris - Visualizing Films Through Color Extraction","stance":"reference","why":"Gallant's 2011 visual mind reading result","quote":"there's a YouTube video of the result naturally :)","text":"I imagine the best case outcome would look something like Jack Gallant's 2011 work on visual mind reading, where they trained a model on brain activity watching hundreds of hours of YouTube and then attempted to reconstruct a view of video not in the training set by correlating the new brain activity with frames from the input... Sorry I'm not explaining very clearly but there's a YouTube video of the result naturally :) [VIDEO] Learned about this from Mary Lou Jepsens 2013 TED talk, what a throwback [LINK]"},{"id":35013755,"author":"SubiculumCode","month":"2023-03","story":"High-res image reconstruction with latent diffusion models from human brain","stance":"reference","why":"2011 basic reconstruction demo","quote":"in 2011, they were already able to do some basic reconstruction","text":"I mean in 2011, they were already able to do some basic reconstruction: see [VIDEO] I could imagine improvements since then,especially with advances in image networks."},{"id":35014225,"author":"andai","month":"2023-03","story":"High-res image reconstruction with latent diffusion models from human brain","stance":"reference","why":"Blends many images into fuzzy approximation of what was viewed","quote":"overlay and blend many images on top of each other to make a fuzzy image approximating what was actually being viewed","text":"There was a video many years ago (early 2010s?) demoing a similar technology, which would overlay and blend many images on top of each other to make a fuzzy image approximating what was actually being viewed. Edit: found it! [VIDEO]"},{"id":35014433,"author":"Lutzb","month":"2023-03","story":"High-res image reconstruction with latent diffusion models from human brain","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of 2011 Nishimoto paper","quote":"Reminds me of this paper [1] from 2011. See it in action in [2]","text":"Reminds me of this paper [1] from 2011. See it in action in [2] 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] Edit: Just realized the paper above is also from Shinji Nishimoto"},{"id":34505063,"author":"yreg","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What is the weirdest or most surreal (recent) technology you have seen?","stance":"reference","why":"Reconstructing seen images from fMRI scans seemed like magic","quote":"Reconstructing what a person is seeing out of their fMRI scan (2011)","text":"I wasn't able to realize the scale of the shift that's about to happen back then. --- I just remembered another example of hearing about something that seemed like pure magic: Reconstructing what a person is seeing out of their fMRI scan (2011): [VIDEO]"},{"id":22754794,"author":"BickNowstrom","month":"2020-04","story":"Machine translation of cortical activity to text with encoder–decoder framework","stance":"reference","why":"2010 image reconstruction from brain activity","quote":"See also the 2010 research on image reconstruction from brain activity","text":"The errors and bias in these systems add to the ethical concerns. While it is arguably a good thing to substitute torture with brain image interrogation, there is a risk of putting too much trust in these systems, subjecting innocents to days of leading investigation, just because the computer said there is something to be found there. Israeli airport security (arguably the best in the world) deploys derivatives of these systems, that look at micro-gestures, elevation of heart rates, pupil dilation, and temperature changes, to see if passengers respond with familiarity to terrorist imagery flashed on a screen as they walk by it. If that already works in practice, imagine the same, but being strapped with hundreds of sensors. See also the 2010 research on image reconstruction from brain activity, and extrapolate that 10 years in the future and applied to military interrogation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20076573,"author":"SmokeGS","month":"2019-06","story":"Evidence of a nocebo response following a nationwide antidepressant drug switch","stance":"reference","why":"Gallant's TED talk recreating vision","quote":"TED talk by Jack Gallant recreating vision","text":"I feel like we are so close to wirelessly monitoring all brain waves (next 50 years) we will probably be able to, in real time, recognize and diagnose abnormal brain patterns. I think it would be promising if we get this sensor tech up and then perhaps deploy nanotech to interfere with maladaptive brain patterns. The ethics of such a device is considerably sticky. TED talk by Jack Gallant [LINK] recreating vision: [VIDEO] another ted talk by John-Dylan Haynes [LINK] paraphrased: \"wireless non-contact mind reading tech may be cheap and mass producible within the next 50 years.\" the only rate limiting step with his method of decoding thoughts is that it takes time under a high energy device to pair associations in the brain. Think of how much screen time we currently use. If we could pair a wireless 'headset' like headphones while looking at pictures on a screen it would be possible to build a brain bank of children->tenenagers. Currently we just need a lot of processing power and reserved bandwidth on the internet to do some cool stuff."},{"id":17965041,"author":"xamuel","month":"2018-09","story":"Deep image reconstruction from human brain activity (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"2011 brain-activity video reconstruction project","quote":"Reminds me of this project from 2011","text":"Reminds me of this project from 2011: [VIDEO] If I were a billionaire, one of the projects I'd do would be to apply this sort of technology at scale, upon volunteers who were witness to poorly recorded historic events. WWII, Beatles concerts, JFK assassination, 9/11... all of them have been recorded from angles/perspectives that exist only in peoples' memories, and the longer we wait, the more of those memories will be lost."},{"id":11825061,"author":"zach","month":"2016-06","story":"VOX.com – A guy trained a machine to “watch” Blade Runner","stance":"reference","why":"Comparable reconstruction of video from brain MRI","quote":"Extremely interesting to compare with Jack Gallant's reconstruction of video from the brain MRI of a viewer","text":"Extremely interesting to compare with Jack Gallant's reconstruction of video from the brain MRI of a viewer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8796027,"author":"Houshalter","month":"2014-12","story":"Introduction to Modern Brain-Computer Interface Design","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fMRI reconstructing video","quote":"With fMRI you can even reconstruct entire videos of what a person is seeing","text":"MRI technology is improving over time, but I don't know how practical it would be in everyday usage. fNIR can potentially do the same thing much cheaper. I believe it's restricted to only the first few cm of brain tissue but that might be plenty. With fMRI you can even reconstruct entire videos of what a person is seeing (e.g. [VIDEO] ). Controlling something simpler should be easier."},{"id":7483015,"author":"chch","month":"2014-03","story":"Yale researchers reconstruct facial images locked in a viewer’s mind","stance":"reference","why":"Nishimoto 2011 method of reconstructing movie trailers from brain responses","quote":"the face generation looks like it could be similar to the techniques used in Nishimoto et al., 2011","text":"Although it's hard to tell from the images presented with the article, the face generation looks like it could be similar to the techniques used in Nishimoto et al., 2011, which used a similar library of learned brain responses, though for movie trailers: [VIDEO] Their particular process is described in the YouTube caption: The left clip is a segment of a Hollywood movie trailer that the subject viewed while in the magnet. The right clip shows the reconstruction of this segment from brain activity measured using fMRI. The procedure is as follows: [1] Record brain activity while the subject watches several hours of movie trailers. [2] Build dictionaries (i.e., regression models) that translate between the shapes, edges and motion in the movies and measured brain activity. A separate dictionary is constructed for each of several thousand points at which brain activity was measured. (For experts: The real advance of this study was the construction of a movie-to-brain activity encoding model that accurately predicts brain activity evoked by arbitrary novel movies.) [3] Record brain activity to a new set of movie trailers that will be used to test the quality of the dictionaries and reconstructions. [4] Build a random library of ~18,000,000 seconds (5000 hours) of video downloaded at random from YouTube. (Note these videos have no overlap with the movies that subjects saw in the magnet). Put each of these clips through the dictionaries to generate predictions of brain activity. Se"},{"id":5497585,"author":"runn1ng","month":"2013-04","story":"Reconstruction Of Visual Images Using Brain Activity Patterns","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier brain-decoding video reconstruction","quote":"This is one year and something old and returns better results, in my opinion.","text":"This is one year and something old and returns better results, in my opinion. [VIDEO] On the other hand, they presented only the best results in that video."},{"id":5497714,"author":"creamyhorror","month":"2013-04","story":"Reconstruction Of Visual Images Using Brain Activity Patterns","stance":"reference","why":"Different approach to decoding brain activity","quote":"This is a fundamentally different approach from the one used in other research","text":"According to the abstract: Here, we present a neural decoding approach in which machine learning models predict the contents of visual imagery during the sleep onset period given measured brain activity, by discovering links between human fMRI patterns and verbal reports with the assistance of lexical and image databases. Decoding models trained on stimulus-induced brain activity in visual cortical areas showed accurate classification, detection, and identification of contents. So it sounds like the fMRI patterns are associated with words via verbal reports from humans, and the video is simply generated from image databases by using those words. In other words, they classify the fMRI patterns based on how well they correspond to various words (measured while the subjects are awake and giving verbal descriptions of images they're looking at), and then the program measures the subjects' fMRI patterns while they're dreaming, determines the strongest 'word signals' in the patterns, and selects images from a photo database corresponding to those words and assembles them into a video based on how strong the similarity score for each word is at each moment in time. It's interesting, but the video seems like more of a \"reenactment\" of a dream than a prediction. It doesn't look like you can infer actions and occurrences within a dream from this. But this is all just my interpretation. edit: This is a fundamentally different approach from the one used in other research (e.g. [VIDEO] me"},{"id":7484681,"author":"yeukhon","month":"2014-03","story":"Yale researchers reconstruct facial images locked in a viewer’s mind","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I feel like the right clip is out of touch with the left clip.","text":"Select the 100 clips whose predicted activity is most similar to the observed brain activity. Average these clips together. This is the reconstruction. Mind to explain to me why the right clip is inconsistent with the left clip? [VIDEO] Starting at 20-second I feel like the right clip is out of touch with the left clip. between 20th and 22nd second I see at least three individuals rendered from the reconstruction. From 26th to the end of the clip I also see multiple individuals. The names also look different from one another... When you say find the closest is that an expected result?"}],"r":[["_YysNM2JoFo",1],["zQGOcOUBi6s",1],["HKQDSgBHPfY",1],["sULa9Lc4pck",1],["EgMqhEMhVV8",1]]},"o0Bi-q89j5Y":{"wy":"HN mentions it occasionally as a source for Wolff's views, such as on worker co-ops, rather than as a widely recommended watch.","m":[{"id":31856300,"author":"ellopoppit","month":"2022-06","story":"Think twice before founding that free-market utopia","stance":"rec","why":"Richard Wolff discussing Marxism and communism","quote":"he made many of the same compelling points you are making","text":"I recently watched a Lex Fridman podcast with Richard Wolff, a philosopher and Marxist economist and he made many of the same compelling points you are making: [VIDEO] >It's really hard for me to see where the rigid conformity came from. Perhaps its from the concept of \"equality of outcome\", everyone is treated the same no matter what they do. >In other venues, we see rigid conformity tied to hierarchy (military, strict schools).. all venues that tend to have figurehead leaders, and disempowered individuals rather than collective empowerment. It's an interesting correlation, but within those organizations there does at least seem to be some elememts of meritocracy involved. Although, Stalin, Hitler, Mao etc. were all military based dictatorships who preached (though perhaps poorly practiced, or misinterpreted) Marxist ideas."},{"id":36635642,"author":"ironmagma","month":"2023-07","story":"Mechanical Turk workers are using AI to automate being human","stance":"reference","why":"Wolff and Fridman on business risk and worker co-ops","quote":"Lex Fridman and Richard Wolff discussed this","text":"> if the bar loses money each month you're still owed a paycheck Only until the bar stops existing. You're on the hook for business continuity whether you're the employer or the employee. Lex Fridman and Richard Wolff discussed this: [VIDEO] Co-ops are indeed the closest thing we have to escaping capitalism in America. However they don't grow very quickly if at all, for the very reason that they aren't efficient at extracting value to fuel growth."},{"id":35137647,"author":"ex3xu","month":"2023-03","story":"Why We Never Have Enough Time","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think your understanding will improve if you view the content of this link as nothing but a scathing review of the book in question. I get the sense that your confusion comes from thinking that the linked article is trying to make points about the subject of the book, when really the point of the review is to trash the book as a waste of time (while recommending better options, linked throughout the review). For instance, when the reviewer states \"Problems with style and pacing are problems of thinking\", the primary intent is not to make some sort of generalized insight applicable to you, but instead to criticize Odell in particular as putting poorly styled, poorly paced, and poorly thought out lines on the page. If you read with this adversarial context in mind, you could imagine the critic's answer to \"Why We Never Have Enough Time\" to be \"Because we have to waste our lives on our jobs -- mine right now being to write a review of a book I think is a waste of everyone's time.\" Catechesis is a term associated with the Catholic Church as a form of systematic instruction of a set of dogmatic, ideological beliefs. Denouncing Odell's book as \"catechistic indexing\" is a serious burn, connoting the sense of a group of religious followers shallowly regurgitating the same dogmatically accepted facts (with or without adequate exposition or even any understanding in the first place), in the style that a religious leader might conduct a catechistic question and answer session. The abs"}],"r":[["15R2pMqU2ok",1]]},"o1dgrvlWML4":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of the scientific method and empiricism as Feynman's classic analogy of watching a game to infer its rules.","q":{"x":"Richard Feynman gave a good explanation of the principle","i":25646667,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25646667,"author":"simonh","month":"2021-01","story":"Parable of Alien Chess","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman explains heuristic for evaluating lines of investigation.","quote":"Richard Feynman gave a good explanation of the principle","text":">It's nonsense, top-to-bottom. This isn't a formal physical theory, just a heuristic for evaluating lines of investigation. Richard Feynman gave a good explanation of the principle, which he said he found helpful in his work. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38159155,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2023-11","story":"Immanuel Kant–What can we know?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's chess analogy for empiricism and universal rules.","quote":"","text":"> that we cannot use empiricism to determine universal rules, we indeed cannot do this and Feynman illustrated this frequently using the analogy of chess ( [VIDEO] ). As Kant argues we can never observe the rules of the physical world as things in themselves, just as we can't directly look at the rules of chess by observing games. We can only get individual hints that we piece together into models of the world in ways we can understand."},{"id":25643300,"author":"leto_ii","month":"2021-01","story":"Parable of Alien Chess","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's gods-playing-chess metaphor for science.","quote":"","text":"I suspect the metaphor is inspired by Feynman's one about gods playing chess [1]. As it's almost always the case, Feynman manages to immediately inspire and illuminate. On the other hand, the present article seems to me to get some important things wrong. For one, it's not the case that overall white and black win 50% of the time each. More importantly though, if somebody is interested in investigating chess, they will supposedly understand that it's some activity that other (somewhat) intelligent beings are undertaking. Even if everything seems like a blur, an alien scientist should not be content with dismissing it as uninteresting randomness. More so, if somebody actually starts investigating seriously, they will immediately obtain useful results about the game, results that should almost certainly provide useful predictions about the game outcome (at least better than \"it's all random\"). I might find, however, some agreement with the spirit of the article. It seems to me that, even though the author doesn't articulate it properly, the idea is that scientists should pursue more fringe theories even if immediate confirmation is lacking. I think there's quite a bit of value in this, as long as your predictions are always checked against reality. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2639501,"author":"sevenproxies","month":"2011-06","story":"Gcc compile error with 2GB of data","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on how science works like learning chess rules.","quote":"","text":"Science is a blackbox. We make theories about the world we live in based upon the results of experiments. Richard Feynman commented method of thinking about science on one of his videos. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UKxQTvqcpSg",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["cIQ36Kt7UVg",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["viJt_DXTfwA",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1],["kF9KretXqJw",1]]},"zp0OEDcAro0":{"wy":"Clojure commenters cite it as the best overview of condition systems in Clojure and of the libraries that attempt them.","q":{"x":"A great overview of the problem and some of the libs is","i":13592727,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13592727,"author":"puredanger","month":"2017-02","story":"State of Clojure 2016 – Results and Analysis","stance":"rec","why":"Great overview of the problem and some libraries.","quote":"A great overview of the problem and some of the libs is","text":"There are some libraries for this. A great overview of the problem and some of the libs is [VIDEO] and a good set of corresponding notes at [LINK]"},{"id":32753450,"author":"the-alchemist","month":"2022-09","story":"What makes a good REPL? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on CL-style condition systems in Clojure.","quote":"","text":"True, the CL REPL is more useful than the Clojure REPL, but CL has a 20-30 year head start in development. Plus, Clojure was developed as a hosted language, and runs on .NET, Javascript, JVM, and even Dart (in-progress). Since exceptions are quite deeply integrated into those platforms (as opposed to conditions), it has \"bled through\" to the Clojure APIs, and, in turn, REPL. Folks have written a CL-style condition/restart library for Clojure ( [LINK] ) too. [VIDEO] video too."},{"id":18706072,"author":"phyrex","month":"2018-12","story":"Clojure 1.10 release","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how to get REPL-on-exception behavior yourself.","quote":"You can do that yourself if you care","text":"You can do that yourself if you care ( [VIDEO] ) but I get what you mean. On the other hand, on a server application a REPL on exception wouldn’t help me."},{"id":13285795,"author":"nialo","month":"2016-12","story":"Why Clojure? (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a condition system is mostly possible in Clojure.","quote":"It turns out that it is, in fact, at least mostly possible to implement a condition type system in Clojure.","text":"It turns out that it is, in fact, at least mostly possible to implement a condition type system in Clojure. See [VIDEO] I did a little bit of googling for Clojure libraries that do this: [LINK] and [LINK] I don't actually recommend any of these and haven't tried them. In particular I think using a non-standard library for your error handling, which is already probably relatively poorly tested, is probably a bad idea. I just wanted to point out that it's possible. I also think I remember something in a Rich Hickey talk (maybe \"Clojure for Lisp Programmers\"?) where someone in the audience asks about condition systems, and he responds that Clojure doesn't have one, but that it should be possible to build it."},{"id":11843545,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2016-06","story":"Java Exception Handling","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"ps: funny today youtube suggested this","text":"The stack unwinding was such a sad thing to me. Reading about CL condition system made me smile widely. You have more data to treat the exception. While reading the abstract, suddenly the word Exception felt absurd. It's rarely an exceptional condition, it's really a parallel planned error checking. Also, being from the Java era, an Exception was mostly a boring way to ensure error checking, while lispers did use exception as cut / tree jmp to provide solution values to an algorithm. ps: funny today youtube suggested this [VIDEO]"}]},"0506yDSgU7M":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as evidence of Linux's rough edges for ordinary users, including the Steam install that removed the desktop environment, and it prompted an apt fix.","q":{"x":"The recent Linux Challenge [0] on Linus Tech Tips has helped raise Linux's profile for gaming.","i":29650986,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":37861783,"author":"gruez","month":"2023-10","story":"The Midwit Smart Home","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">Huh? I have no idea what you're talking about here. [VIDEO] >Get a new sd card and reload from the last backup. 1. How do you do backups? Is it built into home assistant? Do you think the average person knows or will remember to make backups? 2. \"restore from backups\" works if the sdcard just dies. If it's silently corrupting your install and causing weird behavior you won't even know it's sd card's fault unless you go through troubleshooting."},{"id":29650986,"author":"Zhyl","month":"2021-12","story":"Linux Has Grown into a Viable PC Gaming Platform and the Steam Stats Prove It","stance":"reference","why":"Linux challenge raised Linux's profile among gamers","quote":"The recent Linux Challenge [0] on Linus Tech Tips has helped raise Linux's profile for gaming.","text":"The recent Linux Challenge [0] on Linus Tech Tips has helped raise Linux's profile for gaming. Linus Sebastian's enthusiasm for the upcoming Steam Deck has really raised the channel's appetite for Linux Content and as such is exposing it much more to its audience. We also have Anthony Young to thank for laying the groundwork for Linux Content on LTT, and in turn we have Linux content on LTT to thank for a greater awareness, acceptance and adoption of Linux among gamers (helped, of course, by the advancements being done by Valve with Proton, Steam Play, the Steam Deck, SteamOS 3.0, Steam Input, DXVK and all the rest). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29265499,"author":"theandrewbailey","month":"2021-11","story":"Apt 2.3.12: The solver will no longer try to remove Essential packages","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This is in response to Linus (tech tips) trying to install Steam in his Linux daily driver challenge.","text":"This is in response to Linus (tech tips) trying to install Steam in his Linux daily driver challenge. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42635185,"author":"akaike","month":"2025-01","story":"Bye-bye Windows gaming? SteamOS officially expands past the Steam Deck","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"just watch the video, and this is coming from a guy who at least knows one or two things about computers.","text":"Then you have more faith in humanity, or casuals, than I do. :D I mean, this was three years ago, but I don’t think much has changed. [VIDEO] Why would anyone want to bother with a terminal just for gaming, a little bit of browsing, casually installing mods, using Photoshop, etc.? I mean, just watch the video, and this is coming from a guy who at least knows one or two things about computers. Plus, don’t forget, Linux also has its quirks, just like Windows, only in different areas. Like not so awesome Nvidia drivers :P"},{"id":42101063,"author":"GuB-42","month":"2024-11","story":"Linux Asceticism","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"look at the LinusTechTips video series where he tries to use Linux as a daily driver","text":"> Everything just works under Linux If you mean a Linux desktop PC, then no. I have been using a Linux desktop for many years, but not exclusively. I have tried, but I always have had at least a dual-boot with Windows. Yes, you have more control under Linux, but the reason I still use Windows sometimes is that many things just don't work properly. Just look at here for a list: [LINK] Or, from a less technical perspective, look at the LinusTechTips video series where he tries to use Linux as a daily driver: [VIDEO] And I love linux, I have a Linux desktop at home, which is pretty minimal by the way (Debian mini.iso), and that that it doesn't do weird things behind my back is appreciable. But it absolutely doesn't \"just work\"."},{"id":36642399,"author":"NewLogic","month":"2023-07","story":"Windows Update Restored: Fix Windows Update on Windows 95. 98, Me, 2000, and XP","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Linus of LTT managed to uninstall the GUI of his PopOS install within an hour","text":"On the contrary, Linus of LTT managed to uninstall the GUI of his PopOS install within an hour while attempting to install Steam only last year. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34211609,"author":"Eji1700","month":"2023-01","story":"Rant: Year of Linux on the Desktop (2022)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Hell the most simple, concrete, easily documented example in recent times was Linus' attempt to use linux as a gaming platform","text":"It's because I've expanded on my issues with linux over and over and over again. And there's always some laundry list of \"well did you try...\" followed by the kind of forum hunting for answers and CLI inputs that I haven't had to do on any other machine since the 2010's. And that's if people are being reasonable. I've had plenty of insanely nasty interactions (not that it's unique to linux, but the ratio sure is higher) and it's the only time I've ever been told \"well why don't you just write your own driver\". Hell the most simple, concrete, easily documented example in recent times was Linus' attempt to use linux as a gaming platform, where when trying to debug a basic issue, he nuked the machine. [VIDEO] This entire series is an extremely well documented example of what many \"mid level\" users experience is going to be, and 10 minutes into that video, in attempting to install steam on PopOS, a distro designed for gaming, he uninstalls his desktop. This is SUCH a red flag for anyone who's ever worked UI/UX. Seriously, look in detail at what he did about 10 min in. 1. Installs PopOS for gaming. 2. Trys to install steam, it fails with a complicated error that is of very little help. 3. Some amount of time spent on offscreen research. 4. Comes up with sudo apt-get install steam as an answer. And if you're moderately techy at all, that seems like a perfectly reasonable answer (still not the \"just use the installer that was SUPPOSED to work but it doesn't seem malicious). Sudo for"},{"id":33606480,"author":"KronisLV","month":"2022-11","story":"'Be' is nice, end of story","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"like Linus from LTT still ignored such a warning and ruined his install somewhat [2].","text":"> No warnings, nothing that stopped you. I'm not sure I follow why this is a good thing. Having the option is good, but some sort of a sanity check and a warning dialog just seems like looking out for your users. For example, I recently used Linux on the desktop as my daily driver and wrote about my experiences [1]. At one point, I considered that the system was perhaps a bit of a mess in regards to the installed packages and, not wanting to do a clean reinstall, I figured that I'd just reinstall Python in particular. There's nothing mystifying about running an apt remove command, nor is there anything outrageous about the idea of removing some software and then later reinstalling it. However, as it turns out, this would probably break certain parts of the system, so I got a helpful warning instead: After this operation, 1 244 MB disk space will be freed. You are about to do something potentially harmful. To continue type in the phrase 'Yes, do as I say!' By that time I had already entered \"y\" and pressed enter, not bothering to read the message, since the rest of the prompt looked like it normally would, so instead I got this output: Abort. And nothing broke. I think systems should look out more for the users like that, which would give the OS less of a bad reputation. Of course, it won't always save you, like Linus from LTT still ignored such a warning and ruined his install somewhat [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30585462,"author":"Gareth321","month":"2022-03","story":"The campaign to shut down YouTube-dl continues","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Linus' Linux Challenge videos ( ) provide excellent insight into the typical user experience","text":"Not a good alternative for the big middle distribution of computer skills of the computer user base. Web browser only? Great. Highly skilled? Great. Everyone else? It can be a nightmare. Linus' Linux Challenge videos ( [VIDEO] ) provide excellent insight into the typical user experience for anyone beyond web browser only requirements and beneath administrator level. If you find yourself saying \"what an idiot, how did he not know that \"do as I say\" would mangle his OS,\" you're definitively in the highly skilled camp. Linux UX convention relies on the terminal as a matter of course. Almost everything not in the package manager requires use of the terminal. Even many of the package managed applications require tweaks and configuration via terminal. It's an open secret that package managers are largely inferior to the terminal anyway, in every distribution. The almost universal response to \"I am having an issue with the package manager\" is \"use terminal.\" Bottom line: terminal is far less user friendly than an object oriented environment, and is often much slower. Until a flavour of Linux exists which never ever, for any reason ever, requires the use of the terminal, Linux will remain in the low single digits in terms of consumer market share. I don't think this will change, because if you ask the average Linux developer what they think of the terminal convention, they love it, and they think users just need to stop sucking so much."},{"id":29177888,"author":"jjcon","month":"2021-11","story":"Pop _OS to use it own desktop environment, written in Rust","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"If you need evidence that linux is just not ready for non-technical user prime time","text":"Its an idiom ( [LINK] ) My point is that I can make stuff up too! If you need evidence that linux is just not ready for non-technical user prime time just take a look at the ltt video from yesterday: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29202410,"author":"rPlayer6554","month":"2021-11","story":"Microsoft to Block Windows 11 Browser Workarounds","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Somehow the PopOS steam package decided to uninstall the whole DE.","text":"[VIDEO] Linus Tech Tips wanted to try Linux and see how user friendly it was. Somehow the PopOS steam package decided to uninstall the whole DE. Say what you want about the warnings he ignored,why is this even possible on a PopOS type system? Linus has pointed out that without understanding the terminology and packages, you would have no idea what the error message means. I think user friendly distros are much better but have quite a ways to go. Also as Linus points out, even picking the right distro is tough if you don't already know a lot of the Linux terminology."}]},"2xcQIoh3FQQ":{"wy":"Mostly used as a joke or as an example of historical resistance to safety regulation.","m":[{"id":44425333,"author":"tokai","month":"2025-06","story":"The Academic Pipeline Stall: Why Industry Must Stand for Academia","stance":"reference","why":"Old clip showing resistance to safety laws","quote":"I'll post this old clip","text":">as clean air or safe roads Its really on the nose when these two examples has been anything but fundamental. There are many people out there that don't believe these things are worth working towards. In lieu of a thorough argument I'll post this old clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43065052,"author":"connicpu","month":"2025-02","story":"NASA has a list of 10 rules for software development","stance":"reference","why":"Viral 1980s clip of people calling DUI laws communist","quote":"describing the recent expansion of DUI laws as \"Communist","text":"I'm reminded of a clip that went viral a couple years ago with footage from the 80s containing people describing the recent expansion of DUI laws as \"Communist\"[1] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45111598,"author":"techpineapple","month":"2025-09","story":"Hey Tech Bro–Your Dream City Is Doomed","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I assume you mean violent crime? One of the reasons this is so hard is that everyone disagrees on what crimes should be. Drinking and Driving? Not i a libertarian utopia. [VIDEO] Kids? Hope they don’t have wild teen years."},{"id":42651156,"author":"xnx","month":"2025-01","story":"The '90s weren't that great","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"People were also pretty annoyed that they wouldn't be allowed to drink while driving","text":"People were also pretty annoyed that they wouldn't be allowed to drink while driving: [VIDEO] Edit: fixed typo"},{"id":42651234,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2025-01","story":"The '90s weren't that great","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> People were also pretty annoyed that they would be allowed to drink while driving: [VIDEO] Correction: \"…they would not be allowed to drink while…\""},{"id":41630281,"author":"slater","month":"2024-09","story":"Telegram says it will share phone numbers and IPs of 'bad actors' to authorities","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Even worse! Forcing you to wear seat belts and drunk driving, which is just the purest form of communism [VIDEO]"}]},"9EEY87HAHzk":{"wy":"HN shares it as a classic glimpse of early SGI hardware graphics in retro-computing and IRIX threads.","q":{"x":"Classic SGI demo video from 1985","i":36096206,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37506794,"author":"luciusdomitius","month":"2023-09","story":"XFCE Wayland Development Roadmap","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"XFCE is frankly the last full featured Desktop Environment","text":"XFCE is frankly the last full featured Desktop Environment, truly focusing on responsiveness and zero-lag SGI-style: [VIDEO] MacOS X, Windows and the mainstream Linux DEs have nice animations, but it is fun watching them the first day. After that it is just annoying. Really hope that Wayland integration works well for them."},{"id":36096206,"author":"kloch","month":"2023-05","story":"Can SGI’s Enthusiast Community Bring IRIX Back to Life?","stance":"reference","why":"Classic 1985 SGI demo with smooth high-resolution color graphics","quote":"Classic SGI demo video from 1985","text":"Classic SGI demo video from 1985: [VIDEO] I remember when my dad brought a VHS copy of this home. I couldn't believe the high-res smooth color graphics."},{"id":6752009,"author":"kristopolous","month":"2013-11","story":"Don't use 1234 as your password","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"a video of the machine","text":"I found a 68K SGI Iris 2400 machine up and running in college in about 2004. It had a sticker with the hostname on it. Later on that week, I went to the department homepage, got the staff roster and tried to guess the usernames. I telnetted to port 25 and tried RCPT TO hypothesized names, like so $ telnet host 25 MAIL FROM: a@a.com 250 Sender OK RCPT TO: afranks 550 Recipient not found RCPT TO: arty.franks 250 Recipient OK ... With this list of usernames I logged into the FTP to try to guess trivial passwords: $ telnet host 21 USER arty.franks User OK PASS 1234 Login failed PASS password ... Eventually I got a valid username/password combo. Now I can just telnet <host> and log in. I got a line like this: Last login April 12, 1992. $ It had this ancient version of IRIX on it, a hard drive under 100 MB, no X, a version of egcs, some ancient version of perl, no bash, and I think 12MB of RAM? It was fun, but I didn't know what I wanted to do with it. We executed this attack from the school library. Putz'd around a bit, in amazement of how old it was, and that it was still online, and then logged out - never to return. [VIDEO] - a video of the machine"}]},"CPNK0VspQ0M":{"wy":"Commenters link it as a pun or ironic reference in threads about 911 and emergency services.","m":[{"id":24413967,"author":"microtherion","month":"2020-09","story":"'Wild West' mentality lingers in modern populations of US mountain regions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> In many parts of the United States when you call 911, you're lucky if anyone shows up within an hour. And not necessarily only rural parts: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23931459,"author":"PaulHoule","month":"2020-07","story":"Senate Report on 5G: Recipe for Disaster","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Like most 5G articles that attempt to rebut 5G hysteria (5G causes autism, ...) it fails to realize that people are just right to be suspicious about a technology that has no credible benefits and no constituency other than equipment vendors. Telecom carriers have had no interest in 5G. From the invention of the internet to the 3G age telecom vendors felt out of control of their financial destiny (MCI Worldcomm's fraud forced them invest in intercity optic fiber capacity they never would have built otherwise. Most of that capacity lies fallow too because they've had an \"investment strike\" against building optic fiber out to the last mile) With 4G they got back on top and had no interest in capital spending to build out a new generation but carriers had to pretend they were caught up in the hype. So you can get 5G at one corner of Washington Square Park and it is so fast it doesn't matter at all that 99.99% of Manhattan doesn't have it, never mind the rest of the country.) Vint Cerf had made a career out of being clueless about the digital divide. The real problem in America is poor coverage of telecommunication services. Upstate New York has cell phone dead spots in it larger than some European countries, and because of that [VIDEO]"},{"id":18781978,"author":"iheartpotatoes","month":"2018-12","story":"911 emergency services go down across the US after CenturyLink outage","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"From 1990, these guys were so ahead of their time (yea, I know, institutional racism != IT infrastructure, but still...): [VIDEO]"},{"id":11978261,"author":"kens","month":"2016-06","story":"When You Dial 911 and Wall Street Answers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Complaints about 911 have a long history; I'll point out the 1990 hit rap song \"911 is a joke\" by Public Enemy. The video seems extremely dated now, though: [VIDEO]"}]},"KjB6r-HDDI0":{"wy":"It is posted purely as a joke in threads about sponsorship, ads and selling out.","m":[{"id":37617874,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-09","story":"Amazon Prime Video content to start including ads next year","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Happens already. Product placements in TV shows and movies are already a thing. This is nothing new. The Wayne's World 2 movie had a fourth-wall bending/breaking scene that made fun of this back in 1993: * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"},{"id":25920446,"author":"deckard1","month":"2021-01","story":"Twitter acquires Revue","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"In addition to cable going to ads, it deserves mention that many shows today feature brand advertising right in the program. I pay for Netflix, but go watch a Korean drama and they are clearly advertising Subway, KFC, Samsung, etc. right there in the show through the show itself. Movies do this too, and you paid for that expensive ticket. Wayne's World even did a parody of this in 1992: [VIDEO] Advertising is incredibly pervasive in our modern society and only going to increase."},{"id":23460900,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-06","story":"The Lives of Others: When does imagination become appropriation?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"product placement: [VIDEO] (somewhere I once ran across a discussion of \"Starship Troopers\" the book vs. \"Starship Troopers\" the movie, complete with a matrix of reactions depending upon how ironically one thought the ideas presented in each should be taken)"},{"id":10737983,"author":"pmlnr","month":"2015-12","story":"The sad economics of being famous on the internet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It took me a while to realize what the problem is. For a long time, we've seen sponsored content as a bad, evil thing, that is driven by greed and capitalism. ( See corresponding Wayne's World moment [VIDEO] ) This still stands for many situations but it seems like there are people out there only accepting and promoting things they: - believe in - have no issues with ( neither moral, nor technical ) - or even actually use them. All these cases are, in my opinion, valid cases for accepting money and is pretty far from evil. Image a world where all the commercials are done by people actually believing in the product - and not the money behind it."}]},"Lp7E973zozc":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it in discussions of procrastination and the 5-second rule, not as a strong recommendation.","q":{"x":"TL;DR: when tempted by procrastination count backward from 5","i":13814510,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":16723007,"author":"phkahler","month":"2018-03","story":"PeerTube: A decentralized video hosting network, based on free software","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as an example of promoting something of value.","quote":"If you still think people can't be the change they want I'll offer you this","text":">> ps, this comment of mine is not as polite as I want my comments to be. But, when you're calling people lazy and sitting on their butt, I think it's okay to write a not-so-polite comment. Oh I understand. I held back a bit too. You're being an extremist with your comments about taking charge, and Always! And no, I don't propose the OP download the whole of what he sees as \"culture to be preserved\" from YouTube. Why do you insist on taking a simple concept to such an extreme? We can all try to change the world a little bit. I'd prefer the world to be certain ways. While I can't make it that way for everyone I can set an example and influence the small area around me. In a similar way, if someone thinks a set of videos should be preserved they can save some themself - that does not mean they become an archivist for the Smithsonian. While I was being a bit snarky, the sitting on their butt comment was meant to point out that a person watching YouTube is literally doing that. Most things people want to change do take more effort than that. I'm taking the time to write for the same reason - I don't like when people are such extremists in their thinking (all or nothing) so I'm hoping to change a small part of the world (you) in a tiny way. If you still think people can't be the change they want I'll offer you this: [VIDEO] And no, I didn't save a copy for myself ;-) But I am doing my part to promote something I think has some value."},{"id":13814510,"author":"quizotic","month":"2017-03","story":"The 5-Second Rule: The Ultimate Procrastination Killer","stance":"reference","why":"Mel Robbins' talk on beating procrastination by counting backward.","quote":"TL;DR: when tempted by procrastination count backward from 5","text":"For those who aren't riveted by the gorgeous ... sweater ... the link to the relevant TED talk is [VIDEO] TL;DR: when tempted by procrastination count backward from 5"},{"id":6908027,"author":"ThrowAway9392","month":"2013-12","story":"The Selling of Attention Deficit Disorder","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"(Using a throw away account because of the stigma) For those who are curious if ADHD is real? I can tell you, from my personal experience, it is. I grew up with it all my life. It is hell. Then I randomly decided to try Paleo (cutting out gluten, sugar, grain and caffeine). And my ADHD is gone. I can focus, my mental fog is lifted, my lethargy is gone, I have the ability to enjoy normal activities. * My personal Proof: In the last 2 years about 15 times I have cheated on my Paleo diet, by eating bread or a decent amount of sugar. About 12 out of 15 of those times my brain chemistry was quickly affected. I had my old impairments and extreme inability to focus. * I realize the causes for ADHD for me are not necessarily the same as others. It's probably a host of causes that affect the brain in a similar way. However, I am sure there are more people out there who are like me. FOR PEOPLE WHO HAVE MY ALLERGY TO GLUTEN: ADHD IS A REAL DISEASE. AND IT IS HELL. ## What it feels like to have ADD/ADHD Because I can intentionally turn off my ADHD by eating Paleo or turn it back on, like clock work by eating grain or sugar, I can tell you what having ADHD is like in a very exact way. # Eating Paleo for 3 weeks straight (no ADHD): I still don’t feel like doing work often time. Some time work sounds fun and some time it doesn’t. Its the same feeling I saw most my peers in college have towards work. If I don’t feel like doing something I really have to find a reason to make my self do it. ("}]},"M16CGK1T9MM":{"wy":"It is the go-to reference in copyright threads for the oddity that the tower's night view is protected.","q":{"x":"it is illegal to take a picture of the Eiffel Tower at night but NOT during the day","i":24503286,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25853090,"author":"kevincox","month":"2021-01","story":"FedEx shipping damage creates fractured artworks","stance":"reference","why":"Why you can't photograph the Eiffel Tower at night.","quote":"Reminds me of why you can't take a photo of the Eiffel Tower at night.","text":"You may well own the photo but not the subject of the photo. I believe this means that you can not sell the photo without also acquiring a license to the subject. I imagine that this is the same as taking a photo of a stock image. That doesn't make any sense. Reminds me of why you can't take a photo of the Eiffel Tower at night. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24503286,"author":"irjustin","month":"2020-09","story":"Why Is 1 World Trade Center Missing from Spider-Man?","stance":"reference","why":"Why night photos of the Eiffel Tower are illegal.","quote":"it is illegal to take a picture of the Eiffel Tower at night but NOT during the day","text":"On a related note, it is illegal to take a picture of the Eiffel Tower at night but NOT during the day[0]. It's the lighting system and its 'likeness' that cannot be reproduced. A brief search and I don't think anyone has been prosecuted[1]. There are plenty of pictures everywhere that probably don't have proper licensing. Perhaps those who want to use it commercially can get permission relatively easily? [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":19079433,"author":"CaliforniaKarl","month":"2019-02","story":"If Software Is Funded from a Public Source, Its Code Should Be Open Source","stance":"reference","why":"Night view of the Eiffel Tower is copyrighted in the EU.","quote":"apparently the view of the Eiffel Tower at night is copyrighted.","text":"Alot of the conversation I have seen so far has been US-centric. That's fair, given that this was posted during US business hours, but for the US I don't really think this discussion applies as much. Works created by the United States Government are not covered by copyright in the US, effectively making them public domain _in the US_. See [LINK] \"But—\", you say, \"—the code the NSA is publishing has a license attached!\" Indeed, that confused me too, until I found the answer at [LINK] : >…copyright exclusion for works of the U.S. Government is not intended to have any impact on protection of these works abroad (S. REP. NO. 473, 94th Cong., 2d Sess. 56 (1976)). Therefore, the U.S. Government may obtain protection in other countries depending on the treatment of government works by the national copyright law of the particular country. Copyright is sometimes asserted by U.S. Government agencies outside the United States. So, Public Domain within the US, and Copywritten (but OSS-licensed) outside of the US. My understanding is that copyright in EU countries is much more complicated. For example, apparently the view of the Eiffel Tower at night is copyrighted. See [VIDEO] As for anything Classified in the US, there are laws controlling distribution. So, take something GPL-licensed: If you take, use, and modify the software, you are not required to provide the code unless you distribute the product to others. See the question \"Since U.S. Government works are not protected by copyrigh"}]},"TsdOej_nC1M":{"wy":"HN cites it as a historic demonstration of early speech synthesis, including discussions about whether the demo was genuine.","q":{"x":"This extended video has me thinking it probably was legit though","i":36771476,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39007786,"author":"sargstuff","month":"2024-01","story":"An Approach to Sound Synthesis with L-Systems","stance":"reference","why":"Voder as an example of speech production via vocoder-like synthesis.","quote":"","text":"Vocoder more representative of the ADSR approach.[0] Although adding the orchestral music score single track equivalent of speech production looks about the same.[1] Space / technical complexity not withstanding, could generate a full choir & full orchestra with 1 vocoder per singer/ 1 vocoder per orchestra instrument. Suppose chatgpt could assist with reducing/minimizing amount of vocoders needed via waveform overlap reductions at the expense of being specific for given set of song(s). ---- [0] : [VIDEO] [1] : [LINK]"},{"id":36771476,"author":"JKCalhoun","month":"2023-07","story":"Voder Speech Synthesizer","stance":"reference","why":"Extended Voder demo suggesting the phonetic speech demonstration was legitimate.","quote":"This extended video has me thinking it probably was legit though","text":"I was skeptical that you could even type an intelligible phonetic \"She saw me\" with only two phonemes let alone give it the rise and fall demonstrated. I've played with the SP0256 speech synthesis IC and found constructing intelligible words challenging even with all the phonemes available on that silicon. This extended video has me thinking it probably was legit though: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14184377,"author":"kastnerkyle","month":"2017-04","story":"Lyrebird – An API to copy the voice of anyone","stance":"reference","why":"Historic Voder demonstration of early electronic speech synthesis.","quote":"","text":"This model is quite cool, but also quite a bit different than what lyrebird.ai is doing. NPSS has a lot of extra information in the control inputs about pronunciation and timing (the part-of-phoneme timer feature) - this means that most of the \"hard parts\" (in my opinion) for naturalness are control inputs to NPSS/WaveNet style models, rather than variables the model must generate globally and consistently as in lyrebird. At generation time NPSS appears to generate each component autoregressively as well, but I am not clear on whether the demo samples do this or if they use \"true\" values for f0 at least - what forces the model to sing the exact same melody, if many melodies are possible given the underlying audio information? Also note that NPSS has some amount of post-processing, at least reverb and perhaps other common musical mixing - we don't really know how these samples are generated, and I have a hard time decyphering exactly what inputs are required, and what are generated from the paper alone. However, I really, really, really like NPSS - I just don't think the comparison you are making is valid here. These features (f0, duration, pronunciation) are some of the most difficult things to learn to model from datasets of speech and text directly, and I am not sure how they got the subset used (I think only f0 and pronunciation/phoneme) for this NPSS model. Giving creators fine-grained control of the performance (as in NPSS) is quite cool, and if these systems can get fas"}]},"XiQkeWOFwmk":{"wy":"Referenced as evidence of Tesla's humanoid robot progress and its data collection efforts.","m":[{"id":39479257,"author":"loceng","month":"2024-02","story":"Nvidia adds $277B in market value, Wall Street's largest one-day gain in history","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"How about addressing and countering any of my specific points, ideally each one in sequence so you can't cherrypick to avoid the ones where I'm certainly correct - rather than what's essentially ad hominem as your response? [VIDEO] [LINK] etc. Perhaps you're not thinking from first principles and extrapolating from there?"},{"id":39072970,"author":"modeless","month":"2024-01","story":"HaptX gloves provide high-fidelity touch feedback of virtual spaces","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Tesla's motion capture suit for collecting robot training data.","quote":"Tesla has a motion capture suit that can be seen here","text":"These are too complex and expensive for consumer use now and for the foreseeable future. However there is an important application where these may end up being key: teleoperation of robot hands to produce training data for neural nets. We will probably need millions of hours of data of robots doing everyday tasks to train our neural net models. Collecting that data might mean humans puppeteering robots very precisely but also very quickly. Without haptic gloves, human operators would be attempting tasks with no sense of touch at all, and their movements would be unnatural and inefficient, ultimately resulting in the trained robot moving inefficiently too. Tesla has a motion capture suit that can be seen here: [VIDEO] In this video they aren't doing teleoperation but in this other video demonstrating teleoperation you can briefly see a hand in the bottom right corner which looks like it could be wearing a haptic glove: [LINK]"},{"id":36699985,"author":"machdiamonds","month":"2023-07","story":"XAI","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of Tesla bot progress.","quote":"","text":"- You've mentioned that Musk isn't good with cars, yet under his leadership, Tesla has emerged as the world's most valuable automaker. This seemingly contradicts the notion of Musk being inept in the automotive industry. - Your claim about Tesla's autonomous vehicles lagging behind competition appears misinformed. From what I understand, Tesla's self-driving technology, which does not rely on high-definition maps, is far advanced compared to most companies in the same space. Moreover, the self-driving team, led by Karpathy and now Ashok, are highly competent individuals. - To suggest that SpaceX hasn't been successful or valuable to NASA is just wrong. You label the Starship project as 'dumb,' but isn't it a more cost-effective way to launch larger payloads into space? I'm curious if you had similar thoughts when the Falcon 9 was announced? Today, it delivers over double the tons to orbit compared to all other organizations combined. - Regarding the open letter signed by SpaceX employees, less than 3% of the workforce supported it. The letter primarily critiqued Musk's Twitter activity, which is unrelated to his employees' professional roles and contributions. - On the subject of the promised 'Tesla bots,' they appear to be progressing at a considerable pace, as evidenced by the following link: [VIDEO] . - With Neuralink, the ultimate objective is to integrate human brains with AI. More immediate goals include improving the quality of life for individuals suffering from condi"}]},"ZORzsubQA_M":{"wy":"Cited as an example of reducing car dominance and as a model for walkable neighborhoods.","m":[{"id":42510787,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2024-12","story":"South Korea's population faces point of no return","stance":"reference","why":"Barcelona Superblocks as mental model for walkable family neighborhoods.","quote":"","text":"I think there is a potential, limited path to success here (depending on what \"success\" looks like), and it’s likely assisting parents in clustering (via subsidizing moving expenses and affordable family housing) around schools that remain (because many are closing due to funding cuts and the demographic cliff) to maintain walkability for both school commute and socialization. It’s a bandaid, but the only option I see viable considering the cost of re-engineering urban planning to fix decades of car centric policy. Those costs are enormous, and I think in a lot of cases, it’ll be cheaper to allow some places to go terminal/fallow as their population declines. This doesn’t juice the fertility rate (because it doesn't appear anything will), but it will make childhoods suck less for kids already here and those on the way for the foreseeable future (as well as improve parental experience). Barcelona Superblocks is my mental model on this topic. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20211526,"author":"IlegCowcat","month":"2019-06","story":"Superblocks: Barcelona’s plan to take back streets from cars","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier video on the first Barcelona superblocks.","quote":"I remember the 2016 Vox video when they were putting in the first Superblocks.","text":"Ahh this is a new article from 2019. I remember the 2016 Vox video when they were putting in the first Superblocks. [VIDEO] Good to see Barcelona is going ahead with the Superblocks. Found a 2018 video by those great Streetfilms folks: [LINK]"},{"id":14340792,"author":"sreenadh","month":"2017-05","story":"India plans massive shift to electric cars","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Barcelona superblocks reducing car dominance.","quote":"","text":"I believe that India has to leapfrog the whole car obsession and move over to an effective and extensive public transportation and make cities more “superblocks\" like in Barcelona ( [VIDEO] ). Adam ruins Cars ( [LINK] ) is a rather informative piece you should watch."}]},"ZZDwB4-DPXE":{"wy":"HN cites it as an early demo of projectional, AST-based programming whenever editors, code views or non-text programming come up.","q":{"x":"Here are a couple of demo vids from well over 10 years ago","i":12905077,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":39431856,"author":"sweetsocks21","month":"2024-02","story":"Clean your codebase with basic information theory","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of projecting programs into different views with metadata.","quote":"It looked really neat.","text":"Intentional Software was working on a system like this many years ago: [LINK] Part 2: [VIDEO] You can project your programs into different views and add lots of metadata about the program and use that data in various contexts. Also extends to source control and other use cases. It looked really neat. I'm not sure what happened to it."},{"id":12905077,"author":"corysama","month":"2016-11","story":"Ask HN: Is the architecture of programming tools historical accident?","stance":"reference","why":"Old demo of projectional, AST-based programming from Intentional Software.","quote":"Here are a couple of demo vids from well over 10 years ago","text":"It's a shame the Intentional Software crew have been nearly silent for the a decade or so they've been in business. They've been doing projections-of-ASTs based programming for a very long time. They bragged a lot about it early on and I haven't heard much of anything since. Here are a couple of demo vids from well over 10 years ago: [LINK] [VIDEO] Here's an interview from 2009 where one of the crew members talks about an annotated S-expression as the core code representation (IIRC, it's been a long time since I listened to it) [LINK]"},{"id":2494,"author":"amichail","month":"2007-03","story":"SF Chronicle article about new search startups","stance":"reference","why":"Intentional programming compared to a user-programmable search platform.","quote":"This is sort of like intentional programming","text":"I wonder if it would make sense to make a search engine platform where (more technical) users can submit code for domain specific query entry, search, ranking, and presentation of results. Moreover, such code submissions would compete with each other, with more effective ones rising in popularity in their respective domains. Unlike APIs that are currently available, the submissions made by users here would make up the core feature set of the service. This is sort of like intentional programming, but applied to [VIDEO]"},{"id":1408,"author":"amichail","month":"2007-02","story":"I'm looking for a partner or two (here are some of my ideas, feel free to add your own here)","stance":"reference","why":"Intentional programming demo as inspiration for an open search platform.","quote":"","text":"I am interested in seeing what happens when you apply intentional programming to web [VIDEO] idea is to open up your search engine so that anyone can submit code for domain specific query entry, search, ranking, and presentation of results. Moreover, such code submissions would compete with each other, with more effective ones rising in popularity in their respective domains. Unlike APIs that are currently available, the submissions made by users here would make up the core feature set of the service."}]},"bNpx7gpSqbY":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it for its finding that timing is the most important factor in startup success.","q":{"x":"timing is the most important factor in a startups success.","i":21313813,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":32137430,"author":"RonaldOlzheim","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: Does this idea make any sense?","stance":"reference","why":"Timing is the biggest reason startups succeed.","quote":"I think internal motivation and timing are key.","text":"multiple perceptions Example youtube 1: Value creation --> pro it creates low entrey --> con You need to get in --> Overal I don´t think it´s a smart move to let people decide if something is going to valueble when they are not the valid expert in a certain domain --> This video explains it = [LINK] 2: Economical --> pro basicly everyone can start --> con everyone can start --> Overal i think it´s hard to overcome bias. The best way to outperform thoughts and words of bias is to look at actions over time. 3: short term vs long term --> pro it´s intresting in downturn markets for singles now --> con but singles can build easier faster even in downturn markets. --> Overal = I think internal motivation and timing are key. The single biggest reason why start-ups succeed | Bill Gross [VIDEO]"},{"id":22652850,"author":"jv22222","month":"2020-03","story":"This project posts one new startup idea every day for a year","stance":"reference","why":"Timing matters for startup success.","quote":"Timing is an important factor","text":"Some useful reading/viewing on this subject: Ideas are just multipliers: [LINK] Idea vs Execution: [LINK] Timing is an important factor: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21313813,"author":"drchewbacca","month":"2019-10","story":"The most successful people are just the luckiest, a new computer model confirms","stance":"reference","why":"Timing as the biggest factor in startup success.","quote":"timing is the most important factor in a startups success.","text":"There's this ted talk to a similar effect, timing is the most important factor in a startups success. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14320031,"author":"vtange","month":"2017-05","story":"Startup Graveyard – History Shouldn't Have to Repeat Itself","stance":"reference","why":"Timing is a key factor in startup success.","quote":"timing for startups is important, as shown in this TED talk -","text":"Great idea. Site is hugged to death at the moment I think A quick issue that arose in my head is what happens if people turn away from ideas because they see it in the graveyard? Some ideas may have seen the light of day too soon...timing for startups is important, as shown in this TED talk - [VIDEO] With this resource at hand, people might mistaken bad timing for bad idea."}]},"caVEiitI2vg":{"wy":"HN cites it as a well-known example of SIM-swap social engineering that defeated even a carrier PIN, in discussions of SMS-based two-factor authentication.","m":[{"id":30685800,"author":"judge2020","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Is there a pure SMS based UI?","stance":"reference","why":"Account of a SIM swap attack via a retail store.","quote":"he got cold called to set up 'extra security', gave the attacker a PIN number","text":"But, to add, sim swap attacks are a known issue and anything of value becomes a target. The main issue is that retail employees in 'authorized reseller' locations are allowed to make changes to accounts with the PIN of the account holder, but that is often easy to guess or is easy to figure out by anyone that does enough digging into someone's life. [LINK] [VIDEO] (tldr he got cold called to set up 'extra security', gave the attacker a PIN number, and the attacker used that to impersonate them within a T-Mobile store and swap the sim card from the phone into a new phone, thus receiving SMS 2fa codes for their accounts)."},{"id":17928236,"author":"shawn","month":"2018-09","story":"Chrome 69: “www.” subdomain missing from URL","stance":"reference","why":"Example of social engineering past carrier customer support.","quote":"it's getting easier to social engineer your way past AT&T's lovely customer support","text":"Since everyone is wondering why, and since I happened to stumble across a reason during my time as a pentester, here you go: Spearphishing is still one of the most common ways of breaching a corporate network. If I target you, you will likely fall for one of my attempts. If you are a company rather than a person, my odds go way up, because I have N chances to trick someone rather than 1 (where N is roughly the number of people at the company with email access). This is one of those things that everyone says \"Ha, I'm smart. I'd notice. You can't trick me.\" And maybe you are. But you're also distracted. And that's my greatest advantage against you. All I need is to sneak in an unexpected Github prompt that looks completely authentic, and now I have your Github password. Wanna bet you don't have 2FA turned on, even though you know you really ought to? And even if you do, it's getting easier to social engineer your way past AT&T's lovely customer support: [VIDEO] Ok, what's the point? This: Every character in the URL bar unrelated to the apex name is a deadly distraction. Right now, how do you know you're actually on HN instead of some knockoff? \"ycombinator.com\". How many characters do you have to read unrelated to that? \" [LINK] . /item?id=17927972\" The most vital part of a URL for vetting identity is also, usually, the hardest to see. Now, I don't know whether google made this change in order to assist with this. But it's one possible justification, and a step in a good direct"},{"id":17664690,"author":"css","month":"2018-08","story":"Reddit Security Incident","stance":"reference","why":"Example of YouTubers hit by carrier social engineering.","quote":"This happened to some big YouTubers in the past","text":"Either some sort of ss7 exploit [0] or the attackers socially engineered the cell service provider [1]. This happened to some big YouTubers in the past [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12598989,"author":"nbadg","month":"2016-09","story":"On Phone Numbers and Identity","stance":"reference","why":"H3H3 victimized by social engineering despite a PIN.","quote":"It's happened to popular Youtube producer H3H3 (even with a pin)","text":"Using social engineering to bypass this is a big enough issue that even the FTC has a blog post about it [1]. It's happened to popular Youtube producer H3H3 (even with a pin) [2]. Fusion did a thing about it at DefCon this year as well, demonstrating exactly how easy it is. The whole video [3] is worth watching, but I'll like to the relevant bit. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":12163349,"author":"Kortaggio","month":"2016-07","story":"NIST declares the age of SMS-based 2-factor authentication over","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an attacker social-engineering a SIM from T-Mobile.","quote":"an attacker tried to take over h3h3's YouTube account by requesting his SIM card from T-mobile by pretending to be a T-mobile employee","text":"One of the biggest weaknesses of SMS 2FA that I didn't see the article cover is when an attacker can socially engineer their way into your account with your cell service provider. I'm thinking of a high-profile example when an attacker tried to take over h3h3's YouTube account by requesting his SIM card from T-mobile by pretending to be a T-mobile employee: [VIDEO]"}]},"dtjb2OhEQcU":{"wy":"Cited as a related video when threads touch on chaos, predictability and fractals from pendulum state spaces.","q":{"x":"here's a relevant video about the (sometimes not) chaotic nature of double pendulums","i":45135300,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":47702329,"author":"satvikpendem","month":"2026-04","story":"Claude mixes up who said what and that's not OK","stance":"reference","why":"Chaos can be predictable over ranges of input.","quote":"","text":"Correct, it's akin to chaos theory or the butterfly effect, which, even it can be predictable for many ranges of input: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45135300,"author":"EMIRELADERO","month":"2025-09","story":"What Is the Fourier Transform?","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant to the chaotic nature of double pendulums.","quote":"here's a relevant video about the (sometimes not) chaotic nature of double pendulums","text":"As an aside, here's a relevant video about the (sometimes not) chaotic nature of double pendulums: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44577184,"author":"badosu","month":"2025-07","story":"Show HN: Beyond Z²+C, Plot Any Fractal","stance":"reference","why":"Fractal from double pendulum state graph.","quote":"You can make a fractal out of the state graph of a double pendulum","text":"You can make a fractal out of the state graph of a double pendulum: [VIDEO] I don't doubt there could be an iterative formula that maps to it, but I'd be very surprised."}]},"gCKhktcbfQM":{"wy":"Used as a stock example of overly fast cutting and shaky-cam action filmmaking.","m":[{"id":40146274,"author":"jsheard","month":"2024-04","story":"VideoGigaGAN: Towards detail-rich video super-resolution","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":":( \"Our model encounters challenges when processing >200 frame videos\" :) \"Our model is proven production-ready using real-world footage from Taken 3\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":36076003,"author":"ryandrake","month":"2023-05","story":"Alien was released 44 years ago today","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Taken 3 where they used 15 cuts in 6 seconds[1] to show Liam Neeson jumping over a fence.","text":"Your comment reminds me of the classic example: Taken 3 where they used 15 cuts in 6 seconds[1] to show Liam Neeson jumping over a fence. You can barely hold people's attention for 400 milliseconds. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25168306,"author":"nostromo","month":"2020-11","story":"David Fincher’s Impossible Eye","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here are two of the worst abuses of fast cut, shaky cam scenes out there","text":"> The camera moved at the same speed as Affleck, gliding with unvarying smoothness, which is exactly how Fincher likes his shots to behave. I appreciate this so much. There’s a lot of shaky cam out there and it often gives me motion sickness or confuses my understanding of the scene. It also feels like a cheap trick directors use to create faux excitement, or to cover up sloppy filmmaking. Fincher movies are easy to watch and understand visually. He does a lot of wide shots and long shots without sloppy cuts. In Panic Room he did a single shot (although CGI) that lasted several minutes and showed the viewer around the entire set in a single uninterrupted view. It was glorious. I wish this approach was more common. Although I expect it’s not because it’s hard to accomplish. Here are two of the worst abuses of fast cut, shaky cam scenes out there: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"hkDD03yeLnU":{"wy":"Used as a running joke whenever HN discusses Hollywood hacking or nonsense technical jargon.","q":{"x":"Not sure anything can beat the famous CSI hacking the killers IP address using a VB GUI Interface scene.","i":36892201,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48575931,"author":"vunderba","month":"2026-06","story":"Write a GUI Interface with Visual Basic that can be used to track an IP address","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this is one of the many classic pseudohacker babble references from the television show CSI","text":"Lol. For reference, this is one of the many classic pseudohacker babble references from the television show CSI [1]. Brought to you by your average screen writer who probably thought that dragging an icon from the desktop in Windows 95 to the Recycle Bin also deleted the corresponding files. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":37938925,"author":"sundarurfriend","month":"2023-10","story":"Bill Gates demonstrates Visual Basic (1991) [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"for the younguns","text":"Have you ever created a GUI interface in Visual Basic, and seen if you could track an IP address? ( [VIDEO] for the younguns)"},{"id":36892201,"author":"mellosouls","month":"2023-07","story":"Mathematics in Movies","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Not sure anything can beat the famous CSI hacking the killers IP address using a VB GUI Interface scene.","text":"Not sure anything can beat the famous CSI hacking the killers IP address using a VB GUI Interface scene. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35737225,"author":"miles","month":"2023-04","story":"Stability AI releases Image Upscaling API with 2 open-source models behind it","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"will never fail to entertain ;-)","text":"“Enhance!”[0] may no longer qualify as comedy, but this[1] will never fail to entertain ;-) [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24547211,"author":"maccam94","month":"2020-09","story":"Nikola’s founder steps down from board as company faces fraud allegations","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The reference","text":"It's unrealistic that someone would write a GUI interface in response to an active computer attack. The reference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17732743,"author":"dave7","month":"2018-08","story":"Cable networks are speeding up TV shows to cram in ads","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My favourite is CSI New York","text":"My favourite is CSI New York - \"I'll create a GUI Interface using Visual Basic, see if I can track an IP address\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":15556852,"author":"andrewwharton","month":"2017-10","story":"What Do IBM Mainframe Computers Look Like Today?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This feels like a weird \"Mainframes are making a comeback\", submarine article[1] targeted at people likely to buy stock, especially the \"Mainframes Today\" section. There also seems to be a (legally obligated?) disclaimer at the top that they're a stock holder, quickly moving on to why you should buy IBM stock, with enough \"build a GUI in visual basic to track their IP address\"[2] tech-speak gobbledygook to confuse/convince the average investor. That's the only sense I can make of it... [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14982933,"author":"Sleeep","month":"2017-08","story":"Don't Take Security Advice from SEO Experts or Psychics","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The solution to this has been know for a while, its creating a GUI interface in Visual Basic to track the domain's IP address.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14789350,"author":"plus","month":"2017-07","story":"Custos is using Bitcoin to turn digital pirates against each other","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't want to be confrontational, but I really don't understand the point you're trying to make. Why a neural network, why VLC? I understand that you are suggesting it would be possible to strip the watermark, but the way you've said it sounds a lot like \"create a GUI interface using visual basic, see if I can track an IP address\"[0]. Which is to say, you've not said what you would do to defeat it, but how you think it would be implemented... which doesn't really tell us anything about HOW you think it could be defeated. >You just can't effectively watermark data intended for human consumption, our tolerance for intentionally induced noise is too high. If this were true, steganography[1] would be useless in practice (it isn't). I would suggest reading up on digital watermarking[2]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13126027,"author":"hacker_9","month":"2016-12","story":"Mr. Robot Killed the Hollywood Hacker","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Both still make me laugh after the 100th time.","text":"and this [1]! Both still make me laugh after the 100th time. I'd love to have been in the room when the writers actually thought these scenes up. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10103293,"author":"lotharbot","month":"2015-08","story":"Exploring the Hacker Tools of Mr. Robot","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was really confused by this headline -- having not seen the show, but knowing an indie video game by the same name ( [LINK] ) that used the word \"hacking\" to describe RPG-ish battles set inside the circuitry of other robots. It is refreshing to see on-screen depictions of hacking that use legitimate tools in ways they're actually used in, instead of \"GUI interface using visual basic to track the killers IP address\" ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":9631301,"author":"lawl","month":"2015-05","story":"Mr. Robot","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Honestly, the only reason I watched the episode (just now) was that I got linked this clip [0] earlier today. Didn't know it was this show back then, recognized it now. But I have to agree, the lingo is acceptable, although not really perfect or good. I.e. Tor is unsecure because apparently if you control the exit node you can track the origin, which is obviously not true. But hey, they talk about Tor and exit nodes in one sentence that almost makes sense, so that's pretty good, apart from me feeling that claiming Tor isn't secure in a TV show is kind of a campaign... Between creating a GUI in visual basic to track an IP address [1] and reality I'd say it's kind of in the middle. But it's not \"BEEP BEEP COMPUTER THINGS ARE HAPPENING\". [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8105364,"author":"sjtgraham","month":"2014-07","story":"The Business of Fake Hollywood Money","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Can't wait to see fake hollywood bitcoins in the movies. I'm sure they'll just 'cat /dev/urandom' for awhile and call it good. You overestimate Hollywood tremendously. They'll create a GUI interface using Visual Basic and see if they can track an IP address. [1] [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":7053050,"author":"jliechti1","month":"2014-01","story":"The Hacker Who Cracked the Code in Iron Man and The Social Network","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Link to clip","text":"I wonder if the show CSI used a Markov Chain to create the last line in this scene: \"For weeks I've been investigating...the killer with a certain morbid fascination...\" \"This is in real time!\" \"I'll create a GUI interface using Visual Basic...see if I can track an IP address!\" Link to clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6565551,"author":"rschmitty","month":"2013-10","story":"Visual Studio 2013 released to web","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ya, I can't begin to tell you the number of times I have been made fun of by ___ text editor fanatics for using an IDE that has wizard clicky things/drag drop components etc [VIDEO] things like this never help"},{"id":6234685,"author":"timothy89","month":"2013-08","story":"Generate Hollywood-grade technical jargon","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"I'll create a GUI interface using Visual Basic. To see if I can track an IP address.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":6133848,"author":"griffordson","month":"2013-07","story":"XKeyscore: NSA program collects 'nearly everything a user does on the internet'","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thanks, there isn't much funny about any of this, but that got a legitimate laugh out of me. For anyone who doesn't know the reference, a clip from CSI: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4284078,"author":"csense","month":"2012-07","story":"Let’s Convince the Media to Stop Stereotyping Hackers as Criminals","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think that ship's already sailed. There's a confusion in the mind of the general public between \"someone who knows programming\" and \"someone who specializes in breaking into computer systems.\" In case you haven't watched any TV or movies in the last 20 years, here's the news: Any computer expert, regardless of whether they're a good guy or bad guy, can defeat any security system within a matter of minutes, even (or perhaps especially?) those belonging to police/intelligence/military/shadowy evil organization bent on world domination. As we all know, that kind stuff is really just a matter of coding up a GUI in Visual Basic to track the IP address. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41724572,"author":"tivert","month":"2024-10","story":"GitHub's Copilot lies about its documentation. Why would I trust it with my code","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it's even more ridiculous nonsense fantasy than a typical doctor or a lawyer show","text":"> Most non-technical business stakeholders don't really understand what we do. You don't see shows about awesome devs doing awesome shit, like you do for lawyers, doctors, business people, etc. All they know about us is that we're nerds who work like two hours a day and can't be bothered to go into the office. You do, but it's even more ridiculous nonsense fantasy than a typical doctor or a lawyer show (e.g. all the hacker girl scenes like [VIDEO] , [LINK] )."}]},"k4RRi_ntQc8":{"wy":"Used as a joke or aside about bubble sort and as evidence that Obama was tech savvy, not as something recommended for its content.","m":[{"id":5574917,"author":"mortdeus","month":"2013-04","story":"Obama threatens veto of CISPA bill","stance":"reference","why":"Obama's answer on a computer science question.","quote":"Obama is somewhat tech savvy.","text":"While I'm sure the President doesn't research all laws he signs, Obama is somewhat tech savvy. [VIDEO] I wouldn't be surprised if he made the threat based on his own personal moral ideology."},{"id":21443311,"author":"dickeytk","month":"2019-11","story":"Programmers can’t write algorithms without help","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Even Obama knows this","text":"I get your point, but why would you pick bubble sort? That’s one any engineer should be able to implement without any help. It’s the more complex/useful ones worth discussing. Even Obama knows this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17117172,"author":"xxs","month":"2018-05","story":"Apps for which macOS applies compatibillity fixes","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Bubble sort is never a good choice unless so extremely lazy or what to impersonate Obama","text":"Bubble sort is never a good choice unless so extremely lazy or what to impersonate Obama...[0] There are dual-pivot quicksort, smooth sort (in-place, based on Leonardo numbers and takes O(n) time to process a presorted array and O(n log n) in the worst case, and achieves nearly-linear performance on many nearly-sorted inputs), TimSort (that's stable as plus). Morealso changing stable to unstable sort is usually a very bad move. [0]: [VIDEO]"}]},"k5eL_al_m7Q":{"wy":"HN cites it as a playful example of attacking crowd-sourced traffic data and of how automated systems can be fooled.","q":{"x":"He put 99 smart phones in a wagon with all of them running Google maps in drive mode.","i":37174428,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":45365173,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2025-09","story":"That Secret Service SIM farm story is bogus","stance":"reference","why":"Artist faked a traffic jam with 99 phones.","quote":"","text":"Maybe they were going to use them to hack Google Maps and fake traffic jams! An Artist Used 99 Phones to Fake a Google Maps Traffic Jam: [LINK] Google Maps Hacks by Simon Weckert [VIDEO] >99 smartphones are transported in a handcart to generate virtual traffic jam in Google Maps.Through this activity, it is possible to turn a green street red which has an impact in the physical world by navigating cars on another route to avoid being stuck in traffic. #googlemapshacks"},{"id":40362139,"author":"irjustin","month":"2024-05","story":"One malicious car could trick smart traffic control systems in the US (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Fun traffic attack example using a wagon of phones.","quote":"I like the wagon of smart phones attack","text":"As far as traffic attack vectors go, this is semi-intersting. I like the wagon of smart phones attack[0]. Seems more fun and easy to do. I'll bet you could get all of Waymo's cars to reroute or force them to funnel into one street. THAT would be a cool show. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37174428,"author":"mabbo","month":"2023-08","story":"Microsoft AI suggests Ottawa food bank as a “cannot miss” tourist spot","stance":"reference","why":"99 phones in a wagon fake a traffic jam.","quote":"He put 99 smart phones in a wagon with all of them running Google maps in drive mode.","text":"[VIDEO] He put 99 smart phones in a wagon with all of them running Google maps in drive mode. Google interpreted this as a traffic jam, and made other cars avoid the area."}]},"lNX0wCdD2LA":{"wy":"Used as a visual example of how foveated rendering can cut the GPU power needed for VR.","q":{"x":"This is what contrast-preserving foveated rendering looks like in VR","i":13785529,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":19263271,"author":"joefourier","month":"2019-02","story":"How does the Hololens 2 matter?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of foveated, multi-resolution rendering for high field of view","quote":"See: With current GPUs and game engines, you need multiple cameras to achieve such effect","text":"Then you render with multiple cameras rotated around their centre, using the same techniques as cubemap rendering. With high field of view, rendering a single spherical view with homogeneous resolution would be inefficient for VR, not only due to the lack of hardware support (without noticeable artefacts or some subdivision scheme) and the created distortion, but also due to the foveated nature of human vision. You'd want a small view frustum rendered at a much higher pixel per angle blended with a lower resolution rendering of the entire field of view (optimally with a medium resolution view frustum surrounding the smaller one for a more progressive blending). See: [VIDEO] With current GPUs and game engines, you need multiple cameras to achieve such effect, but it has the advantage of drastically lowering the computing demands for high-resolution VR graphics (compare 8k per eye, vs just two blended 1080p views). You could also ray trace a single view of non-homogeneous resolution that directly takes into account the distortion characteristics of the HMD lenses, but it would likely a lot less efficient than traditional rasterization with multiple blended cameras."},{"id":16621270,"author":"michaelbuckbee","month":"2018-03","story":"Creator","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates foveated rendering enabled by eye tracking","quote":"","text":"I was really happy to see eye tracking in this list as I consider that a \"gateway technology\" that opens up a variety of additional really cool features. The big one is probably foveated rendering [1] which both allows for a more natural look as well as (hopefully) more efficient visuals. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":14274935,"author":"drspacemonkey","month":"2017-05","story":"Facebook Is Shutting Down VR Content Division Oculus Story Studio","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how foveated rendering cuts the horsepower needed for VR","quote":"it can greatly reduce the required horsepower to render a convincing VR environment","text":">And that power could be invested into more convincing graphics and lower latency. Fortunately, that problem is pretty close to being solved on a consumer level. A foveated rendering addon module for the Vive is expected to be shipping soon in China, by Q3 in North America [0]. For anyone unfamiliar with the term, foveated rendering means only rendering high-resolution images in your field of view. Basically, you don't see full detail across your entire field of view - only at the point where your eyes are focused. Everything outside that point can be rendered in lower resolution and you'd never notice the difference. It requires high-speed eye tracking to pull off, but it can greatly reduce the required horsepower to render a convincing VR environment [1]. [0] - [LINK] [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":13785529,"author":"Sephr","month":"2017-03","story":"First look: Snapdragon 835 VR Developer Kit headset","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates contrast-preserving foveated rendering in VR","quote":"This is what contrast-preserving foveated rendering looks like in VR","text":"Foveated rendering is going to be the most significant step towards making mobile VR viable, so this is very good news. I'm curious as to how they will handle eye tracking. I hope it uses capacitive lenses that can track your pupil bulges instead of unweildly and power hungry internal cameras + CV processing. This is what contrast-preserving foveated rendering looks like in VR: [VIDEO]"}]},"oLt5qSm9U80":{"wy":"Linked in threads on language and Japan as a famous, exaggerated sketch about natives not accepting foreigners speaking their language.","q":{"x":"There is a famous video about that","i":13048646,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":42100969,"author":"enteeentee","month":"2024-11","story":"The English Paradox: Four decades of life and language in Japan","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"“No matter how long you live in Japan… “ bit more like even if you are born in Japan with a non-Japanese heritage you will never be Japanese. There are people born in Japan, raised in Japan that aren’t considered ok Japanese. Some don’t consider themselves Japanese because that’s the cultural norm. Many other people in other countries would consider themselves a citizen of that country if born there. Japan is different. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13048646,"author":"titanix2","month":"2016-11","story":"Adapting to American culture as a Chinese student","stance":"reference","why":"Famous video about people refusing to understand foreigners speaking their language.","quote":"There is a famous video about that","text":"\"Japanese people will rarely call out any grammar/pronunciation error\" & \"In Japan, if you speak Japanese well, eventually they stop forgiving you as \"oh, he's just a foreigner\", and they hold you to similar standards.\" There is also something else I notice: some people will decide they will not understand you because you look/are foreign no matter how hard you try or even if you don't make not many mistakes. There is a famous video about that[1] but this is not limited to Japan, I also saw it in France too with foreigners trying to speak French. Also even in a cultural area there can be wide differences. Almost all Mandarin speakers I interact with (be they Taiwanese, mainlanders or huaqiao) seemed quite happy to talk their language with me. The Japanese not so much. I even sometimes hide my Japanese language ability and play it dumb to fit more in a group. And I plan not to expose a too great ability the day I got it unless necessary precisely too avoid being judged to harsh by the local standards. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10850101,"author":"jason_tko","month":"2016-01","story":"The Disappearance of Self in Japan","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've lived in Japan for 14 years, and I'm doing a startup here. In my experience, if you stay in Japan for more than a year, you go through several phases. The first phase is confusion. Why am I being treated differently, even though I'm doing and saying the same things as another Japanese person. The second phase that quickly follows is anger. This is bullshit! I know my accent and pronunciation is correct! Why are they saying they don't understand me! WTF! The third is dull acceptance. You're not going to change Japan. They're not like this because they're bad people. They're just inexperienced with foreigners and foreign things, and they're overcompensating and trying to be nice in the way they think is nice. The final stage is fun. This is the best stage. You understand all the social patterns and why everything is happening, so you just relax and start enjoying it. When you go into a restaurant and order in Japanese and the waiters response is \"I can't speak English! Sorry!\" you respond in Japanese \"Oh, I'm terribly sorry. Is there anyone here who speaks Japanese?\" This often (not always) has the interesting effect of changing the \"foreigners cannot speak or understand Japanese\" mindset, if only for a moment. Sounds like you're in between stages two and three. It gets better. To quickly respond to your list: 1) It's your face. It's nothing personal. They're just trying to be accommodating. 2) My typical response is \"I've been using chopsticks since I've been four, since "},{"id":9666823,"author":"Nadya","month":"2015-06","story":"A beauty contest winner making Japan look at itself","stance":"reference","why":"Comedy sketch about foreigners speaking Japanese not being understood.","quote":"there wouldn't be this comedy sketch about it","text":"If your Japanese is near-perfect you can tell them to let you enter the \"no foreigners\" nightclubs and bars claiming you were born and raised in Japan. Just pick a place as your hometown. If it were for racist reasons, they wouldn't let you enter simply because you don't \"look Japanese\". Not to say the issue doesn't exist at all. Just that it isn't prevalent. If it didn't exist, there wouldn't be this comedy sketch about it: [VIDEO] Although the scenario I experienced was that the waitress asked my Japanese friend what I wanted to order rather than asking me. Assuming I didn't speak Japanese. At most places I ordered food at - they would ask me what I wanted directly. So I wouldn't say it's a common experience either. Just that it does happen and it's because few foreigners speak Japanese! It's a habitual thing, not a racist thing. It happened more in the touristy parts of Tokyo than anywhere else (even in the touristy parts of Kyoto!)"},{"id":18574240,"author":"Nadya","month":"2018-12","story":"Japan Is Giving Away 8M Abandoned Houses","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This video pokes some fun at it, taken to an extreme for comedic purpose of course","text":"You misunderstood. You need to look Japanese. It doesn't matter if you actually are Japanese. This video pokes some fun at it, taken to an extreme for comedic purpose of course: [VIDEO]"}]},"poyf3Cnb-MQ":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a reference for watching Shkreli use spreadsheets, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":28971353,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2021-10","story":"Federal judges or their brokers traded stocks of litigants during cases","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence Shkreli is not dumb","quote":"He's by no means dumb [1,2]","text":"Indeed, moreso pride and lack of wisdom. Perhaps a bit of martyrdom. Shkreli liked playing around with these people who literally wanted to slay him. I don't think he understood how seriously evil and petty boomers can be, and how most judges are from that generation. He took one for the team in a sense, showing how the justice system serves as the instrument of vengeance for the executive and legislative branches. He's by no means dumb [1,2] but yes, honest to the point of being blunt. Very much in the spirit of Mike Tyson who is also extremely intelligent but often portrayed as a dolt. [3,4,5] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":21847732,"author":"eindiran","month":"2019-12","story":"You Suck at Excel (2015) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Streams showing Shkreli using Excel","quote":"These are some of the videos in that series where you can see him use Excel","text":"In the 2016 discussion, the link to the Martin Shkreli video is down. These are some of the videos in that series where you can see him use Excel: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22731148,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-03","story":"Neanderthals Feasted on Seafood, Seabirds, Perhaps Even Dolphins","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"If you eat Martin Shkreli's brain, maybe you'll inherit his skill with spreadsheets!","text":"If you eat Martin Shkreli's brain, maybe you'll inherit his skill with spreadsheets! Worth a try. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"rwgpTDluufY":{"wy":"Cited in right-to-repair threads as a repair-shop view of why Apple's program is impractical.","q":{"x":"AFAIK Louis Rossmann has criticized Apple for creating a really unpractical program","i":35543869,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39112664,"author":"wkat4242","month":"2024-01","story":"Samsung expands DIY repair program, adds Galaxy S23 and Fold 5 in US","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's repair program priced to discourage DIY","quote":"Of course, the pricing is meant to disincentivise you from doing it.","text":"Of course, the pricing is meant to disincentivise you from doing it. Apple is doing the exact same ( [VIDEO] ). They don't want to enable DIY repair but they do this because they have to in some areas. I assume this is also the reason they won't just sell you a battery without a screen."},{"id":35543869,"author":"elsjaako","month":"2023-04","story":"Colorado passes agricultural Right to Repair","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann calls Apple's repair program practically worthless","quote":"AFAIK Louis Rossmann has criticized Apple for creating a really unpractical program","text":"AFAIK Louis Rossmann has criticized Apple for creating a really unpractical program, calling it \"virtually worthless\": [VIDEO] Or is there a more recent video where he changes his mind?"},{"id":25140688,"author":"perlpimp","month":"2020-11","story":"Apple Announces App Store Small Business Program","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann on Apple's small business repair initiative","quote":"Louis has a video on such efforts by apple albeit in different area","text":"Louis has a video on such efforts by apple albeit in different area however also \"small bussienss\" initiative. [VIDEO]"}]},"tIIJME8-au8":{"wy":"HN links it as the go-to example of fast robot reactions and robots playing table tennis, with some skepticism that it was staged marketing.","q":{"x":"Kuka had this demo years ago","i":44250244,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44250244,"author":"charleszyong","month":"2025-06","story":"Launch HN: Vassar Robotics (YC X25) – $219 robot arm that learns new skills","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier Kuka demo of fast robot reactions","quote":"Kuka had this demo years ago","text":"Kuka had this demo years ago: [VIDEO] Nowadays, if you’re running a 2–3B parameter foundation model on a standard computer, you’ll probably get a rollout frequency of 1 to 10 Hz. ACT would be faster, but I still don’t think it’s fast enough."},{"id":32434680,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2022-08","story":"Xiaomi beats Tesla to building a working robot","stance":"reference","why":"Kuka robot playing table tennis","quote":"Kuka makes industrial robots as part of its core business and for playing table tennis","text":"I think lots of companies have beaten Tesla to a working robot. Honda's ASIMO has been around for over 20 years: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Kuka makes industrial robots as part of its core business and for playing table tennis: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":28272260,"author":"naveen99","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla Announces a Humanoid Robot “Tesla Bot”","stance":"reference","why":"Robot playing ping pong against a pro","quote":"Or as good as Tim with paddles ?","text":"Is it too much to ask for a humanoid robot to play ping pong with nunchucks as well as Bruce Lee ? Or as good as Tim with paddles ? [LINK] [VIDEO] Or is this the best we can do in my lifetime : robotic soccer [LINK]"},{"id":18621221,"author":"mtrazzi","month":"2018-12","story":"Spinning Up a Pong AI with Deep Reinforcement Learning","stance":"reference","why":"Robots playing table tennis","quote":"They even have robots playing real Pong!","text":"Hum, have not tried that but I guess the robotic part and the computer vision would be pretty standard, so it would be feasible. PS:They even have robots playing real Pong! [VIDEO]"},{"id":12654865,"author":"gregpilling","month":"2016-10","story":"Google Canceled the Launch of a Robotic Arm","stance":"reference","why":"Example of high-end industrial robot arms","quote":"","text":"I own a Panasonic Robot welding cell, and I keep up with numbers of robots sold like that - I know your numbers are correct. My experience with programming the Panasonic is that there is quite a learning curve to get over, to do the most basic things with it. I think the small numbers of robot adoption could be a reflection on this difficulty, combined with high cost. My cell was $85,000. The robot was a great value at that price though, it generates about $2000 an hour worth of product. Paid for in 40 hours of welding, good for 10,000 more hours before its worn out. The Baxter should prove me wrong, but I think it is just an underperforming platform. The current owners of robot tech would view it as under capable compared to arms from Kuka or Motoman or Panasonic etc. [VIDEO] think the non-robot users, those companies getting a first robot, would find Baxter easy to use but would be less inclined to go buy one; getting a robot is a big step. I have to think that ROS getting better and better, with offline programming, will help adoption. We are at the UNIX terminal phase of computing, or at best we are at a Windows 3.1 level. Nobody has made the iphone of robots yet, one that is very capable yet easy to program. Once we can get to a few swipes to get result, robot placement will increase rapidly. I love how repeatable the result is, and how fast it produces parts. It is about 3x more productive than my most productive welder, with equal or better accuracy (we still hand weld"},{"id":9363864,"author":"neya","month":"2015-04","story":"You can load a die but you can't bias a coin (2002) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Robot versus table tennis pro match","quote":"For your viewing pleasure","text":"That's a pretty cool idea! Let me know how it goes :) I think it's totally possible. For your viewing pleasure: [VIDEO] Cheers."},{"id":47870784,"author":"OisinMoran","month":"2026-04","story":"Ping-pong robot Ace makes history by beating top-level human players","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"ended up just being a marketing stunt and felt like a fixed fight","text":"This is great, I remember being sorely disappointed by the hyped up Timo Boll vs Kuka robot 12 years ago. I thought it was going to be a real match and seemed like the robot would destroy him, but ended up just being a marketing stunt and felt like a fixed fight, with no real digging into the tech or why the robot \"lost\". Still some cool footage: [VIDEO]"}]},"tgODUgHeT5Y":{"wy":"HN cites it as a classic example of public skepticism toward a new technology that later proved transformative.","q":{"x":"The infamous Bill Gates and David Letterman interview was in 1995","i":35826743,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35826743,"author":"pcthrowaway","month":"2023-05","story":"Google “We Have No Moat, and Neither Does OpenAI”","stance":"reference","why":"1995 interview showing people not grasping the internet.","quote":"The infamous Bill Gates and David Letterman interview was in 1995","text":"The infamous Bill Gates and David Letterman interview was in 1995: [VIDEO] Lots of people definitely didn't understand what a big deal it was going to be then. In October 1994 a Wired journalist registered mcdonalds.com and then tried to give it to Mcdonalds, but couldn't reach anyone who understood the importance of domain name registration: [LINK] In my recollection it really wasn't til 1998-2001 or so that people (where I lived in the southern U.S. anyway) really started to take notice."},{"id":33853483,"author":"EGreg","month":"2022-12","story":"Stripe launches a fiat-to-crypto onramp","stance":"reference","why":"Example of dismissing the internet when new.","quote":"They said exactly this about the Internet… why do you need it?","text":"Why can’t this argument be used against any new technology? They said exactly this about the Internet… why do you need it? See all the well established solutions at the time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33993295,"author":"chrisco255","month":"2022-12","story":"Gen Z workers are not tech-savvy in the workplace – and it’s a growing problem","stance":"reference","why":"1995 Gates and Letterman show public attitude toward computers.","quote":"conversations between David Letterman and Bill Gates regarding the internet in the 1995","text":"If your grandfather had a tech job in Silicon Valley, and your dad programmed a university mainframe, you aren't representative of the general public. The general public didn't really start to adopt personal computers en masse until the 90s. And if you look at, for example, conversations between David Letterman and Bill Gates regarding the internet in the 1995 ( [VIDEO] ), and Gates tries to explain to him the advantage of it and David pans that he doesn't get it, with the audience laughing along to dismissive jokes about it being unnecessary complexity versus radio and cassette tapes. That was the prevailing attitude regarding computers even well into the 90s (now, they're pervasive)."},{"id":33736308,"author":"EGreg","month":"2022-11","story":"Chief scientist of major corporation can’t handle criticism of the work he hypes","stance":"reference","why":"Gates and Letterman on the internet as example of dismissal.","quote":"Watch this exchange, for instance, between Bill Gates and David Letterman... about \"this internet thing","text":"Well, that sounds exactly like what people said about all the other technologies that were \"on a computer\". Watch this exchange, for instance, between Bill Gates and David Letterman... about \"this internet thing\" [VIDEO] Or the Today show wondering what the point is: [LINK] Technology empowers individuals and smaller communities. That's what it does throughout history. Personal computers. Personal printers. Now you can send email instead of relying on a centralized post office. VOIP disrupted Ma Bell monopolies in our lifetimes, instead of $3 a minute phone calls you can now do full videoonferencing for free. The Web instead of gatekeepers at radio, TV, magazines, newspapers, etc. In all of those cases, you could question why \"on a computer\" matters. Who needs email when there are phonecalls? Who needs the Web anyway when there is email? Who needs online dating sites when there are matchmakers? Nathan Myrhvold at Microsoft told people at Excite that \"search is not a business\". Economist Paul Krugman wrote that by 2005, it would become clear that the Internet's effect on the economy is no greater than the fax machine's. Well, these smart contracts are programmable, and you program against one widespread platform, like JVM or in this case EVM. That's a huge benefit. You can have programmable money, programmable elections, without having to deal with thousands of APIs, or as you are saying \"normal\" physical currency. Governments in fact also want to phase out cash, and even bank"}]},"tjgtLSHhTPg":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of Apple praising the word crazy, though some call it vague feel-good marketing at odds with today's Apple.","m":[{"id":28248314,"author":"29083011397778","month":"2021-08","story":"Dumb Phone","stance":"reference","why":"Apple ad celebrating unconventional ideas","quote":"","text":"I can't say which distro grandparent is using on their Pinephone, but when they say split-screen they could very well mean, literally, what one would find on a desktop. While personally, this sounds cumbersome to me, some people have very unusual ways of using their Pinephones. To me, that's awesome - we've settled on \"how one uses a mobile phone\" based on what Google and Apple have designed. Having new, off-the-wall ideas will lead to some people doing slightly insane things, and I say that in the most positive way possible. Finally, as long as we're straying firmly off-topic: What some people are doing with their mobile Linux devices always reminds me of an Apple ad [0], from when \"Think Different\" was still the motto of an underdog. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27611729,"author":"mastazi","month":"2021-06","story":"Alan Turing's biggest fan remains the real enigma","stance":"reference","why":"Ad showing crazy as a positive connotation","quote":"Edit: the ad was this one","text":"I think it's a matter of angle, in western popular culture \"crazy\" often has a positive connotation, for example I remember seeing an ad with the line \"For the crazy ones\". In other instances, it's the opposite, in my wife's home country for example, \"crazy\" is used as an insult and it's highly offensive. Edit: the ad was this one [VIDEO]"},{"id":6035524,"author":"protomyth","month":"2013-07","story":"China’s plan to build the longest underwater tunnel on the planet","stance":"reference","why":"Apple ad using the word crazy positively","quote":"Apple used the word in a ad campaign[1] early in Steve Job's[2] return.","text":"First, \"crazy\" isn't automatically a negative term. It can even be seen as positive. Apple used the word in a ad campaign[1] early in Steve Job's[2] return. As to the infrastructure, it really has nothing to do with some fear of socialism. It is very much the problem of cost s, regulatory hurdles, and (increasingly) lack of skilled workers to carry out the construction. Court costs are the biggest killer. Every group is going to bring a lawsuit in any big infrastructure project. China doesn't have to worry about that[3]. For all the talk, if high speed rail was $1 million per mile and we had no lawsuits, we would have the things all over the place. 1) [VIDEO] 2) [LINK] 3) sometimes this hurts more than helps when problems arise in the project"},{"id":21233044,"author":"fimdomeio","month":"2019-10","story":"Apple told some Apple TV+ show developers not to anger China","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"of course Apple is not what it used to be and this is just marketing talk","text":"of course Apple is not what it used to be and this is just marketing talk but just look at the \"here's to the crazy ones Apple comercial\": [VIDEO] Can any entity glorify civil disobedience historical figures and bend to the wills of a country known for gross human rights violations? Of course it can but it shouldn't."},{"id":5992578,"author":"alex_c","month":"2013-07","story":"Long copy isn't back","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Both are content-free, vague, feel good ads.","text":"I'll play devil's advocate and ask: how is this different from the celebrated \"Here's to the crazy ones\" ads? Granted, these ads are more bland and forgettable, but that's a difference of degree, not kind. Compare: [VIDEO] [LINK] Both are content-free, vague, feel good ads. I feel like all the criticism in the article would also apply to \"Here's to the crazy ones\", perhaps even more so - at least the new ads actually outline Apple's design philosophy, while the older ad really has nothing to do with... well, anything. So I'll grant that the execution in the new ad falls somewhat short, but does that simply mean the ads don't go far enough with vacuous feel-good stuff?"}]},"vMITcQUe-9M":{"wy":"Posted as a joke in threads about showers and product restrictions.","m":[{"id":38392447,"author":"lqet","month":"2023-11","story":"The curious case of the abominable shower","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It turned out that the Elms had a shower faucet like nothing the staff had ever seen. Water from multiple nozzles sprayed out in every direction with needle-like intensity and astonishing force. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21536194,"author":"crazyjncsu","month":"2019-11","story":"Mist Showers: Sustainable Decadence?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Thanks, but I'll stick with my Commando 450","text":"Thanks, but I'll stick with my Commando 450: [VIDEO] But seriously, even I get concerned with the green movements when they seem to be coming for each part of my life I hold sacred-- a nice warm shower (or two!) is a highlight of my day. And to rationalize: I am also constantly looking for ways to reduce energy usage, so I looked into capturing the heat lost from a shower. First I measured the difference in the shower head outlet temperature and the drain temperature. I found about 75% of the heat is released before even entering the drain. So it goes into my body (yay), the air, or the floor system. I suspect the majority of the next 25% is released in the drain system that is either part of the conditioned space of the house or in the semi-conditioned crawlspace. So the heating cost/fuel of the water is offsetting my HVAC cost/fuel. Additionally, the shower boosts humidity which is the #1 energy efficient method of feeling warmer in a cold, dry house. Notes: If you can leave the water to cool down by not draining a tub, you'll gain even more. Or take a bath. If you're using electricity to resistant-heat your water (highly inefficient), you should address that first. This doesn't apply in the summer, so I bathe in the outdoor pool, usually."},{"id":14737430,"author":"Jach","month":"2017-07","story":"EU Prepares \"Right to Repair\" Legislation to Fight Short Product Lifespans","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Low-flow showers and toilets are the bane of many people's existences. You might be happy with the \"well designed\" ones but a lot of people aren't. (Or don't have whatever the well designed one is -- my newly built office has had so many complaints with its toilets they're replacing them building wide, bets on how much better the new ones will be?) Some people will find solutions that would result in fines if found out. And it's not a new thing either: [VIDEO] Not sure what is meant by working gas can on the car... I personally just hate the gas hoses at stations which have the pressure contraption so I have to keep it physically pushed in to the hole and can't say let it start and clean my windshield, but it also has a hyper sensitive fume/fluid sensor so it cuts off if I try to have a flow over 50%. I love older stations with older pump hoses, no problems. Don't have to break the law for them, yet. Clean clothes and home probably refer to some effective cleaning agents being banned because they're known to the state of California to cause cancer or something. I can't say I have that problem but I might not know what I'm missing."}]},"wPiLLplofYw":{"wy":"Used as a vivid example of spinal pattern generators and morphological control in locomotion discussions.","q":{"x":"the decerebrate cat experiments illustrate this perfectly","i":18064232,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":38631370,"author":"jampekka","month":"2023-12","story":"Tesla FSD Timeline","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a cat walking without a brain.","quote":"a cat can walk without brains [1]","text":"If the babies don't see objects, they don't \"develop\" object permanence. Yeah, here we can summon the \"use it or use it\" copeout, but then the nativist account becomes unfalsifiable. Being able to crawl within minutes (and even somewhat run within hours as horses do) is mostly due to anatomy and \"reflex pathways\". E.g. a cat can walk without brains [1], as can famosly headless chicken. Human babies too have these kinds of reflexes but they are \"abandoned\" well before the baby learns to walk. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18064232,"author":"1_over_n","month":"2018-09","story":"Neuromodulation of spinal networks enables stepping after complete paraplegia","stance":"reference","why":"Decerebrate cat experiments illustrate spinal walking control.","quote":"the decerebrate cat experiments illustrate this perfectly","text":"the decerebrate cat experiments illustrate this perfectly (although perhaps people dont like to talk about it because of the ethics of the experiments) [VIDEO]"},{"id":16892931,"author":"ItsMe000001","month":"2018-04","story":"A Feasible Approach to Human Stasis for Long-Duration Deep Space Missions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Uhm... all kinds of animals actually, but you need ethics approval if you use vertebrates. And I don't think you will find non-crazy volunteers for the invasive experiments... I took neuroscience courses - and a lot of what I learned was made possible by such experiments, so I'm not against them. However, the cost for the animals that get selected is high. You can't do that with humans, only the most benign experiments. But many experiments involves cutting open the head. inserting electrodes at the very least, sometimes you want to see the brain too - there are things you can do, like two-photon microscopy (example: [LINK] -- you have to expose the brain), or inject dyes, and other options). A possible setup (for invasive experiments) is that the animal is drugged, the brain exposed, electrodes inserted, the head fixated. Now you can, stimulate the retina, or the ears, or whiskers, and follow what happens in the living brain. Here is an example for what you get from such an experiment: [LINK] > Mice were head-fixed using a titanium head postimplant... For implantation, mice were anesthetized with isoflurane..., and maintained at 37°C body temperature on a thermal blanket (Harvard Apparatus). Following a local injection of Marcaine (50 μl), the scalp and periosteum over the dorsal surface of the skull were removed. The skull was then coated with a thin layer of cyanoacrylate adhesive (Krazy Glue, Elmer's Products).... (etc.) What sane human would allow an experiment like this"},{"id":8413690,"author":"icegreentea","month":"2014-10","story":"Our Understanding of Giraffes Does Not Measure Up","stance":"reference","why":"Decerebrate cat on a treadmill shows spinal pattern generators.","quote":"here's a 'decerberated' cat on a treadmill.","text":"Remember that a lot of 'standard' movements aren't fully controlled from the brain. Instead, the brain generators modulating signals to structures/systems lower in the spinal - ie increasing or decreasing your rate of walking. There's lots of evidence of these 'Central Pattern Generators' being located below the brain - which would reduce signal lag. For a somewhat disturbing example of this, here's a 'decerberated' cat on a treadmill. [VIDEO] That said, the fact that any vertebrae (even our selves) are able to move and react so well is pretty amazing, given that our own neural lag is in the ~100ms range. It's really a testament to the robustment of the multi-tier control that we (animals in general.. but especially large animals) have evolved."}]}});
